<h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Marks the dual form of nouns and adjectives. Attaches to the singular form if feminine or the plural form if masculine.</li><li>Sometimes added to suffix pronouns attached to dual nouns.</li><li>Converts nouns (including proper nouns and <i>nisba</i>s used nominally), prepositions, independent pronouns, and numerals into masculine adjectives&#58; the masculine <i>nisba</i> adjective ending.</li><li>Forms i-stem masculine nouns from roots.</li><li>Forms prepositional adverbs from certain prepositions.</li><li>Forms the infinitive of anomalous verbs, weak verbs (except for fourth weak verbs with a geminated stem), and causative biliteral verbs.</li><li>Optionally marks the masculine imperfective active participle, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li><li>Optionally marks the masculine geminated perfective passive participle of strong biliteral verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li></ol><p>Hypothesized to be from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-i</i> (genitive-possessive case ending).</p><p>Hypothesized to be from earlier <i>&#42;-iu</i> or <i>&#42;-iju</i>, from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-u</i> (nominative case ending) attached to a root ending in <i>&#42;-i</i> or <i>&#42;-ij</i>.</p><p>From the earlier infinitival ending <i>-t</i>; the consonant of this suffix became silent over time, leaving its remaining vowel to be represented by <i>-j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to the roots of cardinal numbers to form the ordinal numbers second through ninth.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form feminine singular forms of nouns.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Used to form feminine singular forms of adjectives.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) An unpronounced graphical suffix occasionally added to adjectives without regard for gender.</li><li>Converts nouns and prepositions into feminine adjectives&#58; the feminine <i>nisba</i> adjective ending.</li><li>Forms adverbs from certain adjectives; -ly</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Forms the infinitive of anomalous verbs, weak verbs (except for fourth weak verbs with a geminated stem), and causative biliteral verbs.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Forms the pronominal state of the infinitive of transitive third-weak verbs, and occasionally also biliteral and triliteral verbs, used when a suffix pronoun is attached to the infinitive.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Forms the complementary infinitive of all verbs.</li><li>Forms the subjunctive of the irregular verb <i>jnj</i> and the anomalous verb <i>jwj</i>.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Forms the terminative of all verbs.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-t, &#42;-Vt</i> (feminine suffix). See also Arabic ـَة (-a).</p><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-i</i> (genitive-possessive case ending) + the ancestor of the above suffix <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the feminine dual forms of nouns and adjectives</li><li>Used to form the (stem of the) prospective participle.</li></ol><p><i>-t</i> (“feminine ending”) + <i>-j</i> (“dual ending”).</p></html>:/w/
<h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form the masculine plural forms of nouns and adjectives</li><li>Forms a-stem masculine nouns from roots.</li><li>Forms u-stem masculine nouns from roots.</li><li>Forms agent nouns from verbs.</li><li>Forms adverbs from certain adjectives; -ly</li><li>Forms prepositional adverbs from certain prepositions.</li><li>Forms the negatival complement of all verbs except geminate and anomalous verbs.</li><li>Forms the prospective of all verbs except second geminate, strong bi- and triliteral, and (usually) anomalous verbs.</li><li>Forms the prospective passive of all verbs except fourth weak, strong bi- and triliteral, and (usually) anomalous verbs.</li><li>Forms the perfect passive of all verbs except second geminate verbs.</li><li>Optionally marks the masculine singular imperfective passive participle, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li><li>Optionally marks the masculine singular imperfective relative form of all verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li><li>Optionally marks the masculine singular perfective passive participle of strong verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li></ol><p>Possibly derived from a lengthened form of Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-u</i> (nominative case marker).</p><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-u</i> (nominative case marker).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form regular masculine dual forms of nouns and adjectives</li><li>Attaches to the adjective in an adjectival predicate to give its clause admirative exclamatory force&#58; How very… ! How… !</li></ol><p><i>-w</i> (“masculine plural ending”) + <i>-j</i> (“dual ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form feminine plural forms of nouns and adjectives</li><li>Forms masculine collective nouns.</li><li>Forms the complementary infinitive of weak verbs.</li></ol><p><i>-w</i> (plural suffix) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p><p>Compare Hebrew <i>־וּת</i> (-út, “abstract/collective nouns-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Forms the plural imperative of verbs, particularly weak verbs.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Optionally used to form the subjunctive of weak verbs.</li><li>Forms agent nouns from verbs.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Optionally marks the masculine imperfective active participle, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Optionally marks the masculine singular perfective passive participle of strong verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Optionally marks the perfective passive participle of weak verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Optionally marks the perfective relative form of weak verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Marks the perfective passive participle of mostly third-weak transitive verbs, following the stem.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Occasionally marks the perfective active participle of mostly third-weak transitive verbs, following the stem.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Rarely marks the imperfective relative form of all verbs, intervening between the stem and the gender&#47;number endings.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the complementary infinitive of weak verbs.</li><li>Forms the terminative of third-weak verbs, more frequently when passive than when active.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the negatival complement of fourth weak verbs.</li></ol><p>The first consonant <i>y</i> presumably derives from the final (weak) radical <i>j</i> of a fourth weak verb in contact with the suffix <i>-w</i>, which forms the second consonant of <i>-yw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, him, his (see usage notes)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Egyptian *<i>-su</i>, the back vowel /u/ led to labialization of /s/.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>used in <i>ḫrw.fj</i></li></ol><p><i>.f</i> (“he, him, his”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (dual ending).</p></html>:/aʔ/
<h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I, me, my (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) he, him</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;yi</i> (“me”). Compare with Arabic ـيَ (-ya) and Blin <i>yə</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the consecutive form of all verbs.</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>jn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you, your (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) abbreviation of <i>.kj</i> (“I, me”)</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) abbreviation of <i>.kw</i> (“I, me”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-ka</i> or <i>&#42;-ku</i>. Cognate with Arabic ـكَ (-ka), Iraqw <i>-ku</i> and Hausa <i>kai</i>.</p><p>Compare with the Akkadian 1st person singular stative ending <i>𒆪</i> ((_a)-ku /⁠-āku⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) I, me</li></ol><p><i>.k</i> (“you”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (dual ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) I, me</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the potentialis form of all verbs.</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>kꜣ</i> (“so, then, as a result”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we, us, our (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Forms the perfect of all verbs.</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Forms the perfect relative form of all verbs.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Optionally marks the preterite of all verbs.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Optionally marks the perfective active relative form of all verbs.</li></ol><p>Loprieno hypothesizes development from an earlier form <i>&#42;-ina</i>. In any case, perhaps ultimately from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ˀan-</i> (first-person pronoun (plural exclusive?)); compare Iraqw <i>-ni</i>, Arabic ـنَا (-nā).</p><p>Most likely grammaticalized from the preposition <i>n</i> (“to, for”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we two, us two, ours (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Sometimes used to form the perfect of verbs when the subject is omitted.</li></ol><p><i>.n</i> (we, us, our) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (dual marker).</p><p><i>.n</i> (perfect suffix) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (adverbializing suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Old and Late and possibly Early Middle Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) we, us</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she, her, hers (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Egyptian *<i>-si</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>they, them, their (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Egyptian *<i>-sina</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the two of them, their (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>.ṯ</i> (“you, your”)</li><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) abbreviation of <i>.tj</i> (“you, she, her, they, them”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) you</li><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) she, her, they, them</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) you</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) alternative form of <i>.tjwn</i> (“you”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>.ṯn</i> (“you, your”)</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) alternative form of <i>.tjwn</i> (“you”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>.ṯnj</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;Late Egyptian) used as the impersonal subject of an adverbial predicate or verb form; one, someone or something unspecified</li><li>used as a substitute for noun phrases referring to the king &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;Neo-Middle Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) alternative form of <i>.tj</i> (“you, she, her”)</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) the general (unmarked) stative ending, used with all genders and numbers</li></ol><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;Late Egyptian) forms the passive of most verb forms</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) the two of them</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>they, them, their (<i>see usage notes</i>) &lsqb;from c.1400 <small>B.C.E.</small>&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) he, him, they, them</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) alternative form of <i>.wy</i> (“the two of them”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) alternative form of <i>.wn</i> (“we, us”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) we, us</li></ol><p>Possibly from an earlier <i>.nw</i> or from a rebracketing of <i>-wj</i> (“How...!”) + <i>n</i> (first person plural dependent pronoun).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;attached to a stative verb form) they, them</li><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) the two of them</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attached to a stative verb form</i>) they, them</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms the obligative form of all verbs.</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫr</i> (“must, has to”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you, your (see usage notes)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Egyptian *<i>-kʲi</i>, from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-ki</i> (second-person possessive suffix). Compare Iraqw <i>-ki</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you, your (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Egyptian *<i>-kina</i>, the front vowel /i/ led to a palatalization of /k/.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you two, your/you two’s (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>.ṯn</i> +&lrm; <i>wj</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Babylon (an ancient city, the ancient capital of Babylonia in modern Iraq, built on the banks of the Euphrates)</li><li>Babylonia (an ancient empire and geographic region of Mesopotamia, existing from 1850 <small>BCE</small> to 539 <small>BCE</small>, based around the city of Babylon; at its maximum extent, covering parts of modern-day Iraq, Kuwait, Syria and Iran)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠</i> (Bābilim).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat, region of collarbones</li><li>hole, cavity, niche &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wig (shoulder length ?)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watermelon &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>bdd</i> (“a kind of plant”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“bull”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emmer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) weak or faint</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>) to inhale (a fragrance), to sniff (a pleasant scent) &lsqb;since the 22nd Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe (air)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cense, to fumigate (a building or person) (+ <i>m</i>: with (a scent)) &lsqb;since the 22nd Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of a fragrance) to fill (a building), to permeate via censing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of article of clothing.</li><li>used in <i>ḥbs bhn</i> (“to protect”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan, flabellum made of ostrich feathers or flowers &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>fragrance, pleasant scent &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>fragrant substance or perfume with which the eye is made full &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>bhꜣ</i> (“fan”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>From <i>bhd</i> (“to inhale, to cense”); the expected form <i>&#42;bhd</i> of this noun is unattested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flee, to turn tail &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan, flabellum &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Ehret hypothesizes an origin in Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;-bâh-</i> (“to go secretively”) + <i>&#42;r</i>; as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person on the retreat or in flight, fugitive, retreater &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>bhꜣ</i> (“to flee, to turn tail”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (agent nominalizer).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falcon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to acquire or have bad qualities &lsqb;since the end of the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) bad or evil (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: toward (someone))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) useless or good for nothing</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) in a miserable or wretched state</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of animals and things) to be(come) harmful</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of periods of time) to be(come) unpropitious or calamitous</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of milk) to sour, to go bad</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>bjn</i>: bad, evil</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) badness, evil</li><li>harp</li></ol><p><i>bjn</i> (“bad, evil”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine ending”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, of historical usage, equivalent to English Berenice or Bernice, notably borne by Berenice II</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Βερενίκη</i> (Bereníkē).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey bee</li><li>honey</li><li>a kind of flat loaf of bread &lsqb;since the Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>flat cake (of some given substance) in general</li></ol><p>Heavily disputed. The final <i>-t</i> is usually considered a separate feminine ending in origin rather than a part of the root, but this is not wholly certain. Etymological hypotheses include:</p><p>Several linguists have also proposed an areal connection with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰey-</i> (“bee”), noting the existence of a number of parallels between Afroasiatic and Indo-European bee-keeping terminology.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king of Lower Egypt</li><li>hereditary or ancestral king</li></ol><p>Nominalized from <i>bjt</i> (“bee”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”), literally ’the one of the bee’, the bee being an emblem of Lower Egypt.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mine (place from which ore is extracted)</li><li>mining country, mining region</li><li>ore, metal</li><li>example, model</li></ol><p>Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;biʔr- ~ &#42;buʔr-</i> (“pit, cistern”) (whence Hebrew <i>באר</i> (“well”) and Arabic بِئْر (biʔr, “well”)).</p><p>Cognate with Qimant <i>birr-</i> (“metal”), Somali <i>bir</i> (“iron”), Tera <i>vì</i> (“iron”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meteoric iron</li></ol><p><i>bjꜣ</i> (“metal”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>pt</i> (“sky”), thus ‘metal of the sky’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>character, disposition &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>mood</li><li>a good attribute of a person for which he or she is well-liked</li></ol><p><i>bjꜣ</i> (“example, model”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miracle, marvel</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomorrow, the next day &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>morning &lsqb;18th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) pregnant</li></ol><p>Compare Hebrew <i>בֹּקֶר</i> (bṓqer, “morrow, morning”) or Arabic بُكْرَة (bukra, “early morning”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) not; negates the adverbial sentence&#47;first present, the third future, and the optative</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) not; negates a nominal sentence</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with a following noun or pronoun) without; there not being any …</li></ol><p>Related to (and probably developed from) earlier <i>nn</i> (“not”). The spelling with <i>b-</i> may reflect a graphic phenomenon rather than a sound change. Compare the Late Egyptian development of <i>bw</i> from Earlier Egyptian <i>nj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyramidion &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>bnbn</i> (“Benben, the first mound or stone to arise from the primordial waters at the creation of the world”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of taste) to be(come) sweet</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of disposition) to be(come) pleasant or sweet</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>date (fruit) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pierce, to gore</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) (pair of) nipples (of a woman or man)</li><li>(<i>dual only</i>) (pair of) breasts (of a woman)</li></ol><p>From <i>bn</i> (“breast”) +&lrm; <i>-tj</i> (feminine dual suffix).</p><p>The root is of Niger-Congo origin; cf. Proto-Benue-Congo <i>&#42;-bani</i> (“breast”) (> Proto-Jukunoid <i>&#42;byan</i>), and Proto-Bantu <i>&#42;-béénè</i> (“breast”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) deep-bosomedness, big-breastedness (of women)</li></ol><p><i>bntj</i> +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bennu or phoenix, a divine being and a bird into which the dead could transform.</li><li>alternative form of <i>bnr</i> (“date (fruit)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorpost, doorjamb</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) A kind of semi-precious stone.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>beryl, emerald</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic; compare Hebrew <i>בָּרֶקֶת</i> (bāréqet) and Akkadian (/⁠barraqtu⁠/), as well as other loanwords from Semitic, such as Sanskrit <i>मरकत</i> (marakata) and Ancient Greek <i>σμάραγδος</i> (smáragdos), the latter ultimately the source of English <i>emerald</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bes, the god protector of households and mothers, children and childbirth</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immoral act, wrongdoing, sin &lsqb;since the Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>illegal act, crime</li><li>harm, misfortune</li><li>evildoer, wrongdoer, criminal &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to do wrong, to sin, to be(come) guilty of evil deeds</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place, location</li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) position, location where someone or something is</li><li>thing</li><li>(<i>with following adjective</i>) forms abstract nouns; -ness</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) item in a list</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following verb in the <i>sdm.f</i> form) not; forms the negative aorist of verbs</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following verb in the <i>sdmt.f</i> form (from the Middle Egyptian terminative)) not yet</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;chiefly literary,&#32;with following verb in the <i>sdm.f</i> form) not; forms the negative preterite of verbs</li></ol><p>Ehret hypothesizes an origin in Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;bǔ</i> (“place”) and compares Arabic بِـ (bi-, “in, with, by”); as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking.</p><p>Related to (and probably developed from) earlier <i>nj</i> (“not”). The spelling with <i>b-</i> may reflect a graphic phenomenon rather than a sound change. Compare the Late Egyptian development of <i>bn</i> from Earlier Egyptian <i>nn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good, goodness, fineness, pleasantness</li><li>good or pleasing things, what is good</li><li>bread &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>bw</i> (“place, thing, -ness”) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“pleasing, fine, good”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanctum, sanctuary, sacred room in a temple &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>bw</i> (“place, thing, -ness”) +&lrm; <i>ḏsr</i> (“sacred”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>everyone &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the masses, the common people</li></ol><p><i>bw</i> (“place, thing, -ness”) +&lrm; <i>nb</i> (“every”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abomination, loathsome or detested thing, anathema &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detested one, abominable one &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Nominalized from <i>bwt</i> (“abomination”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to emerge</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of people) to introduce (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring forth</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spit</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to vomit</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the Nile and inundation) to gush &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spit out, to spew forth</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of the sources of the Nile) to gush forth (water) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of fields) to gush with (verdure, plants) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of the heart) to reveal, to betray (one’s thoughts) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rebel or be rebellious (+ <i>ḥr</i>: against) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) (<i>of water</i>) to be agitated &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to be(come) obligated to perform corvee labor</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) an agent for tanning leather</li><li>a kind of plant &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Edfu in Upper Egypt &lsqb;since the end of the 12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the city of Semabehdet (Tell el-Balamun) in Lower Egypt</li><li>the 17th nome of Lower Egypt, with its capital at Semabehdet (Tell el-Balamun)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dismemberment (act) &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>dog &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>bḥnj</i> (“to dismember, to punish, to eliminate”).</p><p>From <i>bḥnw</i> (“to bark”) (unattested underlying form probably <i>&#42;bḥn</i>), thus literally ‘barker’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut apart, to dismember (evil ones) (+ <i>m</i>: with (a knife))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut off, to sever (limbs, heads, ears, etc.) &lsqb;Book of the Dead and Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to punish (evil ones) generally &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive off or otherwise subdue (enemies) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remove, to dispose of (a quarrel) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to eliminate (lies) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to render (poison) harmless &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knife &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>bḥnj</i> (“to dismember, to punish, to eliminate”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to hunt &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bḥz</i> (“calf”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) corvee labor</li></ol><p><i>bḥ</i> (“to be obligated to perform corvee labor”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calf</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pylon, monumental gateway flanked by two towers</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a gateway in general</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to run &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the inundation) to rush by &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to abandon (someone), to forsake, to leave in the lurch &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to run through (a place) &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrogance</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>working power, active power, efficacy in acting on and influencing the external world and enforcing order, especially as possessed by a god</li><li>god, seen as possessed of such power</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) the dead, seen as possessed of such power and needing offerings to sustain it; efficacious soul, ba</li><li>ram</li><li>leopard</li><li>leopard skin</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) an animate, efficacious soul, to possess a ba or ba-power</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) dig up earth, hoe</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) destroy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from root consonants <i>b-l</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bbr</i> (“Babylon”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎲𐎠𐎲𐎡𐎽𐎢𐏁</i> (b-a-b-i-ru-u-š /⁠Bābiruš⁠/), borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠</i> (Bābilim).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) weary</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dagger &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps connected to Latin <i>pūgiō</i> (“dagger”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be damp &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) baris (a type of flat-bottomed freighter used on the Nile)</li><li>a type of Nilotic fish</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to work (+ <i>m</i>: on (something); + <i>n</i>: for (someone))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to render service</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pay taxes</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to produce or improve, to work</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) servant</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;birk-</i> (“knee”), with a possible semantic relation of "knee" > "to kneel" > "to serve" > "to work".</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) a humble reference to the speaker; yours truly, your humble servant</li></ol><p>From <i>bꜣk</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>jm</i> (“therein”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Bakenranef, a pharaoh of the Twenty-fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>bꜣk</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>rn</i> (“name”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus ‘servant of his name’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) servant</li></ol><p><i>bꜣk</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work, labor, especially hard manual labor &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>work, product, result of a task &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>execution or implementation of a design &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>taxes &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>tribute (payment made in submission)</li></ol><p><i>bꜣk</i> (“to work, to render service, to pay taxes”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jar</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bubastis, a city in northern Egypt, cult-centre for the worship of Bastet</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bastet, the cat-goddess worshipped at Bubastis</li></ol><p><i>bꜣst</i> (“Bubastis”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine <i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bush</li><li>small bundle, wisp (of plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beit She'an (an ancient Canaanite city in northern Israel)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>בֵּית שְׁאָן</i> (bêṯ šəʾān).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethoron (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>בֵּית חוֹרוֹן</i> (Bêt̲ ḥôrôn).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power of acting on and influencing the external world: might and glory, particularly of a god, the king, or the crown</li><li>galley (boat)</li></ol><p><i>bꜣ</i> (“ba”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, pit</li><li>riches, wonders, marvels</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis, glans</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;buʔuḥ-</i> (“penis”). Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;būḥ-</i>, Proto-Berber <i>&#42;a-βu-βu</i> (&lt; *a-bŭH-bŭH).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beth-Anath (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>בֵּית עֲנָת</i> (Bêt̲ ʿănāt̲).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise (of the sun), to shine brightly (of the milk)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sink (of the sun), to go down (of the sun), to shrink (of eye)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, light</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mythical mountain or mountainous region far in the east where the sun rises, said to be the source of malachite &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>bꜣẖ</i> (“to rise (of the sun)”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baal, a storm and fertility god of the Phoenician and Canaanite pantheons</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;baʿl-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a name) to be detested, to be reviled; perhaps literally ‘to stink’</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of liquids and rays of light) to flow</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to inundate or be inundated, to flood</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to flood with light</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of food) to be present in abundance, to overflow</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people, places, containers, etc.) to have abundance</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inundation, flood</li><li>abundance of food</li></ol><p><i>bꜥḥj</i> (“to flow, to flood”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippopotamus</li><li>skin or fat of the hippopotamus, used medicinally</li><li>horn (of oxen, gazelles, etc.)</li><li>tusk</li><li>hollowed-out horn for holding oil, used by Asiatics</li><li>horn of the moon</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) wing or flank of an army &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) prick, sting &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Takacs (1999) compares it to Proto-Kuliak <i>&#42;dọb</i> (“rhinoceros”). Possibly related to <i>db</i> (“horn, tusk”); compare the possible relation between <i>ḫꜣb</i> (“hippopotamus”) and <i>ḫꜣb</i> (“to be bent”) (in reference to the animal’s tusks).</p><p>Possibly a doublet of <i>ꜥb</i> (“horn”), according to the Rösslerian school of Egyptian comparison.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the metal counterweight in balance scales</li><li>an (earlier) deben, a measure of weight equivalent to about 13.6 grams. &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a (later) deben, a measure of weight equivalent to 12 <i>šnꜥtjw</i> (“rings”) or 10 <i>qdwt</i> (“qites”) (about 91 grams). &lsqb;since the end of the 12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a metal<sup>?</sup> helmet</li><li>builder’s mortar</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go around, to circumambulate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to recur</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to encircle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(+ <i>m</i>) to encircle with</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sidelock of hair, the traditional hairstyle of children</li></ol><p>Speculatively, Ehret claims a derivation from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;dab(n)-</i> (“hair”); as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;Old Egyptian) to need, to require (+ <i>m-ꜥ</i>: from (someone)) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ask for, request &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) funerary meal offered to the dead &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) altar for food offerings to the dead &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>dbḥt</i> (“necessity”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“offering”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘necessity of the offering’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall, to lose consciousness, to faint</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḏdw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to look</li><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to look at [<i>with</i> <b>n</b> <i>or</i> <b>r</b> <i>or</i> transitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conceal, to hide (something)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hide, to be(come) hidden</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castor oil plant</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dgj</i> (“to look”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forehead</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) mountain top, peak</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) here, there</li></ol><p>Probably an Upper Egyptian dialect doublet of <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“here, there”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifth</li></ol><p><i>dj-</i>, root of <i>djw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) the five epagomenal (intercalary) days at the end of the Egyptian year which did not belong to any month</li></ol><p><i>djw</i> (“five”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrjw</i> (“being upon”) +&lrm; <i>rnpt</i> (“year”), thus literally ‘the five upon the year’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pierce, to stab &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pronounce (a name)</li></ol><p>Ehret hypothesizes an origin in Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;dûm-</i>; as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbor, mooring</li><li>locality, quarter, town</li><li>dry land, ground not covered by water &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to touch</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reach (a place)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to join with, to attach oneself to (someone) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take part in (jubilation) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to salve (+ <i>n</i>: someone) with (a salve) &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>union of several things; totality, aggregate</li><li>total, sum</li></ol><p>From <i>dmḏj</i> (“to join together, to assemble”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to join together, to assemble</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) wing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sever, cut off</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Den, a pharaoh of the First Dynasty, literally ‘The Severer (of Heads)’.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to slaughter (animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf, pygmy</li></ol><p>Compare Amharic <i>ድንክ</i> (dənk, “pygmy”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dam</li><li>part, portion</li><li>share</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically heavy</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of limbs during illness) to feel heavy</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) burdensome, onerous, hard to endure (+ <i>r</i>: for (someone))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of royal power) to be(come) weighty or forceful (+ <i>r</i>: to lie heavy on)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) grave or serious</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to taste (a food, a drink, a taste) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to go through, to experience (a bad feeling or event) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to feel, to sense &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a city in Lower Egypt that, together with the city of <i>p</i>, later formed the combined city of Buto &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship, boat</li><li>taste (sensations produced by the tongue)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) experience</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) a kind of baked goods</li><li>(<i>originally in medical papyri</i>) an edible doubled body part&#59; conventionally rendered as loins, but possibly also the kidneys</li></ol><p>From <i>dp</i> (“to taste”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>Possibly related to <i>dpt</i> (“taste”); see above.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocodile</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) edible fruit</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) fruit trees</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive away, to repel</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to offer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flint</li><li>knife</li><li>jar</li><li>a measure of volume used for beer</li></ol><p>Speculatively, Orel and Stolbova claim a derivation from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;dac-</i> (“flint knife”); as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḏws</i> (“to malign, to slander”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stretch out</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) stretched out</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ṭVwl-</i> (“long, far”); compare Arabic طَوِيل (ṭawīl, “tall, long”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to worship, to praise</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thank (someone)</li></ol><p><i>dwꜣ</i> (“to praise”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“to, for”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Duamutef, a mortuary god, protector of the stomach of the deceased.</li></ol><p>A direct genitive construction meaning “the praise of his mother”, composed of <i>dwꜣ</i> (“to praise”) +&lrm; <i>mwt</i> (“mother”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (third person masculine singular suffix pronoun).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Duat, the Egyptian afterworld &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>In early conceptions, the region in the eastern sky where the sun and stars rise, which serves as the abode of the dead king</li><li>Later, the region underneath the earth through which the sun passes at night, in which Osiris and the dead dwell</li></ol><li>The underworld of a city as a place of dead gods &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>Epithet for the grave &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>Epithet for the crypt in the Temple of Dendera &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>praise, adoration, worship &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>adoratrice &lsqb;Old Kingdom, New Kingdom, Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a type of medicinal plant</li><li>Used in the phrase <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">pr&#58;Z1</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./5d87241c.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="d" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./835cdd37.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="wA" alt="glyph" height="90"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">dwA&#58;t</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a96a36c5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="pr" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table> <i>pr-dwꜣt</i> (“‘House of Morning’, a place of purification”) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>Used in the phrase <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Hr&#58;r</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">S&#58;t&#42;U30</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6c9cd1c6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="n" alt="glyph" height="12"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">pr&#58;Z1</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./5d87241c.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="d" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./835cdd37.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="wA" alt="glyph" height="90"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">dwA&#42;t&#58;pr</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, referring to a royal cloakroom official &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hippopotamus goddess personifying the afterworld &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the Duat (afterworld)</li></ol><p><i>dwꜣt</i> (“Duat, afterworld”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>early morning</li><li>tomorrow</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the morning</li><li>tomorrow</li></ol><p><i>dwꜣ</i> (“to rise early, to praise”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) red</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of gods, faces, eyes, and hearts) to be(come) red with fury, to be(come) apoplectic</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>dšr</i>: red</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flamingo</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The desert that surrounds Egypt and the Nile River Valley.</li><li>The Red Crown, deshret</li></ol><p>From <i>dšr</i> (“red”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending), thus literally meaning “the red one”. False cognate of English <i>desert</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) bitter in taste</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of an office) to be(come) bothersome or onerous</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of the heart) to be(come) aggrieved or distressed</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hide, to be(come) hidden &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overthrow, to defeat &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to copulate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig (fruit)</li><li>fig tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic. Compare Akkadian <i>dilbu</i> (“platane, plane tree”), and Hebrew <i>דולב</i> (dolev, “plane tree”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to copulate or masturbate</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>dꜣ</i> (“to copulate”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dꜣr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to repulse or subdue</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to control oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>dꜣr</i> (“to ward off, subdue”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind, appetite, will”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut up, to divide, to cut apart into smaller pieces</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sever, to cut off (roots or body parts)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay waste, to devastate (lands)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to extirpate, to wipe out (abstract things: lies, wrongs, names, etc.)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece, slice, portion</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to defecate</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to urinate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) desolate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) miserable or weak</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nose</li><li>snout</li><li>beak</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be angry (+ <i>r</i>: at)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;funVg-</i>, according to Orel and Stolbova’s very tentative reconstruction. If so, perhaps cognate with Lowland East Cushitic *<i>fung-</i> (“nose”), whence Oromo <i>fuññaan</i>. Also compare Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⴰⴳⵏⴼⵓⵔ</i> (agnfur, “muzzle, snout”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) a people in Canaan or Syria, perhaps originally the forest-dwellers of Lebanon; Phoenicians<sup>?</sup></li></ol><p>Probably from the plural of <i>fnḫ</i> (“carpenter, woodcutter”), in connection with the Egyptian trade in lumber in Canaan.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people or fish) to leap, to spring</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of hearts) to beat strongly or quickly, to palpitate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give up or lose (something)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to depart</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wig ?</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift, to raise</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>making of an offering, generally of food or drink</li><li>cargo of a ship</li><li>yield, returns, including financial interest</li><li>reward</li></ol><p><i>fꜣj</i> (“to carry, to lift”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth, ground</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Geb (literally “Earth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coptos, a city in Upper Egypt at the site of modern Qift</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side of a room</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black ibis</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>perfective of <i>gmj</i> (“to find”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to smash, to break</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To (seek and) find. &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;also used with a genitive or <i>r</i>) To find a way to go.</li><li>(<i>with a genitive</i>) To find a way to do something. &lsqb;Kushite period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To find a way out (of difficulties, etc.). &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To discover, find, locate.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To come across, happen upon.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To meet (someone).</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To ascertain, discern, recognize.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To determine or find to be in a particular state.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To officially establish or determine; to judge, to rule.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To diagnose.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To calculate (a numerical result).</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To achieve, receive (a position).</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To devise (something).</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a limb) To know how to use properly.</li><li>(catenative,&#32;generally negated) To be able to.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To control a land.</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⴳⵎⵉ</i> (gmi, “to look for, search”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black ibis</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to observe</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wick</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) soft</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to plate something with gold</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) cloudy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) silent &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) calm, still, dispassionate &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>also, furthermore, moreover &lsqb;Old Kingdom to early Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>grw</i> (“also, furthermore, any more”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set in order or prepare (the earth by working it)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set up, to found, to construct</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to furnish, to equip</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lie, falsehood &lsqb;since the end of the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>unjust, unfair, or wrong way of thinking</li><li>injustice</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish a house (+ <i>ḥr</i>: on (a foundation)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>to found a household &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in particular</i>) to get married &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>grg</i> (“to set up, to found”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house, household”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dowry</li></ol><p><i>grg</i> (“to establish, to equip”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>. For the specific meaning, compare <i>grg pr</i> (“to found a household, to get married”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>moreover, now, but; marks a new line of thought</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is silent, one who doesn’t talk &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a calm, dispassionate, and self-effacing person, seen as wisely living according to Maat (virtue/truth/cosmic order) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>also, furthermore</li><li>any more</li></ol><p>From <i>gr</i> (“to be still, to be silent”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p><p>Compare the (mostly Old Egyptian) enclitic particle <i>gr</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end, ending</li><li>night</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) be quiet, be peaceful</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half &lsqb;since the early Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>side &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to oil, to anoint</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>migraine headache</li></ol><p><i>gs</i> (“half, side”) +&lrm; <i>tp</i> (“head”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘half of the head’ or ‘side of the head’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribal palette</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tilt</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) twisted, bent, askew</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to abandon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranglehold</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gazelle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf (of a plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) narrow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine&#32;or&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) reed, rush &lsqb;19th Dynasty to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaza (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>עַזָּה</i> (ʿazzāh).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;Pyramid Texts to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>courtyard</li><li>room, hall</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to knead</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ibis</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ebony (wood, tree)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppose, to confront, to attack, to punish</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush of hot air</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>blast from a fire</li><li>hot or burning breath, particularly of supernatural beings</li><li>steam from freshly baked bread</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) heat from the sun</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fever or hot breath as a symptom of disease</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hakor</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) roaring sound (of lions, thunder, etc.)</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>battle cry, particularly of the king or a god</li><li>fame, renown, particularly of the king or a god</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>singular only</i>) shouting and cheering</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest, box</li><li>sarcophagus</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) body as a holder for the heart</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) skull as a holder for the brain</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>India</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎡𐎯𐎢</i> (h-i-du-u /⁠Hiⁿdu-⁠/, “India”), itself from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;síndʰuš</i> (“river”), possibly via Sanskrit <i>सिन्धु</i> (síndhu, “river, stream; Indus”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to load, to burden (something) so that it bends down</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to incline or nod (one’s head) in assent</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heed, to attend to (someone’s words or speech)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to take one’s leave (+ <i>n</i> or <i>ḥr</i>: of (someone or something))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart) to trust (+ <i>ḥr</i>: in)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to incline one’s head or nod in assent</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jar</li><li>a liquid measure of volume equivalent to 32 <i>r(ꜣ)w</i> or <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>10</sub></small> of a <i>ḥqꜣt</i> (about 0.48 litres).</li><li>group of associates</li><li>family</li><li>waves</li><li>jubilation</li><li>acclaim, praise, worship (of man or god)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord used for determining guidelines in construction</li><li>law, ordinance</li><li>statute, written rule or law</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) norm, wont, custom</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to milk</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rome (a major city in Italy)</li><li>Rome, Ancient Rome (an ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa; in full, Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ῥώμη</i> (Rhṓmē), from Latin <i>Rōma</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day (period of daylight)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) calm, patient, at peace</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) satisfied, at peace, content (+ <i>ḥr</i>: with)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) happy (+ <i>ḥr</i>: about)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of words and objects) to be(come) pleasant, pleasing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast or festival day, holiday</li></ol><p><i>hrw</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣb</i> (“festival”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to scurry, to hasten</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vocative, before the name of the person called</i>) O, hey, hail</li><li>a call to someone unspecified; hey</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry of joy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in religious texts</i>) O! (vocative interjection)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>perfective of <i>hꜣj</i> (“to descend”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to send (someone, e.g. on an errand) (+ <i>r</i>: to (some place))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to send (word) (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone); + <i>ḥr</i>: about)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to descend</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall, to drop</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come, to walk (+ <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>: to (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to charge upon, to fall upon (an enemy)</li></ol><p>Sometimes proposed to be from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;hVy&#47;&#42;hVw-</i> (“to fall, to happen”), in which case it may be cognate with Arabic هوى (hawā, “to come down, to fall”) and Iraqw <i>huu’</i> (“fall, drop”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Haker-festival, a religious festival celebrated in Abydos &lsqb;Middle Kingdom, Book of the Dead, and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vicinity, surroundings, neighborhood</li><li>situation</li><li>(<i>of the king</i>) reign</li><li>(<i>temporal</i>) time</li><li>family</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>O! (vocative interjection)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to say</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) statement, report, word (about someone’s good qualities) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>some part of a reed, likely panicles or leaves</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Old Egyptian&#32;and&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) Forms the imperative of biliteral verbs, and in Late Egyptian also of former triliteral and third-weak verbs that have been reduced to two radicals. Optional in Late Egyptian.</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Old Egyptian&#32;and&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) Forms the subjunctive of biliteral verbs.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Forms the participles and relative forms of fientive (non-adjectival) biliteral verbs, triliteral and third-weak verbs that have been reduced to biliterals through sound change, fourth-weak verbs, and a few other triliteral verbs. &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(with an attached second-person suffix pronoun,&#32;chiefly&#32;in religious texts) hail, greetings</li></ol><p>Possibly from an earlier expression <i>j.</i><i>nḏ</i><i>.j</i> <i>ḥr</i>, meaning roughly ‘may I inquire about (you)’, with <i>nḏ</i> in the subjunctive and the first-person suffix pronoun <i>.j</i> omitted.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) easy pickings, easy prey</li></ol><p><i>j.zj</i> (imperative of <i>zj</i> (“go”)) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣq</i> (“plunder”, imperative), thus literally ‘go and plunder’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumpolar star</li></ol><p><i>j.ḫm</i> (“not knowing”, imperfective active participle of <i>ḫm</i>) +&lrm; <i>sk</i> (“destruction”), thus literally ‘(one) not knowing destruction’, because the circumpolar stars never set and so were considered imperishable.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noncircumpolar star; a star that rises and sets over the course of a day</li></ol><p><i>j.ḫm</i> (“not knowing”, imperfective active participle of <i>ḫm</i>) +&lrm; <i>wrḏ</i> (“to be(come) weary”), thus literally ‘(one) not knowing wearying’, because noncircumpolar stars circle in long paths without end.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heart (blood-pumping organ of a person or animal)</li><li>core, heart, center</li><li>mind; seat of thoughts, emotions, character, personality, etc.</li><li>mental faculties</li><li>intellect, understanding, mind, capacity for thought</li><li>conscience, moral sense</li><li>intention, will</li><li>appetite; will or desire for gratification</li><li>mood, emotional state</li><li>goat kid &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>thirsty man</li><li>alternative form of <i>jbw</i> (“refuge”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to wish to, to intend to (do something) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to wish for, to want, to desire (something) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think, to suppose &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jbꜣ</i> (“to dance”) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;lib-</i> (“heart”). Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;libb-</i> and Proto-Berber <i>&#42;ulβ</i>.</p><p>Nominalization of the perfective active participle of <i>jbj</i> (“to be(come) thirsty”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title used in the 18th Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind, intellect”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the god Thoth &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the god Khonsu &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind, intellect”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ibhat or Ibhet, a region in the south of the Eastern Desert toward Lower Nubia, likely located in the Wadi Hammamat or the Wadi Allaqi or encompassing both</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of mineral&#59;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>gneiss</li></ol></ol><p>Probably from <i>jbht</i> (“a region in the south of the Eastern Desert”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ibhyt, a place in Nubia</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) thirsty, to thirst</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to thirst for, to thirst after</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of land) to be(come) barren, dry, parched</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jbr</i> (“labdanum<sup>?</sup>”)</li></ol><p>Perhaps compare Arabic لَابَ (lāba).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knife</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alum &lsqb;since the Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>vitriolic salts in general</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of valuable oil or unguent, applied to the body and hair, used in temple cults, and also used medicinally&#59;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>labdanum</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of royal headdress, the ibes-crown &lsqb;18th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of plant found in the Wadi Natrun, used medicinally &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirst</li></ol><p>From <i>jbj</i> (“to be(come) thirsty”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refuge, shelter, place of refuge &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) person in whom one takes refuge</li><li>purification booth, booth or tent in which bodies are prepared for mummification</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of rope on a ship, perhaps halyards &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Semitic, sometimes equated with Hebrew <i>אבישי</i> (Abishai).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tooth</li><li>tusk</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to laugh, showing one’s teeth &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>) to run or stream with (liquid), to be(come) wet or awash with &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priestly title&#58; libationer &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jbḥ</i> (“to stream with, to be awash with”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird trap, consisting of a net stretched over a framework that snapped shut when triggered &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dance (+ <i>n</i>: because of, out of joy over)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move with a dancing motion, for example of a running ostrich</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) entertainment in general &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>dancer &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>game piece, man (piece in a board game)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stallion &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowing from a Semitic language. Compare Biblical Hebrew <i>אַבִּיר</i> (ʾabbī́r, “mighty, valiant”) (often used of stallions), or perhaps Ugaritic <i>𐎛𐎁𐎗</i> (ỉbr, “bull”) (= Arabic بَعِير (baʕīr)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Barbary sheep, <i>Ammotragus lervia</i> &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>jbꜣ</i> (“to dance”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of dance associated with or performed by dwarves &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jbꜣw</i> (“dances”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “dances of the god”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riverbank</li><li>shore</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egypt</li></ol><p><i>jdb</i> (“riverbank”) +&lrm; <i>-wj</i> (dual ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) senseless, to be(come) deaf and dumb</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine red linen</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to serve in place of (someone), to represent or replace; to be a lieutenant, deputy, or proxy for</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>st</i> as object) to take (someone’s place), to serve in (someone’s role or position)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to govern, to administer</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to serve as (something), to take the role of (something)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) ear</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔuḏn-</i> (“ear”) (whence Arabic أذن and Hebrew <i>אוזן</i>).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herd, flock (generally any large group of animals)</li><li>stitching</li><li>bandage</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;transitive) to stitch up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to withhold</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with <i>m</i>) to withhold from (someone)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb, uterus</li><li>female genitalia: vulva, vagina, etc.</li><li>cow</li><li>female animal in general</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy, youth, young man</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) crew, team</li><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣdw</i> (“pestilence”)</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of bird or other flying animal &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Idu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edom (land, region)</li></ol><p>Compare Hebrew <i>אֱדוֹם</i> (’eḏōm), Akkadian <i>𒌑𒁺𒈪</i> (Udumi), Edomite <i>𐤀𐤃𐤌</i> (ʾdm).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshlands of the Nile Delta</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) vegetation, plants, especially papyrus or boats made of plants &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flee or run away like a wild animal &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rush through or across (a place)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rectangular block of stone &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>rectangle, rectangular form (as opposed to <i>dbn</i> (“round”)) &lsqb;Mathematical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>rectangular parcel of land &lsqb;Mathematical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of linen, ‘four-weave’ linen</li><li>(linen) sheet or blanket</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shrine in Edfu &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>jfdw</i> (“four”), with an original meaning of ‘run on all fours’ and so ‘run away like a wild animal’. Compare <i>jfdt</i> (“all fours”).</p><p>From <i>jfdw</i> (“four”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of linen</li></ol><p>From <i>jfd</i> (“four-weave linen”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘four-weave linen of the god’; or else the last element may represent <i>nṯrj</i> (“divine textile”). The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to quadruple</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jfd</i> (“(linen) sheet”)</li></ol><p>From <i>jfdw</i> (“four”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourth</li></ol><p><i>jfd-</i>, root of <i>jfdw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetrad, foursome, quartet</li><li>four-day period &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>one’s four legs, all fours &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jfdw</i> (“four”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four</li></ol><p>Compare Hausa <i>huɗu</i>, Beja <i>fadhig</i> and Bidiyo <i>paɗaŋ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bone marrow (of cattle)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the afterword as the realm of silence &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the necropolis &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the crypt in a temple &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>j-</i> +&lrm; <i>gr</i> (“to be(come) silent”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance</li><li>dancing song</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stall, to lag, to be held back or go slowly &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold back or restrain (someone) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to mourn, to be sad, to suffer &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to mourn or be sad in general (not only of the heart)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) sorrow, suffering &lsqb;Book of the Dead and 20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>shouting</li><li>(cry of) jubilation &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to arrive, to come to a certain place (+ <i>r</i> or <i>n</i>: to (a place, person, thing, etc.); + <i>ḫr</i>: to (someone of higher status); + <i>m</i>: from (a place), into (something), by means of (a vehicle), as (a role); + <i>ḥr</i>: to, upon (something), from, for the sake of; + <i>m-dj</i>: from (someone))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come here, to move from further to nearby</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to return, to come back</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of future events, time, old age, etc.) to come to pass or come to be, to come, to arrive</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i> and following infinitive) to come (to do something)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, equivalent to English Imhotep, notably borne by Imhotep</li><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Imhotep, a deified administrator and architect</li></ol><p><i>jj</i> (“coming”, perfective active participle of <i>jj</i> (“to come, to return”)) +&lrm; <i>m ḥtp</i> (“in peace, safely”), thus ‘(the one) coming in peace’, ‘(the one) returning home safely’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>welcome (greeting upon someone’s arrival)</li></ol><p>Stative stem of <i>jj</i> (“to come”) +&lrm; <i>.tj</i> (second-person singular stative ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>welcome (greeting upon someone’s arrival)</li></ol><p>Dual form of the perfective active participle of <i>jj</i> (“to come”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) protection</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scoop out, to scoop up, to draw (water) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dig up (land) &lsqb;Coffin Texts to Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dig out (a hole) &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seize, to take hold of &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup, scoop, small bowl &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Wilson suggests this word is a metathesized form of earlier <i>jnk</i> (“to hold”) ~ <i>ꜥnk</i>, in turn perhaps a development from <i>jnq</i> (“to embrace”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone quarry</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of stonemason or quarryman &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of large vessel or jar, usually made of metal and used to hold fragrant oil or water, exported to and taken as tribute from Syria &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare the earlier Middle Egyptian <i>jkn</i> (“cup”), but also (separately) Hebrew <i>אַגָּן</i> (ʾaggā́n, “ritual- or banquet-bowl”), Phoenician <i>𐤀𐤂𐤍</i> (ʾgn, “bowl”), Ugaritic <i>𐎀𐎂𐎐</i> (ảgn, “basin”), Warka Aramaic (ag-gan-nu), <small>[script needed]</small> (ag-ga-nu, “basin or bowl”), Jewish Palestinian Aramaic <i>גָנייא דקדשׁ</i> (“basins of Qadesh”), Classical Syriac <i>ܐܓܢܐ</i> (ʾaggānā, “water pitcher”), Arabic إجّانَة (ʔijjāna, “large bowl”), Akkadian (/⁠agannu⁠/, “large bowl of copper or silver”). The equivalent of the Egyptian term is also attested in cuneiform transcription in the Amarna letters as <small>[script needed]</small> (a-ku-nu /⁠akūnu⁠/), with Albright, Lambdin, and the <i>Chicago Assyrian Dictionary</i> claiming this to be Egyptian in origin; Hoch argues that the reverse is true, and Egyptian borrowed the word from Semitic. In Egyptian, the word appears in lists of items from Syria-Palestine, while in the Amarna letters the word appears in lists of items sent from Egypt; the word is, however, much more widely attested in Semitic languages than in Egyptian. Hoch consequently thinks that the direction of borrowing was into Egyptian, and that the word may not be connected with Middle Egyptian <i>jkn</i> (“cup”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Menkauhor Kaiu, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>jkꜣw-ḥr</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Menkauhor Kaiu, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Like many fifth-dynasty pharaohs’ birth names, this name may simply be a diminutive without any meaning of its own, perhaps of the pharaoh’s throne name <i>mn-kꜣw-ḥr</i> (in which case it may not be the actual name given at birth).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therein, in it, there</li><li>(<i>in reference to a period of time</i>) during it, on it</li><li>(<i>with certain verbs of motion</i>) thereto, into it, thither</li><li>therefrom, thence, from within it, out of it, out</li><li>(<i>in reference to a quantity</i>) thereof, out of it, of them</li><li>therewith, by means of it</li><li>regarding it, concerning it, thereto</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>jmj</i> (“being (in), inherent (in)”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to moan, to groan, to wail, to lament (+ <i>ḥr</i> or <i>m</i>: over) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side, side of ribs (as a body part or as food) &lsqb;Old Kingdom and 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>form, shape &lsqb;Pyramid Texts, Coffin Texts, and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a material of which <i>twt</i>-images can consist, which can also be used for ‘filling’ walls&#59; &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a kind of plaster or similarly plastic material, possibly gesso</li><li>abraded material (crumbs, fragments) from statuettes of protective deities and other sacred images</li></ol></ol><p>The adverbial form of <i>m</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of material, either derived from or used in the making of <i>twt</i>-images, and used medicinally as a healing agent &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jm</i> (“a kind of material”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>twt</i> (“image, statue, picture”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being in, inherent (in)</li><li>being among, of, out of (a quantity or group)</li><li>(<i>in reverse nisba constructions</i>) in which (something) is</li><li>(<i>after an adjective</i>) -est of, -est among; forms the superlative</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is within, contents, insides</li><li>interior, inside</li><li>inhabitant, person living in a place</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to not be</li><li>(catenative,&#32;with a verb in the negatival complement) to not do; (<i>in the&#32;subjunctive</i>) may it not be that…; lest…; so that… does not…</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;with a following verbal adjective) there is none that is; introduces an independent negated adjectival existential clause &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>imperative of <i>rḏj</i> (“to give, to set, to cause”)</li><li>emphasizes a following imperative</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following verb in the subjunctive-prospective) forms the causative imperative</li></ol><p>From <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending). For the initial <i>j-</i>, compare the adverbial and pre-pronominal form of <i>m</i>, <i>jm</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being in the presence of (a king or god)</li><li>being earlier (than), having existed before</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) ancestor</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“being in”) +&lrm; <i>bꜣḥ</i> (“phallus”), constructed on the same model as <i>m bꜣḥ</i> (“in the presence of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person on duty for the day, in particular as a sentry; duty officer</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>hrw</i> (“day”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), literally “he who is in his day”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favorite or close confidante</li><li>understanding, intellect, mind</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), literally “one who/that which is in the heart/mind”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starboard</li><li>west side of a place, especially of Thebes &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the West</li><li>necropolis, realm of the dead, afterworld</li><li>right, right side &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(preceded or followed by <i>zꜣ</i> (“phyle”)) designates a particular phyle of funerary priests</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly in nautical commands</i>) to the starboard, starboardwards, right &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in; interior”) +&lrm; <i>wrt</i>. The identity and meaning of the second term are unclear. It is written identically to <i>wrt</i> (“great one”), but perhaps it is instead to be connected with <i>wrt</i> (“a kind of barque”).</p><p>Any attempt to determine the etymological meaning of the term should probably take it in conjunction with its antonym <i>tꜣ wr</i> (“larboard, port”); setting aside <i>wr</i>/<i>wrt</i>, here one can draw a contrast between <i>jmj</i> in the one term, referring to the interior of something, and <i>tꜣ</i> in the other term, apparently referring to the land exterior to the ship. For <i>tꜣ</i>, compare the etymology of the English term <i>port</i>; because the steering oar is generally found on the right (starboard) side of the ship, it is the left (port) side that moors facing the land. It is then easy to see how the starboard side could be perceived as the ‘interior’ of the vessel, facing away from land. (Alternatively, it may be the steering oar itself that is referred to as ‘being in’ / in the ‘interior’ of the vessel.)</p><p>Thus, the term as a whole has variously been interpreted as ‘that which is inside the barque’, or, taking <i>wrt</i> as ‘great one’ in reference to the ‘great oar’ (steering oar), ‘the one on the inside of the steering oar’. Servajean rejects the latter interpretation and prefers the former, as <i>wrt</i> is nowhere else attested to refer to the steering oar specifically.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forward, anterior, being in front</li><li>previous, former, earlier in time</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person at the front, leader, guide</li><li>paragon, example or pattern for conduct, role model (for good or ill)</li><li>former state of things or earlier time; what was before</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being behind (spatially), found behind, rear</li><li>succeeding, following after (temporally)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) follower, retainer, attendant; (<i>in the plural</i>) retinue</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) successor, descendant, aftercomer, one who will come after; (<i>in the plural</i>) posterity</li><li>an official title, especially used as part of longer titles&#58; undersupervisor &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>ḫt</i> (“what is behind or after”). The nisba equivalent to <i>m ḫt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Amduat, a popular funerary text in the genre of netherworld guides</li></ol><p><i>jmj</i> (“being in”) +&lrm; <i>dwꜣt</i> (“afterworld”), thus ‘(that which) is in the afterworld’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title&#58; councillor, he who is in the bureau (as a priest or official)</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>jz</i> (“administrative chamber, bureau”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overseer, chief administrator, superintendent</li><li>tongue</li><li>utterance</li></ol><p>A ‘reverse <i>nisba</i>’ construction from <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“mouth”), thus literally ‘one in whom the mouth is’, implying ‘one who gives commands’. The written form is abbreviated to omit the weak consonants.</p><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“mouth”), literally “the one in the mouth”. The components are the same as in the etymology above, but the grammatical construction is different.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title&#58; ‘overseer of work’</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj-r</i> (“overseer”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣt</i> (“work”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>general (military leader)</li><li>foreman or boss of a workgang</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj-r</i> (“overseer”) +&lrm; <i>mšꜥ</i> (“army, workgang, expedition”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘overseer of the army/workgang/expedition’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steward; administrator of an estate/house, wealth, property, etc.</li></ol><p><i>jmj-r</i> (“overseer”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘overseer of the house’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high steward</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj-r-pr</i> (“steward”, literally “overseer of the house”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”), literally “great overseer of the house”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheriff</li></ol><p><i>jmj-r</i> (“overseer”) +&lrm; <i>šnt</i> (“dispute”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘overseer of dispute’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title&#58; overseer of the granary &lsqb;chiefly Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jmj-r</i> (“overseer”) +&lrm; <i>šnwt</i> (“granary”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘overseer of the granary’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title: ‘temple-overseer’</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj-r</i> (“overseer”) +&lrm; <i>ḥwt-nṯr</i> (“temple”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘overseer of the temple’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>list of names</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>rn</i> (“name”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), a ‘reverse <i>nisba</i>’ construction meaning ‘the one in which his name is’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helper, acolyte, functionary; also used as a title</li><li>a kind of heart-shaped piece of jewelry</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>st-ꜥ</i> (“activity, efficaciousness”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) attendant, guard</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>sꜣ</i> (“back”), literally “the one in the back”. Other interpretations have also been suggested, for example taking the second term as <i>zꜣ</i> (“protection”) or <i>zꜣ</i> (“phyle, troop”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priestly title</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“earth”), literally “the one in the earth”. Compare the non-titular use of the plural form <i>jmw tꜣ</i> (“snakes; rocks”, literally “those in the earth”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the god Anubis</li><li>the Imiut fetish, the symbol or fetish of Anubis consisting of a stuffed headless animal skin tied face-down to a pole, with its tail terminating in a lotus bud at the top &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>wt</i> (“bandage, mummy wrapping”), although other interpretations have also been suggested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title: ‘chamberlain’: a priest responsible for the crown and ornaments of the king and for his ceremonial dressing and undressing &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>ḫnt</i> (“fore, forecourt”), literally “(one who) is in the forecourt (of the palace)”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title with unclear exact meaning&#58; assistant, regional officer, aide</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm, part, region”). The exact intended meaning of the second part is unclear.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the king &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jmj</i> (“being in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥḥ</i> (“palace”), thus “(the one) being in the palace”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(with a dual object or two objects separated by <i>r</i>) between</li><li>(<i>with a plural object</i>) among, amidst, in the midst of</li></ol><p>Probably originally a derivative of <i>m</i> (“in”); compare <i>jmj</i> (“being in”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>between them</li><li>among them, amidst them</li></ol><p>The adverbial form of <i>jmjtw</i>, from <i>jmjtw</i> (“between, among”) +&lrm; <i>nj</i> (“thereto, for it”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) a group of seven snake gods who defend Ra from Apophis</li></ol><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“being in”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural suffix) +&lrm; <i>jꜣwt</i> (plural of <i>jꜣt</i> (“mound”)) +&lrm; <i>.sn</i> (third-person plural suffix pronoun), literally “those in their mounds”. Some variants leave off <i>.sn</i> for a meaning of “those in the mounds”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) epithet for snakes and water snakes, collectively</li></ol><p>From <i>jmjw</i> (“(those) being in”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“earth”) +&lrm; <i>mw</i> (“water”), literally “those in the earth and water”, modeled after the more common <i>jmw tꜣ</i> (“snakes”, literally “those in the earth”) and <i>jmw-mw</i> (“aquatic animals”, literally “those in the water”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive</i>) to hide, to conceal, to make secret (+ <i>r</i>: from (someone); + <i>m</i>: in (something))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hide, to be(come) hidden or secret</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;by extension) to be(come) rare or precious &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to create, form, shape (in a cosmic, divine sense) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>jmn</i>: hidden, secret, precious</li><li>right (direction) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>western</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of various gods&#58; hidden one, secret one</li><li>a priestly title&#58; hidden one, secret one &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>right side &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>the West</li><li>only used in <i>jrj jmn</i> (“to make one’s abode, to sojourn”): abode, place of stay &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amun, a primeval god, personification of the hiddenness of the primeval waters, worshipped at Hermopolis as a member of the Ogdoad &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>Amun, a god worshipped at Thebes as a member of the Theban Triad, and seen as the king of the gods &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a minor god in the form of a snake worshipped at Prosopis and Thebes &lsqb;Theban Royal Tombs and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic ء م ن (ʔ m n) and Hebrew <i>א־מ־ן</i> (ʾ-m-n).</p><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;yamin-</i>. Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;yamīn-</i> (“right”).</p><p>From <i>j-</i> +&lrm; <i>mn</i> (“to be established, to remain”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jmn-m-jpt</i> (“the form of the god Amun worshipped at Luxor”)</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>jpt</i> (“Luxor, inner sanctum”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “Amun of Luxor”, “Amun of the inner sanctum”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the form of the god Amun worshipped at Luxor &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a male given name, Amenemope</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jpt</i> (“Luxor, inner sanctum”), thus “Amun in Luxor”, “Amun in the inner sanctum”. The personal name is probably to be interpreted as an adverbial sentence, “Amun is in Luxor”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the form of the god Amun worshipped at Karnak &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jpt-swt</i> (“Karnak”), thus “Amun in Karnak”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by a number of pharaohs named Amenemhat in the Twelfth and Thirteenth Dynasties</li></ol><p><i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“forepart, front”), thus ‘Amun is at the fore’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Amenmose</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (“to give birth”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine third-person singular stative ending), thus ‘Amun is born’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Amennakht</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>nḫt</i> (“strong, victorious”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Amun is strong’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for various gods &lsqb;Theban Royal Tombs and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“hidden”) +&lrm; <i>nṯrw</i> (“gods”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “hidden of the gods”, i.e. “most hidden one among the gods”, “most hidden god”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a minor god mentioned in old religious texts &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“hidden”) +&lrm; <i>rn</i> (“name”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘hidden of name’, i.e. ‘he whose name is hidden’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Amun, occasioanlly also used of other gods</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“hidden”) +&lrm; <i>rn</i> (“name”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Hidden of his Name’, i.e. ‘he whose name is hidden’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Amun-Ra &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“sun, Ra”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Amenhotep</li><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Amenhotep (son of Hapu), a deified scribe and architect</li></ol><p><i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp(.w)</i> (“is satisfied”), masculine third-person singular stative form of <i>ḥtp</i> (“to satisfy”), thus literally ‘Amun is satisfied’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priestly title for a priest of Bastet &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“hidden”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “hidden of arm”, i.e. “(the one) with the hidden arm”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>west, the West</li><li>right side</li><li>the Duat, the afterworld</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amaunet, a primeval goddess, female personification of the hiddenness of the primeval waters (as a counterpart to the male Amun), worshipped at Hermopolis as a member of the Ogdoad &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>Amaunet, a goddess worshipped at Thebes as the female counterpart of the king of the gods Amun &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmn</i> (“western”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p><p><i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>western</li><li>right (direction)</li></ol><p><i>jmnt</i> (“west, right”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the West, regions of the earth or sky lying to the west &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>west, western side, western half (of a place or the sky)</li><li>epithet for the afterworld</li><li>epithet for a necropolis</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Imentet, personification of the West &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the feminine of <i>jmntj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher &lsqb;New Kingdom, Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) deaf</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dill</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Imseti, a mortuary god, protector of the liver of the deceased</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buto, a city in the eastern Nile delta, modern Tell Nebesha or Tell el-Farʿun</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster child, ward (of the king of Lower Egypt) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>child (of a deity) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) groaning, lamentation, mourning, grief</li></ol><p>From <i>jm</i> (“to moan, to groan, to lament”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stern of a ship</li><li>designates a particular phyle of funerary priests</li></ol><p>From <i>jmt</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>nḏst</i> (“left side<sup>?</sup>; smallness<sup>?</sup>”). The exact etymological meaning is unclear, but compare the construction of <i>jmj wrt</i> (“starboard”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jmj wrt</i> (“starboard”)</li></ol><p>Feminine form of the more common <i>jmj wrt</i> (“starboard”), formed with the addition of the feminine ending <i>-t</i> to the nisba <i>jmj</i>. See <i>jmj wrt</i> for further etymology. Equivalent to <i>jmt</i> +&lrm; <i>wrt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(often with suffix pronoun attached to <i>jmt</i>) former state of things or earlier time; also, preceding iteration, original; what was before</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>jmj ḥꜣt</i> (“(one) being in front or earlier in time; anterior (one)”), formed with the addition of the feminine ending <i>-t</i> to the nisba <i>jmj</i>. Equivalent to <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inventory of a house’s or estate’s property and goods</li><li>such property itself</li><li>specifically, such property as a bequeathed legacy or estate; bequest</li><li>will, last will and testament (document)</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> jmj,&lrm; pr.</li></ol><p>A nominalized <i>nisba</i> adjective in a reverse <i>nisba</i> construction, <i>jmt</i> (“being in”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house, estate”), literally ‘what the estate is in’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) overseer &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of tool or implement&#59; &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>jmj-r</i> (“overseer”), formed with the addition of the feminine ending <i>-t</i> to the nisba <i>jmj</i>. See <i>jmj-r</i> for further etymology. Equivalent to <i>jmt</i> +&lrm; <i>r</i>.</p><p>Presumably from <i>jmt</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“mouth”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of the uraeus</li></ol><p>From <i>jmt</i> (feminine of <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”)) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), literally “she who is in his front”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vintage of wine from Buto in the eastern Nile delta; Butic (wine)</li></ol><p>From <i>jmt</i> (“the city of Buto in the Eastern Delta”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boat, ship in general &lsqb;Old Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>shipload</li><li>lamentation, wail, cry of mourning &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(followed by <i>n</i>) woe unto …!</li><li>contents of an abscess or boil; pus &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>woe, alas</li></ol><p>From <i>jm</i> (“to lament, to wail”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p><p>From <i>jmj</i> (“being in”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) epithet for snakes</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) epithet for stones</li></ol><p>From <i>jmw</i> (“(those) being in”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“earth”), literally “those in the earth”. Compare the singular form <i>jmj-tꜣ</i>, used as a priestly title.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) water animals, aquatic fauna</li></ol><p>From <i>jmw</i> (plural of <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”)) +&lrm; <i>mw</i> (“water”), literally “those in the water”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) forebears, ancestors, those of times gone by</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) lesser gods who walk in front of a chief god &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From the plural of <i>jmj ḥꜣt</i> (“(one) being in front or earlier in time; anterior (one)”), equivalent to <i>jmj</i> (“(one) being in”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural suffix) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amurru; the land of the Amorites</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jmjtw</i> (“between, amidst”) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(following <i>n</i> (“to, for”) with an attached suffix pronoun) of, belonging to</li></ol><p>Probably originally a derivative of <i>m</i> (“in”); compare <i>jmj</i> (“being in”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a kind of pigment</li><li>inlay paste, dye paste</li><li>a kind of crafting technique</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>jm</i> (“a kind of material”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall, fortification</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic. Compare Phoenician <i>𐤂𐤃𐤓</i> (gdr), Hebrew <i>גָּדֵר</i> (gadér), and Arabic جِدَار (jidār).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sip &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mythological place in the Duat (afterworld)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) kindly, friendly, gracious, or amiable (+ <i>n</i>: toward)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to be(come) popular with, favored by, beloved by</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) charming or pleasing (+ <i>n</i>: toward)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to be(come) charmed, pleased, delighted with &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>jmꜣ</i>: kindly, friendly, gracious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of fruit-bearing tree&#59;</li><li>the wood of this tree, used for making statues and tools</li><li>a part of this tree used medicinally</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) friendly, kindly, amiable (+ <i>n</i>: toward)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) happy, cheerful (+ <i>r</i> or <i>m</i>: about, over)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>ḫr</i>) to be(come) popular with, favored by, beloved by</li></ol><p>From <i>jmꜣ</i> (“to be friendly, charming, pleased”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), literally “to be friendly/pleased of heart”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindness, friendliness, graciousness, charm</li><li>kind behavior, good deeds</li><li>belovedness, well-likedness, favor (+ genitive: possessed by (someone))</li><li>female ibex &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li><li>female wild animal in general</li></ol><p>From <i>jmꜣ</i> (“to be(come) kindly, friendly, or gracious”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>Probably from <i>jꜣm</i> (“to bind for sacrifice”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) building, such as a hut, house, or office</li><li>radiance of the sun; solar splendor or brilliance &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmꜣ</i> (“to be friendly, charming, pleasing”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be worthy of attention, care, respect, and remembrance; to be honored or revered (+ <i>ḫr</i> or <i>n</i>: by)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) &lsqb;22nd Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to honor, to revere (someone)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worthiness of attention, care, respect, and remembrance by future generations; roughly dignity, honor (+ <i>r</i>: due to (someone))</li><li>in particular, the state of dignity or honor attributed to one of the dead who have been judged righteous</li><li>venerable age, old age</li><li>spinal column, backbone, including the spinal cord &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>mythologically, the vertebral canal of the snake through which the sun is said to travel &lsqb;Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor, reverence</li></ol><p>From <i>jmꜣḫ</i> (“to be honored, revered”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for the dead or, sometimes, the aged&#58; revered one, venerable one (+ <i>ḫr</i> or genitive: one honored by (someone); + <i>m</i>: one honored in (a place))</li></ol><p>From <i>jmꜣḫ</i> (“to be honored, revered”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet attached to the dead who have been judged righteous</li></ol><p><i>jmꜣḫw</i> (“revered one”) +&lrm; <i>ḫr</i> (“in the presence of, before”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr-ꜥꜣ</i> (“Elder God”, epithet of Osiris), thus literally ‘one revered before the Elder God’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for the (female) dead or, sometimes, the (female) aged&#58; revered one, venerable one (+ <i>ḫr</i> or genitive: one honored by (someone); + <i>m</i>: one honored in (a place)) &lsqb;chiefly Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jmꜣḫw</i> (“revered one”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jmꜣḫwt</i> (“(female) revered one”) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>indicates interrogativity</li><li>(<i>with following noun indicating the speaker</i>) marks a non-future direct quotation; says, said</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>introduces the agent of a passive construction, when not a personal pronoun; by</li><li>introduces the agent of an infinitive, when not a personal pronoun</li><li>introduces an emphasized subject (one that serves as the rheme of the clause), when not a personal pronoun</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>introduces the emphasized (non-participial) element in a participial statement, when not a personal pronoun</li></ol></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>j</i> (“to say”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (third-person masculine singular stative ending) +&lrm; <i>jn</i> (“by”, the preposition above). The feminine form is a univerbation of <i>j</i> (“to say”) +&lrm; <i>.t(j)</i> (third-person feminine singular stative ending) +&lrm; <i>jn</i> (“by”, the preposition above), and the plural and dual are derived from the perfect of the verb <i>j</i> (“to say”) with a third-person plural or dual suffix pronoun (<i>.sn</i> or <i>.snj</i>, respectively).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Intef, Antef, or Inyotef</li></ol><p><i>jn</i> (perfective relative form of <i>jnj</i> (“to bring”)) +&lrm; <i>jt</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus literally ‘(he) whom his father brought’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anhur/Anhor/Onuris, Egyptian god of war and hunting.</li></ol><p>From <i>jn</i> (“(one who) carries”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrt</i> (“(what is) above, heaven”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to separate with walls, to enclose with walls, to wall in</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Memphis &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the main fortress of Memphis, located toward the north of the city and also functioning as a palace and ceremonial complex</li><li>the first nome of Lower Egypt, with its capital at Memphis</li></ol><p>From <i>jnb</i> (“wall”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”), literally “White Wall”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence</li><li>fortification, blockade</li></ol><p>From <i>jnb</i> (“to wall”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afflicted, troubled, or sick man</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring, to get, to fetch</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to acquire, to get</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to attain (a goal)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to have recourse, to turn [<i>with</i> <b>m</b> <small>‘to’</small>]</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Nyuserre Ini, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Like many fifth-dynasty pharaohs’ birth names, this name may simply be a diminutive without any meaning of its own, perhaps of the pharaoh’s throne name <i>n-wsr-rꜥ</i> (in which case it may not be the actual name given at birth). Alternatively, some have tentatively attempted to explain it with various meanings, such as ‘the delayed one’ or ‘the (one with the bushy?) eyebrows’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying pole</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I, me (see usage notes)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold in hand &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ʔanāku</i>. By surface analysis, <i>jn</i> (independent pronoun morpheme) +&lrm; <i>.k</i> (first person stative ending).</p><p>Wilson suggests an origin in a later development of <i>jnq</i> (“to embrace”). Also compare <i>jkn</i> (“to seize”), which may be a further development of this word.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin of a human or animal</li><li>hide, pelt, removed skin of an animal</li><li>leather</li><li>color of skin</li><li>hues, colors (of the sky, cloth, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) exterior, appearance, form</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic. Compare Proto-Berber <i>&#42;elăm</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we, us (see usage notes) &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prince, princess, child of a Pharaoh</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anubis, god of the cemetery, worshipped at Abydos</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Anput, the wife of Anubis</li></ol><p><i>jnpw</i> (“Anubis”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to embrace</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) sandstone</li></ol><p><i>jnr</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”) +&lrm; <i>mnḫ</i> (“effective, worthwhile”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>rwḏt</i> (“hard stone”), thus literally ‘worthwhile white stone of hard rock’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandstone</li></ol><p><i>jnr</i> (“stone”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“pleasing, fine”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>rwḏt</i> (“hard stone”), thus literally ‘fine white stone of hard rock’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) an edible plant, possibly anise, used medicinally &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>calf (of leg), thigh</li></ol><p>Uncertain etymology. With different determinatives the word is associated with the soles of a human foot or the hoof of an animal, perhaps suggesting a correlation to its known anti-fungal properties, anethole still being used to treat athlete's foot and thrush.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) testicles</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valley</li><li>Nile tilapia, <i>Oreochromis niloticus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, of historical usage, notably borne by the Achaemenid king Darius</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎢𐏁</i> (d-a-r-y-u-š /⁠Dārayauš⁠/), shortened form of <i>𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎺𐎢𐏁</i> (d-a-r-y-v-u-š /⁠Dārayava(h)uš⁠/). Compare 𐊑𐊗𐊀𐊕𐊆𐊊𐊁𐊒𐊖- (ñtarijeus-).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cargo, delivery; produce, gifts, or supplies brought</li></ol><p>From <i>jnj</i> (“to bring, to get”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) eyebrow</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to surround, to enclose, to wall in (a place) (+ <i>m</i>: with, in (a wall, columns, etc.)) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to frame, to rim, to decorate the edges of (a door, vessel, or other object) (+ <i>m</i>: with (inscriptions, metal, gold, etc.)) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of rays of light) to surround, to embrace the whole of, to fill</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to surround, to encircle (enemy forces)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of companions) to surround, to accompany (someone) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to clothe with (an article of clothing) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enclose protectively</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) picture frame</li></ol><p><i>jnḥ</i> (“to surround, to frame”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chin &lsqb;Old and Early Middle Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to count</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to assess as complete by counting</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to examine, to check, to evaluate, to review (someone or something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take account of, to take heed of</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to account, to assess, to recognize, to consider (something) (+ <i>r</i>: as)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fully account for, collect, or set in order (the bones of the dead)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recognize (someone) (+ <i>m</i>: as)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to respect, to esteem (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to allot, to assign, to apportion (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to charge (something) against (someone), to hold (someone) liable for something</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to detail (someone) for work (+ <i>r</i> or <i>m</i>: for (some particular work))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to transfer (someone) (+ <i>m</i>: from; + <i>r</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to impose, to assess, to exact (taxes, dues, revenues collected, etc.) (+ <i>r</i>: from)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m-ꜥ</i>) to claim (something) from (someone)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to assemble, to muster, to collect together</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;often of the heart/mind) to be(come) shrewd, judicious, discerning, circumspect (+ <i>m</i>: in)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accounting</li><li>regard, esteem, opinion or assessment of someone</li><li>only used in <i>rḏj jp ḥr</i> (“to hit someone on (a body part)<sup>?</sup>”) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>stairway &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jp-jb</i> (“shrewd, judicious, discerning, circumspect”)</li></ol><p>From <i>jp</i> (“shrewd, judicious”, perfective active participle of <i>jp</i> (“to count, to assess, to assign, to be(come) judicious”)) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), thus “shrewd in mind”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect oneself; to regain or have one’s wits or senses about oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>jp</i> (“to count, to assess, to assign, to recognize”) +&lrm; <i>ḏt</i> (“body, self”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shrewd, judicious, discerning, circumspect</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the god Thoth, and occasionally other gods &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jp</i> (“counting, assessing, shrewd, judicious”, perfective active participle of <i>jp</i> (“to count, to assess, to assign, to be(come) judicious”)) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “shrewd of mind”. The divine epithet can alternatively be understood with <i>jb</i> as the object of <i>jp</i>, thus “(one who is) assessing the heart/mind”, “assessor of the heart/mind”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furniture in general &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) those</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>pf</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Epeiph</li><li>the festival of Epiphi, taking place on the first or second day of the next month, Mesore</li></ol><p>Derived from the earlier (Memphite) name of the month, <i>jpt-ḥmt</i>, with reduplication of the first element, or perhaps directly from the short form <i>jpt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these, those</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>pn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aphek (an ancient Canaanite city, known as Antipatris during the Roman period)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>אֲפֵק</i> (ʾăp̄ḗq).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oipe, a dry measure of volume equivalent to 4 <i>ḥqꜣwt</i> (about 19.2 litres or 0.54 bushels) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>vessel for measuring oipes</li><li>count, enumeration</li><li>census</li><li>accounting, reckoning, weighing up of accounts (literal or figurative) (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with)</li><li>number, quantity (of something)</li><li>inner or private chamber of a building in general</li><li>women’s apartments, gynaeceum, harem &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>inner sanctum of a temple</li><li>cup &lsqb;Kushite and Greco-Roman Periods&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the temple of Luxor</li><li>the goddess Ipet</li><li>the month of Epeiph</li><li>the festival of Epiphi, taking place on the first or second day of the next month, Mesore &lsqb;18th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jp</i> (“to count, to assess, to assign”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>From <i>jp</i> (“to count, to assess, to assign”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>; as shown by the Coptic descendants, the construction is not the same as in the term of measurement above.</p><p>Probably shortened from the fuller form <i>jpt-ḥmt(.s)</i>; see that entry for further details.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royal harem</li><li>private chamber or office of the king in general</li><li>collectively, the women of the royal harem</li></ol><p>From <i>jpt</i> (“private chamber, women’s apartments, inner sanctum”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “private chamber/women’s apartments of the king”. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the temple of Luxor</li></ol><p>From <i>jpt</i> (“Luxor”) +&lrm; <i>rst</i> (“southern”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the temple of Karnak</li></ol><p>From <i>jpt</i> (“select, inner sanctum”) +&lrm; <i>swt</i> (plural of <i>st</i> (“place”)), literally “the (most) select of places”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Epeiph</li><li>the festival of Epiphi, taking place on the first or second day of the next month, Mesore</li><li>epithet for the goddess Taweret &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jpt</i> (meaning disputed) +&lrm; <i>ḥmt</i> (“female ‘Majesty’ or ‘Incarnation’ (pharaoh, goddess)”), sometimes also found with suffixed <i>.s</i> (“her”). The first element, <i>jpt</i>, has been interpreted in various ways; the <i>Thesaurus Linguae Aegyptiae</i> takes it as the name of the goddess Ipet, so that the whole phrase can be rendered “Ipet, her Majesty”, while Allen takes it to mean “select”, so that the phrase can be rendered “(She who is) Select of Incarnation”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;rare) those</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>tf</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these, those</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>tn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these two, those two</li></ol><p>Dual form of <i>tn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these two, those two</li></ol><p>Secondary dual form of <i>tn</i>, joining the (earlier?) form <i>jptnj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;archaizing) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>tw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these two</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;archaizing) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p>Dual form of <i>tw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian&#32;or&#32;archaizing&#32;Middle Egyptian,&#32;rare) these</li></ol><p>Sethe considers this form to have originated as a miswriting of <i>jptw</i>; Faulkner instead considers it a back-formation from the dual form <i>jptwtj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these two</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;archaizing) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p>Secondary dual form of <i>tw</i>, joining the (earlier?) form <i>jptwj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;archaizing) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akhmim or Panopolis, a city in Upper Egypt</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) inventory, list, tax record</li><li>levy, duty, payments &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>pw</i>.</p><p>From <i>jp</i> (“to count, to assess, to assign”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) these two</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;archaizing) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p>Dual form of <i>pw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig as an offering, probably specifically a piglet &lsqb;20th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;lpḥ</i> , from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ḥVlp- ~ &#42;lVpḥ-</i> (“pig, hog”). Compare Proto-Berber <i>&#42;e-lăf</i> (“wild boar, pig”) (whence Tashelhit <i>ilf</i> (“wild boar, pig”)) and Arabic حَلُّوف (ḥallūf, “pig”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jpt</i> (“inner chamber”) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jpt</i> (“inner chamber”) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder</li></ol><p><i>j-</i> +&lrm; <i>qd</i> (“to build”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) excellent, worthy, or irreproachable</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) reliable, trustworthy, or dependable</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>jqr</i>: excellent, worthy, reliable</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very, exceedingly</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;waqar-</i>; compare Hebrew <i>יָקָר</i> (yāqār, “expensive, precious”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enter (a place) &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to come across to meet (someone)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of rays of the sun, etc.) to illuminate, to shine on &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i> or <i>m</i>) to go to (a place)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i> or <i>m</i>,&#32;of floodwater and rays of the sun) to reach, to touch (a place)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḫr</i>,&#32;of a fragrance) to reach (someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle axe</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) military strikeforce as an extension of royal power</li></ol><p>Perhaps compare <i>qḥ</i> (“to break stones”), <i>qḥqḥ</i> (“to hammer”), if not <i>jqḥ</i> (“to enter, to illuminate”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>r</i> (“so, regarding”)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>jrj</i> (“regarding it, with respect to it”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>jrj</i> (“pertaining (to)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to do (an action) (+ <i>r</i>: against (someone); + <i>n</i>: for; + <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with; + <i>m</i>: using, by means of)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to act, to do (+ <i>m</i> or <i>mj</i> or <i>ḫft</i>: as, in accordance with; + <i>mj</i>: as, like)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to do (good, evil, justice, wrong, etc.) (+ <i>r</i>: against (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to act as (a function), to act in the capacity of</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make, to craft, to construct (concrete things) (+ <i>m</i>: from, out of)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of gods) to create, to bring into being with divine power</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make, to bring about (intangible or abstract things)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make, to issue, to promulgate (laws, decrees, prescriptions, commands) (+ <i>n</i>: for (someone); + <i>r</i>: against (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to write (texts, documents)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beget (children)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to erect, to build (buildings and monuments)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cultivate, to grow (plants)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to work (fields)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay out (gardens and pools)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to prepare (food and drink)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pass (time), to spend (time)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to celebrate (a festival)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to perform, to undertake (a specified procedure with a defined endpoint)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry out, to execute (a command, decree, etc.) (+ <i>r</i>: against or regarding (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to perform, to make, to offer (a sacrifice)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to perform, to carry out (a ceremony)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;medicine) to prepare and apply, to use (a medicine or remedy)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to perform (a miracle or wonder)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;mathematics) to work out (a value or calculation)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to achieve (a purpose or result)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recite (a spell, praise, or text), to read or speak aloud</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to play (an instrument)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;medicine) to treat (a sickness or ailment)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to appoint (priests or officials) (+ <i>n</i>: to appoint (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gather, to marshal (troops, armies, herds)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gather, to acquire, to amass (cattle, grain, or slaves)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to traverse (lands)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to make into, turn into, change into (something); also, to put into (writing)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i> or <i>ḥr</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to make for, to steer towards (a place or direction)</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;catenative,&#32;with a verb in the subjunctive) to cause, make, let, allow</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;transitive,&#32;with prefixed <i>j.</i> and following infinitive) forms the imperfective active participle</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;transitive,&#32;with prefixed <i>j.</i> and following infinitive) forms the perfective active and passive participles, initially of verbs with four or more radicals, but after the New Kingdom increasingly of other verbs as well</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>regarding it, with respect to it, concerning it, thereto</li><li>(<i>after a preposition</i>) Forms prepositional adverbs from certain prepositions.</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to, relating to, belonging to</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one pertaining to</li><li>keeper, guardian, custodian</li><li>companion, comrade</li><li>duty</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“adverbializing suffix”).</p><p>From <i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”). The noun is simply a nominalized use of the <i>nisba</i> adjective.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with following suffix pronoun and prepositional phrase</i>) to make (one’s) abode (somewhere); to sojourn, to take (one’s) place (at) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>with following suffix pronoun and prepositional phrase</i>) to have (someone else) take up a place or abode (somewhere) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“abode”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a plant) to flower, to blossom</li></ol><p>From <i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrrt</i> (“flower”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;of gods and the dead</i>) to take on a new form (+ <i>m</i>: to assume the form of, to transform into, to manifest as)</li><li>to grow up (somewhere), to undergo one’s upbringing (in some place)</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”) +&lrm; <i>ḫprw</i> (“form”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go off with or walk with (someone)<sup>?</sup></li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣ</i> (“to tread<sup>?</sup>”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>gs</i> (“side”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cease</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣbw</i> (“cessation”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a court title: keeper of the diadem</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“one belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>nfr-ḥꜣt</i> (“diadem”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowman</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“one belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>pḏt</i> (“bow (weapon)”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hereditary nobleman</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“one belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>pꜥt</i> (“patricians”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room-keeper</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“(one) pertaining to”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥt</i> (“room”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorkeeper</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“keeper”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“door”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘keeper of the door’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(tree) trunk &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(wooden) beam &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) together with, along with, with (comitative)</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;less commonly) and, in addition to</li></ol><p>Contracted from earlier <i>r mn</i> (“together with”), from still earlier <i>r mn m</i> (“up to, as far as”, literally “to remain in”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, of historical usage, notably borne by Arsinoe II</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀρσινόη</i> (Arsinóē).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li><li>duty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of various goddesses, especially Hathor&#59; the Eye of Ra</li><li>the sun</li><li>Egypt</li></ol><p><i>jrt</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“sun, Ra”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘the Eye of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the actual Eye of Horus in mythology</li><li>(<i>in funerary offering spells</i>) the offering given to the deceased</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an ointment used in mummification &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in wine texts</i>) an epithet for wine</li><li>(<i>in cosmetic texts</i>) an epithet for <i>wꜣḏ</i>-cosmetic</li><li>(in <i>qbḥw</i> texts) an epithet for water</li></ol><li>epithet for various goddesses as the personified eyes of Horus</li><li>an epithet for fire &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jrt</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Eye of Horus’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue, a blue mineral</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rite, ritual, ceremony</li><li>product, creation</li><li>form</li><li>(<i>in titles</i>) maker, creator</li></ol><p><i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p><p><i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Alara, a pharaoh of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) women &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A knife &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>used in conjunction with <i>nj</i> (or sometimes <i>nn</i>) to negate nominal sentences, adjectival sentences of possession, and rhemes of emphatic clauses.</li><li>(inserted after the first word or phrase of a clause,&#32;archaic by Middle Egyptian) serves as a complementizer to convert a sentence into a subordinated noun or adverbial clause&#59; that, for</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jz</i> (“tomb”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>jzr</i> (“tamarisk”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to go to (bed)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiver for archery &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>language</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אַשְׁפָּה</i> (ʾašpâh) or a cognate Semitic word. Later reanalysed as a root <i>jsp-</i> + <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashkelon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jzr</i> (“tamarisk”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jsṯ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>lo, behold</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recompense, remuneration &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assur</li><li>Assyria (an ancient Semitic Akkadian kingdom and at times an empire, extant from the mid-23rd century <small>BCE</small> to 608 <small>BCE</small> and centred on the Upper Tigris river in northern Mesopotamia (present day northern Iraq, but at its maximum extent including parts of modern Iran, Syria, Israel, Palestine and Egypt))</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Akkadian <i>𒀸𒋩</i> (Aššur, “Assur”). Doublet of ꜣšꜣwꜣrw.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>converts a nonverbal sentence into a dependent adverbial (circumstantial) clause&#59; while, when, although</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>sṯ</i>, from Old Egyptian <i>sk</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li><li>an ancestor</li><li>barley</li><li>grain in general</li></ol><p>Blaz̆ek (2002) compares Proto-Berber <i>&#42;təy</i> (whence Tuareg <i>ti</i>).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley of some kind; see the etymology section above for discussion</li></ol><p><i>jt</i> (“barley”) +&lrm; <i>mḥ</i>. There are differing views regarding the interpretation of the second element; traditionally it was considered a derivative of <i>mḥw</i> (“Lower Egypt”), making the whole compound mean “Lower Egyptian barley” in contrast with <i>jt-šmꜥ</i> (“Upper Egyptian barley”). Allen instead considers the second element to be <i>mḥ</i> (“full”), making the whole compound mean “full barley” in contrast with <i>šmꜥ</i> (“‘thin’ barley, i.e. barley with fewer grains”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Nu</li></ol><p><i>jt</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>nṯrw</i>, plural of <i>nṯr</i> (“god”), in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Father of the Gods’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Upper Egyptian barley</li></ol><p><i>jt</i> (“barley”) +&lrm; <i>šmꜥ</i> (“Upper Egypt”) in a direct genitive construction. Allen instead apparently considers the second element a <i>nisba</i> adjective, <i>&#42;šmꜥj</i> (“Upper Egyptian”), with unwritten weak <i>j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jṯj</i> (“to take”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;rare) alternative form of <i>tn</i> (“this, that”) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;rare) alternative form of <i>tj</i> (“this”) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suffocation</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the disc of any celestial body, including the sun, the moon, the stars, and the planets</li><li>the Aten; the disc of the sun in particular, often as the manifestation of the solar god</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set oneself against, to oppose</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to contradict</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) alternative form of <i>tn</i> (“this, that”) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust, ashes &lsqb;since the Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>material of the ground, earth, soil &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>ground, earth (as opposed to sky, etc.) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>floor, flooring (bottom surface of a room or building)</li><li>piece of land</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magical substance, ‘dust of the courtyard’ &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jtnw</i> (“dust, ground”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>h</i> (“courtyard<sup>?</sup>”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) row (of people, shrines, shrubs, etc.)</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the dual</i>) chapel, temple</li><li>niche to hold the statue of a god</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>jtrw</i> (“river”), with a basic meaning of ‘line, row’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river</li><li>The Nile River.</li><li>a schene, a measure of length equivalent to 20,000 <i>mḥw</i> (“cubits”) (about 10.5 kilometres or 6.5 miles).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Schneider reconstructs the name as Northwest Semitic <i>*ʾadōnī-rōʿē-yāh</i>, "My lord is the shepherd of Yah"; both Schneider and Shalomi Hen identify <i>*-yāh</i> with an abbreviated form of the toponym <i>yhwꜣ</i>, attested to during the 13th century BCE, whose relationship to the theonym Yahweh is still debated. Schneider takes the phrase <i>*rōʿē-yāh</i> as a whole as a divine epithet for Yahweh in his association with the toponym.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sire, sovereign</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>jt(j)</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pull</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) asserts the reality of a following statement with a verb in the perfect, perfect passive, or imperfective, or else with an adverbial or adjectival predicate, probably serving as a realis modal particle&#59; see usage notes</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) introduces an adverbial clause with a pronominal subject&#59; see usage notes</li><li>(chiefly&#32;Old Egyptian,&#32;rare,&#32;with a following bare noun phrase) there is, there are; introduces an independent existential clause</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) introduces a subordinate circumstantial clause</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) introduces a sentence in the third future</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>island</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) regions, lands (+ genitive: of (the earth, Egypt, etc.)) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>wrongdoing, misconduct, evil &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>wrongness, injustice, error</li><li>misfortune; wrong or evil suffered</li><li>dog &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) hunchback &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sever, to cut off (something) (+ <i>r</i>: from (something else)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sever, to cut through (something)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wail, to lament (+ <i>n</i>: for, over (someone)) &lsqb;since the Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) to complain about (someone)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective passive participle of <i>jwj</i> (“to leave boatless, to strand”): boatless</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a settlement in Egypt at what is now El-Mahamid El Qibly near Gebelein &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jw</i> (“island”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jtrw</i> (“river”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mythical region of the afterworld &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jw</i> (“island”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣmw</i> (“burning”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>sḫt</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>sjsj</i> (“fire”), thus literally ‘The Isle of Burning in the Field of Fire’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Iuseneb</li></ol><p>From the imperfective active participle of <i>jj</i> (“to come”) +&lrm; <i>snb</i> (“to be healthy”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus literally ‘(the one) coming healthy’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man on the rise, someone quickly becoming important &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jw</i> (“comes”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“becomes great”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”), a reciprocal sentence, literally ‘no sooner does he come than he becomes great’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>bjt</i> (“a kind of flat loaf of bread”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive</i>) to part, to separate, to remove (something or someone) (+ <i>r</i> or second direct object: from (something else)) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;in administrative use) to separate out, to requisition<sup>?</sup> (people)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>r jwd</i> (“between”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of incense</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separation &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwd</i> (“to separate”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh</li><li>(<i>as food</i>) meat, including fish meat</li><li>body</li><li>epithet of the god Ra when he passes through the afterworld at night &lsqb;Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) body, corpse (of some particular god) &lsqb;Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Tashelhit <i>tifiyyi</i> (“flesh, meat”), and Iraqw <i>fuʔuni</i> (“meat”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jgrt</i> (“epithet for the afterworld”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive</i>) to load, to burden (someone) (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>: with (something)) &lsqb;Second Intermediate Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bear, to carry (something) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to arrive, to come to a certain place (+ <i>r</i> or <i>n</i>: to (a place, person, thing, etc.); + <i>ḫr</i>: to (someone of higher status); + <i>m</i>: from (a place), into (something), by means of (a vehicle), as (a role); + <i>ḥr</i>: to, upon (something), from, for the sake of; + <i>m-dj</i>: from (someone))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come here, to move from further to nearby</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to return, to come back</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of future events, time, abstractions, etc.) to come to pass or come to be, to come, to arrive</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i> and following infinitive) to come (to do something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to leave boatless, to maroon, to strand</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to bar from, to exclude from (a boat) &lsqb;22nd Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) boatless, stranded</li></ol><p>Compare <i>jw</i> (“to sever, to cut off”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) wrong</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lament or wail &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Probably a reduplication of <i>jw</i> (“dog”). The verb is then likely denominal, with its meaning deriving from the cry of the dog.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exaggeration, untruth, nonsense, humbug &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to Saite Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>jw</i> (sentence-initial particle) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (“surely”). The phrase <i>jw ms</i> was so often used to begin sentences intended to persuade that it became an idiom for untruth or exaggeration.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>color &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>complexion, skin color</li><li>(<i>of a god</i>) look, appearance</li><li>character, nature, sort</li><li>pillar, column</li><li>one of the mythical pillars seen as holding up the sky</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) person upon whom someone or something else depends; crucial supporter, maintainer, sustainer, succorer</li><li>‘the Pillar’, ‘the Succorer’ as an epithet for gods, in the Greco-Roman Period especially for Osiris</li><li>air or wind, seen as a support holding up the sky &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unite (something) (+ <i>n</i>: with (something else)) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>ḥnꜥ</i>) to join (someone) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the stative</i>) together &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;lwn-</i>; compare Arabic لَوْن (lawn, “color, type”), Ugaritic <i>𐎀𐎐</i> (ản, “pigment”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nasal bone &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>edge of an obelisk &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwn</i> (“pillar”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>fnḏ</i> (“nose”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title of disputed significance, possibly a priestly title or a title for a high administrator of some kind &lsqb;chiefly Old Kingdom, rarely also Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jwn</i> (“pillar, succorer”) +&lrm; <i>knmwt</i> (“oasis-dwellers<sup>?</sup>”) in a direct genitive construction, thus possibly ‘succorer of the oasis-dwellers’, although the exact signification of <i>knmwt</i> is disputed.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priestly title</li><li>epithet of the god Horus when young</li></ol><p>From <i>jwn</i> (“pillar, succorer”) +&lrm; <i>mwt</i> (“mother”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘succorer of his mother’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the god Shu &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwn</i> (“air, wind, seen as holding up the sky”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”), thus ‘the Great Air’, ‘the Great Supporter (of the sky)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hermonthis, a settlement in Egypt, the cult center of the god Montu, at what is now Armant</li></ol><p>From <i>jwn</i> (“pillar”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanctuary (of a god) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>jwn</i> (“pillar”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bow (weapon)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dendera</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>jwn</i> (“pillar”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) nomadic desert Nubian</li><li>(rare,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) eastern desert nomad in general, for instance in the Sinai or Wadi Hammamat</li></ol><p>From <i>jwnt</i> (“bow”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending), thus ‘(one) relating to the bow’, ‘bowman’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heliopolis</li></ol><p>Likely from the plural of <i>jwn</i> (“pillar, column”), thus literally meaning ‘Pillars’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thebes</li><li>Hermonthis</li></ol><p><i>jwnw</i> (“Heliopolis”) +&lrm; <i>šmꜥw</i> (“Upper Egypt”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘the Heliopolis of Upper Egypt’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypostyle hall &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latopolis or Esna, a settlement in Upper Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>jwn</i> (“pillar”) +&lrm; <i>-yt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) accompanies <i>bn</i> to negate an adverbial or nominal sentence, following after the negated element</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conceive (a child), to become pregnant with</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be conceived (+ <i>n</i>: by (a father))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) pregnant (+ <i>m</i>: with (a child))</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>n</i>,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of land) to be made fertile by the flooding of (the Nile)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of land) to be(come) full of, to abound with (grain)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beans &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>jwr</i> (“to conceive”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A settlement in Canaan. Jericho</li></ol><p>Possibly, though doubtfully, from Hebrew <i>יְרִיחוֹ</i> (yərīḥō).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, scales, especially handheld scales &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature to Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Old Egyptian</i>) introduces a negated noun clause with realis mood; that not</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the negative relative adjective — allows a clause to serve as a negated relative clause, usually with an adverbial or verbal predicate &lsqb;chiefly Old Egyptian&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(introducing a direct relative clause,&#32;with <i>jwtj</i> serving in place of a subject) which is not, who is not, not being</li><li>(introducing an indirect relative clause,&#32;with a later resumptive pronoun) such that it is not the case that, for which it is not the case that</li><li>(<i>introducing a noun phrase with suffix pronoun</i>) which has no, who has no, not having, without</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>introducing a direct relative clause</i>) he who is not, one who is not, that which is not</li><li>(introducing an indirect relative clause,&#32;with a later resumptive pronoun) he for whom it is not the case that, one for whom it is not the case that, that for which it is not the case that</li><li>(<i>introducing a noun phrase with suffix pronoun</i>) he who doesn’t have, one not having, one without</li><li>(<i>without a following relative clause or noun phrase</i>) he who doesn’t exist, one who doesn’t exist, that which doesn’t exist</li></ol><p>From <i>jwt</i> (negation particle) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending). The noun <i>jwtj</i> is in turn simply a nominalized use of the adjective <i>jwtj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has nothing, one of the have-nots, dispossessed person, poor person</li></ol><p>From <i>jwtj</i> (“one for whom it is not the case that”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“to, for”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“him”), thus literally ‘one for whom (there is) not (something) for him’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jwtj-n.f</i> (“one who has nothing”)</li></ol><p>From <i>jwtj</i> (“one for whom it is not the case that”) +&lrm; <i>sw</i> (“him”). Allen considers the syntax of this term not satisfactorily explained.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jwtj-n.f</i> (“one who has nothing”)</li></ol><p>From <i>jwtj</i> (“one for whom it is not the case that”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”). No good explanation exists for the syntax of this term.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) the dead</li><li>putrefaction, corruption, what is decayed &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From the plural of <i>jwtj</i> (“which is not”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jtnw</i> (“ground, earth, floor”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the nonexistent; that which doesn’t exist</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) synonym of <i>jwt</i> (“that not”)</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the feminine of <i>jwtj</i> (negative relative adjective).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ntt jwtt</i> (“everything imaginable”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) the stretches of alluvial land in the Nile delta separated by the arms of the river &lsqb;Kushite and Greco-Roman Periods&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jww</i> (“islands”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrjw jb</i> (“being in the midst”, literally “being upon the heart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>elegant speech</i>) house &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>dwelling-place of a god, sanctuary</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) streets, public places collectively</li><li>quarter of a town</li><li>wrongdoing, misconduct, evil &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jw</i> (“wrongdoing”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of mash, gruel, or paste &lsqb;Medical Papyri to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>לוּשׁ</i> (lōš, “to knead”) and other descendants of Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;lawaṯ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to moisten, to wet (something) (+ <i>m</i>: with) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to water (plants, fields, barren land), including by flood &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sprinkle or apply (a liquid healing substance) (+ <i>m</i>: on or in) &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make an offering of (water, beer, or another liquid) &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to weep, to cry, to mourn &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deck with lotuses &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwḥ</i> (“to wet, to water”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in, with”) +&lrm; <i>zšn</i> (“lotus flower”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inundation &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwḥ</i> (“to moisten, to water”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox, especially as a sacrificial animal</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) long-horned cattle in contrast to <i>wnḏw</i> (“short-horned cattle”)</li><li>(<i>with following genitive of an antelope, gazelle, ibex, etc.</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>male animal</li></ol><li>beef</li></ol><p>Cognate with Proto-Agaw <i>*ləw-</i>, Proto-Cushitic <i>&#42;ɬoʔ-</i> (“cow(s)”), and Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;layʔ-</i> (“bull”), whence Akkadian <i>𒄞</i> (lûm) and Arabic لَأًى (laʔan).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwꜣ</i> (“ox”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female substitute worker or helper &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li><li>throat &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haunch of beef (as food)</li><li>human thigh</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) an heir, to receive one’s inheritance</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to act as heir in performing the funerary duties for one’s predecessor</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inherit (something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inherit from, to succeed (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to endow, to present, to reward (someone) (+ <i>m</i>: with (something)) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inheritance, heirdom</li><li>heiress &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwꜥ</i> (“to inherit”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heir</li><li>image of a dead person in the form of a statue, seen as an ‘heir’ in the sense of being left behind on the earth after his death &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a ring or armband given as a reward &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jwꜥ</i> (“to inherit”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garrison, occupational force; troops stationed in or occupying a particular place &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jwꜥ</i> (“to inherit; to endow”) +&lrm; <i>-yt</i>; the underlying sense may thus be a ‘complement’ of troops with which a place is endowed.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unfavorable occurrences; mishap, harm, trouble &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>ill deeds; mischief, wrong, harm, offence</li></ol><p>From <i>jj</i> (“to come”), thus literally ‘that which has come’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomb (including both walled structures and rock-cut tombs) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>often dual during the Old Kingdom</i>) administrative chamber, bureau</li><li>workshop, especially for the preparation of ointments &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom; chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) old, used, from bygone times &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) in decline, deteriorating, decaying</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falsehood, lie</li><li>wrong, sin, wickedness</li><li>injustice, wrong (done to someone)</li><li>disorder or chaos in the state or cosmos</li><li>uncleanliness &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evildoer, villain</li></ol><p><i>jzft</i> (“wrong, falsehood, evil”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Neferefre, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Unclear. Like many fifth-dynasty pharaohs’ birth names, this name may simply be a diminutive without any meaning of its own.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a region of the eastern sky close to the horizon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamarisk</li></ol><p>According to the Rösslerian school of Egyptian comparison, cognate with Arabic أَثْل (ʔaṯl, “tamarisk”) (whence English <i>athel</i>).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work-gang, team or crew of workers &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>crew of a boat, especially the solar barque &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>troop, band, or company of soldiers &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>entourage or following of a king or god &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>ancient times, antiquity, yore</li></ol><p>From <i>jz</i> (“old, ancient”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Djedkare Isesi, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Like many fifth-dynasty pharaohs’ birth names, this name may simply be a diminutive without any meaning of its own. Alternatively, some have tentatively attempted to explain it as derived from the imperative verb <i>jz</i> (“go!”) as an expression possibly uttered by a midwife at the time of birth.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of sacred tree in Heliopolis</li><li>the fruit of the aforementioned tree</li><li>a fruit in general</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>what?</li></ol><p><i>jḫ</i> (“what”) +&lrm; <i>st</i> (“common third person dependent pronoun”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry (here), to bring out &lsqb;Book of the Dead and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spit or sneeze out</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spittle</li><li>anything spat out</li><li>image &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox</li><li>bull or cow, neat, head of cattle</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥḥ</i> (“palace”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥḥ</i> (“to capture, to enclose”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow</li></ol><p>From <i>jḥ</i> (“ox; head of cattle”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stable-building</li><li>weakness</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>what?</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>then, thus; introduces a clause with consequent optative meaning, expressing something the speaker wishes or requests to happen as a result</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) bank, embankment (of a river, valley, or fortress)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḫr</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twilight; dawn or (especially) dusk &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature, Book of the Dead, and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take, to seize</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overcome</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry off, to take away &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to steal &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Presumably related to <i>jṯj</i> (“to seize”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Pyramid Texts</i>) alternative form of <i>jꜣbt</i> (“east wind”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastern</li><li>left-hand, left (direction)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>left (direction)</li><li>left side (of someone or something)</li><li>(<i>originally&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) left arm &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>jb</i> (“heart”). Suffixed with <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>east, the East</li><li>left side</li><li>east wind</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the feminine of <i>jꜣbj</i>.</p><p>The <i>Wörterbuch</i> considers this a further derived nominalized <i>nisba</i> adjective, presumably formed from <i>jꜣbt</i> (“east”) +&lrm; <i>-(j)</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending); however, the form <i>&#42;jꜣbtj</i> with the <i>nisba</i> ending written out is nowhere attested with this meaning. Such an analysis is, nonetheless, supported by the masculine gender of this term.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastern</li><li>left-hand, left (direction)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>east, the East</li><li>eastern side (of something)</li></ol><p><i>jꜣbt</i> (“east, left”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) easterners</li><li>east side, the east of a place &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jꜣbtj</i> (“eastern”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural suffix).</p><p>Ultimately from <i>jꜣbt</i> (“east”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>east, the East</li><li>(<i>18th Dynasty</i>) alternative form of <i>jbṯt</i> (“bird trap”)</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the feminine of <i>jꜣbtj</i> (“eastern”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to suffer</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to experience a lack or deficiency (+ <i>ḥr</i>: of)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to torment, to hurt, to make suffer</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣdw</i> (“pestilence”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dew</li><li>pouring rain in a storm</li><li>‘dew’ or ‘sweat’ of a god, in figurative reference to the god’s pleasant scent</li><li>divine might or influence</li><li>pestilence, affliction</li><li>lack, deficiency, need</li><li>net, especially one used for fishing or fowling</li><li>a kind of field or tract of land</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pastures for livestock, pastureland</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p><p>From <i>jꜣd</i> (“to suffer, to experience a lack”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p><p>Perhaps compare <i>jꜣd</i> (“kind of cord on a door-bolt”), <i>jwd</i> (“to ensnare”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestilence, plague, epidemic &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jdw</i> (“young man”)</li></ol><p><i>jꜣd</i> (“to suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>. Compare Arabic دَاء (dāʔ, “disease, ailment”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw, talon &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;liʔif-&#47;&#42;liʔaf</i> (“fingernail, claw”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to praise, to adore</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) an epithet for braids of hair, probably meaning “dancers” &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old man, particularly as an experienced person</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mourn</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wail, to lament (+ <i>n</i> or <i>ḥr</i>: over, because of)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourning</li><li>lament, wail</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourner</li><li>an epithet for various gods</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a location in the afterworld</li></ol><p>From <i>jꜣkb</i> (“to mourn”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> (agent nominalizer).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourning-woman</li><li>an epithet for various goddesss</li></ol><p>From <i>jꜣkby</i> (“mourner”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jkw</i> (“stone quarry”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bind (cattle) for sacrifice &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jmꜣ</i> (“to be(come) kindly, friendly, or gracious”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣm</i> (“to burn”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jmꜣ</i> (“a kind of tree”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to climb up, to ascend (+ <i>r</i>: to; + <i>ḥr</i>: on, by means of (a ladder)) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to leap</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) leeks</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) vegetables, greens (in general)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;wrḳ-</i>. Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;waraq-</i> (“green”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of eyes) to be(come) dim, clouded, weak</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart) to be(come) weak</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of poison) to be(come) ineffectual</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) grapes</li><li>grape</li><li>vine, grapevine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rushes, reeds</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) bald</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mound, especially as a sacred site or location in the Afterworld &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>ruin, mound of ruins</li><li>cultivated land, fields &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(chiefly&#32;in the plural,&#32;expressively) regions, lands, foreign lands &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) spine, backbone &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;of humans or animals) back</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) middle of a body of water (lake or river)</li><li>standard for carrying cultic images and objects</li><li>Part of a place name in the Afterworld. &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iat, a goddess who serves as nurse to the dead, likely a goddess of milk &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any one of the many sacred cult sites where a body part of the dismembered Osiris was claimed to be buried, most commonly the site on Bigeh; abaton &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jꜣt</i> (“mound”) +&lrm; <i>wꜥbt</i> (feminine of <i>wꜥb</i> (“clean, pure”)), thus literally ‘pure mound’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutilation &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) mound-dwellers</li></ol><p><i>jꜣṯ</i> (“to be(come) injured, mutilated”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>. The expected form <i>jꜣṯw</i> is unattested with this meaning, although it exists with other related significations.</p><p><i>jꜣt</i> (“mound”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) praise, adoration</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) old age &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>old man</li><li>most senior official</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) old &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣw</i> (“old age”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office, dignity, position &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>function, role, duty</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;by metonymy) officials, officeholders</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) occupation, profession</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) old age &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>old woman &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) alternative form of <i>ꜥwt</i> (“four-legged animals, herds”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) Egypt &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jꜣwj</i> (“to be(come) old”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p><i>jꜣw</i> (“old man”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p><p>From the plural of <i>jꜣt</i> (“mound”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>officeholder, dignitary &lsqb;19th Dynasty and Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jꜣwt</i> (“office”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣwt</i> (“old woman”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly Middle Kingdom</i>) alternative form of <i>ꜥš</i> (“to summon, to call”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achaemenid</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎧𐎠𐎶𐎴𐎡𐏁𐎡𐎹</i> (Haxāmanišiya).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunlight, sunshine</li><li>radiance</li><li>used as an epithet for various gods, particularly Osiris and Ra</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) injured, to be(come) mutilated</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hurt, to be(come) painful</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) distorted</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) missing, to abate or disappear (+ <i>r</i>: from)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place of execution</li><li>scene of carnage, shambles</li></ol><p><i>jꜣṯ</i> (“to be(come) injured, mutilated”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>. Compare <i>jꜣtjw</i> (“mutilation”) of the same derivation.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of plant, possibly a reed</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣꜣ</i> (“mound of ruins”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a land in Syria/Palestine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of club or scepter</li><li>rod, branch &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>&lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣꜣt</i> (“rod, branch”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kilt &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>It relates to the handling of calves.</i> &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥš</i> (“to call, to summon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomb &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥr</i> (“to ascend, to approach”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of ceremony &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jꜥ</i> (“washing”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”), thus ‘washing of the face’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unite, to put together (the limbs or bones of a dead person or Osiris)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;in the stative) to be collected, assembled, to be all together (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: to be united with)</li><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive</i>) to unify, to unite, to assemble (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to join (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to present (offerings or gifts) (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give a gift to (someone) (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>: of (something)) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rake together and heap up (grain) with a pitchfork &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl, especially a small bowl used for incense (attached to a censer’s handle) or a scribe’s water bowl</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury a body</li></ol><p><i>jꜥb</i> (“to unite”) +&lrm; <i>ẖꜣt</i> (“corpse”), thus literally ‘to unite a corpse’ (i.e., with the earth).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wring out (wet clothes) &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to press, to squeeze out (wine or grape juice) &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash (the body, limbs, clothes, tools, oneself, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash off, to wash out, to wash away (inscriptions, blood, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to treat (food) with water or milk &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wet (wooden rods) in order to bend (them)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) happy, to rejoice</li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) to make (someone) happy, to satisfy (someone)</li><li>to satisfy oneself by giving vent to one’s desires: to slake one’s ardor, to take vengeance on one’s enemies, etc.</li></ol><p><i>jꜥj</i> (“to wash”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), thus ‘to wash the heart/mind’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate the heavier gold out from lighter gangue by the action of flowing water; to wash gold</li></ol><p><i>jꜥj</i> (“to wash”) +&lrm; <i>nbw</i> (“gold”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> jꜥj,&lrm; ḥr.</li></ol><p><i>jꜥj</i> (“to wash”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”), thus ‘to wash the face’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) baboon</li><li>an epithet of the god Thoth</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a constellation, the Baboon</li></ol><p>One early writing of the word shows the form <i>ꜥnr</i>, which Erman and Grapow take to represent the earliest known form of the word; however, Vycichl considers this a pseudo-archaic secondary development, since none of the known plural forms conserve the consonant <i>r</i> in this position.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female baboon</li></ol><p><i>jꜥn</i> (“baboon”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woe, grief &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>lament, wail, cry of lamentation</li><li>greetings, cry of respect (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>a cry of lamentation&#58; woe, alas (+ <i>n</i>: woe to (someone))</li><li>a cry of reverential greeting&#58; greetings, hail, praise (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to ascend (+ <i>r</i>: to (a place or (less often) person); + <i>n</i> to (a person or (less often) place)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of royal dignity) to arise &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a wound) to extend, to penetrate (+ <i>n</i>: to (bone, etc.))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>, <i>m</i>, or <i>ḫr</i>) to approach, to come to (someone or something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to approach, to come to (someone or something) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up (someone or something) to oneself, to lift &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Speculatively, from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ʕal-</i>; as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking. If so, perhaps compare Hebrew <i>עָלָה</i> (ʕālā, “to ascend, rise, go up”), Arabic عَلِيَ (ʕaliya, “to be high, exalted”), عَلَى (ʕalā, “to climb”), Tarifit <i>aři</i> (“to climb, ascend”), Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⴰⵍⵢ</i> (aly, “to climb, go up”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uraeus</li><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥryt</i> (“dwelling-place of a god”)</li></ol><p>From <i>jꜥr</i> (“to ascend”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling-place of a god &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakfast</li><li>only used in <i>jꜥw n jb</i></li></ol><p><i>jꜥj</i> (“to wash”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>, literally ‘washing’. The ‘breakfast’ sense is shortened from <i>jꜥw-r</i> (“breakfast”, literally “washing of the mouth”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angry speech, venting</li></ol><p><i>jꜥw</i> (“washing”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), thus literally ‘washing of the heart/mind’. For the meaning compare <i>jꜥj jb</i> (“to satisfy, to satisfy oneself, to give vent to one’s desires, etc.”, literally “to wash the heart/mind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakfast</li></ol><p><i>jꜥw</i> (“washing”, from <i>jꜥj</i> (“to wash”)) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“mouth”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘washing of the mouth’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the moon</li><li>the god Iah, personification of the moon</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;warḫ-</i> (“moon, month”) and Proto-Berber <i>&#42;a-ʔyur</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festival of the full moon</li><li>epithet for the god Khonsu as the full moon</li></ol><p>From <i>jꜥḥ</i> (“moon”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ahmose</li><li>a female given name, Ahmose</li></ol><p><i>jꜥḥ</i> (“the moon/Iah”) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (“to give birth”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine third-person singular stative ending), thus ‘the moon is born’ or ‘Iah is born’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ahhotep</li></ol><p><i>jꜥḥ</i> (“Iah/the moon”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus literally ‘Iah/the moon is content’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣꜥ</i> (“kilt”) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣꜥꜥ</i> (“to plaster”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byblos, a town in the Levant &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Semitic <i>*gubl-</i>; compare Phoenician <i>𐤂𐤁𐤋</i> (gbl), Akkadian <i>𒁺𒆷</i> (/⁠gubla⁠/), Hebrew <i>גְּבַל</i> (gəḇál), Arabic جُبَيْل (jubayl).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) obsidian</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remove clothing or bandages from (a part of the body); to bare, to expose, to uncover &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reveal, to disclose (something hidden) in general &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to clear (the sky) of clouds &lsqb;since the First Intermediate Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take off or remove (a piece of clothing)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to strip, to plunder, to despoil &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea-going ship</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crete, the Minoan civilisation</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) discreet</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hindquarters, butt</li><li>bottom (of jar)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Arabic خلف (ḵalfa, “behind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) trustworthy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confidant, trusted person</li></ol><p><i>kfꜣ</i> (“to be(come) discreet”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to clamor, to cry out</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Kek or Kuk, personification of the darkness of the primeval waters &lsqb;since the Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to complete</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to total, to sum up to</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>kmm</i>: black</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;kum-</i> (“to be black”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, of historical usage, notably borne by the Achaemenid king Cambyses</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Persian <i>𐎣𐎲𐎢𐎪𐎡𐎹</i> (k-b-u-ji-i-y /⁠Kaᵐbūjiyaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) black</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egypt, the Nile Valley</li><li>the Egyptians</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of jar</li></ol><p>From <i>km</i> (“black”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>, interpreted as a reference to the fertile black soil of the Nile Delta.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a compendium of Middle Kingdom letters and correspondence, later used as a model from which to train scribes</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gina (an ancient Canaanite settlement, identified with modern-day Jenin)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>גִּנָּה</i> (ginnāh).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canaanites</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) the two horns of Amun’s headdress</li></ol><p>Suffixed with <i>-tj</i> (dual suffix). Some form of (loan) relationship with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ḳarn-</i> and/or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> has been suggested (see those pages for more) but is problematic to establish.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bow down (+ <i>n</i> or <i>ḥr</i>: before (someone))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend down (in order to pick something up)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend (something)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowing, bow, obeisance</li></ol><p><i>ksj</i> (“to bow down”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) little, small in size</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of children) to be(come) little, very young in age</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of siblings and offspring) to be(come) younger (relative to other siblings or offspring)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of time) to be(come) short, brief</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) little, sparse, small in quantity</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) trifling, weak, unimportant</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little girl</li><li>louse</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) you (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>other, another &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of monkey</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in religious and medical texts</i>) stands at the start of phrases giving variant readings, glosses, or interpretations&#59; alternatively; otherwise said; alternative reading: …</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;in letters) stands at the start of phrases giving further information&#59; furthermore; and another thing: …</li></ol><p><i>ky</i> (“another”) +&lrm; <i>ḏd</i> (“speaking”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘another one of speaking’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ka (Something like "life-force"; the part of the soul which the living have and the dead do not)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>personality, essence</li></ol><li>goodwill - especially of the king</li><li>sustenance, food</li><li>bull</li><li>bull as a sacrificial victim</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Predynastic king Ka</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with following subjunctive or nonverbal predicate</i>) so, then, as a result; expresses future consequence</li><li>(<i>with following suffix pronoun/subject and imperfective</i>) then; expresses subsequent action</li><li>(<i>with following suffix pronoun indicating the speaker</i>) marks a future direct quotation; will say</li></ol><p>From <i>kꜣj</i> (“to plan”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Tuthmosis III, a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>kꜣ</i> (“bull”) +&lrm; <i>nḫt</i> (“strong, victorious”) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜥ</i> (“to appear”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>wꜣst</i> (“Thebes”), thus literally ‘The Victorious Bull Appearing in Thebes’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Choiak</li></ol><p><i>kꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i>, literally ‘Ka Upon Ka’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) harsh</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to plan, to plot</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bush, shrub</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Neferirkare Kakai, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Unclear. Like many fifth-dynasty pharaohs’ birth names, this name may simply be a diminutive without any meaning of its own, perhaps of the pharaoh’s throne name <i>nfr-jr-kꜣ-rꜥ</i> (in which case it may not be the actual name given at birth). Alternatively, some have tentatively attempted to explain it as <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force, sustenance”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>.j</i> (“my”), thus ‘my life-force is a life-force’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vineyard</li></ol><p>From an earlier form <i>kꜣnw</i> before undergoing a sound change that caused the coalescence of the two final consonants into <i>m</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of the eyes) To be blind.</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) To blind (someone).</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) Used in the phrase <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./cb5476b2.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="k" alt="glyph" height="23"/><br /><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="82"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d6a1e5d7.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="mn" alt="glyph" height="32"/><br /><img src="./6c9cd1c6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="n" alt="glyph" height="12"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./74158595.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="D6" alt="glyph" height="35"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ad66c539.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="wa" alt="glyph" height="37"/><br /><img src="./9b32fa22.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="a" alt="glyph" height="30"/> <img src="./b207efda.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z1" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f9c3d9c7.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="D4" alt="glyph" height="30"/><br /><img src="./76993ec3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="t" alt="glyph" height="27"/> <img src="./b207efda.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z1" alt="glyph" height="40"/> <img src="./3706e58c.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="F51A" alt="glyph" height="35"/></td>
</tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, meaning <i>blind in one eye</i>.</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of the earth during a storm) To be dark.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) blind man</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vintner, gardener</li></ol><p><i>kꜣm</i> (“vineyard, garden”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Knossos, an ancient city of Crete</li></ol><p>Compare Minoan <i>𐘾𐘯𐘠</i> (ka-nu-ti).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canaan</li></ol><p>Related to Hebrew <i>כְּנַעַן</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelter</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hide, to shelter, to take cover &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put a roof on</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cense, to fumigate &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incense</li></ol><p><i>kꜣp</i> (“to cense”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocodile</li><li>roof</li><li>bittern</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chapel, shrine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gardener</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>kꜣš</i> (“Kush”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work, labor</li><li>vagina, vulva</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) unripe sycamore figs</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker, laborer &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Related to <i>kꜣt</i> (“work”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cush, a kingdom in northern Sudan</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Kashta, a pharaoh of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>kꜣš</i> (“the land of Cush”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) in, for, during</li><li>(<i>with certain verbs of motion</i>) into</li><li>(<i>with most verbs of motion</i>) from within, out of, from</li><li>(<i>of material</i>) made of, consisting of</li><li>(<i>of contents, e.g. of a group</i>) consisting of, comprising</li><li>by means of</li><li>in a state of</li><li>in the capacity of, in the role of, as, being</li><li>together with, along with</li><li>(<i>with following infinitive</i>) forms the periphrastic imperfective of a verb</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly Old and Middle Egyptian,&#32;with a suffix pronoun) behold, lo, look</li><li>(<i>without a suffix pronoun</i>) whether, although</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>imperative of <i>jmj</i> (“to not be, to not do”): don't</li></ol><p>Often suggested to be cognate to Hebrew <i>בְּ־</i> (bə-), Arabic بِـ (bi-); however, more recent scholarship disputes this on phonological grounds, as Egyptian <i>m</i> is not held to regularly correspond with Semitic <i>&#42;b</i> by either the traditional school of Egyptian comparison or the Rösslerian school.</p><p>Possibly originally a verb form, an imperative meaning ‘see’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in front of, before, in the presence of (a king or god)</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>bꜣḥ</i> (“phallus”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hastily, speedily, in a hurry, headlong</li></ol><p>From <i>m</i> (“ in, by means of”) +&lrm; <i>jfd</i> (“to flee, to rush across”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>rejoicing, in merriment, making merry &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jbꜣ</i> (“dance, entertainment”); the latter word has its Greco-Roman Period spelling.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an instant, in the twinkling of an eye &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>km</i> (“black”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i> (“moment”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>likewise, similarly, equally</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as well as, and likewise, and similarly</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in, as”) +&lrm; <i>mjtt</i> (“likeness, the like”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>daily</li><li>(<i>in calculations</i>) per day</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>mnt</i> (only used in <i>m mnt</i>).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew, again</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in, in the role of, as”) +&lrm; <i>mꜣwt</i> (“new thing”), thus ‘as a new thing’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>an element appended to various official titles &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>prwj</i> (“two houses, two estates”), thus ‘in the two houses’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>amidst</li></ol><p>Cognate with Hebrew <i>בְּקֶרֶב</i> (bəqereḇ, “amidst”). By surface analysis, <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>qꜣb</i> (“intestines, middle”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>violently</li><li>(with suffix pronoun,&#32;of an action) through (one’s) power</li><li>(with suffix pronoun,&#32;of a quality) by virtue of (one’s) power</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in, by means of”) +&lrm; <i>sḫm</i> (“power, violence”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>spatial</i>) behind, following after</li><li>(<i>temporal</i>) after</li><li>in charge of</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>sꜣ</i> (“back”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>right now, this instant &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>freely, unhindered, unobstructed, with free access</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>wsṯn</i> (“going freely, unhindered”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>wḥm</i> (“to repeat”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>wḥm</i> (“to repeat”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“part, portion”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all at the same time, all at once</li><li>all together</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>zp</i> (“time, instance, matter”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>periphrastic imperfective of <i>ḏd</i></li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) as (someone) says &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) introduces a direct quotation; saying, with the words, as follows</li></ol><p><i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>ḏd</i> (infinitive of <i>ḏd</i> (“to say”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before (someone), in (someone’s) sight</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from above &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>upward, above &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in, from”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrt</i> (“upper side”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of travelling</i>) safely</li><li>conventionally added after expressions of welcome such as <i>jjwj</i> and <i>jj.tj</i></li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> m,&lrm; ḥtp.</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“peace”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>spatial</i>) in front (of), before</li><li>(<i>temporal</i>) before</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>downstream, northward</li></ol><p>From <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḫd</i> (“stream”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without knowing, in ignorance of</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḫm</i> (“to remain ignorant of, to not know”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in front of</li><li>inside, within</li><li>into</li><li>out of, from</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in front</li><li>therein</li><li>out</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in, into, out of”) +&lrm; <i>ḫnt</i> (“in front of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(relative) future, later time, hereafter</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after (of time)</li><li>behind (of space)</li><li>because of</li><li>after which, following which, when</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“during”) +&lrm; <i>ḫt</i> (“throughout”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>inside</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ẖnw</i> (“interior”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>daily</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ẖrt</i> (“(what is) under”) +&lrm; <i>hrw</i> (“day”), where <i>ẖrt</i> is the feminine form of the <i>nisba</i> adjective <i>ẖrj</i> used as a noun.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with attached suffix pronoun</i>) under one’s power, in one’s grasp</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> m,&lrm; ꜣmmt.</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣmmt</i> (“grasp”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>instantly, immediately &lsqb;From the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>synonym of <i>m ꜣt nt</i></li></ol><p><i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with a following noun or infinitive</i>) in the same moment as…, at the instant of…</li></ol><p><i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i> +&lrm; <i>nt</i>, feminine form of <i>n(j)</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always, at all times, constantly &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i> +&lrm; <i>r</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the act (of doing something)</li></ol><p><i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i> +&lrm; <i>.f</i>, literally “in his moment”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) to the extent (of)</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) throughout</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> m,&lrm; ꜣw.</li><li>dead, deceased</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣw</i> (“length”).</p><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣw</i> (“death”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eternally, for eternity, in perpetuity</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣwt</i> (“length”) +&lrm; <i>ḏt</i> (“(static) eternity, forever”). The final <i>ḏt</i> was originally adverbial, but later reinterpreted as a noun in a direct genitive construction, hence the later variation <i>m ꜣw n(j) ḏt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>along with, together with, in the company of (people or things) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>in the whole of, throughout (a place)</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥb</i> (“joining, union”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>forms masculine abstract nouns from verbs, with the resulting root structure &#47;ˈmiCCiC&#47;</li><li>forms agent nouns from verbs, with the resulting root structure &#47;ˈmaCCaC&#47; or &#47;mVCˈCiCVw&#47; if masculine and &#47;maCˈCaːCit&#47; or &#47;mVCˈCiCwVt&#47; if feminine</li><li>forms instrumental nouns from verbs, with the resulting root structure &#47;ˈmaCCaC&#47; or &#47;mVCˈCiCVw&#47; if masculine and &#47;maCˈCaːCit&#47; or &#47;mVCˈCiCwVt&#47; if feminine</li><li>forms passive nouns from verbs, with the resulting root structure &#47;miˈCuːCiC&#47; if masculine and &#47;miˈCuCCit&#47; if feminine</li><li>forms nouns from other nouns with no apparent change in meaning, with the resulting root structure unchanged but for the addition of &#47;ma-&#47; and the loss of final <i>-w</i> in masculine nouns</li><li>forms nomina loci from verbs and nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;m-</i> (nominal prefix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>no &lsqb;Middle to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;followed by a sentence negated with <i>bn</i>) on the contrary, no</li></ol><p><i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>bjꜣt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) with</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) from</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;before an infinitive) and</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) of</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following subjunctive) don’t let …, don’t make…; forms the negative causative imperative</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (imperative of <i>jmj</i> (“to not do”)) +&lrm; <i>dyt</i> (probably the infinitive of <i>rḏj</i> (“to give”)). The second element takes a written form that differs considerably from the ordinary forms of the infinitive of <i>rḏj</i> but despite this is probably best considered the infinitive.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jmjtw</i> (“between, amidst”)</li></ol><p>From <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jmjtw</i> (“between, amidst”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jmjtw-nj</i> (“between them, amidst them”)</li></ol><p>From <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jmjtw-nj</i> (“between them, amidst them”) or, equivalently, from <i>m-jmjtw</i> (“between, among”) +&lrm; <i>nj</i> (“thereto, for it”); in either case the basic components are the same.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following infinitive) don’t; forms the negative imperative (prohibitive) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (imperative of <i>jmj</i> (“to not do”)) +&lrm; <i>jr</i> (negatival complement of <i>jrj</i> (“to do”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following subjunctive) don’t let …, don’t make…; forms the negative causative imperative</li></ol><p><i>m-jr</i> (negative imperative auxiliary) +&lrm; <i>djt</i> (infinitive of <i>rḏj</i> (“to give”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) makes a subjunctive-prospective <i>sḏm.f</i> construction serve as a dependent temporal clause</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>as, when, at the time of, upon (some occurrence)</li><li>after, since</li><li>while</li></ol></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) because of</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḏr</i> (“limit, since, because”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the possession of, possessed by</li><li>from the possession or hand of, from</li><li>with (someone)</li><li>by means of, through</li><li>because of</li><li>Used after <i>ḫpr</i> (“to happen”) to indicate whom the action happened to; to</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> m,&lrm; ꜥ.</li></ol><p><i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“hand”), literally ‘in the hand’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>seeing that</li></ol><p><i>m-ꜥ</i> (“in the possession”) +&lrm; <i>ntt</i> (“that”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>mdw</i> (“word, speech”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) sharp</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) violent</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ds</i> (“knife”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoken word</li><li>words, speech, things said</li><li>words, text, things written</li><li>command, order, instruction</li><li>magic word, spell</li><li>(<i>law</i>) plea</li><li>used as a generic object for certain verbs such as <i>wḏ</i> (“to command”) and <i>wḏꜥ</i> (“to judge”)</li><li>staff, rod</li><li>staff as a weapon</li><li>staff as a kind of scepter, staff of authority, baton; also given as a grave good</li><li>sacred staff with the head of a god, revered as a symbol of a particular god or nome &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>used in various titles&#58; custodian, attendant, maintainer</li></ol><p>From <i>mdwj</i> (“to speak”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of staff or scepter</li></ol><p><i>mdw</i> (“staff”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>š</i> (“lake”), thus ‘staff of the lake’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with following indirect genitive of name of a god</i>) sacred staff revered as a symbol or scepter of a particular god, often bearing the head of that god &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mdw</i> (“staff”) +&lrm; <i>šps</i> (“noble, august”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacred standard bearing the sign of a particular nome, especially of Dendera &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>mdw</i> (“staff”) +&lrm; <i>šps</i> (“noble, august”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>spꜣt</i> (“nome”), thus ‘noble staff of the nome’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son who assumes the duties of his aged father, allowing the father to remain in office, supported by his son, who carries out the father’s responsibilities as deputy &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mdw</i> (“staff”) +&lrm; <i>jꜣw</i> (“old age”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘staff of old age’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priestly title: ‘herdsman of the white bull’ &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mdw</i> (“staff, custodian”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“bull”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”) with the first two elements in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘staff/custodian of the white bull’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacred literature traditionally written in hieroglyphic writing</li><li>Egyptian hieroglyphic writing</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> mdw,&lrm; nṯr.</li></ol><p>From <i>mdw</i> (“word”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘the god’s word’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title: ‘staff of the common people’ &lsqb;chiefly Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mdw</i> (“staff, custodian”) +&lrm; <i>rḫyt</i> (“common people”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘staff/custodian of the common people’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priestly title: ‘herdsman of Apis’ &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mdw</i> (“staff, custodian”) +&lrm; <i>ḥp</i> (“Apis”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘staff/custodian of Apis’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of staff or scepter</li></ol><p><i>mdw</i> (“staff”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘staff of Horus’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people, mouths, or tongues) to speak (+ <i>n</i> or <i>ḫr</i> or <i>ḫft</i>: to; + <i>ḥr</i>: about; + <i>ḥnꜥ</i> or <i>m-dj</i> or <i>jrm</i>: with (someone); + <i>m</i>: with (the mouth))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dispute, speak in opposition, contest, contend (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with; + <i>m</i>: in (some matter, e.g. a will))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i> or <i>ḥr tp</i>) to put in a good word for (someone)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of birds) to cry</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart) to beat &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the sky) to thunder &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to claim</li><li>to address</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>words, speech, things said &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>words, text, things written</li><li>words constituting the contents of something specified; wording, text (of a command, letter, etc.)</li><li>language, manner of speech &lsqb;since the Amarna Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>of the heart/mind</i>) thoughts, words thought internally &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>law</i>) plea</li><li>matter, affair, issue (+ genitive: of, concerning (someone or something)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>with following term</i>) forms abstract nouns; -ness &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>used as a generic object for certain verbs such as <i>wḏ</i> (“to command”) and <i>wḏꜥ</i> (“to judge”)</li></ol><p><i>mdwj</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a responsibility, something one is responsible for</li></ol><p><i>mdwt</i> (“matter”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literature</li></ol><p><i>mdwt</i> (“words, speech”) +&lrm; <i>nfrt</i> (feminine of <i>nfr</i> (“good, fine, beautiful”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turquoise</li></ol><p>From <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>fkꜣt</i> (“turquoise”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skirmisher</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to be(come) forgetful &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) to forget, to no longer know (something)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) to forget, to no longer recognize (someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgetfulness, negligence &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mhj</i> (“to be(come) forgetful”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>who?, what?; a general interrogative pronoun for people and things</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>following a verb in the imperative</i>) strengthens an imperative; please, now</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>like, just as</li><li>in accordance with</li><li>as well as</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>imperative of <i>jj</i> (“to come”)</li></ol><p>Compare with Hebrew <i>מי</i> (mí).</p><p>Possibly to be identified with the proclitic particle <i>m</i> (“see, behold”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how?</li></ol><p><i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>jḫ</i> (“what”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how?</li></ol><p><i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>mj</i> (“what”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li><li>in the manner of, like</li></ol><p>From <i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>qd</i> (“shape, form”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li></ol><p><i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>qj</i> (“character, form”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macedon (Macedonia)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Μακεδονία</i> (Makedonía, “Macedonia”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megiddo</li></ol><p>From Semitic; compare Hebrew <i>מגידו</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>today</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>today</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axe</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to moor (a boat)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;euphemistic) to die</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to successfully reach one’s goal &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mjnj</i> (“to moor”) +&lrm; <i>zp</i> (“(one’s) cause”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mooring post; a wooden stake hammered into the ground to which a boat’s prow rope is tied</li></ol><p>From <i>mjnj</i> (“to moor (a boat)”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>similarity</li><li>abbreviation of <i>mjtw</i> (“likeness”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>mjtj</i> (“similar”)</li></ol><p>A nominalized <i>nisba</i> adjective, from <i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine nisba ending”).</p><p>Abbreviated writings.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>similar, equal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the equal (of …), the like (of …)</li><li>likeness, faithful representation &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mjt</i> (“similarity”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”), ultimately from <i>mj</i> (“like”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as well, also</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likeness, the like</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>mjtj</i> (“similar”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>, with <i>mjtj</i> itself ultimately derived from <i>mj</i> (“like”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one like; equal; likeness</li></ol><p><i>mjt</i> (“similarity”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>, ultimately from <i>mj</i> (“like”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) cat; tomcat</li><li>likeness</li></ol><p>Most likely onomatopoeic, from the animal’s cry (meow). Compare Chinese <i>貓</i>&nbsp;/ <i>猫</i> (māo), Lao <i>ແມວ</i> (mǣu).</p><p>Other hypotheses connect it to Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;m-r</i> or <i>&#42;m-l</i>, the former on the basis of Alagwa <i>mariy-amo</i> (“wild cat”), Lamé Ngete <i>mēr</i> (“serval”) and <i>méríán</i> (“wild cat”), Pévé <i>merian</i> (“wild cat, serval”), Zime-Dari Pévé <i>mīēr</i> (“genette”), and the latter on the basis of Musgu <i>ámíl</i> (“African civet”), Bedanga Sokoro <i>melā</i> (“cat”). Either one of these could also be plausibly connected with the ancestor of Egyptian <i>mꜣj</i> (“lion”).</p><p>From <i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analogous</li></ol><p><i>mjw</i> (“likeness”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) cat; molly</li></ol><p>From <i>mjw</i> (“cat”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liver (of a human or animal)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to protect</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection, especially magical protection</li></ol><p><i>mkj</i> (“to protect”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>. Compare with Hebrew <i>מגדּלה</i> and Ugaritic <i>𐎎𐎂𐎄𐎍</i> (mgdl).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>among</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>among them, therein</li></ol><p>Both Gardiner and Allen suggest the preposition could be a reduplication of <i>m</i> (“in”). Gardiner takes the adverb separately and considers it apparently an 18th Dynasty development from <i>jmj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>among them, therein</li></ol><p><i>mm</i> (“among”) +&lrm; <i>jrj</i> (“regarding it”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giraffe</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mm</i> (“among”)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mm</i> (“among them”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) established, to be(come) fixed or set</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stick, to stay, to remain fixed, to endure (+ <i>m</i>: in (a place), as (a role); + <i>ḥr</i>: on (a seat, throne, foundation, etc.), in (one’s rightful place); + <i>ẖr</i>: with, having)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to be firm, steadfast, unwavering, stouthearted, especially in battle</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the legs or feet) to be firm of step, steadfast</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;mathematics) to be(come) left over, to be(come) the remainder or amount remaining</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to suffer, to be sick</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to suffer from (an ailment)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feel pain or have an ailment in (a body part)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be troubled about (something)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substitute for an unknown male name&#59; so-and-so, N.N. &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>sick or suffering man &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Menkaure, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>mn</i> (“established”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣw</i> (“life-forces”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) in two direct genitive constructions, thus ‘the established (one) of the life-forces of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Menkauhor Kaiu, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>mn</i> (“established”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣw</i> (“life-forces”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) in two direct genitive constructions, thus ‘the established (one) of the life-forces of Horus’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Memphis &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Shortened from <i>mn-nfr-ppy</i> (“the pyramid of Pepy”), from <i>mn</i> (“to endure”) +&lrm; <i>nfr(w)</i> (“beauty”) +&lrm; <i>ppy</i> (“Pepy”) with the final two words in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘The beauty of Pepy endures’. The shortened name could be reinterpreted as <i>mn</i> (“enduring”) +&lrm; <i>nfr(w)</i> (“beauty”), ‘enduring of beauty’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Ramesses I, a pharaoh of the Nineteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>mn</i> (“to become established or fixed”) +&lrm; <i>pḥtj</i> (“might”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘The might of Ra is firmly established’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Tuthmosis III, a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>mn</i> (“established, enduring”) +&lrm; <i>ḫpr</i> (“manifestation, form”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘the Established One of the Manifestation of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian (of the gods)</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>nhzj</i> (“to be awake”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Menes, uniter of Upper and Lower Egypt and founder of the First Dynasty of Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heavy beaded necklace and musical instrument with a crescent-shaped plate in the front and a keyhole-shaped amuletic counterweight in the rear, considered sacred to Hathor; menat-necklace</li><li>the keyhole-shaped counterweight of such a necklace</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herder, herdsman</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move back and forth, to quake, to wobble</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to move</li></ol><p>Perhaps by metathesis from the earlier-attested <i>nmnm</i> (“to move about, to quiver”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) herd of cattle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substitute for an unknown female name&#59; so-and-so, N.N.</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) human thigh</li><li>(<i>in the dual</i>) lap (upper legs of a seated person)</li><li>ox haunch</li><li>suffering</li><li>swallow (bird) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>only used in <i>m mnt</i> (“daily”)</li></ol><p>From <i>mn</i> (“so-and-so”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p><p>From <i>mn</i> (“to suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porter; doorkeeper</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trees &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>orchard, plantation &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>plants in general &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>a white aromatic plant smelling of <i>ꜥntjw</i> (myrrh<sup>?</sup>) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>monument</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Min</li></ol><p>Uncertain, possibly a loanword from Semitic; compare Hebrew <i>מָן</i> (mān). Alternatively, possibly connected to <i>mnw</i> (“trees, orchard, plantation”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pigeon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root (of a plant)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the ‘root’ of a disease, which kills one</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breast (of both sexes)</li></ol><p>Prefixed with <i>m-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stomach</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheek</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beeswax &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>papyrus plant or stalk &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) gift, tribute</li></ol><p>A borrowing from Semitic; compare Hebrew <i>מִנְחָה</i> (minḥā, “gift offering, afternoon prayer”), Arabic مِنْحَة (minḥa, “gift”), Phoenician <i>𐤌𐤍𐤇𐤕</i> (mnḥt), Ge'ez <i>መሐና</i> (mäḥäna).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be effective</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be worthwhile</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>mnḫ</i>: effective, worthwhile</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothing, garment</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Phaophi</li></ol><p>From <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>wnḫ</i> (“to clothe”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the war god Montu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mentuwoser</li></ol><p><i>mnṯw</i> (“Montu”) +&lrm; <i>wsr</i> (“to be powerful”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus literally ‘Montu is powerful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mentuhotep</li><li>a female given name, Mentuhotep</li></ol><p><i>mnṯw</i> (“Montu”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Montu is content’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wet nurse</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) milch cow</li></ol><p><i>mnꜥ</i> (“to nurse, to suckle”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people and body parts) to be(come) physically ill or in pain, to be(come) sick or diseased, to ail, to ache (+ <i>m</i> or <i>m-dj</i>: with (an ailment) or in (a body part)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to feel pity or compassion (+ <i>n</i>: for (someone), to hurt for, to be sorry for)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically painful, to sting, to cause physical pain (+ <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>: to)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of events, words, emotions, etc.) to be(come) mentally painful, sad, distressing, unpleasant, to cause mental pain (+ <i>r</i> or <i>m bꜣḥ</i> or <i>ḥr jb</i> or <i>ḥr jb n</i>: to)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of striking power, rage, etc.) to be(come) fierce, capable of inflicting pain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bind, to tie (+ <i>m</i>: to or with) &lsqb;since the Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie together, to tie (pieces of wood, flax, etc.) up &lsqb;since the Second Intermediate Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fetter or bind (a captive or a captive’s limbs), to tie (a person) up &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to join (someone), to attach oneself to (someone) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the mouth) to be suffering from an ailment of some kind such that the mouth is (figuratively) ‘bound’ &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ailment, illness, disease &lsqb;since the Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>physical pain</li><li>mental distress, misery, sorrow</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) sick man &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>canal, ditch, waterway &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>pond or pool, especially an artificial one &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(rare,&#32;by confusion with <i>mw</i>) water &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>metal libation basin</li><li>pyramid (monumental building) &lsqb;from the Pyramid Texts through the Saite Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) heap of corpses &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>fighting bull &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) ellipsis of <i>mr-wr</i> (“Mnevis”) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Afroasiatic. Possible Berber cognates include the forms represented by Kabyle <i>u-mr-an</i> (“sorrows”), <i>a-mur</i> (“colic, stomachache”), and <i>a-mrir</i> (“embarrassment, great difficulty”). Possible Cushitic cognates include Oromo <i>marar</i> (“to be sick”), Baiso <i>marni</i> (“to be sad”), and Sidamo <i>marar-s</i> (“to be sick”). A proposed Omotic cognate is Yemsa <i>mer-o</i> (“illness”). Also compare the root Arabic م ر ض (m-r-ḍ, “related to ailment”).</p><p>Possibly from a Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;m-r</i> (“river, channel”). Compare South Omotic *<i>mir-</i> (“river”), with reflexes including Aari <i>mɨri</i> (“river, stream”) and Dime <i>mɪ́rɛ</i> (“river”). A possible Semitic cognate is Sabaean <i>𐩣𐩧</i> (mr, “part of an irrigation system”). Possible Cushitic cognates include Borana Oromo <i>mērī</i> (“watering trough”), Tsamai <i>mīre</i> (“pond”), and possible Chadic cognates include Fali <i>mirə̂</i> (“river”), Muskum <i>mìrà</i> (“oxbow lake, marigot”).</p><p>Highly disputed etymology. Hypotheses include:</p><p>Perhaps from Proto-Afroasiatic. Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;mir(Vʔ)-</i> (“bull”): compare Akkadian <i>𒈪𒅕𒋾</i> (mīrtu, “young cow”), <i>𒄞𒀖</i> (mīru, “young bull”), Hebrew <i>מְרִיא</i> (mərīʾ, “fatted steer”). Possible Cushitic cognates include Hadiyya <i>mōr-â</i> (“bull”), Mbugu <i>ki-mole</i>, <i>ki-more</i> (“ox, bull”), possible Omotic cognates include Wolaytta <i>mārā</i> (“young bull”), Yemsa <i>omoru</i> (“bull”), and possible Chadic cognates include Mafa <i>maray</i> (“sacrificial bull”), Mofu-Gudur <i>maray</i> (“fattened bull”), Mafa <i>mari</i> (“bull”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very sad (+ <i>n</i>: about) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>mr</i> (“painful”) +&lrm; <i>snm</i> (“mourning, grief”), literally “painful of grief”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the main canal in the Faiyum, the Bahr Yussef, connecting Lake Moeris to the Nile &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the town of Moeris near Crocodilopolis (modern Faiyum), along the northern side of the Moeris canal &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>Mnevis, a solar bull god venerated in Heliopolis, considered to be embodied in a living black bull selected by the priesthood there and later sometimes treated as an aspect of the god Atum-Ra &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>mr</i> (“canal, pool”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”).</p><p>From <i>mr</i> (“bull”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Nemtyemzaef Merenre I and II, two pharaohs of the Sixth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>mr.n</i> (perfect relative form of <i>mrj</i> (“to love”)) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘(the one) whom Ra has loved’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Meresankh</li></ol><p><i>mr</i> (“loves”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“she”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“life”), thus literally ‘she loves life’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;of family members, rulers and subjects, or people and gods) to love (someone), to have affection for, to be fond of</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to love (something abstract: truth, life, goodness, battle, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be fond of, to love (something one possesses)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to want, to desire (something one does not possess)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to desire to be in (a place)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with infinitive) to want (to do something)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>sḏm.f</i>) to want, to wish (that something be done)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic <i>*markabt-</i> ("chariot"). Compare Hebrew <i>מֶרְכָּבָה</i> (merkāḇā), Ugaritic <i>𐎎𐎗𐎋𐎁𐎚</i> (mrkbt /⁠markabtu⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ailment, illness; sickness or pain &lsqb;since the Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>something bad inflicted on someone, evil, misfortune &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>mr</i> (“to be sick, to be painful”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p><p>Feminine singular of the perfective passive participle of <i>mrj</i>, thus literally ‘the beloved’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Meretnebty</li></ol><p><i>mr(y)t</i> (feminine perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>nbtj</i> (“the Two Ladies”), literally ‘(she) whom the Two Ladies love’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desert</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Meru</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black stork</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) serfs</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>mrj</i> (“to love”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective passive participle of <i>mrj</i>: beloved</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mr</i> (“(fighting) bull”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Meryamun</li><li>a common epithet sometimes added to the birth names or throne names of pharaohs</li></ol><p>From <i>mry</i> (perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amon”), literally ‘(one) whom Amon loves’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Merneptah or Merenptah</li></ol><p>From <i>mry</i> (“beloved”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Pepi I Meryre, a pharaoh of the Sixth Dynasty</li><li>a male given name, Meryre, Merire, or Meryra</li></ol><p>From <i>mry</i> (perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), literally ‘(one) whom Ra loves’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shore or riverbank with a natural embankment or levee &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a landing place for ships on shore; wharf, quay &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>harbor &lsqb;chiefly since Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Meryt</li></ol><p>Compare Beja <i>mari</i> (“side, direction”). Suffixed with <i>-t</i> (feminine ending). Related to <i>mrj</i> (“to run aground, to strand (a ship)”), but most likely this verb was derived from the noun.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Meritamen</li></ol><p><i>mryt</i> (feminine perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amon”), literally ‘(she) whom Amon loves’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Meritites</li></ol><p><i>mryt</i> (feminine perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>jt</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“her”), thus literally ‘(she) whom her father loves’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Meritaten</li></ol><p>From <i>mryt</i> (feminine perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>jtn</i> (“Aten”), literally ‘(she) whom the Aten loves’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Merit-Ptah</li></ol><p>From <i>mryt</i> (feminine perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”), thus literally ‘(she) whom Ptah loves’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Merytre</li></ol><p>From <i>mryt</i> (feminine perfective relative form of <i>mrj</i>) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘(she) whom Ra loves’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of buildings and tools) to fall to ruin, to decay &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of one’s ba or corpse,&#32;usually negated) to perish, to die</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of plans, words, festivals, etc.,&#32;usually negated) to come to nought, to perish, to be erased</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to anoint &lsqb;Late Period literature&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to coat or provide (a clay figurine) with (enamel)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lacquer (wood)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asphalt &lsqb;Late Period literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Apparently a back-formation from <i>mrḥt</i> (“oil, fat”); compare the older verb <i>wrḥ</i> (“to anoint”), from which <i>mrḥt</i> itself may originally derive.</p><p>Based on the form of the Coptic descendants, apparently the same word as <i>mrḥt</i> (“oil, fat”) in origin; with the loss of syllable-final <i>-t</i>, the originally-feminine word was reanalyzed as a masculine noun when used in the sense of ‘asphalt’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>anointing oil, also used medicinally or as an offering</li><li>fish oil, used as fuel for burning &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) tree oil</li></ol><li>fat (of any kind of animal)</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>wrḥ</i> (“to anoint”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of oil used in embalming the head &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mrḥt</i> (“oil, fat”) +&lrm; <i>jḥtjt</i> (“throat”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘oil of the throat’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring, child</li><li>calf</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>surely; can connote particular persuasiveness, astonishment, reproach, or objection</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>perfective of <i>msj</i> (“to give birth to”)</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵎⵙ</i> (ms, “to be from, be related to, be a close relative/descendant of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a black mineral pigment used as eye makeup and in medicinal preparations for treating eye disease; mesdemet</li><li>the mineral material from which this pigment is made, as used for making small cultic images and other objects &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>sdm</i> (“makeup; to put on makeup”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rival</li></ol><p>From <i>msḏj</i> (“to hate”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give birth to</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to form, to create, to produce</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ms</i> (“child”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recognition &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>sjꜣ</i> (“to perceive, to recognize”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the barque in which the sun sails during the evening and nighttime; the night-barque of Ra</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal hide</li><li>leather (as a clothing material)</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;masak-</i>. Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;mašk-</i> and Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⴰⴽⵙⵓⵎ</i> (aksum).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a toponym of ambiguous signification commonly found associated with Horus as part of his epithet <i>nb-msn</i>&#59; it may refer to any or all of the following&#58;</li><li>a settlement near or identical to Tjaru in Lower Egypt</li><li>the city of Edfu in Upper Egypt</li><li>(<i>possibly</i>) the city of Buto (Pe) in Lower Egypt</li></ol><p>Related to <i>msnw</i> (“harpooner”); the toponym is closely connected since the earliest times with Horus in his role as harpooner of enemies.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mythological region of the sky and the afterworld, perhaps identical with the longer form <i>msqt-sḥdw</i>, and possibly to be identified with the Milky Way</li><li>part of the Theban necropolis</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (nomen loci-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>sqr</i> (“to strike, to beat”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending), thus perhaps ‘beaten path’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a region of the sky to which a dead king was said to ascend, perhaps the Milky Way &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>msqt</i> (“a mythological place of the dead”) +&lrm; <i>sḥdw</i>, plural of <i>sḥd</i> (“star”), in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) the royal children collectively; princes and princesses</li></ol><p>From <i>msw</i> (“children”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”) in a direct genitive construction. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthing, the process of childbearing</li><li>birth (of someone)</li></ol><p><i>msj</i> (“to birth”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The fifth and last of the five epagomenal (intercalary) days (<i>djw ḥrjw rnpt</i>) added to the end of the year in the ancient Egyptian calendar.</li></ol><p><i>mswt</i> (“birth”) +&lrm; <i>nbt-ḥwt</i> (“Nephthys”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘birth(day) of Nephthys’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Mesore</li></ol><p><i>mswt</i> +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Birth of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The third of the five epagomenal (intercalary) days (<i>djw ḥrjw rnpt</i>) added to the end of the year in the ancient Egyptian calendar.</li></ol><p><i>mswt</i> (“birth”) +&lrm; <i>stẖ</i> (“Set”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘birth(day) of Set’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The first of the five epagomenal (intercalary) days (<i>djw ḥrjw rnpt</i>) added to the end of the year in the ancient Egyptian calendar.</li></ol><p><i>mswt</i> (“birth”) +&lrm; <i>wsjr</i> (“Osiris”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘birth(day) of Osiris’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The second of the five epagomenal (intercalary) days (<i>djw ḥrjw rnpt</i>) added to the end of the year in the ancient Egyptian calendar.</li></ol><p><i>mswt</i> (“birth”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘birth(day) of Horus’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The fourth of the five epagomenal (intercalary) days (<i>djw ḥrjw rnpt</i>) added to the end of the year in the ancient Egyptian calendar.</li></ol><p><i>mswt</i> (“birth”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣst</i> (“Isis”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘birth(day) of Isis’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supper</li><li>a kind of waterfowl</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hate, to strongly dislike</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to show a strong aversion to (regardless of one's internal emotions); to shun or scorn</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rebuke, to show one’s dissatisfaction with (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to not want</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear</li></ol><p>From <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>sḏr</i> (“to lie down”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mzḥ</i> (“crocodile”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abode or resting-place of a god, often in reference to a temple</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (nomen loci-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>sḫn</i> (“resting-place”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adze used as a ritual instrument used in the “opening of the mouth” funerary ritual to allow the deceased to eat and drink once more</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the Big Dipper</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the dual</i>) nostril</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>swꜣḏ</i> (“to freshen”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vein, artery, muscle</li><li>suppository</li><li>alternative form of <i>mwt</i> (“death”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mwt</i> (“to die”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rectangular strip of linen &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>mt</i> (“a kind of linen<sup>?</sup>; strip<sup>?</sup>”) +&lrm; <i>jfd</i> (“sheet”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) precise or exact</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) scrupulous or exact, to be(come) precise in knowing, judging, and acting correctly (+ <i>n</i>: for (someone), + <i>m</i>: in (a place))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) correct, the right one</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) rightful, proper</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) customary, usual</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of seasons, offerings, festivals) to be(come) regular</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correctness, exactness, scrupulousness</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to witness</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correctness, rectitude</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with attached suffix pronoun and following infinitive) forms the conjunctive</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥnꜥ</i> +&lrm; <i>ntf</i> and similarly + <i>nts</i>, + <i>ntk</i>, + <i>ntṯ</i>, etc., with the final sound in each case reinterpreted as the corresponding suffix pronoun (thus <i>-f</i> in <i>ḥnꜥ ntf</i> as suffixal <i>.f</i>) and so treated separately.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semen</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) offspring, progeny, son</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) floodwater as a source of fertility for the fields &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>venom, especially of snakes or scorpions</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>mt</i> (“anatomical vessel or duct”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water (substance)</li><li>area covered by water, water as opposed to land or sky</li><li>body of water; sea, lake, river, etc.</li><li>rain</li><li>fluid, especially bodily fluid</li><li>semen</li><li>blood</li><li>juice or sap from a plant</li></ol><p>An origin in a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;maʔ-</i> has been suggested, but as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking; compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;māy-</i>. The word is a frozen plural, with an original root <i>&#42;m</i> or <i>&#42;mj</i>, the latter of which is supported by the phonetic use of the hieroglyph to write <i>nmjw</i> (“dwarf”). See Related terms below.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moab</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li><li>death</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) weight</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) the primordial waters &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>Mut, the vulture goddess worshipped as part of the Theban Triad</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to die</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of ships) to sink</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to die inside, to despair</li></ol><p>Likely a babble word in origin, like many of its Afroasiatic parallels. Militarev and Stolbova derive it from a supposed Proto-Afroasiatic form instead, but also note it may be a nursery word. If not, perhaps cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔimm-</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;mawut-</i> (“to die, to kill”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(maternal or paternal) grandmother</li></ol><p><i>mwt</i> (“mother”) +&lrm; <i>mwt</i> (“mother”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘mother of the mother’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Mutnofret</li></ol><p><i>mwt</i> (“Mut”) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“to be beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>.t(j)</i> (feminine singular stative ending), thus ‘Mut is beautiful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>ritual dancers of some kind at funerals</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a wound) to be(come) watery, moist &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>mw</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>likewise</li><li>accordingly</li></ol><p>From <i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (adverbializing suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring in, to introduce, to present with (+ <i>n</i>: bring in to, present (someone) with)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place to drink, drinking place</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (nomen loci-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>zwr</i> (“to drink”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of food offering made from emmer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocodile</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army</li><li>footsoldiers</li><li>workgang</li><li>campaign, military expedition</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to march</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to campaign</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic مَشَى (mašā, “to walk”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to physically strike (someone or something) with an arrow or spear</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to physically strike with (an arrow or spear) &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;in fixed expressions) to hit the target when shooting arrows &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of fire) to meet (someone) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive in (a stake or pale) &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to symbolically drive an implement, originally one’s little finger, into (the mouth) of the deceased in the Opening of the Mouth ritual</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to press hard on, as if to drive in</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to obey, to follow (an order)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to practise (a virtue)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to follow, to stay true to (a path)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to distribute, to share out to the proper target</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to impose (something) as a duty in the form of corvee labor or taxes &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dues or obligations in the form of corvee labor or taxes &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Medjed, a minor god mentioned in the Book of the Dead</li></ol><p>The proper noun is derived from the participle of the verb, thus perhaps meaning ‘the one who hits the mark’, ‘the true-striking’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be loyal or obedient (+ genitive: to (someone)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mḏd</i> (“to stay true to”) +&lrm; <i>mṯn</i> (“road”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be loyal or obedient (+ genitive: to (someone)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mḏd</i> (“to stay true to”) +&lrm; <i>wꜣt</i> (“road”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Two Ladies name&#x20;notably borne by Khufu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>mḏd</i> (“to strike true, to stay true to, to drive in, to share out, to impose as an obligation”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“concerning, according to, facing, against, etc.”) +&lrm; <i>nbtj</i> (“the Two Ladies, i.e. the goddesses Nekhbet and Wadjet”). The exact sense is disputed, with a number of proposed hypotheses, but it must certainly be related to Khufu’s serekh name <i>mḏdw</i>, itself of disputed interpretation; a phrase attested in the Coffin Texts CT VI, §307x–308b, may also feature a related construction: <i>jn &#91;N.N.&#93; jr mḏdwt nbtj</i> (“It was [N.N.] who undertook the sharing out of the two crowns (literally, “Two Ladies”)”). Gundlach proposed a meaning of ‘he who shares out in accordance with the Two Ladies’, which Baud rejected in favor of ‘he who receives his dues in accordance with the Two Ladies’. Leprohon instead suggested ‘[he] who has adhered to the Two Ladies’, though this interpretation leaves the preposition unexplained, as <i>mḏd</i> normally takes the thing being stayed true to as a direct object.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>share, portion of something divided &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mḏd</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Khufu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>mḏd</i> (“to strike true, to stay true to, to drive in, to share out, to impose as an obligation”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>. The exact sense is disputed, with a number of proposed hypotheses, but it must certainly be related to Khufu’s Two Ladies name <i>mḏd-r-nbtj</i>, itself of disputed interpretation. Baud takes the term as an active participle synonymous with the epithet <i>šzp-mḏd.f</i> (“(he who) receives his dues”), or alternatively suggests an interpretation such as ‘he who reaches the goal’, but rejects earlier suggestions by Montet (‘he who imposes’), Sethe (‘faithful, attached’), Barta (‘he who is guided, directed’), and Gundlach (‘he who shares out’). Leprohon interprets it as a passive participle, ‘[he] who has been adhered to/followed’ (= ‘stayed true to’).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn (to someone) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn aside, to deter (the wind) &lsqb;12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enclose, to envelop &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment or oil in general</li><li>a particular kind of ointment</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic. Compare Proto-Berber <i>&#42;măraw</i> (whence Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵎⵔⴰⵡ</i> (mraw)) and Proto-Chadic <i>&#42;gʷam-</i> (with metathesis, if not borrowed from Niger-Congo), whence Hausa <i>gōmà</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty</li></ol><p><i>mḏw</i> (“ten”) +&lrm; <i>-tj</i> (feminine dual ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hew (wood or stone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to build (a ship)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A unit of measure for dates and loaves &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papyrus scroll</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Medjay (initially a nomadic people from Nubia, later a sort of police force)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fill</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to physically fill (a container, vessel, granary, ship, etc.) (+ <i>m</i> or (<i>occasionally</i>) <i>ẖr</i>: with)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of people) to fill (a space), to crowd</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>) to fill (one’s hands or arms) with (something); also, to embrace (someone)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>) to fill (a person, one’s body, or one’s mouth) with (food)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>) to fill (a place) with (beauty, joy, scent, etc.)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to complete, to make complete with all members or a full complement, to make whole</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pay in full</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inlay (+ <i>m</i>: with) &lsqb;since the end of the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) full</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;of containers, one’s arms, etc.) to be(come) physically full (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>: of)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>) to be(come) replete with, full of, abounding in</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of animals) to be(come) pregnant, gravid</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) complete with all members, full, whole, fully assembled</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to hold or seize</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to seize with one’s hand, grasp, lay hold of</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to hold fast, to grasp</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to seize (something) for oneself, to take</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to capture (a person), to hold (someone) captive</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to capture, to conquer (a city)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to hold fast to (orders, plans, etc.), to not deviate from</li></ol><li>(catenative,&#32;with <i>r</i> and following infinitive) to begin, to set about (doing something)</li></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) Forms ordinal numbers greater than nine from cardinal numbers.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Forms ordinal numbers greater than one from cardinal numbers.</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Also forms the ordinal number for one (‘first’). &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forearm</li><li>a cubit, a measure of length equivalent to 28 <i>ḏbꜥw</i> (“digits”) or 7 <i>šzpw</i> (“palms”) (about 52.5 centimetres).</li><li>cubit rod, rod the length of a cubit</li></ol><p>From a form of the verb <i>mḥ</i> (“to fill, to be(come) full”) above; the original sense may have been the number ‘brought to fullness’, ‘made complete’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry up, to run</li></ol><p><i>mḥ</i> (“to fill”) +&lrm; <i>rdwj</i> (“legs”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a centaroura, a measure of area equivalent to one hundredth of a <i>sṯꜣt</i> (“aroura”) or 100 square <i>mḥw</i> (“cubits”) (about 0.0028 hectares).</li></ol><p>From <i>mḥ</i> (“cubit”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘a cubit of land’; since land was parcelled into strips of 1 by 100 cubits, one such strip of 100 square cubits was considered ‘a cubit of land’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in water</li><li>to be(come) full of water, to be(come) flooded</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of the heart) to be(come) preoccupied and thus forgetful</li><li>to be concerned or think [<i>with</i> <b>ḥr</b> <small>‘about’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;usually of serpents) to encircle, to coil around</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Coiled One (a serpent spirit)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mehen, a board game &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a swear word used as a term of abuse for someone; shitter, shithead</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥs</i> (“feces”), with the second element in reduplication.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Nile Delta.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northern</li></ol><p>From <i>mḥt</i> (“Nile delta”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) northerners</li></ol><p>Nominalized plural of <i>mḥtj</i> (“northern”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>north</li></ol><p>From the feminine form of <i>mḥtj</i> (“northern”), used as an abstract noun.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lower Egypt (an ancient kingdom and region of the Empire of Egypt)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inlay (of precious stones, etc.)</li><li>filling (of the mouth with food)</li><li>alternative form of <i>mḥꜥw</i> (“flax, linen”) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mḥ</i> (“to fill”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>north wind</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Mehyt</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flax &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>linen; thread or cloth made of flax fiber</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring (a balance scale) into equilibrium, to balance (scales) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to match, to equal &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make (two things) equal or level, to counterpoise &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to level (the floor), to make level or flat &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to friendlily incline (one’s heart/mind) (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone)) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to adjust (one’s mouth) to (one’s bones) in the Opening of the Mouth ritual &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be perfectly balanced or (figuratively) right-acting &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>,&#32;Late Egyptian) to be like, to resemble (someone or something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie up, to bind (people or captured birds) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) binding rope</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) part of a chariot&#58; loop of rope, strap<sup>?</sup></li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) woodshed</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a unit by which figs are counted, perhaps a wreath of figs</li></ol><p>Ultimately a derivation of <i>ḫꜣj</i> (“to measure”). Compare the noun <i>mḫꜣt</i> (“balance scales”), apparently formed with the nominalizing prefix <i>m-</i>, which suggests that this verb is probably a back-formation from the noun, as already put forward by Gardiner.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, scales</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fair measurer of rightness or worth, especially in reference to fair-dealing officials</li></ol><p>From <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜣj</i> (“to measure”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way, road, path (+ genitive: leading to (a place), followed by (a person)) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>course, path, trajectory (of the stars in the sky, etc.)</li><li>way of leading one’s life, particularly the right way</li><li>guide or scout along a path &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>sheikh, leader of a band of desert nomads or bedouins</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arena for bullfighting, bull ring &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (nomen loci-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>ṯwn</i> (“to gore”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to challenge, to taunt, to flout</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by the Predynastic king Mekh</li></ol><p>If the writing is an abbreviated form of <i>jmj-ẖt</i>, possibly from <i>jmj</i> (“being in”) +&lrm; <i>ẖt</i> (“group of gods”), thus literally meaning ‘(the one) being in the group of gods (i.e. the Ennead)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferryboat, especially in the afterworld</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>ẖnj</i> (“to row”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food-storehouse, granary</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guts, entrails, especially the intestines</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>razor</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) shoulder blade, scapula</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>ẖꜥq</i> (“to shave”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) stern</li><li>oryx</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oryx</li></ol><p><i>mꜣ</i> (“oryx”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion</li></ol><p><i>mꜣj</i> +&lrm; <i>ḥzꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Western mountain &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladder</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>jꜣq</i> (“to climb up”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stick, staff, rod</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic, probably Hebrew <i>מַקֵּל</i> (maqqēl).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor and powerless person, wretch &lsqb;since the end of the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜣr</i> (“to repulse, to oppress”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lioness</li></ol><p><i>mꜣj</i> (“lion”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandrake</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) new</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a new thing, something new</li></ol><p><i>mꜣwj</i> (“to be new”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foetus</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granite &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn up (+ <i>n</i>: through (fire))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a heart) to be afraid (+ <i>n</i>: for)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>,&#32;of a heart) to care about</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to look at, to see (+ <i>m</i>: as)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sight, vision</li><li>appearance, aspect</li><li>inspection, supervision, oversight</li><li>means of recognition</li></ol><p>Compare with Arabic أَمَرَ (ʔamara, “to instruct, to show how or what to do”) and Akkadian <i>𒅆</i> (amārum, “to look at, to see, to learn by experience”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Maathorneferure</li></ol><p><i>mꜣꜣt</i> (“seeing”, feminine imperfective active participle of <i>mꜣꜣ</i>) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>nfrw</i> (“beauty”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), with the last two words in a direct genitive construction and in apposition to the rest, thus ‘(one) seeing Horus, the beauty of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>early title of the queen &lsqb;chiefly Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>mꜣꜣt</i> (“seeing”, feminine imperfective active participle of <i>mꜣꜣ</i>) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>stẖ</i> (“Set”), thus ‘(the one) seeing Horus-Set’, with ‘Horus-Set’ a reference to the king.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to direct, to guide, to conduct</li><li>(<i>reflexive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to be(come) true, to be(come) correct</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) genuine, real, true</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fulfil a title or familial relation as one should; to be a real or true (brother, servant, etc.)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;law) to be(come) innocent</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) just or fair</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make an offering of</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) temple</li><li>place &lsqb;since the end of the Third Intermediate Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be among the dead who have been judged righteous</li><li>to be justified, to be right</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>said after mentioning the name of a dead person</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rightness, justification</li></ol><p><i>mꜣꜥ</i> (“(to be) true, just”) +&lrm; <i>ḫrw</i> (“voice”), thus literally ‘(to be) true of voice’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li><li>right action, righteousness, virtue</li><li>blamelessness, innocence</li><li>justice, fairness</li><li>right order in the state or cosmos</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maat, the goddess personifying the above concepts</li></ol><p>An abstract noun formed from <i>mꜣꜥ</i> (“to direct, to be just, to be true”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>righteous</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) two goddesses present at the judgement of the dead</li><li>(dual only,&#32;ellipsis for <i>wsḫt nt mꜣꜥtj</i>) The hall of judgement of the dead</li></ol><p><i>mꜣꜥt</i> (“truth, righteousness”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p><p><i>mꜣꜥt</i> (“truth, righteousness”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (dual ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sailing</i>) wind blowing in the right direction (to drive one’s boat)</li><li>fresh air or breeze for breathing</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the right direction in life</li></ol><p><i>mꜣꜥ</i> (“to direct, to guide”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of hunting spear</li></ol><p>Unclear. Presumably consisting of the unattested root <i>*ꜥbr</i> with prefix <i>m-</i>.</p><p>Semantically, this word might have originally referred to a “lunar month of thirty days” as suggested by A. Ember (1926); cf. Nefusa <i>u-yěr</i> (“moon; thirty”). Based on this, Takacs (2007) compares the root <i>*ꜥbr</i> ~ <i>*ꜥrb</i> (via metathesis) to Proto-Agaw <i>*ʔärb-</i> ~ <i>ʔärf-</i> ("moon"), and further to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʿ-r-p</i> (“cloud”) (whence Hebrew <i>ערפל</i>).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be(come) observant or attentive</li><li>to be(come) brave</li></ol></ol><p>Suggested to be connected with <i>kꜣj</i> (“to plan, to think”), but this remains questionable.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the barque in which the sun sails through the sky during the morning and daytime; the day-barque of Ra</li></ol><p><i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḏw</i> (“dawn, sunlight”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roast on a skewer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people and the dead) to be(come) fortunate, to flourish or prosper</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of actions and intentions) to be(come) successful, to succeed</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of things) to be(come) excellent or flawless</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comb</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomb</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>to, for (dative)</li><li>in the direction of, towards</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) for, until</li><li>because of</li><li>used idiomatically with certain verbs</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>nj</i> (“of, belonging to (genitival adjective)”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we, us (see usage notes)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵏ</i> (n, “of”) and Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;lV-</i>, whence Hebrew <i>לְ־</i> (lə-, “to, for, of”) and Arabic لِـ (li-, “to, for, belonging to”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>for the sake of (someone)</li><li>so as to, in order to (do something)</li><li>so that, in order that (something happens)</li></ol><p>From <i>n</i> (“for”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>for the purpose of, because</li><li>(<i>with following verb in the subjunctive</i>) in order that</li></ol><p><i>n</i> (“for”) +&lrm; <i>mrwt</i> (“love”), thus literally ‘for the love (of)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because, for</li></ol><p><i>n</i> (“to, for”) +&lrm; <i>ntt</i> (“that”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>real, proper, true, genuine</li></ol><p><i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>wn mꜣꜥ</i> (“reality, true quality”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>upward, above &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>n</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrt</i> (“upper side”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with attached suffix pronoun</i>) at one’s pleasure, at will, as one wishes &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>n</i> (“to, for”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣbb</i> (active imperfective participle of <i>ꜣbj</i> (“to desire, to want, to be happy”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with a verb in the infinitive) in order to</li></ol><p><i>n</i> (“to, for”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣbw</i> (inflection of <i>ꜣbj</i> (“to desire, to want, to be happy”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>inasmuch as</li></ol><p><i>n</i> (“to, for, because of”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣt</i> (“greatness”) +&lrm; <i>nt</i> (“of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>forms intransitive or reflexive verbs from existing verbs</li><li>alternative form of <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) before labial consonants</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by the Predynastic king Neheb</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>n(j)</i> (“belonging”) +&lrm; <i>hb</i> (“plow”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally meaning ‘belonging to the plow’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Necho</li></ol><p><i>n(j)</i> (“of, belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣw</i> (plural of <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”)), thus literally ‘(the one) belonging to the kas’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Nynetjer, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li><li>a Two Ladies name&#x20;notably borne by Nynetjer, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>n(j)</i> (“of, belonging (to)”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”), thus literally meaning ‘belonging to the god’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favored person, favorite, darling</li></ol><p>From <i>n(j)</i> (“of, belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>st-jb</i> (“favorite place, preference, favor”), literally “(one) belonging to the favor of…’”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Nyuserre Ini, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>n(j)</i> (“of, belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>wsr</i> (“power”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘(one) belonging to the power of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>all, every</li><li>every other</li><li>all sorts of, all kinds of</li><li>(<i>especially in negated clauses</i>) any</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord, master, ruler (of a place or people)</li><li>(without following genitive noun,&#32;often as a term of address) master, superior</li><li>master (of a span of time)</li><li>owner, possessor, bearer (of an object)</li><li>possessor (of an abstract quality)</li><li>an epithet of the king</li><li>an epithet of various gods, especially Osiris</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sin, damage, impurity &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Ehret attempts to derive this term from a Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ruub-</i> (“to send”); as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking, and in this case the derivation is not particularly plausible.</p><p>From <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>&#42;bw(j)</i> (“abomination”) with regular dissimilation of <i>m-</i> to <i>n-</i> before a labial; for the stem, compare <i>bwt</i> (“abomination”), <i>bwj</i> (“to abominate”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-liked or charming one</li></ol><p>From <i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣt</i> (“friendliness, charm, belovedness”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “possessor of friendliness/charm/belovedness”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for the dead&#58; revered one, venerable one (+ <i>ḫr</i>: one honored by (someone))</li></ol><p>From <i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣḫ</i> (“dignity, honor, reverence”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “possessor of reverence”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Nebka, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force, sustenance”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘possessor of life-force/sustenance’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of the god Thoth</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>mdw-nṯr</i> (“sacred writings, hieroglyphic script”, literally “god’s word”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘lord of the god’s word’, i.e. ‘master of sacred writings’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) honest person, one who does what is right</li><li>epithet for various gods, especially Ptah</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) epithet for gods who pass judgement collectively</li><li>epithet for the king</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Sneferu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li><li>a Two Ladies name&#x20;notably borne by Sneferu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>mꜣꜥt</i> (“righteousness, truth”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘possessor of righteousness/truth’.</p></html>:/nibmuʕaʔˈɾiːʕə/
<h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Amenhotep III, a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nb-mꜣꜥt</i> (“possessor of righteousness/truth”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> +&lrm; (“Ra”), a nominal sentence meaning ‘Ra is the possessor of righteousness/truth’. Contemporary cuneiform transcriptions of the name include Akkadian <i>𒉌𒅁𒈬𒀀𒊑𒀀</i> (ni-ib-mu-a-ri-a) and Hurrian <i>𒉌𒅎𒈬𒌋𒊑𒄿𒀸</i> (ni-im-mu-u-ri-i-a[š] /⁠nimmōrīa-⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Sneferu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nb-mꜣꜥt</i> (“possessor of righteousness/truth”) +&lrm; <i>snfr</i> (“to make pleasing, fine, beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>wj</i> (“me”), thus ‘the possessor of righteousness has made me fine’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Amun-Ra as the source of the pharaoh’s authority</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (plural of <i>nst</i> (“throne”)) +&lrm; <i>tꜣwj</i> (“the Two Lands, Egypt”), thus literally ‘Lord of the Thrones of the Two Lands’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a very common epithet of various gods, originally of Horus of Behdet and Ra</li><li>an epithet for a dead king</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>pt</i> (“sky”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘lord of the sky’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Atum</li><li>an epithet of the god Osiris</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>r ḏr</i> (“whole, to the limit”), thus meaning ‘Lord to the Limit’, i.e. ‘Lord of All’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Nebra, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“sun, Ra”). The exact meaning of this is debated, as there are several possible ways to interpret the name:</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various gods &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>sḫm</i> (“power, capacity to overpower”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘possessor of overmastering power’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Atum</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>tm</i> (“totality”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Lord of Totality’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Wepwawet</li><li>an epithet of the god Anubis</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ-ḏsr</i> (“necropolis”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘lord of the necropolis’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common pharaonic epithet</li></ol><p>From <i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣwj</i> (“the Two Lands”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Lord of the Two Lands’, i.e. Upper and Lower Egypt.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet used of kings and creator gods</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>wꜥ</i> (“sole”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common epithet for the king: ‘Lord of Appearances’</li><li>used as a title preceding the fifth (given) name in the royal titulary &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Sahure, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li><li>a Two Ladies name&#x20;notably borne by Sahure, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜥw</i> (“appearance in glory, shining-forth”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘possessor of appearances in glory’. Sometimes the second part is instead interpreted as <i>ḫꜥw</i> (“crown”), etymologically the same word.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Osiris</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣbḏw</i> (“Abydos”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘lord of Abydos’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the sun god &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣꜥꜥw</i> (of unknown meaning) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Lord of <i>ꜣꜥꜥw</i>’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead person who has successfully completed the transition to the afterlife (and so become ‘living’), especially as an epithet for Osiris</li><li>epithet for various gods</li><li>sarcophagus</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“life”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘possessor of life’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the king</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥḥ</i> (“palace”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “lord of the palace”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to swim &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the sun) to ‘swim’ in the sky</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring forth or make flow (the inundation)</li></ol><p>Possibly metathesized from <i>bnbn</i> (“to make flow, to swell”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christ’s thorn jujube (<i>Ziziphus spina-christi</i>)</li><li>timber of the Christ’s thorn jujube</li><li>fruit of the Christ’s thorn jujube</li></ol><p>Put by Koehler-Baumgartner to Arabic بَلَس (balas, “fig”) / Ge'ez <i>በለስ</i> (bäläs, “fig; sycomore”); because the Egyptian word has been glossed to mean sycomore, but this is not done so anymore. Instead the sycomore is in Egyptian <i>nht</i>, and Arabic has a similar word for the Christ’s thorn jujube in نَبِق (nabiq).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistress, lady (woman with authority over something)</li><li>(female) owner, possessor</li><li>basket</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nebet</li><li>Ombos (an ancient Egyptian city; modern Naqada)</li><li>Ombos (an ancient Egyptian city; modern Kom Ombo)</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p><p>From <i>nbw</i> (“gold”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) well-liked or charming one</li></ol><p>From <i>nbt</i> (“mistress, lady, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣt</i> (“friendliness, charm, belovedness”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “possessor of friendliness/charm/belovedness”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for the (female) dead&#58; revered one, venerable one (+ <i>ḫr</i>: one honored by (someone))</li></ol><p>From <i>nbt</i> (“mistress, lady, (female) possessor”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣḫ</i> (“dignity, honor, reverence”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “possessor of reverence”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title, one of the few commonly held by women: a woman who manages a household estate; conventionally rendered as ‘mistress (or ‘lady’) of the house’ &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) noble women collectively</li></ol><p><i>nbt</i> (“lady”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘lady of the house’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various goddesses &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>nbt</i> (“lady, possessor”) +&lrm; <i>sḫm</i> (“power, capacity to overpower”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘possessor of overmastering power’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for the queen</li></ol><p><i>nbt</i> (“lady, mistress”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Lady of the Land’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Nephthys</li></ol><p><i>nbt</i> (“lady”) +&lrm; <i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Lady of the Enclosure’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Two Ladies; the goddesses Wadjet and Nekhbet.</li><li>Conventionally written before a king’s Two Ladies name.</li></ol><p><i>nb</i> +&lrm; <i>-tj</i></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li><li>an object made of gold</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Amenemhat II, a pharaoh of the Twelfth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>nbw</i> (“golden”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣw</i> (“kas”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘golden (one) of the kas of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>that</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrong, wrongdoing</li><li>something wrong, erroneous</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to exhale or blow (+ <i>r</i>: on)</li></ol><p>Compare with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;napaḫ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) of superior or admirable quality, to be(come) fine, good, or wonderful</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) pleasant or gratifying to the senses</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) beautiful or fair, to look good</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of scents and flowers) to smell good</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of food and drinks) to taste good</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of periods of time) to be(come) pleasant or enjoyable to live through</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to acquire or have good or pleasant qualities, to be capable, agreeable, or benevolent</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of work) to be done well or competently</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of medicine) to be(come) effective</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of names) to acquire or have a good reputation</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>nfr</i>: fine, beautiful, pleasant, etc.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine or good person</li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) benefactor (of someone), welldoer</li><li>(<i>with following plural genitive</i>) the best (person) among…, the best one of…</li><li>good, goodness, pleasingness (in opposition to <i>bjn</i>)</li><li>good qualities or deeds (of a person)</li><li>something fine, good, beautiful, pleasing</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good, sure, fine</li><li>correct, that’s right</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>not even, not at all</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;fir-</i> (“to be good”) with an additional <i>*nV-</i> prefix. Compare also Tamahaq <i>ⵉⴼⵔⴰⵔ</i> (ifrar, “best”) and Saho <i>feer</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relaxation, recreation</li></ol><p>From <i>nfr</i> (“pleasingness, goodness”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) in a direct genitive construction with the first element nominalized, thus literally ‘goodness of heart/mind’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Neferirkare Kakai, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>nfr</i> (“pleasing, fine, good”) +&lrm; <i>jr</i> (perfective relative form of <i>jrj</i> (“to do, to make”)) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force, sustenance”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘the fine one whom the life-force of Ra made’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Neferkare or Neferkara</li><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Pepi II Neferkare, a pharaoh of the Sixth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nfr</i> (“pleasing, fine, good”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘the fine (one) of the ka of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Neferkasokar, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nfr</i> (“pleasing, fine, good”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”) +&lrm; <i>zkr</i> (“Sokar”), thus literally ‘the fine (one) of the ka of Sokar’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nefermaat</li></ol><p><i>nfr</i> (“to be beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>mꜣꜥt</i> (“Maat, justice”), thus literally ‘Maat/justice is beautiful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferneferuaten</li></ol><p>From <i>nfr</i> (“beautiful (one)”) +&lrm; <i>nfrw</i> (“beautiful ones”, plural of <i>nfr</i>) +&lrm; <i>jtn</i> (“Aten”) in a series of direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘beautiful one of the beautiful ones of Aten’, i.e. ‘most beautiful one of Aten’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferneferure</li></ol><p>From <i>nfr</i> (“beautiful (one)”) +&lrm; <i>nfrw</i> (“beautiful ones”, plural of <i>nfr</i>) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) in a series of direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘beautiful one of the beautiful ones of Ra’, i.e. ‘most beautiful one of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The god Nefertum, personification of the first plant, a lotus, that mythically bloomed after the primeval waters receded and gave rise to the sun.</li></ol><p><i>nfr</i> +&lrm; <i>tm</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Neferhotep</li></ol><p><i>nfr</i> (“beautiful (one)”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus literally ‘the beautiful one is content’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferhetepes</li></ol><p><i>nfr</i> (“to be beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“peace”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“her”), thus literally ‘her peace is beautiful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the White Crown, Hedjet &lsqb;since the 13th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>nfr</i> (“White Crown”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏt</i> (“White Crown”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Neferefre, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>The reading and interpretation of this name are disputed. Depending on the presence or absence of honorific transposition, <i>rꜥ</i> may be read as either following or preceding <i>nfr.f</i>. Furthermore, it is unclear if the <i>f</i> in this name is to be read as a suffix pronoun <i>.f</i> (“he, his”) or simply a phonetic complement to the sign for <i>nfr</i>. Verner has proposed that the name was originally intended to be read as <i>rꜥ-nfr</i> (“Ra is beautiful”) but was soon misconstrued as <i>rꜥ-nfr.f</i> (“Ra, he is beautiful”). Leprohon accepts this idea of a later misconstrual but reads the name with <i>rꜥ</i> at the end, so that the king’s name does not focus on the sun god but rather on the king himself; he proposes an analysis following the sequence of <i>nfr</i> (“to be pleasing, fine, good”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘he is perfect [= fine] (in the manner of) Ra’ (from an original form <i>nfr-rꜥ</i> (“the perfection of Ra”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something pleasing, beautiful, or good; good thing, fine thing</li><li>beautiful, fine, or good woman</li><li>epithet for various goddesses</li><li>maiden, lass; nubile or marriageable young woman</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nofret</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nefertiti</li></ol><p><i>nfrt</i> (“good one, beautiful one”) +&lrm; <i>jj</i> (“to come”) +&lrm; <i>.tj</i> (feminine third-person singular stative ending), thus literally ‘The Beautiful One has Come’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nefertari</li></ol><p><i>nfrt</i> (“beautiful (one)”) +&lrm; <i>jrj</i> (“with respect to it, thereto”), thus roughly ‘the most beautiful (one) among them’. The <i>t</i> and following <i>j</i> in this name are often transposed for aesthetic reasons.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nefertiabet</li></ol><p><i>nfrt</i> (“beautiful (one)”) +&lrm; <i>jꜣbt</i> (“east”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘the beautiful (one) of the east’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nefertkau</li></ol><p><i>nfrt</i> (“beautiful (one)”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣw</i> (plural of <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”)) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘the beautiful one of kas’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferthenut</li></ol><p><i>nfrt</i> (“beautiful (one)”) +&lrm; <i>ḥnwt</i> (“lady”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Neferti</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty</li><li>perfection</li><li>depletion, deficiency</li><li>zero; used when a subtraction results in zero in accounts and mathematical texts, or to mark a baseline for construction</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferu</li><li>a male given name, Neferu</li></ol><p>From <i>nfr</i> +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p><p>Related to <i>nfr</i> (“not even, not at all”).</p><p>Non-lemma forms.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferubity</li></ol><p><i>nfrw</i> (“beauty”) +&lrm; <i>bjtj</i> (“king of Lower Egypt”), thus literally ‘the beauty of the king of Lower Egypt’. The written form apparently demonstrates honorific transposition; however, some authors read the name without transposition, as <i>bjtj-nfrw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferuptah</li></ol><p>From <i>nfrw</i> (“beauty”) +&lrm; <i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘the beauty of Ptah’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferure</li></ol><p><i>nfrw</i> (“beauty”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘The Beauty of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neferusobek</li></ol><p>From <i>nfrw</i> (“beauty”) +&lrm; <i>sbk</i> (“Sobek”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘the beauty of Sobek’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition. Some authors instead read the name without transposition as <i>sbk-nfrw</i> (“Sobek is perfect”), following the later Greek form <i>Σκεμίοφρις</i> (Skemíophris), but others consider this order of elements a later development.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) cows, cattle &lsqb;18th Dynasty and Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>nfr</i> +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>until (a time) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>to, up to (a place) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>nfryt</i> (“end, limit”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“regarding, to, towards”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nf</i> (“that”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longhorned ox</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some, a few, a little, a bit (+ <i>n(j)</i> or <i>m</i>: of) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to yell</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to yell loudly or yell a lot, to roar</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>nhm</i> (“to yell”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to quake with fear &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>nh</i> (“to shake”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to leap (+ <i>m-ꜥ</i>: to leap out of reach of, to escape)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;medicine,&#32;of blood vessels) to pulse, to beat</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw down &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people or bulls) to copulate</li></ol><p>Perhaps compare Hebrew <i>נָאַף</i> for the ‘copulate’ sense, but as the Egyptian verbs are likely identical, with a basic meaning of ‘to leap’, a connection with the Hebrew term is doubtful.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sycamore</li><li>deciduous tree in general</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, lack, deficiency</li><li>deficiency incurred, loss</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) awake, to wake up &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to awaken (someone), to wake (someone) up</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) the plural indefinite article</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>nhj</i> (“some, a few, a little”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of, belonging to</li><li>made of (a material or composition)</li><li>of, possessing (a quality)</li><li>introduces the agent of an infinitive when it is a personal pronoun</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;with a following noun or pronoun) there is no, there are no; introduces an independent negated existential clause</li><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) not; negates any sentence</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) not; negates a nominal sentence, an adjectival sentence of possession, or the rheme of an emphatic clause (used with <i>js</i>; see Usage Notes below)</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) not; negates most verbal predicates besides infinitival, imperative, and subjunctive forms&#59; forms the negation of the perfect, perfect passive, terminative, perfective, imperfective, prospective, and prospective passive.</li><li>no, not any; negates an individual noun</li><li>not; negates other individual words</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for it, to it, thereto, therefor</li><li>because of it, therefore</li></ol><p><i>n</i> (“to, for”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p><p>Oréal hypothesizes that <i>nj</i> originated as a verb expressing nonexistence or absence in a stative construction, drawing a speculative connection with the attested verb <i>nj</i> (“to rebuff, to drive away”).</p><p>A cognate relation to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;lā</i> (“no, not”) has also been suggested.</p><p><i>n</i> (“to, for”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (adverbializing suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>and not, but not, rather than; introduces a word or phrase negated in contrast to the preceding word or phrase.</li><li>unless, except</li></ol><p><i>nj</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>js</i> (negative particle).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favored person, favorite, darling</li></ol><p>From <i>nj</i> (“of, belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), thus ‘(he who is) belonging to the heart/mind’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to punish, especially with death</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be punished</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a supernatural evil being due punishment, especially Apophis</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) an earthly evildoer</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an afterworld god, ‘the Punisher’</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greeting</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>,&#32;or&#32;transitive) to call to or call upon &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stammer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ostrich</li><li>ostrich egg (used medicinally)</li><li>ostrich feather</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelter</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to copulate</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic نَكَحَ (nakaḥa) and Arabic نَاكَ (nāka), Akkadian <i>𒈾𒀀𒆪</i> (nâku).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some, a little bit</li></ol><p>From <i>n-</i> +&lrm; <i>ktt</i> (“to be small”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fornicator, adulterer</li></ol><p>From <i>nk</i> (“to copulate”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>jn</i> +&lrm; <i>mj</i></li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to traverse</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bestir oneself, to move about &lsqb;chiefly Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the earth) to quake &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps a reduplication of <i>nmj</i> (“to traverse”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen cloth (used in ritual)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come, to arrive (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone)) &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>m-</i> (noun-forming prefix) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ms</i> (“to twist, to weave”) with regular dissimilation of <i>m-</i> to <i>n-</i> before a labial consonant; for the stem, itself unattested, compare the extended verb <i>msn</i> (“to twist, to weave”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vaginal discharge</li></ol><p>Related to <i>nms</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughterhouse</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nemti or Nemty, a falcon god.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Nemtyemzaef Merenre I and II, two pharaohs of the Sixth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>nmtj</i> (“Nemty”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in, in the role of”) +&lrm; <i>zꜣ</i> (“protection”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus literally ‘Nemty is his protection’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nemtinakht</li></ol><p>From <i>nmtj</i> (“Nemti”) +&lrm; <i>nḫt</i> (“strong, victorious”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Nemti is strong’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stride, march, walking &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>manner of stepping or walking; gait, stride</li><li>goings, journeys, travels, course</li><li>procedure, course of proceeding or action</li></ol><p>From <i>nmt</i> (“to stride over, traverse”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf, deformed person &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a dwarf figurine associated with the god Ptah and conventionally called a Pataikos &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orphan</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nimlot</li></ol><p>Of Libyan origin, presumably from a predecessor or extinct sister language of Proto-Berber.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) biased, to be(come) partial</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>this, that</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(since Middle Egyptian,&#32;with a following noun or pronoun) there is no, there are no; introduces an independent negated existential clause</li><li>(since Middle Egyptian,&#32;with a following noun or pronoun) without; there not being any …; introduces a subordinated negated existential clause</li><li>(<i>since Middle Egyptian</i>) not; negates an adverbial or adjectival sentence</li><li>(<i>since Middle Egyptian</i>) not; negates a subjunctive main clause with future meaning</li><li>(since Middle Egyptian,&#32;uncommon) not; negates a nominal sentence &lsqb;since the 12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(since Middle Egyptian,&#32;used without anything negated following) or not; contrasts with a preceding clause or phrase</li></ol><p>Oréal hypothesizes an origin in a contraction of <i>nj</i> (negative particle) +&lrm; <i>wn</i> (“exists”), following Vergote and rejecting an earlier hypothesis by Osing and Loprieno that would suggest an origin in <i>nj</i> (negative particle) +&lrm; <i>jn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) inert</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) tired, weary</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>nj</i> +&lrm; <i>jnk</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inertness, rigor mortis</li><li>the hidden, lost, perpetually dark, infinite primeval waters</li><li>Nu, father of the gods, the divine anthropomorphisation of the primeval waters, a member of the Ogdoad.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Naunet</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spleen</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smooth selvage or hem of a linen sheet &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>umbilical cord</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sleep</li></ol><p><i>n-</i> +&lrm; <i>qdd</i> (“to sleep”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;transitive) to inscribe, to scratch</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) ripe sycamore figs</li></ol><p><i>nqꜥ</i> (“to scratch”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>. Sycamore figs were scraped or gashed in order to make them ripen more quickly; otherwise, they would be full of insects and inedible by the time they ripened.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fear, to be afraid (+ <i>n</i>: of)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in awe (+ <i>n</i>: of)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overawe</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terror, fear</li></ol><p><i>nrj</i> (“to fear”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongue</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;lis-</i> (“tongue”). Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;lišān-</i> and Proto-Berber <i>&#42;iləs</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>nj</i> +&lrm; <i>sj</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire, flame</li></ol><p><i>nsr</i> (“to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat, throne</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>nj</i> +&lrm; <i>sw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king of Upper Egypt</li><li>king of (Upper and Lower) Egypt, especially as a divine power (as opposed to <i>ḥm</i>, which refers to a particular incarnation that holds that power)</li><li>a kind of spear or javelin &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Traditionally (since Sethe 1911) derived from <i>n(j)</i> (“of, belonging”) +&lrm; <i>swt</i> (“sedge”) in a direct genitive construction with <i>n(j)</i> used as a noun, literally ‘the belonging one of the sedge’, i.e. ‘the one to whom Upper Egypt belongs’, the sedge being an emblem of Upper Egypt. The odd glyph order in the written form is probably due to a preference for forming compact groups of hieroglyphs.</p><p>However, this interpretation has been challenged, as Helck, Schenkel, and others have argued that the plant represented by <i>swt</i> is not the emblem of Upper Egypt at all; Schenkel also suggests the reconstructed vocalization of the word does not support such an etymology. Peust goes so far as to say the word has no special connection with Upper Egypt, and derives it as borrowed from Sumerian <i>𒉺𒋼𒋛</i> (ensi<sub>2</sub>) with the Egyptian noun-forming suffix <i>-w</i>. This might also be suggested by the alternate reading <i>nzw</i> and the cuneiform rendering 𒅔𒋛𒅁𒅀 (<i>in-si-ib-ia</i>) for the compound form nswt-bjtj from a Ramesside-era Hittite letter.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dual king of Upper and Lower Egypt; pharaoh</li><li>Conventionally written before the cartouche containing a king’s praenomen.</li></ol><p><i>nswt</i> +&lrm; <i>bjtj</i>, literally ‘the belonging one of the sedge, the one of the bee’, or, more simply, ‘he of the sedge and the bee’, the sedge and the bee being emblems of Upper and Lower Egypt, respectively, according to the traditional etymology. Sometimes it is instead read as <i>n(j)</i> +&lrm; <i>swt</i> +&lrm; <i>bjt</i>, literally ‘the belonging one of the sedge and of the bee’, thus ultimately expressing the same meaning. In recent times the derivation and reading of <i>nswt</i> has been questioned; if it is not in fact derived from <i>swt</i> (“sedge”), then <i>nswt-bjtj</i> may just be a compound of two words meaning ‘king’. The term is also attested in cuneiform script as 𒅔𒋛𒅁𒅀 (<i>in-si-ib-ia</i>) from a Ramesside-era Hittite letter.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Amun, occasionally also used of other gods</li></ol><p><i>nswt</i> (“king”) +&lrm; <i>nṯrw</i> (plural of <i>nṯr</i> (“god”)) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘king of the gods’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Huni, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>nswt</i> (“king”) +&lrm; <i>ḥw</i> (“hitting, striking”), thus ‘the striking king’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) tenant farmers of lands belonging to the king &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>nswt</i> (“king”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingship (of Egypt) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>nswt</i> (“king (of Egypt)”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neith, a primeval goddess of war and creator deity</li></ol><p>Possibly from an earlier <i>nrt</i> (“Neith”), a feminine form of <i>nrw</i> (“terror, fear, terrible one”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Nitocris or Netiqret</li></ol><p><i>nt</i> (“Neith”) +&lrm; <i>jqr</i> (“to be excellent”) +&lrm; <i>.t(j)</i> (feminine singular stative ending), thus literally ‘Neith is excellent’.</p><p>While correct uses of this name are attested for non-royal individuals, the name’s application to a sixth dynasty pharaoh/queen likely originated as an error for <i>nṯrj-kꜣ-rꜥ</i> made long after the time of the pharaoh who bore that name.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accounting, bookkeeping</li></ol><p><i>nt</i> (feminine of <i>nj</i> (“of, belonging to”)) +&lrm; <i>ḥsb</i> (“calculation”), thus ‘(what is) belonging to calculation’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Neithhotep</li></ol><p>From <i>nt</i> (“Neith”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”). The interpretation is unclear; a reading as ‘Neith is content’ in this order does not seem possible: if <i>ḥtp</i> were in the stative, it would need the feminine stative ending <i>.tj</i> rather than an unwritten masculine stative ending, and in any case theophoric names using the stative did not appear until the Middle Kingdom. A solution may be found in honorific transposition, in which case the intended reading would be <i>ḥtp-nt</i>. Indeed, just such a name is attested in later times.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior who fights from a chariot, charioteer</li></ol><p><i>nt</i> (feminine of <i>nj</i> (“of, belonging to”)) +&lrm; <i>ḥtrj</i> (“team of animals”), thus ‘(what is) belonging to the team of animals’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the way of doing things, custom, practice, ritual</li></ol><p><i>nt</i> (feminine of <i>nj</i> (“of, belonging to”)) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“hand, state, condition”), thus ‘(what is) belonging to the hand/state of affairs’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>to swelter, to parch</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, him (see usage notes)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to besprinkle, to water (a plot of land)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the relative adjective — allows a clause to serve as a relative clause, usually with an adverbial or verbal predicate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(introducing a direct relative clause,&#32;with <i>ntj</i> serving in place of a subject) which is, who is, being</li><li>(introducing an indirect relative clause,&#32;with a later resumptive pronoun) such that, for which, concerning which</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>introducing a direct relative clause</i>) he who is, one who is, that which is</li><li>(introducing an indirect relative clause,&#32;with a later resumptive pronoun) he for whom, one for whom, one such that, that for which</li><li>(<i>without a following relative clause</i>) he who exists, one who exists, that which exists</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>nt</i> +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending), where <i>nt</i> is a nominalized use of the feminine form of the genitival adjective <i>n(j)</i>. The noun <i>ntj</i> is in turn simply a nominalized use of the adjective <i>ntj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;in the plural,&#32;euphemistic) the dead &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ntj</i> (“(one) who is”) +&lrm; <i>jm</i> (“therein”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has things, one of the haves</li></ol><p><i>ntj</i> (“one for whom”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“to, for”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“him”), thus literally ‘one for whom (there is something) for him’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whoever (is), anyone who is, everyone who is, everything that is</li></ol><p><i>ntj</i> (“one who is, that which is”) +&lrm; <i>nb</i> (“all, every”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes)</li></ol><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>nj</i> +&lrm; <i>ntk</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Afro-Asiatic. Compare Arabic أنت (ʔanta) and Blin <i>ʔənti</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she, her (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>they, them (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ntṯ</i> (“you”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>introducing a direct relative clause</i>) she who is, one who is, that which is</li><li>(introducing an indirect relative clause,&#32;with a later resumptive pronoun) she for whom, one for whom, one such that, that for which</li><li>(<i>without a following relative clause</i>) she who exists, one who exists, that which exists</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>introducing a noun clause</i>) serves as a complementizer to convert a verbal or nonverbal sentence with realis mood into a subordinated noun clause&#59; that</li></ol><p>Various uses of the feminine form of the relative adjective <i>ntj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is and what is not; everything imaginable</li></ol><p>Compound formed from <i>ntt</i> (“that which exists”) +&lrm; <i>jwtt</i> (“that which doesn’t exist”), the nominalized feminine forms of <i>ntj</i> (relative adjective) +&lrm; <i>jwtj</i> (negative relative adjective), in coordination.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is desired or wanted</li></ol><p>From <i>ntt</i> (“that which is”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind, will”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) they, them (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) this, that</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;formal) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>nj</i> +&lrm; <i>wj</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(moment of) time</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to deviate, to turn aside</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swaddling clothes (of a child)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inaccuracy, deviation (of a measuring cord)</li><li>ethical deviation, aberration, corruption</li></ol><p>From <i>nwd</i> (“to deviate”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gather</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>town, city</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) sky</li><li>adze</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thebes &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the City, personified as a deity</li><li>the goddess Nut (literally “Sky”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>local, municipal</li></ol><p><i>nwt</i> (“town”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba adjective ending”).</p><p><i>nwt</i> (“town”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“dual ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naucratis &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ναύκρατις</i> (Naúkratis).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to totter</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) water</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave or swell of water</li></ol><p>From <i>nwy</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, cord</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) scorched</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heat</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>‘adze of Wepwawet’, an instrument used in the Opening of the Mouth ritual</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn up</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive forth, to expel</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>šnj</i> as object) to dress (the hair)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>neshmet-barque, the sacred barque in which (the image of) Osiris was carried in procession during his festival at Abydos</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>nšm</i> (“to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunderstorm, rage, disaster</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to breathe</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to breathe heavily, to pant</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe (air)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smell (a scent)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make breathe</li></ol><p>Compare Hebrew <i>נָשַׁף</i> (nāšap̄, “to exhale, to blow”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hairdresser</li></ol><p>From <i>nš</i> (“to dress the hair”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inquire, to ask</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to protect, to preserve, to save</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flour</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult</li></ol><p><i>nḏ</i> (“to inquire”) +&lrm; <i>r(ꜣ)</i> (“mouth”), thus literally “to inquire the mouth”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Old Egyptian) to endure &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>&#42;nḏd</i>, from <i>n-</i> (intransitivizer) +&lrm; <i>ḏdj</i> (“to endure”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Old Egyptian) to endure &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>&#42;nḏd</i>, from <i>n-</i> (intransitivizer) +&lrm; <i>ḏdj</i> (“to endure”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to taste sweet</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to smell sweet or pleasant</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;often of air, water, or temperature) to be(come) pleasant or refreshing</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) healthy or well</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>nḏm</i>: sweet, pleasant</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;nx̣m</i>. Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;nʿm-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to consult, to ask for advice (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i> or + <i>m-ꜥ</i>: to consult with, to ask (someone) for advice)</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>nḏ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to work wood, to carpenter (+ <i>m</i>: with, using) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grab hold</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seize, to grab hold of</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) small or little in size</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) lesser in rank or degree</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the function of body parts) to be(come) feeble or faint</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>nḏs</i>: small, feeble</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person physically small in size</li><li>commoner, poor or lowly person</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) person, individual</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subject, servant (especially used of foreign people and lands)</li></ol><p><i>n(j)</i> (“of, belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>ḏt</i> (“(funerary) estate”), thus ‘(the one) belonging to the funerary estate’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering gift for the dead, especially one given by the towns of a funerary estate (<i>pr-ḏt</i>) &lsqb;Old to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>New Year’s gift &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) foreign tribute &lsqb;19th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>nḏt</i> +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i>. Compare <i>j.nḏ ḥr</i> (“hail, greetings”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) &lsqb;Middle to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>stinginess, meanness, thrift</li><li>badness, baseness</li></ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>presumptuousness</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly formed from <i>nḏt</i> (“servant, slave”). The verb <i>nḏy</i> is of later date and may best be seen as a back-formation.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be parched with thirst &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to suffocate from lack of air</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirst</li><li>alternative form of <i>mḏꜣ</i> (“a unit of measure for dates and loaves”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>nꜣḏꜣrw</i> (“chip, ostracon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nꜣḏꜣrw</i> (“chip, ostracon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) runny or watery</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>nḏꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Choiak</li></ol><p><i>nḥb</i> +&lrm; <i>kꜣw</i>, plural of <i>kꜣ</i>, in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Apportioner of kas’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck</li><li>lotus bud</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) strong</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to save, to rescue</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>a particle used to emphasise the truth of an independent clause; surely, assuredly.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>) to match up to, to equal or resemble in a positive way</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to live as long as (a time), to have one’s lifetime equal or match up to</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the dual</i>) a kind of loaf or cake of bread</li><li>a kind of forced labor</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>nḥr</i> (“to match up to, to equal or resemble”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nubian</li></ol><p><i>nḥs</i> (“Nubia”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to believe, to trust</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tooth</li><li>tusk</li></ol><p><i>n-</i> +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“to be white”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyclical eternity</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) (sesame) oil, used for cooking, as a salve, and as fuel for lamps</li></ol><p><i>n(j)</i> (“(one) of, belonging to”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḥ</i> (“million”), thus literally ‘(the one) belonging to a million (of years)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>nḫj</i>: lamentable, pitiful</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Nekhbet</li></ol><p>From <i>nḫb</i> (“the city of Nekheb/El-Kab”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine <i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;usually of a door-bolt or door) to draw back, to open</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to lament (± <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;in an adjectival predicate with suffixed <i>-wj</i>) to be(come) lamentable or pitiful</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) a child</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrine</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hieraconpolis</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth, childhood</li></ol><p><i>nḫn</i> (“to be a child”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childhood &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>nḫn</i> (“to be a child”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) victorious</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) strong</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory</li><li>strength</li><li>strong one</li><li>champion</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nectanebo</li></ol><p><i>nḫt</i> (“strong, victorious (one)”) +&lrm; <i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘The strong one of his lord’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has grown capable of fending for himself; an adult</li><li>a strong person</li></ol><p><i>nḫt</i> (“strong”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘(one) strong of arm’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complaint</li><li>lamentation</li></ol><p><i>nḫj</i> (“to lament”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fish-shaped pendant</li></ol><p>From <i>nḫꜣ</i> (“to dangle”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flail, as part of the royal insignia</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>nḫꜣ</i> (“flail”), from <i>nḫꜣ</i> (“to dangle”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>god</li><li>dead person (as a god)</li><li>king (as a god)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the currently living king, often appended as a title either before all other titles or immediately before or after the king’s names, sometimes in contrast to <i>nṯr-ꜥꜣ</i> for the dead former king</li><li>epithet for the junior king in a coregency, in contrast to <i>nṯr-ꜥꜣ</i> for the senior king</li><li>(<i>less commonly</i>) an epithet for various gods, especially Osiris as king of the afterworld</li></ol><p><i>nṯr</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“pleasing, fine, good, beautiful”), thus ‘the pleasing/fine/good/beautiful god’. The use of the term in contrast with <i>nṯr-ꜥꜣ</i> (“the elder god”) shows that the implication of <i>nfr</i> here was probably one of youthful beauty or, euphemistically, simply youth, as also seen in <i>nfrt</i> (“young woman”). A rendering such as ‘the youthful god’ is thus perhaps truer to the intended meaning than the traditional rendering ‘the good god’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the dead former king, in contrast to <i>nṯr-nfr</i> for the currently living king</li><li>epithet for the senior king in a coregency, in contrast to <i>nṯr-nfr</i> for the junior king</li><li>a very common epithet of various gods, especially Osiris and Ra</li></ol><p><i>nṯr</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great, elder”), thus (conventionally) ‘the great god’ or (more accurately) ‘the elder god’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) divine</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make divine</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine, holy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magic rope</li><li>natron</li></ol><p><i>Nisba</i> adjectival form of <i>nṯr</i> (“god”), suffixed with <i>-j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Netjerikare, a pharaoh of the Sixth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>nṯrj</i> (“divine (one)”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“sun, Ra”) in a series of direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘Divine One of the Ka of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Djoser, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li><li>a Two Ladies name&#x20;notably borne by Djoser, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>nṯrj</i> (“divine”) +&lrm; <i>ẖt</i> (“torso”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘(the one who is) divine of torso’. Alternatively, but perhaps less likely, if <i>ẖt</i> is to be interpreted with its other meaning of ‘group of gods’, the intended sense may be ‘divine one of the group of gods (i.e. the Ennead)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goddess &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine suffix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adze used in the “opening of the mouth” funerary ritual to allow the deceased to eat and drink once more.</li></ol><p>From <i>nṯr</i> (“god”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horus and Set</li><li>Shu and Tefnut</li><li>the fifth nome of Upper Egypt, centered at Coptos and also containing Dendera and Naqada</li></ol><p>From <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>-wj</i> (dual ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the seven sacred oils used in mortuary rituals, made of pine resin, wood pitch, and bitumen &lsqb;since the Fifth Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>n-</i> +&lrm; <i>ẖnm</i> (“to unite with”), thus ‘reunion’ oil.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become rigid or blunt<sup>?</sup></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old and Late Egyptian</i>) Used for vocative reference</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) the aforementioned ones, the said ones (see usage notes)</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) the plural definite article; the</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following noun) those of, these of</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) these, those</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with attached suffix pronoun) forms possessives; my, our, your, his, her, its, their, depending on which suffix pronoun is attached</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nayfaurud</li></ol><p><i>nꜣy</i> (demonstrative determiner) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣw</i> (plural of <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great (one)”)) +&lrm; <i>rwḏ</i> (“to be firm, to prosper”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine plural stative ending), thus literally ‘his great ones are prospering’, with ‘his great ones’ referring to his ancestors. Alternatively, it is possible that <i>ꜥꜣw</i> is not the plural of <i>ꜥꜣ</i> here but the abstract noun meaning ‘greatness’, thus ‘his greatness is prospering’; either interpretation is plausible.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following noun) they of, these of, those of</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>nꜣy</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nꜣḏꜣrw</i> (“chip, ostracon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chip, splinter (of wood, rock, etc.)</li><li>ostracon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to travel by boat, to sail</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Nile catfish, <i>Clarias gariepinus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Narmer, the first pharaoh of the First Dynasty of Egypt, considered the founder of a unified Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>nꜥr</i> (“catfish”) +&lrm; <i>mr</i> (“to be painful, stinging, fierce”), literally “The Painful/Stinging/Fierce Catfish”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of sacred tree</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be smooth or finely ground</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat</li><li>plinth</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pe, a city in Lower Egypt, where the Pharaohs were crowned.</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) alternative form of <i>pj</i> (“this”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following noun) he of, this of, that of</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>pꜣ</i> (“the, this”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Peftjauawybast</li></ol><p><i>p(ꜣy)</i> (demonstrative determiner) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”) +&lrm; <i>ṯꜣw</i> (“breath”) +&lrm; <i>(m)</i> (“in, from”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥwj</i> (dual of <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”)) +&lrm; <i>bꜣstt</i> (“Bastet”), thus ‘his breath (comes) from the arms of Bastet’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box, chest</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>that</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pf</i> (“that”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread open, to unfold</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open space, field</li><li>battlefield</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) this</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>this, that</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alleged serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Elephant, a Protodynastic pharaoh whose historicity is highly debated</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overturn</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Pepi I Meryre, Pepi II Neferkare, and Pepi III, pharaohs of the Sixth and Sixteenth Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li><li>household, inhabitants of a house collectively</li><li>property collectively, possessions, estate</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) garden or estate as a piece of land</li><li>(<i>of the king</i>) palace</li><li>(<i>of gods</i>) temple</li><li>(<i>of gods</i>) temple property and administration, temple estate</li><li>(<i>of the dead</i>) tomb</li><li>(<i>usually with qualifying word</i>) building with some other particular use</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) nest (of the <i>bꜣybꜣy</i> insect) &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>case, chest, holder (for ointments, bows, mirrors, etc.)</li></ol><p>Possibly from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;par-</i> (“house”). Compare a root in Proto-Berber <i>&#42;w-f-r</i> (“to enclose”); Tamahaq <i>afarra</i> (“enclosure”), Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵉⴼⵔⵉ</i> (ifri, “cave”), <i>ⴼⴼⵔ</i> (ffr, “to hide”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomb &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>nḥḥ</i> (“eternity”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funerary workshop, mortuary, place where bodies are embalmed &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“pleasing, fine, good”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) upper floor of a building, upstairs &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrj</i> (“upper”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lower floor of a building, ground floor, downstairs &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ẖrj</i> (“lower”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) a kind of royal palace contrasted with <i>pr-nswt</i>, traditionally rendered as ‘palace of the king of Lower Egypt’ &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house, palace”) +&lrm; <i>bjtj</i> (“king (of Lower Egypt<sup>?</sup>)”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bubastis</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>bꜣstt</i> (“Bastet”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘House of Bastet’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room for performing ablutions and ceremonially donning clothes and adornments, and where the Opening of the Mouth ritual is conducted; dressing room, robing room &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>dwꜣt</i> (“morning”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of the morning’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury of Lower Egypt &lsqb;early Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>dšr</i> (“red”), thus ‘red house’; compare <i>pr-ḥḏ</i> (“treasury”, literally “house of silver, white house”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in mathematical papyri</i>) height of a pyramid</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“(one that) comes forth”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“from”) +&lrm; <i>ws</i> (“height<sup>?</sup>”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mammisi, a subsidiary chapel attached to a larger temple and associated with the nativity of a god</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (later form of <i>mst</i>, the infinitive of <i>msj</i> (“to give birth”)) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of birth’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>library, archive</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>mḏꜣt</i> (“book, scroll, document”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of the scroll’, ‘house of the book’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>villa, country house &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>mꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>nbw</i> (“gold”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of gold’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royal palace &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a kind of royal palace contrasted with <i>pr-bjtj</i>, traditionally rendered as ‘palace of the king of Upper Egypt’</li><li>royal court, king together with his household and entourage</li><li>central administration, royal bureau</li><li>temple (as the palace of a god)</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house, palace”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”) in a direct genitive construction. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Persenet</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“to come forth”) +&lrm; <i>snt</i> (“sister”), thus ‘a sister comes forth’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forced labor camp or workshop, labor establishment, often for captives and slaves &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li><li>storehouse administration</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>šnꜥw</i> (“labor camp, storehouse”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of the labor camp’, ‘house of the storehouse’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funerary estate, foundation or endowment for the maintenance of a tomb &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>tomb itself</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ḏt</i> (“eternity”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘house of eternity’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“silver”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of silver’. However, this may be a reanalysis, and the name may have originally been intended as <i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”), thus ‘white house’, in contrast to the treasury of Lower Egypt, <i>pr-dšr</i> (literally “the red house”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harem &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ḫnr</i> (“harem”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of the harem’. Since the 19th Dynasty the second element is sometimes written and reanalyzed as <i>ḫntj</i> (“foremost”), thus ‘foremost house’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pr-ḫnr</i> (“harem”) &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple scriptorium</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“life”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of life’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armory, arsenal</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥḥꜣw</i> (“weapons”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘house of weapons’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palace</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) pharaoh &lsqb;c. 1450 <small>BCE</small>&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great, big”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>home</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> pr,&lrm;.sn.</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>.sn</i> (“their”), thus literally ‘their house’. The word in the sense of ‘home’ is distinguished from a simple reading of ‘their house’ by the lack of any antecedent to the suffix pronoun <i>.sn</i> (“their”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to emerge, to come out, to come forth, to go forth (+ <i>m</i>: from; + <i>r</i>: to, into (a place), against (someone); + <i>n</i>: to (someone); + <i>ḥr</i>: through (a door))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of besieged people) to come out, to surrender</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of land) to emerge or reappear after a flood, to no longer be flooded</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to escape (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>: from (fire, custody, danger in war, etc.))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to ascend, to go up (+ <i>r</i>: to (a place); + <i>tp</i> or <i>r tp</i>: onto; + <i>ḫr</i> or <i>n</i>: to (someone); + <i>r</i>, <i>m</i>, or <i>ḥr</i>: up (a stairway))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of celestial bodies, gods, kings, and the dead) to appear, to emerge, to manifest (+ <i>m</i>: from, out of)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of fire, voices, wind, storms, etc.) to break out, to burst forth, to emerge</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be divinely created, to emerge (+ <i>m</i>: by, from (a god), from (a body part of a god))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be born (+ <i>m</i>: from (a person, a god, a belly, an egg, etc.))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of goods,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to originate in (a place), to have one’s source or origin in</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of goods and offerings) to be produced, supplied, provided (+ <i>m</i>: by, from (a place); + <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people and names) to become well known, renowned, to come to stand out</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;mathematics) to be subtracted</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battlefield</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;par-&#47;&#42;pir-</i> (“to go out”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal</li></ol><p><i>prj</i> (“to emerge”) +&lrm; <i>ẖr</i> (“under”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>‘Emergence’, the growing season; one of three Egyptian seasons, spanning from November to March &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>ritual procession of a god (+ <i>m</i>: from; + <i>r</i>: to) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in names of festivals</i>) festival centered around such a procession</li><li>fruit (of a tree) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>crops, grain &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>seed corn &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>seed, offspring, progeny, children &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>prj</i> (“to emerge”).</p><p>From <i>prj</i> (“to emerge”); the different Coptic descendants show that the derivation is not the same as that of <i>prt</i> (“season of Emergence”). Compare Hebrew <i>פְּרִי</i> (pri, “fruit”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Procession of Min, a harvest or fertility festival in which (a statue of) the god Min was conveyed out of his temple accompanied by a white bull</li></ol><p><i>prt</i> (“procession”) +&lrm; <i>mnw</i> (“Min”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the annual heliacal rising of Sirius</li></ol><p><i>prt</i> (“emergence”) +&lrm; <i>spdt</i> (“Sirius”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘emergence of Sirius’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the First Procession, a festival featuring a procession led by the god Wepwawet and enacting a part of the mysteries of Osiris</li></ol><p><i>prt</i> (“procession”) +&lrm; <i>tpt</i> (“first”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) synonym of <i>prt-tpt</i> (“the First Procession, a festival featuring a procession led by the god Wepwawet and enacting a part of the mysteries of Osiris”)</li></ol><p><i>prt</i> (“procession”) +&lrm; <i>wp-wꜣwt</i> (“Wepwawet”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Procession of Wepwawet’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a funerary offering of which the spirit of the deceased is called to partake, generally made under the auspices of Osiris and consisting of at least bread, beer, cattle, and fowl; invocation offering</li></ol><p><i>prt</i> (infinitive of <i>prj</i> (“to emerge”)) +&lrm; <i>ḫrw</i> (“voice”), thus ‘emergence of the voice’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Great Procession, a festival in which (a representation of) the body of Osiris was conveyed from his temple to his tomb in a funerary barque</li></ol><p><i>prt</i> (“procession”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣt</i> (“great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Ancient Greek, of historical usage, equivalent to English Philip, notably borne by Philip III of Macedon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Φίλιππος</i> (Phílippos).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) collection of houses, settlement, compound &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>pr</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to spit on (someone or something) (+ <i>m</i>: to spit on with (spittle, fire, etc.))</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive&#32;(with <i>r</i>, <i>ḥr</i>, or <i>n</i>)&#32;or&#32;transitive) to spit on (something sick) in order to heal it &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spit on (a person) out of contempt &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spit on (something) in order to harm it &lsqb;New Kingdom to Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i> or <i>ḥr</i>) to spit on (Apophis or a representation of Apophis)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spit out (+ <i>m ḫt</i>: to contemptuously spit out behind (something))</li></ol><p>Cognate with Arabic بَزَقَ (bazaqa, “to spit”) and probably Proto-Berber <i>&#42;ăβzəg</i> (“to be wet, to swell”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cook</li></ol><p><div>From earlier <i>fsj</i>, <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./bc349d67.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="f" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a980819b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Q7" alt="glyph" height="92"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>.</div></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Psamtik</li></ol><p>Uncertain; one possible derivation, based on the attested longer form <i>pꜣ-zꜣ-n-mṯk</i>, is <i>p(ꜣ)</i> (“the”) +&lrm; <i>z(ꜣ)</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>mṯk</i> (“the Libyan deity Meṯek”), thus ‘the man of Meṯek’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to divide, to split, to spread out</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ritual instrument (for Opening of the mouth)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peseshkef (a ritual instrument made from obsidian used in the “opening of the mouth” funerary ritual to allow the deceased to eat and drink once more)</li></ol><p>From <i>psš</i> (“an instrument for Opening of the mouth”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣf</i> (“obsidian”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>share, portion</li><li>apportionment, distribution, sharing out</li><li>half</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Peseshet</li></ol><p>From <i>psš</i> (“to divide, to share out”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part, division</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) spine, backbone &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;of humans or animals) back &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new-moon festival</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninth</li></ol><p><i>psḏ-</i>, root of <i>psḏw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nineness, ennead &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the (Great) Ennead; the collection of nine gods worshipped at Heliopolis and taking part in the Heliopolitan creation myth, together representing the sum of all the elements of the created world: Atum, Shu, Tefnut, Nut, Geb, Osiris, Isis, Set, and Nephthys.</li><li>one of the lesser Enneads of nine gods</li></ol><p><i>psḏ-</i> (“nine”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Nine Bows; a generic term for the enemies of Egypt.</li></ol><p>Possibly a reinterpretation of earlier simple <i>psḏt</i> (“nine, nineness, ennead”), feminine of <i>psḏw</i> (“nine”), when written with nine bow signs.</p><p>The reinterpretation consists of <i>psḏt</i> +&lrm; <i>pḏt</i> (“bow”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;tišʕ-</i>. Compare also Bidiyo <i>pisiɗaŋ</i> (“seven”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sky, heavens</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) alternative form of <i>jptn</i> (“these, those”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) alternative form of <i>jptntj</i> (“these two, those two”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trample</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>who? what?</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to behold, to see</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to get to know (someone)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to see</li><li>(<i>catenative</i>) to see that</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) behold, lo, look; presents the contents of a statement</li></ol><p><i>pw</i> (“this”) +&lrm; <i>tr</i> (interrogative particle).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Ancient Greek, of historical usage, equivalent to English Ptolemy, notably borne by several members of the Ptolemaic dynasty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πτολεμαῖος</i> (Ptolemaîos).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ptah, creator god and patron of craftsmen, worshipped at Memphis</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make, to form, to create</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ptahmose</li></ol><p><i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (“to give birth”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine third-person singular stative ending), thus ‘Ptah is born’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ptahwer</li></ol><p><i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”), literally ‘Ptah is great’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ptahshepses</li></ol><p><i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”) +&lrm; <i>špss</i> (“to be noble, esteemed”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Ptah is noble/esteemed’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) this</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;in nominal sentences,&#32;following the initial nominal or pronominal element) this is…; it is…</li><li>(Middle Egyptian,&#32;formal) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ptr</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>pj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Puimre</li></ol><p><i>pwj</i> (a proper name) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in (the role of)”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus perhaps ‘<i>pwj</i> is Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Punt, an ancient Kingdom southeast of Egypt by the Red Sea.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Sea Peoples that invaded Egypt during the reign of Ramesses III, likely to be identified with the later Philistines</li></ol><p>Likely cognate with Akkadian <i>𒆳𒉿𒇷𒄑𒋫</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>Pilistu), <i>𒆳𒉺𒆷𒀾𒋫</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>Palastu) and Hebrew <i>פְּלֶשֶׁת</i> (Pəléšeṯ, “Philistia”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) this is…</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>pw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flea</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Piye, a pharaoh of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) alternative form of <i>pj</i> (“this”)</li></ol><p>According to von Beckerath, from Meroitic <i>&#42;𐦧𐦢</i> (&#42;pi, “to live”), with the ankh glyph in the name merely serving as a determinative. Leprohon instead interprets the name as <i>pꜣ</i> (“O”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫy</i> (“living one”), but notes that the reading is uncertain and considers it possible that the ankh is to be read as a prospective form of the verb <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“to live”). In this case the name is indeed simply <i>py</i> and the ankh is not a part of it.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(loaf of) a kind of bread, used especially as an offering to the dead &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bite</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of insects and scorpions) to sting</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be distraught</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to split apart</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) bone fracture</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stretch out, to spread out</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw (a bow)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;pVg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stride widely, to go far</li></ol><p><i>pḏ</i> (“to stretch”) +&lrm; <i>nmtt</i> (“stride, walking”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the stretching the cord ceremony (+ <i>ḥr</i> or <i>m</i>: for (a temple, building))</li></ol><p><i>pḏ</i> (to stretch) +&lrm; <i>šs</i> (“cord”), thus ‘stretching the cord’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow (weapon)</li></ol><p>Possibly from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;pVg-</i>. Equivalent to <i>pḏ</i> (“to stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowman, archer, especially as an epithet for kings or certain gods</li><li>barbarian, foreigner (literally ‘bowman’)</li></ol><p>Nominalized from <i>pḏt</i> (“bow”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reach (a place or person)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to attack</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attain venerable old age; to grow old in honor</li></ol><p>From <i>pḥ</i> (“to reach”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣḫ</i> (“dignity, honor, reverence”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to run</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, might</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rear, back</li><li>end</li><li>buttocks</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to go around, to circle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Euphrates</li></ol><p><i>pẖr</i> +&lrm; <i>wr</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;medicine</i>) prescription, remedy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old and Late Egyptian</i>) O (vocative reference)</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) the aforementioned, the said</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) the definite article; the</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following noun) he of, this of, that of</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pedubast</li></ol><p><i>pꜣ</i> (“the”) +&lrm; <i>dj</i> (“given”) +&lrm; <i>bꜣstt</i> (“Bastet”), thus literally ‘the given of Bastet’, i.e. ‘the one whom Bastet has given’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Pyhia</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Phamenoth</li></ol><p><i>pꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> +&lrm; <i>jmn-ḥtp(.w)</i>, literally ‘the one of Amenhotep’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Pauni</li></ol><p><i>pꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> +&lrm; <i>jnt</i>, literally ‘the one of the wadi’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Phaophi</li><li>the festival of Phaophi</li></ol><p>From <i>pꜣ</i> (“the”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“(one) of”) +&lrm; <i>jpt</i> (“Luxor, inner sanctum”), literally “the one of Luxor”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Mecheir</li></ol><p><i>pꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> +&lrm; <i>pꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>mḫrw</i>, literally ‘the one of the censer’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Pharmouthi</li></ol><p><i>pꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> +&lrm; <i>rnn-wtt</i>, literally ‘the one of Rennutet’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Pachon</li></ol><p><i>pꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> +&lrm; <i>ḫnsw</i>, literally ‘the one of Khonsu’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Panehesy</li></ol><p><i>pꜣ</i> (“the”) +&lrm; <i>nḥsj</i> (“Nubian”), thus literally ‘the Nubian’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Psusennes I, a pharaoh of the Twenty-First Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>pꜣ</i> (“the”) +&lrm; <i>sbꜣ</i> (“star”) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜥ</i> (“appearing”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>nwt</i> (“city”), literally “the star appearing in the city”, a reference to Thebes.</p><p>Ancient Greek <i>Ψουσέννης</i> (Psousénnēs) reflects the later (Demotic-era) shift of /χ/ → [ʃ] in /pəsiβʃəʕənˈneːʔə/. The preposition <i>m</i> appears as <i>n</i> due to assimilation with <i>nwt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fly</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) this, that</li></ol><p>Compare with Classical Syriac <i>ܦܪܚ</i> (peraḥ).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wafer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) thin</li></ol><p>Compare with Akkadian <i>𒉺𒀀𒄣</i> (pa-a-qu /⁠pāqu, piāqu⁠/, “narrow”) and Arabic ف و ق (f w q, literally “the small notch of the clavicle, the top of the shoulders, the thin dimensions of the coronal plane looking down from above the torso”), verb forms VII and VIII showing its connection to shortness, to become thin, emaciated, to wear out, and to die of coughing or shortness of breath, gasping.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flat piece or object; plate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>potsherd</li><li>plate of the skull</li><li>(officinal) turtle shell</li></ol><li>fine linen cloth</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>pꜣqj</i> (“to be thin”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine nominal ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(catenative,&#32;with a verb in the infinitive) to have done in the past, to have once done</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) this, that, these, those</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of baked goods used as an offering, considered the food of gods and the dead</li><li>The beginning of time, antiquity</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>primaeval (especially of gods or sacred places)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>primaeval god</li></ol><p>From <i>pꜣwt</i> (“primaeval time”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with attached suffix pronoun) forms possessives; my, our, your, his, her, its, their, depending on which suffix pronoun is attached</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to suffer or faint &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball</li><li>kneecap, knee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic. Compare Tashelhit <i>afud</i> (“knee”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) turned upside down, to be(come) inverted</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) overthrown, defeated</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) social stratum of nobility: elite, patricians</li><li>loaf of bread</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cool, to be(come) cool</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) calm</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cool breeze</li></ol><p>From <i>qbb</i> (“to be cool”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) death</li><li>spring, fountain</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to purify</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a libation</li></ol><p>Connected to <i>qbb</i> (“to be cold”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qebehsenuef, a mortuary god, protector of the intestines of the deceased.</li></ol><p>A verbal construction meaning “refresher of his brothers” composed of <i>qbḥ</i> (“to refresh, to purify”) +&lrm; <i>snw</i> (“brothers”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“third person pronoun”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often treated as a plural</i>) cool water</li><li>(<i>often treated as a plural</i>) a libation of water</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sky as a body of water, the sea of stars</li></ol><p><i>qbḥ</i> (“to refresh, to cool, to make a libation”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geba (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>גֶּבַע</i> (Geb̲aʿ).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to build</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form</li><li>character, nature</li><li>extent</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sleep</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To go around.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To return.</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) To surround.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Qatna (a Bronze Age city in present-day western Syria)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a qite, a measure of weight equivalent to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>10</sub></small> <i>dbn</i>, or 9.1 grams &lsqb;New Kingdom, from the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of Syrian pine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kadesh, a city in the Levant &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>The goddess Qetesh &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Related to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ḳudš-</i> (“holy”). Cognate with Biblical Hebrew <i>קָדוֹשׁ</i> (qɔḏoš, “holy”), Hebrew <i>קודש &#47; קֹדֶשׁ</i> (qṓḏeš), Old South Arabian <i>𐩤𐩵𐩪</i> (qds¹) and Arabic قُدْس (quds, “purity, holiness”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of Qetesh, as syncretized with Astarte and Anat</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to vomit &lsqb;medical papyri and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of floodwater) to gush upon (the fields) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vomit &lsqb;medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Cusae in Upper Egypt &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare <i>qꜣꜥ</i> (“to vomit”), Oromo <i>hooqqisuu, hooqqisiisuu</i> (“to vomit”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think of, to care about</li><li>to mourn &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to create</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, appearance, nature</li><li>creator</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend, to bow</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to subjugate</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to incline (oneself)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>council, court</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to embrace</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to surround</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) brave and sturdy, to be(come) stalwart</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to prevail, to conquer</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) fat</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheaf, bundle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a yellow pigment</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palanquin</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bravery, valor</li><li>strength</li><li>victory</li></ol><p><i>qnj</i> (“to be brave, to prevail”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) numerous, many &lsqb;since the Amarna Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) an elite unit of soldiers or bodyguards &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>qnj</i> (“to be brave”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>, thus ‘the Braves’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree bark, used in the preparation of kyphi &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>qq</i> (“to peel”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend together, to crook</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack, bag</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm</li><li>stormcloud</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) frog</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fire (pottery)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stoke (a kiln)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to broil (food) out of (bones)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, cavity</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also dual or plural</i>) the Cavern, a region of the afterworld</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sepulchre</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bury</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial</li></ol><p>From <i>qrs</i> (“to bury”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffin, sarcophagus</li></ol><p><i>qrs</i> (“to bury”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Greek, of historical usage, equivalent to English Cleopatra, notably borne by Cleopatra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Κλεοπάτρα</i> (Kleopátra).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>qrwjwꜣpꜣdrꜣ</i> (“Cleopatra”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for holding fish &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>qrḥ</i> (“pot”) +&lrm; <i>mr</i> (“libation basin”), presumably in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ḳas-</i>. Compare with Proto-Berber <i>&#42;e-ɣăst</i> (Tashelhit <i>ixss</i>) and Hausa <i>ƙàshī</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically painful</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically pained</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) mentally distressed or aggrieved</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of work or acts) to be(come) difficult or hard to do</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of times) to be(come) hard or troubled</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ordeal, pain</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something difficult</li><li>trouble, difficulty, misfortune</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caesar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Καῖσαρ</i> (Kaîsar), from Latin <i>Caesar</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hammer, to beat (metal: copper, gold, or silver) into a sheet &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hammer at, to carve (a sculpture)</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>qḥ</i> (“to break stones”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) metalworkers &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>qḥqḥ</i> (“to hammer (metal)”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Qahedjet, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>qꜣ</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏt</i> (“White Crown”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘(the one who is) high of White Crown’, i.e. ‘he whose White Crown is high’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Qa'a, a pharaoh of the First Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>qꜣ</i> (“raised”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”), thus literally ‘(the one) raised of arm’, i.e. ‘the one whose arm is raised’</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle, midst</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ḳrb-</i> or <i>&#42;ḳlb-</i>; compare Akkadian <i>𒈛𒊮𒈫</i> (qerbum, “inside”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest, breast &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>part of the leg<sup>?</sup> &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>crown, parting</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plant that grows by creeping; creeper</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) high, to be(come) tall</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) exalted</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of sound) to be(come) loud</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to be(come) proud, arrogant, haughty</li></ol><p>From <i>qꜣj</i> (“to be(come) high”) +&lrm; <i>sꜣ</i> (“back”), thus literally ‘to be(come) high of back’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) boisterous or loud</li></ol><p>From <i>qꜣj</i> (“to be(come) high”) +&lrm; <i>ḫrw</i> (“voice”), thus literally ‘to be(come) high of voice’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of riverboat, perhaps a barge</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag, bundle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bind, to tie &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>qjs</i> (“to vomit”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope ladder &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;of the sky</i>) height, distance above the ground &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>height, distance from base to top, tallness (as a dimension)</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) length (of one’s back)</li><li>length (of a building) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>full loudness, top of one's voice &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>qꜣꜣ</i> (“hill, mound”)</li></ol><p><i>qꜣj</i> (“to be(come) high, tall, loud”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gezer (an ancient Canaanite city in the Shephelah region)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>גֶּזֶר</i> (gézer).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill, mound, raised ground</li></ol><p>From <i>qꜣj</i> (“to be high”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to vomit, to spew out</li></ol><p>Compare <i>qjs</i> (“to vomit”), <i>qꜣ, qꜣꜣ</i> (“to be putrid; to vomit”), Ge'ez <i>ቄአ</i> (ḳeʾä), present <i>ይቂእ</i> (yəḳiʾ, “to vomit”), Tigre <i>ቀኣ</i> (ḳäʾa, “to vomit”), Arabic قَاءَ (qāʔa), present يَقِيءُ (yaqīʔu, “to vomit”), Hebrew <i>קָא</i> (qāʾ), present <i>יָקִיא</i> (yāqīʾ, “to vomit”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheet of papyrus</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth (of humans or animals)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;chiefly in titles) speaker, mouthpiece</li><li>utterance, statement</li><li>especially, ritual utterance, spell</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) language, manner of speech</li><li>opening, hole</li><li>bodily orifice, opening of the human body in general, including eyes, ears, nostrils, the vulva, and open wounds</li><li>entrance to a building or other enclosed structure, doorway</li><li>entrance to a land or place in general</li><li>mouth of a river</li><li>water’s edge, waterline</li><li>(<i>especially&#32;in compounds</i>) place or thing seen as an opening from one point to another, passage, thoroughfare</li><li>a measure of volume equivalent to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>32</sub></small> of a <i>hnw</i> or <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>320</sub></small> of a <i>ḥqꜣt</i> (about 15 millilitres); mouthful</li><li>part, piece, fraction</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) used as a numerator of 1 in fractions, literally “piece of (the denominator)”.</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>regarding, with respect to, concerning, according to</li><li>in order to, for (the purpose of)</li><li>(<i>with following infinitive</i>) forms the periphrastic prospective of a verb</li><li>(with verbs of motion,&#32;of places) to, towards</li><li>(in adverbial sentences,&#32;of places, roles, or functions) headed for, destined for, bound for</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) at, in, on</li><li>against, in opposition to</li><li>from, apart from (<i>ablative</i>)</li><li>(after an adjective or adverb,&#32;forming the comparative) than, by comparison to</li><li>(generally in sentence-initial form <i>jr</i>) introduces the protasis of a conditional sentence; if, when, as</li><li>(<i>with a verb in the terminative as object</i>) until</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with attached suffix pronoun</i>) so, regarding (that, you, me, etc.), as for (that, you, me, etc.)</li></ol><p>The actual reading of this word is uncertain, as it is always written as an ideogram, but evidence from Coptic suggests the original was <i>rꜣ</i>. The extension in meaning to ‘part, piece, fraction’ might be either by way of a mouthful being used as a standard share in some distribution of food or goods, or else extended from its meaning of ‘opening’ &gt; ‘division’.</p><p>Possibly cognate to Hebrew <i>אֶל</i> (ʾel, “to, at”), Arabic إِلَى (ʔilā, “to, until, near”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>beside</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>gs</i> (“side”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>cry conventionally used when conveying the dead to their final resting places&#58; to the west!</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> r,&lrm; jmntt.</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding, to, against”) +&lrm; <i>jmntt</i> (“west”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) very, exceedingly</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>jqr</i> (“what is excellent or exceedingly so”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between (+ <i>r</i> or <i>r jwd</i>: between … and …) &lsqb;New Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>among, with (someone or something)</li><li>entrusted to, as the concern of (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>jwd</i> (“to separate”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why?</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“concerning”) +&lrm; <i>mj</i> (“what”).</p><p>Perhaps also compare Hebrew <i>לָמָּה</i> (lāmmā, “why”), Aramaic <i>למא</i> (“why”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>until</li><li>as far as</li></ol><p><i>r</i> +&lrm; <i>mn</i> +&lrm; <i>m</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>according to the will of &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>r</i> (“regarding, according to”) +&lrm; <i>mḏdw</i> (“(that which is) being followed or struck”, passive participle of <i>mḏd</i>) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind, will”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) the Egyptian language &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>r(ꜣ)</i> (“mouth, speech”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>kmt</i> (“Egypt”). See also Coptic <i>ⲣⲉⲙⲛ̀ⲭⲏⲙⲓ</i> (remǹkhēmi).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>to wit, as follows, inasmuch as</li><li>used in letters to introduce a topic</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“concerning, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>ntt</i> (“that”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forever</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding, in”) +&lrm; <i>nḥḥ</i> (“(cyclical) eternity”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>spatial</i>) behind, following after</li><li>(<i>temporal</i>) after</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding”) +&lrm; <i>sꜣ</i> (“back”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>up to, as far as (of a place) &lsqb;since Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li><li>to, toward (a place) &lsqb;since Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li><li>until (of a time) &lsqb;since Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>r</i> +&lrm; <i>šꜣꜥ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in exchange (for)</li><li>instead (of)</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding”) +&lrm; <i>ḏbꜣ</i> (“to replace”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>periphrastic prospective of <i>ḏd</i></li><li>introduces a direct quotation; saying, with the words</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) introduces an object clause following verbs of speech and perception; that</li></ol><p><i>r</i> +&lrm; <i>ḏd</i> (infinitive of <i>ḏd</i> (“to say”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>ḏr</i> (“limit”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>just as (someone) wishes or pleases; according to the desire of &lsqb;Late Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>r ḏr</i> (“whole, entire; to the limit of”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind, will”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in front of, (spatially) before</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) in the time preceding, (temporally) before</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the front, forward</li><li>first, before anything else</li><li>previously, (temporally) before</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“concerning, regarding”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front, beginning”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>into</li><li>within, inside</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>inside</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding”) +&lrm; <i>ẖnw</i> (“interior”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with attached suffix pronoun</i>) at one’s pleasure, at will, as one wishes &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding, according to”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣbb</i> (active imperfective participle of <i>ꜣbj</i> (“to desire, to want, to be happy”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣw</i> (“length”), thus “to the length” or “to one’s length”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>beside, next to</li><li>up to</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greatly</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣt</i> (“what is great”), thus ‘with respect to what is great’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;medicine</i>) the digestive tract, seen as also connected with other organs and capable of influencing them medically</li></ol><p>From <i>r</i> (“mouth, opening, passage”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘the mouth of the heart’, or perhaps less literally ‘the passage of the interior’, with <i>jb</i> used as it is in <i>ḥr jb</i> (“in the midst of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jmjtw</i> (“between, amidst”) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>jmjtw</i> (“between, amidst”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>jmjtw-nj</i> (“between them, amidst them”)</li></ol><p>From <i>r</i> (“regarding, with respect to”) +&lrm; <i>jmjtw-nj</i> (“between them, amidst them”) or, equivalently, from <i>r-jmjtw</i> (“between, among”) +&lrm; <i>nj</i> (“thereto, for it”); in either case the basic components are the same.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) introduces direct speech after <i>ḏd</i></li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) introduces the content of a letter after introductory greeting formulae</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) introduces a new section of text or paragraph in a letter</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>r ntt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false door</li><li>temple</li></ol><p><i>r(ꜣ)</i> (“mouth”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>whichever</li><li>(<i>after a series of nouns</i>) expresses disjunction between the preceding nouns; or.</li></ol><p><i>r</i> +&lrm; <i>pw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the necropolis of Giza, particularly as the domain of Sokar</li><li>any given necropolis in general</li><li>the Duat (afterworld) or a region of it</li></ol><p><i>r</i> (“place of passage”) +&lrm; <i>sṯꜣw</i> (“pulling, towing”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘passage-place of towing’, perhaps in reference to the ramps or corridors along which the coffin was dragged into the tomb.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libu, a North African tribe &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the region west of Egypt; Libya &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot, leg</li><li>bud, shoot</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grow</li></ol><p>Compare Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⴰⴹⴰⵔ</i> (aḍar, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean, to be supported (by) or propped up [<i>with</i> <b>ḥr</b> <small>‘on’</small>]</li><li>to depend or rely [<i>with</i> <b>ḥr</b> <small>‘on’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time, age, era</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lachish (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>לָכִישׁ</i> (lāḵīš).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to light (a fire) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to light a fire &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of fire) to burn &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn (something) &lsqb;Late Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of fire) to heat (something) &lsqb;19th Dynasty medical texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Phamenoth</li></ol><p><i>rkḥ</i> (“fire, burning”) +&lrm; <i>nḏs</i> (“small”), thus literally ‘Small Burning’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Mecheir</li></ol><p>From <i>rkḥ</i> (“fire, burning”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great”), thus literally ‘Great Burning’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>perfective of <i>rmj</i> (“to cry, to weep”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cry, to weep &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly equivalent to <i>jrt</i> (“eye”) + <i>mw</i> (“water”). Compare Somali <i>illin</i> (“tear”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder, arm</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>often&#32;in the dual</i>) shoulder, upper arm &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the dual</i>) arm of a person in general, especially when in reference to moving it into a particular configuration</li><li>(<i>in the dual</i>) arms of a balance scale</li></ol><li>side, half</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>side of a place, person, mountain, etc.</li><li>one side or half of a body of water &lsqb;Papyrus Westcar&rsqb;</li><li>row of rowers along one side of a boat, one side of a rowing crew &lsqb;Papyrus Westcar&rsqb;</li><li>side of beef &lsqb;Papyrus Westcar&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the dual</i>) side rails (uprights) of a ladder</li></ol><li>a measure of length equivalent to 20 <i>ḏbꜥw</i> (“digits”), 5 <i>šzpw</i> (“palms”), or <small><sup>5</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>7</sub></small> of a <i>mḥ</i> (“cubit”) (about 37.6 centimetres)</li><li>a measure of length equivalent to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>2</sub></small> of a <i>ḫt</i> (“rod”) or 50 <i>mḥw</i> (“cubits”) (about 26.25 metres)</li><li>a measure of area equivalent to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>2</sub></small> of a <i>sṯꜣt</i> (“aroura”) or 50 <i>mḥw-tꜣ</i> (“centarouras”) (about 0.14 hectares)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) bearer, carrier, supporter (usually a priest), especially of a sacred object such as a cultic image or chapel &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the supports imagined to hold up the sky &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pillars, columns &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry, to bear up (a person or an object, especially a sacred object such as a cultic image, divine barque, chapel, or statue) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wear, to bear (a crown) &lsqb;Theban royal tombs and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to support, to hold up (the sky) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and New Kingdom magical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to match, to equal or outweigh &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring (something) (+ <i>r</i>: to (a place); + <i>n</i>: to (someone)) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry off, to carry away &lsqb;New Kingdom and 25th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i> or <i>ḥr</i>) to support oneself on &lsqb;chiefly Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebanon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic; compare Hebrew <i>לְבָנוֹן</i> (ləḇānôn).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to chastise, to beat (with a stick) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>&#42;rm</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of field</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ram-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) tears &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>rmj</i> (“to weep”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>human, human being, person</li><li>someone, anyone</li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) person in (someone’s) service, commissioner, agent (of someone)</li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) member (of a group or organization)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) people, humans</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the people, the masses</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;with following genitive) household; housemates or relatives (of someone)</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;with following genitive) population, dwellers, inhabitants (of a particular place)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Egyptians in contrast to Libyans, Nubians, Asiatics, etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>rmṯ</i> (“person, people”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) identity</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) reputation</li><li>young animal</li></ol><p>Of non-Afro-Asiatic origin. Cognate with Proto-Nilotic <i>&#42;ka-ʀin</i> (“name”) (with prefixal <i>*ka-</i>), Moro <i>irəŋ</i>, and Proto-Mumuye <i>&#42;ríǹg</i> (“name”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common name of a person, especially a nickname</li><li>good reputation</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> rn,&lrm; nfr.</li></ol><p><i>rn</i> (“name”) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p><i>rn</i> (“name”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“her”) +&lrm; <i>rs</i> (“to wake”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘her name is awake’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring up, to nurse</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Renenutet, a goddess of the harvest</li><li>the month of Pharmouthi</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man, youth</li></ol><p>Related to <i>rnpj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) young</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) fresh</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year of hardship, distress, need &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>rnpt</i> (“year”) +&lrm; <i>mḏd</i> (“driving in, striking true, staying true to”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”) in two direct genitive constructions, thus perhaps ‘year of the driving in of the arm’(?). The exact sense of <i>mḏd</i> intended here is unclear.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regnal year</li></ol><p>From <i>rnpt</i> (“year”) +&lrm; <i>ḥsb</i> (“counting”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘year of counting’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) fresh plants and fruits collectively &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>rnpj</i> (“to be fresh”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rafah (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>רָפִיחַ</i> (Rāp̲îaḥ).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn aside, to fend off (+ <i>r</i>: from)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to oppose or defy, to be hostile to</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to incline</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be oppositional, to be defiant &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>rqj</i> (“to fend off, to oppose”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) pig, boar</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, from the pig’s roaring or grunting.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) awake, to awaken</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>entirely</li><li>at all</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>southern</li></ol><p>From <i>rsw</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>south</li><li>south wind</li></ol><p>Allen considers this a nominalized <i>nisba</i> adjective formed from <i>rsw</i> (“south”) +&lrm; <i>-(j)</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending); however, no form <i>&#42;rswj</i> with the <i>nisba</i> ending written out is attested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) something insubstantial or trivial</li><li>(<i>only in set phrases in morning litanies</i>) awakening</li><li>south &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>rs</i> (“to be awake”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>, thus literally ‘awakening (in sleep)’.</p><p><i>rsj</i> (“southern”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bake; only used in <i>t-rtḥ</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baker &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baker</li></ol><p><i>rtḥ</i> (“to bake”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion</li></ol><p>Cognate to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔarway-</i> (“wild beast; lion”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Book of the Dead</li></ol><p><i>r(ꜣ)w</i> (plural of <i>r(ꜣ)</i> (“mouth, utterance”)) +&lrm; <i>nw</i> (masculine plural of <i>nj</i> (“of”)) +&lrm; <i>prt</i> (infinitive of <i>prj</i> (“to come forth”)) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in, during, from”) +&lrm; <i>hrw</i> (“day”). The literal meaning is thus ‘Utterances of Coming Forth in the Day’. This is traditionally interpreted to mean ‘coming forth by/into day’, but note that every other use of <i>m</i> with <i>prj</i> takes the meaning of ‘from, out of’ rather than ‘in’, suggesting the intended meaning may be ‘coming forth <i>from</i> the day’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stairway</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get away (+ <i>r</i>: from (a place or thing))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go away, to depart</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to leave, to depart (a place)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to escape (something bad)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) two thirds</li></ol><p>Dual form of <i>r(ꜣ)</i> (“part, piece, <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>…</sub></small>”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door, gate &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whole world, whole wide world &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>rww</i> (“regions, lands”, late plural variant of <i>jw</i> (“island”)) +&lrm; <i>ḏr</i> (“limit”) +&lrm; <i>jꜣḫw</i> (“sunlight”) in a series of direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘regions of the limit of sunlight’, i.e. ‘all lands so far as the sun shines’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) firm</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rudamun</li></ol><p>From <i>rwḏ</i> (“to be firm, to succeed”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”), thus ‘Amun is firm/Amun succeeds’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hard stone in general</li><li>sandstone</li></ol><p><i>rwḏ</i> (“to be firm”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>. As ‘sandstone’, a shortening of the full phrase <i>jnr ḥḏ nfr n rwḏt</i> (literally “fine white stone of hard rock”); sandstone, despite being relatively soft as far as rocks go, is often harder than limestone, the other principal stone used by the Egyptians for building.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The god Resheph</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Eblaite <i>𒀭𒊏𒊓𒀊</i> (ra-sa-ap). Cognate with Ugaritic <i>𐎗𐎌𐎔</i> (ršp), and Hebrew <i>רֶשֶׁף</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rejoice (+ <i>m</i> or <i>n</i>: to rejoice over; + <i>r</i> with following infinitive: to be happy to do)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>rš</i> or <i>ršw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rejoice</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy</li></ol><p><i>ršw</i> (“to rejoice”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give, to provide with</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to serve as a source of (water, dew, etc.), to provide (something) passively</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to extend (one’s hand or arm), to give (one’s hand) (+ <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to send forth, to emit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give forth (blood, air, one’s voice, etc.), to exude, to run with, to emit</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw or thrust (a weapon)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to send (a letter)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to issue, to promulgate (laws, decrees, prescriptions, commands)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put, to set, to place (+ <i>r</i>: in, on, at (a place); + <i>ḥr</i>: upon; + <i>m</i>: in)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to appoint (+ <i>m</i> or + <i>r</i>: as; + <i>r</i> or <i>ḥr</i>: to do (something))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set down in writing (+ <i>m</i> or <i>r</i>: in (a document))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set (fire) (+ <i>m</i> or <i>r</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to set oneself or lay oneself in some position, particularly prostrate on the ground</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn (one’s face, back, etc.) (+ <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>: to, toward)</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;often of the sun or gods) to reveal oneself</li><li>(catenative,&#32;with a verb in the subjunctive) to cause, make, let, allow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go or come &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>only used in <i>rḏj jb m sꜣ</i>, <i>rḏj jb n</i>, <i>rḏj jb ḫnt</i>, and <i>rḏj m jb</i></li></ol><p>From <i>rḏj</i> (“give, set”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble oneself with, to make an effort over, to take care about or be anxious about (doing something)</li><li>to trouble oneself over, to take care about or be anxious about (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>rḏj</i> (“to give, to set”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) +&lrm; <i>m sꜣ</i> (“behind, after”), literally “to set one’s heart/mind behind”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set one’s attention or desires on; to turn one’s heart toward</li></ol><p>From <i>rḏj</i> (“ give, set”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“to, for”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attend to, to give attention to</li></ol><p>From <i>rḏj</i> (“to give, to set”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) +&lrm; <i>ḫnt</i> (“at the head of”), literally “to set one’s heart/mind at the head of”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> rḏj,&lrm; jnj.</li></ol><p><i>rḏj</i> (“let”) +&lrm; <i>jnt</i> (subjunctive of <i>jnj</i> (“get”)) +&lrm; <i>.tw</i> (passive suffix). The final <i>-t</i> of <i>jnt</i> always coalesces with the initial <i>t-</i> of <i>.tw</i> and is left unwritten.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hit someone on (a body part, in particular the hand or forehead)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>rḏj</i> (“to give, to set”) +&lrm; <i>jp</i> (“whack<sup>?</sup>, accounting<sup>?</sup>”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“upon”). The second element is perhaps to be identified with <i>jp</i> (“accounting”); if not, it seemingly only appears in this phrase and never independently.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send (out)</li></ol><p><i>rḏj</i> (“let”) +&lrm; <i>jwt</i> (subjunctive of <i>jwj</i> (“come”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to (an idea or conclusion); to determine that</li><li>to bring (someone) to a notion, idea, or conclusion; to prompt</li></ol><p>From <i>rḏj</i> (“to give, to set”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), literally “to put in (one’s) mind”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to call one’s attention to</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to command</li></ol><p><i>rḏj</i> (“give, put”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”), thus literally ‘to put in (one’s) face’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay attention to</li></ol><p><i>rḏj</i> (“give”) +&lrm; <i>mꜣꜥ</i> (“temple”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“to”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give (someone) free travel, to let go</li></ol><p><i>rḏj</i> (“to give, to let”) +&lrm; <i>wꜣt</i> (“road”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“to, for”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show the way</li></ol><p><i>rḏj</i> (“to give, put, cause”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“on”) +&lrm; <i>wꜣt</i> (“road”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fluid, liquid</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual and plural only</i>) companions, comrades; used by workmates to address each other, and by the king to address his officials &lsqb;Old Kingdom, Middle Kingdom (literary), and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the pair of gods Horus and Set, rarely also used of other pairs of gods &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>The dual form of the defective noun <i>rḥw</i> (“companions, comrades”), thus literally ‘The Two Companions’, restricted in historical times to particular uses.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to learn, to come to know or experience</li><li>(transitive,&#32;in the stative or perfect) to know (something)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;in the stative or perfect) to be acquainted or familiar with (someone)</li><li>(catenative,&#32;in the stative or perfect,&#32;with a verb in the infinitive) to know how to, to be able to</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge</li><li>knowledgeable or wise man, scholar, often seen as living according to Maat (virtue/truth/cosmic order)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a court title: ‘acquaintance of the king’</li></ol><p><i>rḫ</i> (“know”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to butcher, to slaughter</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the common people, as opposed to the upper strata of society</li><li>royal subjects collectively</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) humanity</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Retjenu (Canaan and Syria)</li><li>the people of Retjenu</li></ol><p>Perhaps a loan word from a toponym cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔarṣ́-</i>. Compare Hebrew <i>ארצנו</i> (artsénu, “our land”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose</li><li>an item of baked goods shaped like a goose</li><li>incense in the shape of a goose</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tura</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in fixed expressions</i>) day (period of daylight) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) sun of one particular day &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Ra (literally “Sun”) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the king &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>only in the plural</i>) one of a number of minor ‘Ra-gods’ or sun gods &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>daily</li></ol><p><i>rꜥ</i> (“sun, day”) +&lrm; <i>nb</i> (“all, every”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ramose</li></ol><p><i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (“to give birth”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine third-person singular stative ending), thus ‘Ra is born’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ramesses, Ramses, or Rameses</li></ol><p>From <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (perfective active participle of <i>msj</i> (“to give birth to”)) +&lrm; <i>sw</i> (“him”), thus literally ‘Ra is the one who begot him’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Djedefre, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) +&lrm; <i>ḏd</i> (“stability, lastingness”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus ‘Ra is his lastingness’. Possibly, if the name is written with honorific transposition, it could also be <i>ḏd</i> (“endures”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘He endures, (namely) Ra’; however, both the Greek rendering of the name and the meaning support the former interpretation.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Ra-Horakhty, a syncretism of Ra and Horus</li></ol><p><i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr-ꜣḫtj</i> (“Horus of the Akhet”) in apposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rahotep</li></ol><p><i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Ra is content’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The object depicted in the hieroglyph <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A cloth</li></ol><li>A vessel or container (made of gold) &lsqb;18th dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>A type of waterfowl, perhaps the Eurasian teal (<i>Anas crecca</i>), especially as an offering for the dead &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>sr</i> (“type of goose”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>snb</i> (“health”), in the formula <i>ꜥnḫ wḏꜣ snb</i> (“life, prosperity, health”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sj</i> (“she, her”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>sw</i> (“he, him”) &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form a causative verb from a non-causative verb.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;s-</i> (causative prefix). Compare Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵙⵙ-</i> (ss-).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rebel, to revolt (+ <i>r</i> or + <i>ḥr</i>: against)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebel</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebellion, revolt</li></ol><p>From <i>sbj</i> (“to rebel”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sobek, the crocodile-god worshipped at Crocodilopolis and Kom Ombo</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sebekemhab</li></ol><p><i>sbk</i> (“Sobek”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣb</i> (“festival”), thus ‘Sobek is in festival’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sobekhotep</li><li>a female given name, Sobekhotep</li></ol><p><i>sbk</i> (“Sobek”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Sobek is content’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linen) bandage, especially as a wrapping for a mummy &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) a type of fish &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly derived from <i>zbn</i> (“to slide, to tumble”); if so, then presumably the (unattested) original form of <i>sbnw</i> was <i>&#42;zbnw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) wise</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) precious or splendid</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leg</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortification wall (of a city, temple district, etc.) &lsqb;Since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>temporarily raised wall to trap wildlife or close off a besieged city</li><li>fortress</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) wall of a room</li><li>Figuratively used of kings and gods as a ‘wall’ or ‘fortress’</li><li>compound (of household or temple)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cry out, to scream (in pain or lamentation) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of birds) to produce a birdcall, to cry, to call</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry, scream, shriek &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Arabic سَبَّحَ (sabbaḥa, “to praise, to glorify”) and Hebrew <i>שבח</i> (shibéakh, “to praise, to appreciate”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrier</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>star</li><li>meteor, falling star</li><li>gate, doorway</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to teach</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tire, to make weary</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>bꜣgj</i> (“to be weary”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to brighten</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>bꜣqj</i> (“to be bright”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupil &lsqb;Old & Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Related to <i>sbꜣyt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher, tutor &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>master of a craft</li><li>teaching, education &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>sbꜣ</i> (“to teach”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instruction, teaching &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>sbꜣ</i> (“to teach”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) clothed (+ <i>m</i>: in, with)</li><li>alternative form of <i>sḏ</i> (“to break, to force open”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tail</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringe (of a cloth)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) & (<i>intransitive</i>) to hide one's self from</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put makeup on (a part of the body, particularly the eyelids) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to apply medicinal salves to (a part of the body, particularly the eyelids) &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to apply medicinal salves &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) makeup, especially for the eyelids; eyepaint &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to attach, to make adhere</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>dmj</i> (“to touch”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to embalm</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who breaks (something, e.g. bones); breaker</li></ol><p><i>sd</i> (“to break”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> (agent noun-forming suffix) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian bittern, <i>Botaurus stellaris</i></li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tremble, to quake (+ <i>n</i> or <i>tp ꜥ</i>: before (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make tremble</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yesterday &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yesterday &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to devastate</li></ol><p>From an (unattested) reduplication of <i>fk</i> (“become desolate”) prefixed with <i>s-</i> (causative prefix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn to ashes &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ashes &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Apparently a development of earlier <i>ssf</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to loosen, to untie (ropes, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unlatch, to release</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take off (clothes, adornments, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to purify oneself of, to remove (evil)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>fḫ</i> (“to loosen, to give up”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventh</li></ol><p><i>sfḫ-</i>, root of <i>sfḫw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) offerings</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) shit</li></ol><p>Sometimes considered to be cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;šabʕ-</i>, from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;sṗɣ</i> (“seven”). However, the correspondences are problematic and have been challenged.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to loosen</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>fḫḫ</i> (“to loosen”), itself a reduplication of <i>fḫ</i> (“to loosen, to give up”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the seven sacred oils used in mortuary rituals, made from the resin of the <i>ꜥš</i>-tree &lsqb;since the Second Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the resin of the <i>ꜥš</i>-tree</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to soften</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>gnn</i> (“to be soft”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to silence</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiet, silence</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>gr</i> (“to be silent”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to descend, to bring down</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>hꜣj</i> (“to descend”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she, her (see usage notes)</li><li>she &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Cognate to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;šiʔa</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to quell, to render powerless</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>jdj</i> (“to be subjugated”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to keep back, to detain</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>jhm</i> (“to move slowly, to keep back”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fog &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>seal made of clay</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seal with clay</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hesitate, to wait (+ <i>n</i>: to wait for)</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>jnj</i> (“to delay”); however, the intransitivity of this verb speaks against it being a causative.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deliver, to give over (a person) (+ <i>n</i>: to (another person, a fate, life, execution, etc.))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to assign, allot, deliver, entrust (a thing) (+ <i>n</i>: to (a person))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to check, examine, inspect (a person or thing)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to visit, to come see, to check in on (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to manage, to organize (a land, a household, etc.)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to check up, to check and revise something</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspection, check</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>jp</i> (“to count, to assess, to assign”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixth</li></ol><p><i>sjs-</i>, root of <i>sjsw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;šidṯum</i>, Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵙⴹⵉⵙ</i> (sḍis), Hausa <i>shidà</i>, and Awngi <i>ሴዛ</i> (sedza, “four”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lighten (a load)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make easy</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>jzj</i> (“to be light”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to perceive, to recognize</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perception, particularly the ability to see what must be done</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prognostication, ability to determine the prognosis</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Sia, personification of perception</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;of measures of barley or fields) to cut short, to downsize (deceitfully)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of people) to mutilate</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>jꜣṯ</i> (“to be injured”); despite the derivation from <i>jꜣṯ</i>, it appears the expected form <i>&#42;sjꜣṯ</i> is unattested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wipe</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scythia, an ancient region of the eastern Iranian Plateau.</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎿𐎣</i> (s-k /⁠Saka⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to perish, to expire</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wipe out, to eliminate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to complete, to make whole</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spend (a period of time or lifespan) through to the end</li><li>(transitive,&#32;mathematics) to bring a sum up to a desired value by supplying a missing addend (+ <i>m</i>: in order to sum up to (the desired value))</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to end (a letter)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to finish, to put an end to, to destroy (enemies or their spirits, hearts, or limbs)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of hair) to be(come) grey</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) old and wise</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>km</i> (“to complete”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of crocodiles and people) to be(come) greedy or voracious (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to lust after) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make (someone) bow down</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ksj</i> (“to bow down”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to destroy</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>sk</i> (“to wipe out, to destroy”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troopship</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox for plowing</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to plow</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Predynastic Egyptian king Hsekiu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to respect (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to help or succour (someone)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of priest that played a role in funerary ritual, notably performing the opening of the mouth ceremony</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the half-month festival, a festival taking place on the fifteenth day of the lunar month</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to report (something to a superior)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to announce (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inform on, to lodge a complaint against (someone)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>ḥr</i>,&#32;rare) to report (something to a superior) &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a report</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to raise a complaint, to complain</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>report</li><li>accusation, complaint</li><li>acknowledgement or answer to a letter</li><li>statement, remark</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to establish, to fix, to set</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set up, to fix, to set firmly in place (a throne, a crown, a person, ornaments) (+ <i>n</i>: for (someone); + <i>ḥr</i>: on)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set up, to lastingly establish (something abstract: borders, laws, righteousness, festivals, someone’s rank, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to finish creating, to establish, to make fast (the sky or earth)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to erect, to establish, to build (a building, part of a building, monument, or stela) (+ <i>ḥr</i>, <i>m</i>, or <i>r</i>: at (a place))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reattach (severed body parts)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fortify (the heart/mind), to make firm, steadfast, unwavering, stouthearted, especially in battle</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make (one’s legs or feet) steadfast or firm of step</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tighten (a knot), to secure (a rope)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set (a person) upright, on one's feet (as opposed to lying down, inverted, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to appoint (a person) (+ <i>m</i>: to (an office))</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make endure, stay, remain (+ <i>ḥr</i>: upon (a place))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to immortalize in writing, to write down with the intention of preserving</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring (a land) into order</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to settle or fix oneself in place, to plant oneself (+ <i>m</i>: in (something); + <i>r ḥꜣt</i>: in front of (someone); + <i>r</i>: against (someone))</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;imperative) hold position, stand fast</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to stand still, to not move</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to hold position in battle (+ <i>r ḥꜣt</i>: in the face of; + <i>ḥr</i>: on (the battlefield))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to halt, to stop moving</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to stick, to stay, to remain fixed, to endure</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to dwell, to stay in a place</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to be found at a place</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of goose</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>mn</i> (“to be established, to remain”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to quake</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>mnmn</i> (“to move about”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make functional or effective</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>mnḫ</i> (“to be effective”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a court title: royal friend, courtier</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inflict pain on, to hurt</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>mr</i> (“to be ill or in pain, to suffer”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a court title: ‘sole companion’ of the king</li></ol><p><i>smr</i> (“royal friend, courtier”) +&lrm; <i>wꜥtj</i> (“single”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Semerkhet, a pharaoh of the First Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>smr</i> (“royal friend, courtier”) +&lrm; <i>ẖt</i> (“group of gods”), literally ‘companion of the group of gods (i.e. the Ennead)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to give birth</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to assist (a woman) in bearing a child, to deliver (a woman)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>msj</i> (“to give birth”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to praise &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eldest</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eldest one</li></ol><p><i>sms</i> (“to be old”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title: ‘elder of the domain’ &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>smsw</i> (“eldest”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house, estate”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘eldest of the house, eldest of the estate’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear, to examine</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammock? &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;šamaʕ-</i> (“to hear, to listen”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant (living organism)</li><li>plants collectively, vegetation</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>probably, perhaps, maybe; introduces an independent clause that is uncertain</li></ol><p>Possibly contracted from an adverbial sentence <i>sj</i> +&lrm; <i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>wn</i>, meaning ‘it is something that may be’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papyrus skiff</li><li>twig, branch</li><li>(<i>in compound words</i>) A bunch of dates (fruit)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) A verb relating to the building of wooden ships.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to ram or beat firmly</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) left (direction)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) left side</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to forget, to no longer know (something) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to forget, to no longer recognize (someone)</li><li>(<i>catenative</i>) to forget (to do something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to neglect, to overlook, to pay no heed to (someone or something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to not think about (something unpleasant: death, worries, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫm</i> (“to not know”), apparently with metathesis.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to slaughter, to kill, to slay</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild bull</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to renew</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>mꜣwj</i> (“to be(come) new”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to judge in favor of (someone) in court; to vindicate, to find justified (+ <i>r</i>: against)</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>mꜣꜥ-ḫrw</i> (“to be judged righteous, to be justified”). Equivalent to <i>smꜣꜥ</i> (“to set right”) +&lrm; <i>ḫrw</i> (“voice”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any closely related male family member, such as a cousin, uncle, or nephew</li><li>male lover or suitor</li><li>husband</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) a person of equal status who belongs to the same group or shares common characteristics; fellow</li><li>coworker, colleague</li><li>one of two opposing disputants in court, litigant</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>they, them (see usage notes)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to kiss</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smell</li></ol><p>Related to <i>snwj</i> (“two”). Compare with Migaama <i>sin</i>, Beja <i>san</i> and Blin <i>shan</i>.</p><p>Compare with Hausa <i>sansana</i> (“to smell”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Senenmut</li></ol><p><i>sn</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>mwt</i> (“mother”), thus literally ‘Mother’s brother’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to prostrate oneself, to kowtow</li></ol><p><i>sn</i> (“to kiss, to smell”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“land, ground”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) healthy &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heal, to make healthy &lsqb;since the Amarna Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>health &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>recovery from illness</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>snb</i> (“be healthy”, in the imperative) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“for”) +&lrm; <i>.j</i> (“me”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>farewell, goodbye, have a safe trip</li></ol><p><i>snb</i> (“to be(come) healthy”) +&lrm; <i>.tj</i> (second-person singular stative ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to converse</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Senbi</li></ol><p>From <i>snb</i> (“to be healthy”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Senedj, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>snḏ</i> (“fear”), thus ‘the frightful (one)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to let breathe or vent out</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>nfj</i> (“to blow, to breathe out”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beautify, to embellish</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to improve or complete</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“to be pleasing or beautiful”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Sneferu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>snfr</i> (“to make pleasing, fine, beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>wj</i> (“me”), thus ‘[he] has made me fine’; compare the longer form in the serekh name <i>nb-mꜣꜥt-snfr-wj</i> (literally “the possessor of righteousness has made me fine”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the two of them (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feed (someone or something), to provide with food</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feed (a person or animal) (+ <i>m</i>: with (a food)) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fatten, to force-feed (poultry) by gavage &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ‘feed’ (gods or the dead) by providing offerings of food; to sacrifice food to &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feed into (a brazier) (+ <i>m</i>: to supply a brazier with (frankincense)) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feed (food), to provide (food)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feed (a food) (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone)) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to offer (food) as a sacrifice (+ <i>ḥr</i>: upon (an altar)) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to supply (necessities) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to eat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to eat, to feed on (food) (+ <i>m</i>: to eat of; + <i>ḥr</i>: to eat from (an altar)) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of fire, worms, etc.) to consume (corpses) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people or the heart/mind) to be(come) sad, to mourn, to grieve &lsqb;18th Dynasty literature and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourning, grief, sorrow &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wnm</i> (“to eat”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of rank or rampant weed</li></ol><p><i>snm</i> +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>second</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, fellow</li><li>equal</li></ol><p><i>sn-</i>, root of <i>snwj</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to suckle &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Prefixed with <i>s-</i>. The root is sometimes hypothesized to be from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;nuuk</i>. Compare Akkadian <i>𒂊𒉈𒆪</i> (enēqu, “to suck”) and Hebrew <i>יָנַק</i> (“to suck”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fraternize with</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>sn</i> (“to kiss, to smell”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister</li><li>wife</li><li>flagpole</li><li>plinth</li></ol><p><i>sn</i> (“brother”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine ending”), related to <i>snwj</i> (“two”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two</li></ol><p>Suffixed with <i>-wj</i> (dual ending). The root <i>sn-</i> is likely the same root found in <i>sn</i> (“brother”). Compare it also with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ṯin-</i> and Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵙⵉⵏ</i> (sin).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of creeping plant</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to boil</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>nwḫ</i> (“to be scorched”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sharpen, to file</li></ol><p>From an (unattested) reduplication of <i>nšm</i> (“to cut”) prefixed with <i>s-</i> (causative prefix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) afraid (+ <i>n</i>, <i>ḥr</i>, <i>r</i>, or <i>m</i>: of; to fear)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to respect, to revere; to hold in respect, awe, or reverence</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be afraid (to do something)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>snḏw</i> (“fear”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make pleasant</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sweeten</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make oneself comfortable, to sit and rest</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>residence</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>nḏm</i> (“to be pleasant or sweet”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear</li><li>timid or fearful person</li></ol><p><i>snḏ</i> (“to fear”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie up, to bind (people, animals, or body parts) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to magically render (poison) harmless</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of a snake) to constrict, to coil around (someone or something)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause (a door-bolt) to draw back; to draw back (a door bolt)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>nḫbḫb</i> (“draw back (of a door-bolt)”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rear (a child)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>nḫn</i> (“to be a child”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation of a building or wall; underbuilding, substructure</li><li>foundation pit</li><li>floor plan</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) creation</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of people’s character) something firm on which one can rely</li></ol><p>From <i>snṯj</i> (“to found”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incense</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) sharp, to have a sharp point</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) pointy, to have a pointy shape</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sharpen &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people or body parts) to be(come) adept, skilful, effective</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of speech, ideas, laws, etc.) to be(come) effective</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effectiveness</li><li>triangle</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Sirius, A bluish-white star in the constellation Canis Major; Alpha (α) Canis Majoris. A vertex of the Winter Triangle and the brightest star in the night sky.</li><li>Sothis, the divine personification of Sirius</li></ol><p>From <i>spd</i> (“to be sharp”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Sopdu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to prepare</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie with rope, to bind</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to arrive (+ <i>r</i>: to arrive at, to get to (some place), to reach (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reach, to get to (some place or person)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to petition, to appeal (+ <i>n</i>: to; + <i>ḥr</i>: against or about (something))</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rib of a human or animal</li><li>ribs as food</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic سَفَر (safar)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to succeed, to accomplish a goal</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> spr,&lrm; r,&lrm; dmj.</li></ol><p><i>spr</i> (“arrive”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“to”) +&lrm; <i>dmj</i> (“harbor”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petitioner</li></ol><p><i>spr</i> (“to petition”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the dual</i>) lip</li><li>edge, shore</li><li>horizon</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>infinitive of <i>spj</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;śapat</i> (“lips”). Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;śapat-</i> and Iraqw <i>slufi</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lasso (animals or people)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;figuratively) to capture (someone) with one’s words</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nome, district</li><li>estate, farm</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cool (something), to make cool</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>qbb</i> (“to be cool”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A room of some kind found in luxurious houses. &lsqb;Old Kingdom to Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>cool room for storing food and drink</li><li>bathroom</li></ol></ol><p><i>sqbb</i> (“to make cool, to refresh”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> +&lrm; <i>-j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to refresh (the heart or the dead) (+ <i>m</i>: with (water or offerings)) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to give ease (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>qbḥ</i> (“to libate, to purify, to be cool”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>sqdj</i> (“to travel by boat”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to travel by boat, to sail</li><li>(transitive,&#32;by extension) to travel (in general)</li></ol><p>Prefixed with <i>s-</i> (causative prefix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a punch</li><li>a wound</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strike, to smite</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to work or beat metal</li></ol><p>Compare with Akkadian <i>𒄿𒊕𒄫</i> (i-šaq-qir /⁠šaqāru, išaqqir⁠/, “to pierce, to decimate”) and Arabic سَقَرَ (saqara, “to strike with hot heat, sunstroke, to burn up or scorch, to hurt or damage especially with heat”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise, to elevate</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>qꜣj</i> (“to be high”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to predict, to foretell</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>official, magistrate</li><li>hair of an animal or woman</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to grow</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>rd</i> (“to grow”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) warm</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to warm, to heat up</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to rest, to relax</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to bring to rest, to relax</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warmth, heat</li><li>temperature, level of heat or coldness of something</li><li>fever</li><li>passion, fervor</li><li>(<i>with a qualifier specifying the kind of mood</i>) mood in general</li></ol><p>Cognate to Hebrew <i>שָׂרָף</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make (someone) cry</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>rmj</i> (“to cry”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorn</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian goose (<i>Alopochen aegyptiaca</i>) &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remove</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>rwj</i> (“to depart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strengthen</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make endure, to perpetuate (offerings to the gods)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>rwḏ</i> (“to be firm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;medicine) to treat, to foster</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make known, to show or publicly reveal (someone or something) (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to denounce, to accuse (someone) (+ <i>n</i>: of, regarding; to fault for, to blame for)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notoriety, infamy, bad reputation (+ <i>ḫr</i>: before (someone)) &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms and 26th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>palace façade, particularly as a seat for the Horus-falcon and as an enclosure containing the serekh name &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) throne, seat of a king or god</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>rḫ</i> (“to know, to learn”). The noun is derived from the verb.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn to ashes &lsqb;since the Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ashes &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) horse, stallion</li></ol><p>A Northwest Semitic loanword. Compare with Ugaritic <i>𐎝𐎝𐎆</i> (s̀ūs̀awu).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A horse, <i>especially</i> a mare.</li></ol><p><i>ssm</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive,&#32;of a person or a nose) to breathe &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smell (a scent) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>sn</i> (“to smell, to kiss”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sharpen</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make ready, to prepare</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to supply (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>spd</i> (“to be sharp”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sate (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative ending) +&lrm; <i>sꜣj</i> (“to be(come) sated”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she, her, it, they, them; by Middle Egyptian often, but not exclusively, used for inanimate objects (see usage notes)</li><li>she, it, they &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne of the king or of a god seen as a king &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>seat of the dead in the heavens or in the sun-god’s barque</li><li>palace of the king</li><li>residence</li><li>household</li><li>administrative office</li><li>(<i>with a god’s name</i>) temple or home of a god in the sky or duat</li><li>grave</li><li>building</li><li>place, location</li><li>position or rank</li><li>rightful place or seat</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favorite place</li><li>preference, desire, wish</li><li>fondness, affection, favor</li><li>preferred or favored person, favorite</li></ol><p>From <i>st</i> (“seat, place, residence”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘residence of the heart/mind’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sun temple of Neferirkare Kakai</li></ol><p>From <i>st-jb</i> (“favorite place”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘favorite place of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the king &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>st</i> (“shooting”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“in order to”) +&lrm; <i>mḏd</i> (“hit the mark”), thus ‘(one) who shoots to hit’, i.e. ‘one who shoots unerringly’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activity, action</li><li>ability, efficaciousness</li></ol><p>From <i>st</i> (“seat, place”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “place of the arm”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to light (a lamp)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to illuminate, to light</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shoot (with an arrow)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pour (water)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to choose, to select</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;medicine) to gag</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the choice(st), the select; the best or most preferable one</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to protect [<i>with</i> <b>r</b> <i>or</i> <b>ḥr</b> <i>or</i> <b>ḥꜣ</b> <small>‘a person, or, more rarely, a temple’</small>] &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title used for a bodyguard of the king during his travels, literally protector &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>used as a component of various other titles&#59; see the Derived Terms section below &lsqb;Old Kingdom, Middle Kingdom, and 26th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the palace or court together with its inhabitants &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the palace as a building &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a temple or part of a temple in which a god is enthroned or in which a god’s image is set up &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) the judgement hall in the afterlife</li></ol><p><i>stp</i> +&lrm; <i>zꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a common epithet sometimes added to the throne names of pharaohs</li></ol><p>From <i>stp.n</i> (perfect relative form of <i>stp</i>) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘(he) whom Ra has chosen’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition. It is attested as 𒐼𒋼𒅁𒈾𒊑𒀀 (<i>šá-te-ep-na-re-a</i>) in Akkadian cuneiform documents dating to the reign of Ramesses II (<i>c.</i>&nbsp;1250 <small>B.C.E.</small>).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choice cut of meat</li></ol><p><i>stp</i> (“to select”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>she, her (see usage notes) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) secretions, boil</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>st</i> (feminine third-person singular dependent pronoun) + an element <i>-t</i> that forms older independent pronouns from dependent pronouns, although note that <i>stt</i> is attested some time before <i>st</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reconcile, to make similar or compatible</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) rays (of the sun)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>twt</i> (“to resemble, to befit”).</p><p>From <i>stj</i> (“to light”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Set</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>stš</i> (see alternative forms).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Setnakhte</li></ol><p>From <i>stẖ</i> (“Set”) +&lrm; <i>nḫt</i> (“to be strong, to be victorious”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Set is strong’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Seth-Peribsen, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>stẖ</i> (“Set”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“to come forth”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (third-person masculine singular stative ending) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, will”) +&lrm; <i>.sn</i> (“their”), thus literally ‘Set, their will has come forth’, possibly implying ‘Set, (for whom) their will has come forth’, but the exact meaning is unclear.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Seti</li></ol><p><i>stẖ</i> (“Set”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending), thus literally ‘Sethian’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to light (on fire), to set on fire, to ignite</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“to burn, to be hot”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, him (see usage notes)</li><li>he &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>used with an ordinal number, indicates a day of the month &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;su</i>. Cognate to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;šuʔa</i> and Central Atlas Tamazight <i>-ⴰⵙ</i> (-as).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to delay</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wdfj</i> (“to be(come) late, to delay”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to offer for sale</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to buy</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wn</i> (“to open”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>price &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>swn</i> (“to trade”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Aswan &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>swn</i> (“to trade, to buy”), <i>swnt</i> (“trade, price”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weary, to exhaust (someone) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wrḏ</i> (“to be(come) weary”). A spelling with <i>ḏ</i> is unattested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to broaden, to widen</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wsḫ</i> (“to be broad”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, him (see usage notes) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>but; indicates contrast of the current clause with the preceding one</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint of beef, particularly as an offering</li><li>sedge (plant)</li></ol><p>From <i>sw</i> (masculine third-person singular dependent pronoun) + an element <i>-t</i> that forms older independent pronouns from dependent pronouns.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bequeath</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wḏ</i> (“to command”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make sound or whole, to heal</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wḏꜣ</i> (“to be sound”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communication, message</li></ol><p><i>swḏꜣ</i> (“to make whole”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“mind”, literally “heart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egg &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>swḫꜣ</i> (“to spend the night”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to befool, to make a fool of (someone)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spend the night (+ <i>m</i>: in (a place))</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspeaking or ineffectiveness in speaking (usually of spells, magic, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wḫꜣ</i> (“to be foolish”).</p><p>Prefixed with <i>s-</i> (causative prefix); the stem is perhaps <i>wḫ</i> (“to be dark”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go past, to physically pass (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to pass by (a person, place, or afterworldly being); also, to pass through (a gate), to pass along (a path); + <i>r</i>: to)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;uncommon) to go past, to pass by (someone or something)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;uncommon) to overtake, to pass (someone moving in the same direction)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) to stop by at, to visit in passing, to drop in on (a tomb, sacrificial altar, statue, or other place to remember the dead)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come to an end, to pass</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;euphemistic) to die, to pass away</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) to pass over, to overlook, to take no heed of (people, laws, etc.)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>,&#32;of information) to escape (one’s) knowledge, to slip past (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep or drive (someone) away, to remove (someone) (+ <i>m</i>: from (a place)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive away (darkness or night) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Hebrew <i>שׁור</i> (“to journey”), Arabic سَارَ (sāra, “to depart, travel”).</p><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wꜣj</i> (“to be far”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make green</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to freshen</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wꜣḏ</i> (“to be green, to be fresh”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to clean, to purify</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>wꜥb</i> (“to be clean”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to mollify, to soothe, to agree well with</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>zf</i> (“to be(come) mild, merciful”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pass, to spread, to strew</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut (linen)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning bolt, thunderbolt &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>bandage for wrapping mummies and cultic images; mummy wrappings &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>medical bandage, bandage for wounds</li><li>strip of linen upon which words are to be written in hope of bringing about their efficacy, for example words of magic spells or New Year’s wishes</li><li>fillet, headband, typically with two feathers attached &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) window</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) particularly, the magnificent window of the royal palace where the king makes public appearances</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of stars, lightning, etc.) to flash &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to adorn with a fillet (+ <i>m</i>: to adorn with, to tie on (an adornment)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guidance, direction, conduction</li><li>written instruction specifying how something is to be done</li><li>assignment, task to be carried out</li><li>conduct, behavior</li><li>situation or state of affairs at a particular place</li><li>(<i>in calculations</i>) division, distribution &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a secret form in which gods manifest in the Amduat (in contrast to their proper bodies)</li><li>whetstone</li><li>butcher &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>sšmj</i> (“to lead, to guide”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead, to guide</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>šmj</i> (“to go”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heat, to warm</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>šmm</i> (“to become hot”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cultic image of a god and his sacred barque</li><li>image of a god in general</li><li>earthly visible form of a god (in contrast to the ba)</li></ol><p><i>sšmj</i> (“to lead, to guide”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) bright in color or illumination</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shine, to be(come) bright with radiance</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to illuminate, to brighten with light</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light, illumination</li><li>the brightness of dawn or day</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) melon(s), possibly muskmelon(s) or cucumber(s)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain, corn</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to coat, to overlay (+ <i>m</i>, <i>ḥr</i>: to coat with, to spread with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gild or gold plate (+ <i>m</i>: with (gold))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wet (one’s hand in a bowl of water)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of a hand) to stroke (along something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to milk, to express milk from (a cow)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to milk, to express (milk) from a cow</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw out (poison)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to suckle (a cow’s udder) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen cloth</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>štꜣw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deprive (someone) (+ <i>m</i>: of (something)) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to empty (a container) &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unload (a ship) &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unload (goods) (+ <i>m</i>: from (a ship)) &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to empty in the abstract sense, to make devoid or free (+ <i>m</i>: of (a quality, typically a negative one)) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lose (something) by theft &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>šwj</i> (“to be empty, devoid”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break into pieces &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to force open or cut open</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to defeat, to break the power of</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to eliminate or disperse (bad weather, disease, etc.)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to make traversable (places or paths)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of wounds) to break out</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harm, evil done</li><li>obstacle, impediment</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recount</li></ol><p>From <i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḏd</i> (“to say”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to supply (people, gods, or lands) with food, to feed (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to supply or endow (altars or temples) with offerings (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to supply, provide to, or endow in general (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḏfꜣ</i> (“to provision”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hear</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to obey, to heed</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to listen (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put on trial, to try</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;šamaʕ-</i> (“to hear, to listen”). Unrelated to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sh₂em-</i> (“to sing”) (Sanskrit <i>सामन्</i> (sāman)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a servant (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone), to serve (someone)) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant (+ genitive: to, of) &lsqb;18th Dynasty to Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>sḏm</i> (“to hear, to obey”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥš</i> (“call, summons”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) servant &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>sḏm ꜥš</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearer</li><li>judge</li></ol><p><i>sḏm</i> (“to hear”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lie down</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go to bed for the night, to go to sleep</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spend the night</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeper</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;uncountable) confinement to bed; bedrest, bedriddenness</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sexual intercourse</li></ol><p><i>sḏr</i> (“to lie down”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire, flame</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to slander</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḏwj</i> (“to be evil”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to transport</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cylinder seal, typically on a necklace</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of star &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to starve (someone), to inflict hunger on</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥqr</i> (“to be hungry”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to install as ruler</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥqꜣ</i> (“to rule”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive off, to drive away</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥrj</i> (“to be distant”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to destroy</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥtm</i> (“to be destroyed”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make content, to propitiate, to calm</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to brighten</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“to be white”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a bright discoloration as a symptom of disease or injury, including leucomas and burn wounds</li></ol><p><i>sḥḏ</i> (“to brighten”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make festive</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣbj</i> (“to celebrate a festival”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strip, to bare</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣj</i> (“to be bare”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to invert</li></ol><p>Prefixed with <i>s-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to invert</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>sḫd</i> (“to invert”), itself prefixed with <i>s-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically powerful or vigorous &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) powerful, capable (+ <i>m</i>: through the power of (magic, the Eye of Horus, food, etc.); + <i>mm</i>: among (the gods); + <i>ḫr</i>: before (a god); + <i>ḫnt</i>: at the head of (people); + <i>m ḫt</i> or <i>m sꜣ</i>: in pursuit of (enemies)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of poison, magic, names, etc.) to be(come) potent, effective</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a quality such as might) to be(come) strong, intense, great</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or (<i>since the 18th Dynasty</i>) <i>r</i>&#32;or&#32;(<i>rarely</i>)&#32;transitive) to have or take control of, to have power over (enemies, people, gods, places, oneself, etc.) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;(<i>rarely</i>)&#32;transitive) to have the use of, to be able to make use of (one’s limbs)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to take possession of (a thing)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>,&#32;of knives, lions, snakes, poison, magic, etc.) to have power to freely harm (someone), to have injurious power over &lsqb;chiefly Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>r</i>,&#32;of fire) to burn, to consume (someone) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of sleep) to overpower, to overtake (the body)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> and infinitive) to be(come) capable (of doing something), empowered or entitled (to do something)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to be(come) bold, daring, courageous, spirited</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to be(come) overbold, impudent, audacious</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the face)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be(come) defiant-looking or spirited</li><li>to be(come) grim</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, capability, especially capability to overpower &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>violence</li><li>being of divine power, Power; chiefly used as an epithet for various gods, exceptionally also for kings &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(with following genitive,&#32;or else plural) earthly image or incarnation (of a god), cult image, including statues, paintings, standards, people, etc. &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) gods in general &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>sekhem scepter &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>sistrum &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) sword &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Sekhemib-Perenmaat, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li><li>a Two Ladies name&#x20;notably borne by Sekhemib-Perenmaat, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p>If <i>pr.n</i> is the perfect form of <i>prj</i>, the name consists of <i>sḫm</i> (“powerful, (<i>of the heart/mind</i>) daring, audacious”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) +&lrm; <i>pr.n</i> (“has gone forth”) +&lrm; <i>mꜣꜥt</i> (“Maat”), thus ‘(the one) daring of heart (for whom) Maat has come forth’. However, <i>pr.n</i> can also be interpreted as a participle and a preposition, <i>pr n</i> (“going forth for”), in which case the name would mean ‘(the one) daring of heart going forth for Maat’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potentate, very powerful person or ruler, especially one of the feudal lords of the Middle Kingdom &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, powerful”) +&lrm; <i>jr</i> (“do, act”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (third-person masculine singular suffix pronoun); the exact interpretation is not altogether clear. Taking <i>sḫm</i> as a noun and <i>jr.f</i> as a modifier, a meaning such as ‘acting power’ is sometimes suggested; this, however, fails to explain the suffix pronoun, which rather suggests an interpretation of <i>jr</i> as a relative form instead of a participle, in which case the sense would be something like ‘the power that he makes’. Alternatively, some have taken <i>sḫm</i> as a participle and <i>jr</i> as a writing of the noun <i>jrw</i> (“visible form”) for an overall sense of ‘powerful of visible form’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard crowned with a sekhem scepter and a cult image of Anubis</li><li>cult image or incarnation of Anubis in general</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, cult image”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>jnpw</i> (“Anubis”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for the king</li><li>epithet for various gods</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, cult image, incarnation”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various gods</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, cult image, incarnation”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>sḫmw</i> (plural of <i>sḫm</i>).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various gods</li><li>standard-like object in the form of a sekhem scepter with two eyes painted on the upper section</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, cult image, incarnation”) +&lrm; <i>nṯrw</i> (plural of <i>nṯr</i> (“god”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the four rudders of heaven</li><li>epithet for various gods</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, cult image, incarnation”) +&lrm; <i>pt</i> (“sky”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Power of the sky’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gold name&#x20;notably borne by Tuthmosis III, a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“powerful”) +&lrm; <i>pḥtj</i> (“strength”) +&lrm; <i>ḏsr</i> (“sacred”) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜥw</i> (“appearances”), thus literally ‘Powerful of Strength, Sacred of Appearances’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various gods, especially Wepwawet</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, cult image, incarnation”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣwj</i> (“the Two Lands, Egypt”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Power of the Two Lands’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Sekhemkhet, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“powerful”) +&lrm; <i>ẖt</i> (“torso”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘(the one who is) powerful of torso’. Alternatively, but perhaps less likely, if <i>ẖt</i> is to be interpreted with its other meaning of ‘group of gods’, the intended sense may be ‘powerful one of the group of gods (i.e. the Ennead)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet used as part of the name of various Ptolemaic pharaohs</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“power, cult image, incarnation”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“living”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”), thus ‘living image of Amun’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Sekhmet, personification of overmastering power &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) power, capability, especially capability to overpower &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) fertility, capacity for procreation<sup>?</sup></li><li>epithet for fire &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>sḫm</i> (“to be powerful, to have power over”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resting place (for the shades of the dead) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>sḫnj</i> (“to alight, to bring or come to rest”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>perfective of <i>sḫntj</i> (“to promote, to advance”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to promote, to advance, to cause to be in front</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to send upstream</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫntj</i> (“to go forward or upstream”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫpj</i> (“travel, walk”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring about</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫpr</i> (“to come into being, to become”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plan</li><li>idea, piece of advice</li><li>conduct</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fell, to cast down</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lower one’s voice</li><li>alternative form of <i>sšr</i> (“to coat, to stroke, to milk”)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫr</i> (“to fall”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captain of a ship</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>sḫr</i> (“plan”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field, marshland</li><li>open country, countryside</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trap, to snare</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to close (a net)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weave</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the oases found west of the Nile delta collectively, sometimes in particular the Siwa Oasis</li></ol><p>From <i>sḫt</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣw</i> (“a kind of tree”, in the plural) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “field of <i>ima</i>-trees”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a region of the afterworld that serves as the home of those judged righteous after death</li></ol><p><i>sḫt</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>jꜣrw</i> (“rushes, reeds”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “field of reeds”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Wadi El Natrun</li></ol><p><i>sḫt</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>ḥmꜣt</i> (“salt”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Field of Salt’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer, peasant</li><li>weaver</li></ol><p><i>sḫt</i> (“field”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i>.</p><p><i>sḫt</i> (“to weave, to trap”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breadth, width &lsqb;Old Kingdom to Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>wsḫ</i> (“to be(come) broad, breadth”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (noun-forming suffix). For the loss of initial <i>w-</i> compare <i>ꜥbw</i> from <i>wꜥb</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recall, to remember</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring to mind</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mention</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜣj</i> (“to measure, to examine”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remembrance, memory</li></ol><p><i>sḫꜣj</i> (“to remember”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make appear</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜥj</i> (“to appear”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smell, scent, odor (pleasant or unpleasant) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the seven sacred oils used in mortuary rituals, made from a mixture of various seeds, flowers, and frankincense &lsqb;end of the Third Dynasty to Ptolemaic Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>sṯ</i> (“scent”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣb</i> (“festival”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘scent of the festival’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satis, a war, hunting, and fertility goddess</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to distinguish, to elevate, to crown</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ṯnj</i> (“to elevate, to be distinguished”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the lands northeast of Egypt; roughly ‘Asia’</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise, to lift up</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ṯzj</i> (“to raise, lift up”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) clouds &lsqb;since the Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Likely shortened from <i>sṯzw-šw</i> (“the raisings/props of Shu”), from <i>sṯzj</i> (“to raise, lift up”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pull</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring or usher (someone) in</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weave</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spin (yarn)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an aroura, a measure of area equivalent to 100 <i>mḥw-tꜣ</i> (“centarouras”) or 10 000 square <i>mḥw</i> (“cubits”) (about 0.28 hectares).</li><li>flat plane</li><li>roller for portage<sup>?</sup></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to decorate, adorn</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ẖkr</i> (“to be(come) adorned of decorated”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sšr</i> (“to coat, to stroke, to milk”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of cereal &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to 26th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hare, particularly the desert hare &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) back</li><li>the back (of something)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dappled cow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Unknown. Thomas Schneider proposed an origin from a Semitic form *<i>šadī-ʿammī</i>, glossed as “my uncle is my rock” or “ʿAmmu is my rock”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sk</i> (“Scythia”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inferior kind of cloth, most likely sackcloth &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) sated</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shechem, Sichem (an ancient Canaanite city in Samaria, near modern Nablus, Palestine)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>שְׁכֶם</i> (šəḵem).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Babylonia (an ancient empire and geographic region of Mesopotamia, existing from 1850 <small>BCE</small> to 539 <small>BCE</small>, based around the city of Babylon; at its maximum extent, covering parts of modern-day Iraq, Kuwait, Syria and Iran)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gather or put together &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of a land) to keep in order or under control &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fold one’s arms &lsqb;19th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;medicine) to contract (one’s pupils or uterus) by means of medicine</li><li>(transitive,&#32;medicine) to hold in one’s urine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic, ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ṯalg-</i>; compare Hebrew <i>שֶׁלֶג</i> (šéleḡ), Akkadian <i>𒊭𒆷𒄖</i> (šalgu).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to attack headlong</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive back</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sokho (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>שֹׂכֹה</i> (śōḵōh).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toe</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set foot on, to arrive at</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Orion (constellation)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an unknown-gender given name of historical usage, notably borne by Sahure, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Leprohon interprets the name as <i>sꜣḥ</i> (“to endow”) +&lrm; <i>wj</i> (“me”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘Ra has endowed me’; others have interpreted the first part as a single passive participle, with a reading along the lines of <i>sꜣḥw</i> (“arrived at, reached”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘(he who is) reached by Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make useful or effective</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜣḫ</i> (“to be useful or effective”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make green or verdant</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜣḫꜣḫ</i> (“to be verdant”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) wise</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wise man</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to saw out</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to castrate (cattle)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to circumcise (slave)</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥb</i> (“to be dirty”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beautify</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnj</i> (“to be beautiful”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make live, to vivify, to give life or keep alive</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feed, to nourish</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“to live”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring in or send in</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥq</i> (“to enter”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make ascend, to raise up</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>jꜥr</i> (“to ascend”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wreck</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to multiply, to make numerous</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥšꜣ</i> (“to be numerous”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ennoble, to distinguish</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wrap (the image of a god) in bandages</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mummify</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;usually as part of a causative after <i>rḏj</i>) to be(come) distinguished or noble</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distinction, rank, dignity</li><li>dignitary, privileged or distinguished person</li><li>mummy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set up, to install, to erect</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥḥꜥ</i> (“to stand”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enlarge</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make great or important</li></ol><p><i>s-</i> (causative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣj</i> (“to be great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toast, toasted bread</li></ol><p><i>t</i> (“bread”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣšr</i> (“roasted”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following noun) she of, this of, that of</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>tꜣ</i> (“the, this”) +&lrm; <i>nt</i> (“of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bread</li></ol><p><i>t</i> (“bread”) +&lrm; <i>rtḥ</i> (“baked”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white bread</li></ol><p><i>t</i> (“bread”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>that</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spit (+ <i>m</i>: to spit (something) out) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts, rarely later&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spittle &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;tuf-</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orphan</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tfnwt</i> (“Tefnut”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Tefnut &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tf</i> (“that”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transgressor, wrongdoer, one who violates one’s social obligations</li><li>(<i>with following genitive</i>) one who is disloyal, rebellious, insubordinate against (someone), one who violates one’s social obligations to (another)</li></ol><p><i>th</i> (“(one) transgressing, violating, deviating from”) +&lrm; <i>mtn</i> (“way, road, path”), thus literally ‘one deviating from the path’. This term is not attested with the older writing of <i>mtn</i>, namely <i>mṯn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to transgress, to do wrong</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to wrong, to abuse, to lay hands on, to violate (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to defeat (the enemy, evil ones, etc.) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to commit wrong against (a god), to sin</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to damage, to harm (an object, building, or animal)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to mutilate (limbs, body parts, corpses)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to invade, to trespass into, to attack (a land or settlement)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to transgress, to violate, to trespass over (a boundary) &lsqb;19th and 20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to falsify (weights and measures)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stray from or defy one’s orders or what has been said, to be heedless or insubordinate</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to deviate from, to disobey, to stray from (a path, a plan, one’s orders, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fail to keep (one’s appointed time), to neglect, to miss (a set time)</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic تَاهَ (tāha, “to wander, lose one's way”) and possibly Hebrew <i>תֹּהוּ</i> (tóhu, “desolation, nothingness”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perforation</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transgressor</li><li>opponent, adversary (of someone) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>thj</i> (“to transgress”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) this</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>converts a sentence with adverbial or verbal predicate into a dependent adverbial (circumstantial) clause&#59; yea, while, when, although</li></ol><p>Loprieno considers <i>tj</i> a development of <i>(j)sṯ</i>, <i>stj</i> (“while”), from Old Egyptian <i>sk</i>. Allen instead sees it as related to <i>tjw</i> (“yes”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Takelot</li></ol><p>Of Libyan origin, presumably from a predecessor or extinct sister language of Proto-Berber.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ancient capital of various polities, most notably Aram-Damascus, existing from the 12th to 8th centuries <small>BCE</small> in modern Syria</li></ol><p>A borrowing of unknown origin, perhaps from a Semitic language. Probably cognate with the word Damascus</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff (of wood)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image, figure, picture</li><li>hieroglyph, sign, character, letter, grapheme</li><li>form, shape</li><li>portrayal, representation</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trample on (a foe) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fight</li><li>to enter (+ <i>m</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>yes</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Predynastic Egyptian king Tiu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Tiaa</li></ol><p>Possibly to be interpreted as a variant of <i>tꜣ</i> (“the”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ(t)</i> (“great (one)”), thus literally ‘the great’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to violate, to attack</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to approach</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn, to light, to illuminate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>candle, taper, torch</li></ol><p><i>tkꜣ</i> (“to illumine, to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be complete</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to complete, to finish</li><li>(catenative,&#32;with a verb in the negatival complement) to not do, to not be</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>everything, totality, completion</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Atum</li></ol><p>Compare with Hebrew <i>תַּם</i> (tam, “to be complete, finished”), Arabic تمام (tamām, “complete; completeness”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to close</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sled(ge)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat (in general) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>this, that</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) old</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go astray, to be(come) lost &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ṯnm</i> (“beer”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Tenemu, personification of the ‘lostness’ of the primeval waters</li></ol><p>From <i>tnm</i> (“to go astray, be lost”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ṯnj</i> (“where?”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ṯnw</i> (“number”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spur-winged lapwing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li><li>individual person, (if possessed) servant</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on top of, atop</li><li>above</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(spatially) before, in front of &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>‘upon the shoulder’, said of the place where a crown rests &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>tp</i> (“atop”) +&lrm; <i>rmn</i> (“shoulder, arm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(temporally) before</li><li>(spatially) before, in front of</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forebear, ancestor</li></ol><p><i>tp</i> (“atop”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>‘New Year’s Day’: a festival day at the start of the Egyptian year, the first day of the first month of <i>ꜣḫt</i>, originally aligned to the heliacal rising of Sirius in summer</li></ol><p><i>tpj</i> (“first”) +&lrm; <i>rnpt</i> (“year”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘first of the year’. The form with <i>tpj</i> written out in full (as opposed to written as <i>tp</i>) is unattested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being on top of, standing atop</li><li>(Old Egyptian,&#32;Middle Egyptian) first</li><li>chief, head, main</li><li>best</li></ol><p>From <i>tp</i> (“atop”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of Anubis</li></ol><p><i>tpj</i> (“(one) standing atop”) +&lrm; <i>ḏw</i> (“mountain”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus literally ‘he who stands atop his mountain’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cumin (<i>Cuminum cyminum</i>)</li><li>(<i>spice</i>) cumin</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe (air) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine oil</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the feminine of <i>tpj</i> (“first”), thus from <i>tp</i> (“atop”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine <i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point or span of time, particular time (+ genitive: time of (a season, a part of the day, a date, a festival, an event, etc.))</li><li>duration of time (+ genitive: lasting, consisting of (some measure)) &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>proper time for something</li><li>time when something will reach fulfilment, particularly of the end of pregnancy or death</li><li>season, time of year</li><li>time when someone is alive, lifetime (of people, gods, ancestors) &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the dual</i>) day and night</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) divisions of time</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Interrogative particle&#59; indicates that the phrase is a question</li><li>really?, actually?</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) Used to emphasize a preceding adjective with the admirative suffix <i>-wj</i></li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>twr</i> (“to respect”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Teti, a pharaoh of the Sixth Dynasty</li><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Hor-Aha and Sekhemkhet, pharaohs of the First and Third Dynasty</li><li>a female given name, Teti</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) this</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) used as the impersonal subject of an adverbial predicate or verb form; one, someone or something unspecified</li><li>used as a substitute for noun phrases referring to the king &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative spelling of <i>ṯw</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>tj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Loprieno suggests an origin from <i>tj</i> (“while”) +&lrm; <i>wj</i> (first-person pronoun), with <i>tj</i> from earlier <i>(j)sṯ</i>, from Old Egyptian <i>(j)sk</i>. Stauder argues against this hypothesis and instead proposes a development from <i>ntt</i> (complementizer) +&lrm; <i>wj</i> (first-person pronoun) with subsequent reanalysis. Regardless of origin, later reinterpreted as an element <i>tw</i> + the suffix pronoun <i>.j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Loprieno suggests an origin from <i>tj</i> (“while”) +&lrm; <i>ṯw</i> (second-person masculine dependent pronoun), with <i>tj</i> from earlier <i>(j)sṯ</i>, from Old Egyptian <i>(j)sk</i>. Stauder argues against this hypothesis and instead proposes a development from <i>ntt</i> (complementizer) +&lrm; <i>.k</i> (second-person masculine suffix pronoun) with subsequent reanalysis. Regardless of origin, later reinterpreted as an element <i>tw</i> + the suffix pronoun <i>.k</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>we &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>In origin from <i>tj</i> (“while”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (first-person plural dependent pronoun), with <i>tj</i> from earlier <i>(j)sṯ</i>, from Old Egyptian <i>(j)sk</i>. Later reinterpreted as an element <i>tw</i> + the suffix pronoun <i>.n</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Loprieno suggests an origin from <i>tj</i> (“while”) +&lrm; <i>ṯn</i> (second-person feminine dependent pronoun), with <i>tj</i> from earlier <i>(j)sṯ</i>, from Old Egyptian <i>(j)sk</i>. Stauder argues against this hypothesis and instead proposes a development from <i>ntt</i> (complementizer) +&lrm; <i>.t</i> (second-person feminine suffix pronoun) with subsequent reanalysis. Regardless of origin, later reinterpreted as an element <i>tw</i> + the suffix pronoun <i>.t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (plural) &lsqb;since the 17th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>In origin from <i>tj</i> (“while”) +&lrm; <i>ṯn</i> (second-person plural dependent pronoun), with <i>tj</i> from earlier <i>(j)sṯ</i>, from Old Egyptian <i>(j)sk</i>. Later reinterpreted as an element <i>tw</i> + the suffix pronoun <i>.tn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>used as the impersonal subject of any adverbial predicate; one, someone or something unspecified &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>In origin from <i>tj</i> (“while”) +&lrm; <i>tw</i> or <i>.tw</i> (impersonal pronoun), with <i>tj</i> from earlier <i>(j)sṯ</i>, from Old Egyptian <i>(j)sk</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dor (an ancient Canaanite coastal city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>דֹּאר</i> (dōʾr).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to show respect to (people or statues of the dead), to treat reverently &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to act respectful, to show respect (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to worship, to honor (a god)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of a priest or son of the dead) to honor (the dead)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of the king) to honor, to magnify, to bestow honors on (one’s officials)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hail, to greet respectfully</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to keep one’s distance from or respect (someone’s possessions), to not violate (someone’s property) &lsqb;21st Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn away, to reject (someone unwanted as a companion, something unwanted to drink, etc.) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and archaizing texts of the Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make (something sacred) ritually clean with water, natron, or incense; to cleanse, to purify (+ <i>r</i>: of (evil, pollution, etc.)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) ritually pure or clean &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the sky) to be(come) clear (+ <i>r</i>: of (clouds))</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ritual purity or cleanliness &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>cleaner-priest &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>epithet of the king or the god Horus &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of plant, possibly a kind of reed, used medicinally and said to be given to the dead in the afterworld</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a body of water in the afterworld &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likeness, image</li><li>statue, especially of a man or a god</li><li>drawn or painted picture</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) form, shape</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>, <i>r</i>, or rarely <i>ḥr</i>) to resemble, to be like or look like</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>, <i>r</i>, or rarely <i>ḥr</i>) to nearly equal or match up to, to approach (in the figurative sense)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to befit, to be fitting or proper to</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to resemble</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name meaning “the Living Image of Amun”, of historical usage, equivalent to English Tutankhamon, notably borne by Tutankhamon, a pharaoh of the 18th Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>twt</i> (“image”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“living”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”), with <i>twt</i> and <i>jmn</i> in a direct genitive construction. The compound is based on the pharaoh’s earlier name, <i>twt-ꜥnḫ-jtn</i> (literally “living image of the Aten”). A longer form of <i>twt-ꜥnḫ-jmn</i> is found as <i>twt-ꜥnḫ-jmn-ḥqꜣ-jwnw-šmꜥw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, equivalent to English Tutankhamon-Heqaiunushemau, notably borne by Tutankhamon-Heqaiunushemau, a pharaoh of the 18th Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>twt</i> (“image”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“living”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) +&lrm; <i>ḥqꜣ</i> (“ruler”) +&lrm; <i>jwnw-šmꜥw</i> (“Thebes”, literally “Heliopolis of Upper Egypt”), thus literally meaning ‘The Living Image of Amun, Ruler of the Heliopolis of Upper Egypt [i.e. Thebes]’. The compound is based on the pharaoh’s earlier name, <i>twt-ꜥnḫ-jtn</i> (literally “living image of the Aten”). A shorter form of <i>twt-ꜥnḫ-jmn-ḥqꜣ-jwnw-šmꜥw</i> is found as <i>twt-ꜥnḫ-jmn</i> (“Tutankhamon”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, equivalent to English Tutankhaten, notably borne by Tutankhaten (later Tutankhamon), a pharaoh of the 18th Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>twt</i> (“image”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“living”) +&lrm; <i>jtn</i> (“Aten”), with <i>twt</i> and <i>jtn</i> in a direct genitive construction. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) this is…</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) O (vocative reference)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>tw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to physically support, to hold up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to support, to help or sustain (the poor)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift up, to raise (+ <i>r</i>: to (a place))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be supported (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to be supported by, to lean on (a person or scepter))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beseech, to appeal to (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to plead about or raise (a claim), to appeal regarding</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the seven sacred oils used in mortuary rituals, made of wood pitch, frankincense, and pine &lsqb;since the Fifth Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>twꜣ</i> (“to beseech, to appeal to, to support”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Latin, of historical usage, notably borne by the Roman emperor Tiberius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tiberius</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Latin, of historical usage, equivalent to English Titus, notably borne by Titus, a Roman emperor who ruled from 79 to 81 <small>CE</small></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Titus</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smash (heads), to split (wood) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get drunk &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Thoth</li></ol><p>A nominalized <i>nisba</i> adjective, formed from <i>tḫ</i> (“plumb bob”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”), thus literally ‘(the one) of the plumb bob’, an epithet of Thoth.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obelisk &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to confuse, to confound, to disorder (words, thoughts, reckoning of time, enemies, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to disorder, to mess up (hair)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to crumple (papers) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confusion, disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunkard &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>epithet of two cleaner-priests (<i>wꜥb</i>) at the Min-festival &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>tḫj</i> (“to get drunk”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land, realm, country</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) the collective people of a land or country</li><li>ground</li><li>dry land, ground not covered by water</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old and Late Egyptian</i>) O (vocative reference)</li><li>(<i>Middle Egyptian</i>) the aforementioned, the said</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) the definite article; the</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with following noun) she of, this of, that of</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egypt</li></ol><p>Traditionally read and analyzed as <i>tꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>mrj</i>, thus literally ‘beloved land’. However, in part due to the unusual writing of the latter word, alternative readings have recently been proposed, such as James P. Allen’s suggestion of <i>tꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>mr</i>, literally ‘land of the hoe’, later revised to ‘canal-land’, or Timofey Shmakov’s <i>tꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>m</i> +&lrm; <i>rnpwt</i>, literally ‘land in flowering’ (i.e. ‘Flowery/Blossomy Land’). In these cases, the <i>j</i> in the word would not mark a separate consonant but rather a sound change of <i>r</i> to <i>j</i>, as is otherwise common in words such as <i>zwr</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lower Egypt</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>mḥw</i> (“Lower Egypt”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Tentamun</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (feminine relational pronoun) +&lrm; <i>nt</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”), thus literally ‘she of Amun’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative name for the Land of Punt</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘god’s land’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>all of humankind, everyone</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>tmw</i> (“complete”), a form of <i>tm</i> (“to be complete”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ta-wer, the eighth nome of Upper Egypt, containing the cities of Thinis and Abydos</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”), thus literally ‘The Great Land’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a region near Edom inhabited by a tribe of Shasu (Semitic nomads)</li></ol><p>From <i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>šꜣsw</i> (“Shasu”) +&lrm; <i>yhwꜣ</i>, with the first two elements in a direct genitive construction and the last in apposition, thus literally ‘<i>yhwꜣ</i>, the land of the Shasu’. <i>yhwꜣ</i> is apparently a toponym; some have hypothesized the toponym to be derived from a theonym, which would make it one of the earliest known references to Yahweh.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necropolis</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>ḏsr</i> (“sacred”), thus ‘sacred land’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Tatenen, personification of the primeval mound, the first land that arose from the primeval waters at the creation of the world</li></ol><p>From <i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>ṯnn</i> (or possibly <i>ṯnnj</i> abbreviated), an inflection of <i>ṯnj</i> (“to lift up, to be distinguished”), thus literally meaning “land that is raised” or “land that becomes distinct”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a distant land described as a source of dwarfs</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣḫtj</i> (“Akhet-dweller”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural suffix) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘land of the Akhet-dwellers’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Tybi</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣbt</i>, literally ‘The Offering’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tefnakht</li></ol><p><i>tꜣy</i> (possessive determiner) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”) +&lrm; <i>nḫt</i> (“strength”), thus literally ‘his strength’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Taharqa</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) this, that</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tantamani or Tanutamun</li></ol><p>The <i>Lexikon der Ägyptologie</i> notes that this name is evidently Cushitic. Leprohon, however, considers it a variant of the Egyptian name <i>tꜣ-nt-jmn</i> (literally “She of Amun”), which is otherwise only attested as a feminine name. In any case, the second element is certainly <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”). The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) Upper and Lower Egypt, considered as a single entity: Egypt.</li></ol><p><i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) +&lrm; <i>-wj</i> (dual suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;with attached suffix pronoun) forms possessives; my, our, your, his, her, its, their, depending on which suffix pronoun is attached</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) not; used to negate the subjunctive or prospective in wishes and commands</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>area, district</li><li>administrative district</li><li>nome &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wj</i> (“I, me”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn, to roast</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shine</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the sun or inundation) to rise</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the east, land of the sunrise</li></ol><p><i>wbn</i> (“to rise (of the sun)”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound, injury, especially if forcibly inflicted</li></ol><p><i>wbn</i> (“to rise”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) bright, to brighten</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brightness</li></ol><p><i>wbḫ</i> (“to become bright, to brighten”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butler, cupbearer</li><li>a priestly title &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to confide [<i>with</i> <b>n</b> <small>‘in someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>wbꜣ</i> (“to open”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) butler or cupbearer; maidservant</li></ol><p><i>wbꜣ</i> (“butler, cupbearer”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) late, to delay (+ <i>m</i>: to delay in (doing something))</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put, to set</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to push</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dismiss</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set down as an offering</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to make an offering to</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dedicate (a building) (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to install as a king or god, to enthrone (+ <i>ḥr</i>: on (a throne))</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>nḫbt</i> as object) to set down (the royal titulary) in writing</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cast down, to overthrow (enemies)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) heavy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butler, waiter</li></ol><p>Cognate with Arabic وَصِيف (waṣīf, “young man, servant”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering-table</li></ol><p><i>wdḥ</i> (“to pour out, to libate, to cast”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of snake &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lung</li><li>conversation, talk</li><li>subject or theme of discussion</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to discuss, to talk about</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to support, to agree with (someone’s plea)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to reproach, to speak ill of (someone)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feebleness, weakness</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to escape, to slip away (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ḥr</i>: from, out of) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people or arrows) to miss the target (in shooting)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people or plans) to fail, to not succeed (+ <i>m</i>: in, at, because of)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fail, to come to ruin, be undone</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be lacking (+ <i>m</i>: in)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I, me (see usage notes)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>An admirative particle: How wonderful!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mummiform inner coffin</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spurn, to reject, to thrust aside (someone or something) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature to Kushite Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacred barque</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the barque in which the sun sails through the sky</li></ol><p>From <i>wjꜣ</i> (“sacred barque”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḥw</i> (plural of <i>ḥḥ</i> (“million”)), literally “sacred barque of millions”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) thick</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niche (in a wall)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open (a door)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to open up, to permit access to oneself, to open the door (+ <i>n</i>: for (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open (a container)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to unlatch (a bolt or latch)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open the way into (a place), to open up, to make (a building, fortress, city, sanctum, tomb, cavern, land, the sky, the underworld, etc.) freely accessible</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open (a path), to make traversable</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread wide, to open (one’s hands, arms, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open (one’s eyes, nose, mouth, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stretch (one’s legs) out for walking</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to let (someone) out of confinement, to release</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of thieves) to break in to, to forcibly open (a building)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to become open, to open</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) opening of a door</li><li>fault, blame</li><li>desert hare</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reality, true being or quality</li><li>genuine, true, or real thing</li><li>(<i>with attached suffix pronoun</i>) one’s proper place or worth</li></ol><p><i>wn</i> +&lrm; <i>mꜣꜥ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>consecutive of <i>wnn</i></li><li>denotes consequent action in a narrative; so then</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>obligative of <i>wnn</i></li><li>denotes consequent action in a narrative; so then &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frivolous</li></ol><p><i>wnf</i> (“to be loose-fitting”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by the Predynastic king Wenegbu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive,&#32;usually in the imperative) to rush, to hurry</li><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) to pass by</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ignore, to take no heed of (evil, speech, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to neglect or dismiss (a person)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Unas, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Like many fifth-dynasty pharaohs’ birth names, this name may simply be a diminutive without any meaning of its own. Alternatively, some authors have tentatively proposed to read it as <i>wn-js</i>, from a form of <i>wnn</i> (“to exist”) + <i>js</i> (“intensifying particle”), thus meaning ‘Who exists indeed’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to eat (something as food)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to eat in general (+ <i>m</i>: to eat of, to feed on)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to take solid medicine</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to have or consume usufruct of a property &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take in, to consume (something abstract: magic, power, hunger, etc.)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive,&#32;of fire) to consume (something), to burn (something) up</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of sickness) to eat away at parts of the body</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eating, consumption of food</li><li>food</li><li>appetite</li><li>taking or ingestion of medicine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right (direction)</li></ol><p><i>wnm</i> (“to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p><p>As speculated by W. Vycichl (1959), this word might have originally meant “eating hand”. Furthermore, Takacs (1999) pointed out that the semantic shift from the original sense to “right hand/side” is an African sprachbund's feature; cf. Swahili <i>mkono wa kulia</i> (“eating hand; right hand”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food (for humans) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) fodder for animals</li></ol><p><i>wnm</i> (“to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li></ol><p><i>wnm</i> (“to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to exist, to be present</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet or alternative name for the god Osiris.</li><li>a male given name, Wenennefer</li></ol><p><i>wnn</i> (“(one who) continually exists”) +&lrm; <i>nfr</i> (“to be good, perfect, beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (third-person masculine stative ending), thus literally ‘one who continually exists in being good/perfect/beautiful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in nominal sentences</i>) indeed, truly, really</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>terminative of <i>wnn</i></li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>serves as a complementizer to convert a verbal or nonverbal sentence with realis mood into a subordinated noun clause&#59; that</li><li>alternative form of <i>wnnt</i> (“truly, indeed”)</li></ol><p>From the terminative of <i>wnn</i> (“to exist”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move about</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to move about (a place)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(unequal) hour (one twelfth of the period of daylight or one twelfth of the period of night) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>nonspecific span of time; moment, time, hour</li><li>hourly duties or service, work to be performed regularly &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) hourly staff, workers assigned duties to be performed regularly &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) in particular, the hourly staff or hour-priesthood of a temple</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) the hourly staff or hour-priesthood of a temple</li></ol><p>From <i>wnwt</i> (“hour, hourly duties, hourly staff”) +&lrm; <i>ḥwt-nṯr</i> (“temple”). The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short-horned cattle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) fellow humans, people</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) tenants</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only&#32;in the dual</i>) two strips of cloth given as an offering for the dead &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>wnḫ</i> (“to clothe, *cloth”) +&lrm; <i>-wj</i> (masculine dual ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the beginning of the day &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>wp</i> (“opener”) +&lrm; <i>hrw</i> (“day”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘opener of day’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>‘New Year’s Day’: a festival day at the start of the Egyptian year, the first day of the first month of <i>ꜣḫt</i>, originally aligned to the heliacal rising of Sirius in summer</li><li>the festival day of the king’s birthday</li><li>a month of the year, apparently the last, corresponding to Mesore, the fourth month of <i>šmw</i></li></ol><p><i>wp</i> (“opening”) +&lrm; <i>rnpt</i> (“year”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘opening of the year’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Wepwawet</li></ol><p><i>wp</i> (“opener”) +&lrm; <i>wꜣwt</i> (plural of <i>wꜣt</i> (“way”)) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘opener of the ways’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to split, to open</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sidelock of hair, the traditional hairstyle of children &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>a type of priestess of a particular district &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to incinerate (evil ones) &lsqb;New Kingdom, Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horns, particularly of cattle</li><li>brow</li><li>summit</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the opening of the mouth ceremony, a funerary ritual that symbolically reanimates the deceased and lets them regain the senses and physical powers they had in life</li></ol><p><i>wpt</i> (infinitive of <i>wpj</i> (“to open”)) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“mouth”), thus ‘opening of the mouth’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>except</li></ol><p>From <i>wpj</i> (“to open, to divide”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>except</li><li>(<i>at the start of a clause</i>) in particular, particularly</li></ol><p><i>wpw</i> (“except”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“upon”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>message</li><li>behest</li><li>task, mission, assignment</li><li>business, work, duties</li><li>office, position to serve in</li><li>inventory, register (of cattle, food, land, households, etc.) &lsqb;Middle to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>subordinates, people (under someone)</li><li>household</li><li>crowd</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one entrusted with a message or task, messenger, envoy, emissary</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a spirit that brings death</li></ol><p>Nominalized from <i>wpwt</i> (“message, task”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>wrr</i>: big, great, important, chief</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) how much?</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greatness, importance</li><li>large amount or degree</li><li>one who is important, great</li><li>a chief or leader of a (generally foreign) group of people</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>much, very</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the title of the high priest of Heliopolis</li></ol><p><i>wr</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>mꜣw</i> (“seers”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘great(est) of the seers’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various gods or the king</li><li>a kind of collar &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>curving serpent-headed adze or blade used as a ritual instrument in the opening of the mouth ceremony, a funerary ritual to allow the deceased to eat and drink once more</li></ol><p><i>wr</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>ḥkꜣw</i> (plural of <i>ḥkꜣ</i> (“magic”)) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘(the one) great of magic powers’, i.e. ‘one with great magic’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the high priest of Ptah at Memphis</li></ol><p><i>wr</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>ḫrp</i> (“(those) directing”) +&lrm; <i>ḥmwt</i> (“craftsmanship”), with the first two elements in a direct genitive construction indicating the superlative, thus meaning “the greatest of the directors of craftsmanship”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the dead god Osiris</li></ol><p><i>wrdw</i> (“weary”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘the weary-hearted’. No writing with <i>ḏ</i> in <i>wrḏw</i> is attested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grow, to rise, to be high</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grasp</li><li>to wind, to wriggle (of toes)?</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood, high point of inundation</li><li>towering figure</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) canopy, awning, coverings of a shelter or tent &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>roof of a building, roofing, perhaps sometimes in particular battlements, crenellations, parapets along a roof &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;wurVm-</i>. Possibly related to <i>wrm</i> (“to grow, to rise; flood, high point of inundation”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) A pair of protruding body parts. &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>testicles</li><li>fingernails</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>wrm</i> (“to grow, to rise”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically big, large</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) much, numerous, many in number or great in quantity</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of abstract things) to be(come) great in degree or magnitude, strong, extreme</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of time,&#32;rare) to be(come) long</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) older, senior, the elder among several</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;usually&#32;of people,&#32;royal possessions&#32;or&#32;sacred animals) to be(come) great, important, distinguished, exalted</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people,&#32;in titles) to be(come) the chief or main one</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the Nile) to be(come) high, swollen with floodwater, to be in inundation</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of festivals) to be(come) great, magnificent</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the White Crown</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uraeus &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>wrr</i> (“to be great”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headrest for sleeping on one’s side, typically made of wood or alabaster</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) great one (common epithet for goddesses)</li><li>used with unclear meaning in <i>jmj wrt</i> and related terms</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sacred barque</li><li>the Red Crown</li><li>alternative form of <i>wrrt</i> (“the White Crown”)</li></ol><p><i>wr</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (adverbializing suffix).</p><p><i>wr</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the queen</li></ol><p>From <i>wrt</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣt</i> (“friendliness, charm, belovedness”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “great of friendliness/charm/belovedness”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spend the day</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchman</li></ol><p><i>wrš</i> (“to spend the day”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) weary or tired, to tire</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to die</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to anoint</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lack &lsqb;since the 22nd Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with <i>m</i>) to lack (something)</li></ol><li>to withhold, to leave without &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(only used in the phrase <i>pr-m-ws</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>height (of a pyramid)</li><li>crack or some other reference point from which the height of a pyramid is measured</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to saw, to cut &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>to trim (+ <i>m</i>) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Osiris</li><li>(<i>in funerary literature</i>) conventionally appended before the name of the deceased&#58; Osirian form of a dead person, deceased person as Osiris (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li></ol><p>Several proposals have been made for the etymology and meaning of the original name; as Smith (2017) notes, none are fully convincing. Most take <i>wsjr</i> as the accepted transliteration, following Adolf Erman:</p><p>However, recently alternative transliterations have been proposed:</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Serapis</li></ol><p>From <i>wsjr</i> (“Osiris”) + <i>ḥp</i> (“Apis”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fertilize, to procreate &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Latin, of historical usage, notably borne by Vespasian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Vespasianus</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) mighty, to be(come) powerful</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>wsr</i>: mighty, powerful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) oar</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Userkare, a pharaoh of the Sixth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>wsr</i> (“powerful”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) in two direct genitive constructions, thus ‘the powerful (one) of the life-force of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Userkaf, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>wsr</i> (“to be(come) powerful”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus ‘his life-force is powerful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Usermontu</li></ol><p><i>wsr</i> (“to be powerful”) +&lrm; <i>mnṯw</i> (“Montu”), thus literally ‘Montu is powerful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Usermaatre</li><li>Pharaoh Ramesses II, after the first part of his throne name, Usermaatre Setepenre (<i>wsr-mꜣꜥt-rꜥ stp.n-rꜥ</i>).</li></ol><p>From <i>wsr</i> +&lrm; <i>mꜣꜥt</i> +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i>, meaning "The righteousness of the Sun (Ra) is powerful". It is attested as 𒉿𒀸𒈬𒀀𒊑𒀀 (<i>wa-aš-mu-a-re-a</i>) in Hittite cuneiform documents dating to the reign of Ramesses II (<i>c.</i>&nbsp;1250 <small>B.C.E.</small>). In the Demotic period, the given name <i>wsr-mꜣꜥt-rꜥ</i> is frequently represented by the unetymological pronunciation spellings <i>ns-mn-rꜥ</i> and <i>smn-rꜥ</i>, which resemble contemporary Greek transcriptions (see below).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Wosret</li></ol><p><i>wsr</i> (“powerful”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending), literally ‘the powerful one’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might, power</li></ol><p><i>wsr</i> (“to be powerful”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawdust</li></ol><p>From <i>wsj</i> (“to saw”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) broad, to be(come) wide</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of movements) to be(come) extensive, wide-ranging</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) abundant, extensive, rich (+ <i>m</i>: in)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of names) to be(come) renowned, widely known, famous</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;impersonal,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to have space for (someone)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>wsḫ</i>; broad, wide</li><li>imperfective active participle of <i>wsḫ</i>; broad, wide</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breadth, width</li><li>broad collar or necklace &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>barge</li></ol><p>Sometimes hypothesized to be from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;wsɣ</i>; compare Arabic وَسُعَ (wasuʕa), وَسِعَ (wasiʕa, “to be wide”). Also compare Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵙⵙⵉⵖ</i> (ssiɣ, “to pass, give, extend, expand”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breadth, width</li><li>wide hall or court</li><li>barge</li></ol><p><i>wsḫ</i> (“to be broad”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go freely, to stride unimpeded, unhindered (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ḥr</i>: in (a place)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to freely take a place or seat (+ <i>m</i>: in (a boat))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of limbs) to have freedom of movement, to be able to move freely</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to make free, to have free rein</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bandage, to bind up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to embalm</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandage</li><li>mummy-wrapping</li><li>embalmer</li><li>bandager</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flee</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beget</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to neglect, to ignore (an order, oath, etc.), to break (one’s oath or promise)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to abate, to waive (tax arrears)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) idle, sluggish, neglectful</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to dawdle, to delay</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neglectfulness, dereliction</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) idleness, time off, leave, lack of work for workers</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neglectful or negligent person, idler, sluggard, shirker</li></ol><p><i>wzf</i> (“to neglect”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to urinate</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of people or animals,&#32;with urine as object) to urinate</li><li>(transitive,&#32;medicine) to discharge or excrete (something diseased)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) bald</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) empty or devoid</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) full of gaps or holes (and thus destroyed)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to desolate, to lay waste (a place)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to answer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to address (someone); to direct speech to &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to question (someone), including to question (someone) judicially; to direct questions to &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to welcome (someone) with words or a gesture, to salute &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to esteem, to honor (someone) (+ <i>ḥr</i>: because of) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of gods,&#32;in formulaic oracular pronouncements) to assent, to agree &lsqb;21st Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to invite to speak &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>wšd</i> (“to address, to question, to welcome”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“for, to”) +&lrm; <i>mdwt</i> (“words, speech”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear (of grain-bearing cereal plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wring the neck of (birds) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;by extension) to sacrifice, to make an offering of (bread, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) dried out, to wither</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) barren (unable to bear children)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>wzš</i> (“urinate”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to command</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to decree</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give over, commend, entrust, or assign (someone or something) (+ <i>n</i>: to (some person or fate))</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command</li><li>decree</li><li>stela</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to command, to govern (+ <i>n</i>: to command (someone))</li></ol><p><i>wḏ</i> (“to command”) +&lrm; <i>mdw</i> (“word”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to (someone))</li><li>to move, to shift</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shore, sandbank</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to depart</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stray</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, decree</li></ol><p>From <i>wḏ</i> (“to command”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expedition, journey away</li><li>(military) campaign</li></ol><p>From <i>wḏj</i> (“to depart, to stray”) +&lrm; <i>-yt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) intact, sound, unhurt, in good condition</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i> or <i>ḥr</i>) to be(come) kept safe from, to be preserved in the face of (something evil) &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of matters, affairs, states) to be(come) in a good state or condition</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of roads) to be(come) passable, traversable</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;mathematics) to be(come) left over in a calculation</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to leave intact or sound &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go in procession, to proceed ceremoniously (+ <i>r</i>: to (a place))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go in general, to proceed, to make one’s way (+ <i>r</i>: to (a place); + <i>n</i>: to (a person); + <i>ḥr</i>: on (a path), through (a door))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pass on, to go along</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;euphemistic) to pass on, to pass away, to die</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the sun) to set</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;imperative) come in!</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enter (a place) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with following <i>r</i> and infinitive) to go to do (something)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) soundness, well-being</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) to have one’s heart/mind be in a good mental and physical state</li><li>to be(come) satisfied or glad</li><li>to satisfy or content oneself with a piece of information; used formulaically to introduce messages and news</li></ol><p><i>wḏꜣ</i> (“to be(come) sound or hale”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>originally</i>) the (dismembered and subsequently restored) Eye of Horus, a mythological conception of the full moon &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the Eye of Ra</li><li>the eye of a god in general</li><li>epithet for various goddesses as the personified eyes of a god</li><li>an amulet in the form of the Eye of Horus</li><li>used in the names of constellations &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the dual</i>) the actual eyes of a god or person &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a volumetric measure of grain, possibly equivalent to the <i>ḥqꜣt</i> (“heqat”)</li><li>sky, heaven</li><li>roof of a temple</li><li>a type of sword or knife</li></ol><p>From <i>wḏꜣ</i> (“to be intact”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to judge, to decide</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to separate, to sever</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Set, literally ‘the Judged’, i.e. ‘the Condemned One’</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a herald</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to repeat, to do or say again</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoof &lsqb;Middle to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and 18th Dynasty literature&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>repetition</li><li>repeated howling</li></ol></ol><p>Probably from <i>wḥm</i> (“to repeat”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribe</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to separate by pulling away &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pluck (fruit)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to uproot (papyrus, flax, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to quarry (stone)</li></ol><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to dig up (earth) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to kill (evil ones) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cauldron</li><li>cauldron in the afterworld in which evildoers are punished</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Oasis region collectively</li><li>any one of the seven oases in this region</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) oasis-dwellers &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>wḥꜣt</i> (“oasis”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to loosen or untie (cords, bonds, knots, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remove (the bandage from a wound)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to resolve (difficulties), to unravel (problems)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to explain, to expound, to clarify</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to interpret, to explain the meaning of (an incomprehensible word or event)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to interpret, to translate (foreign speech)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to understand</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to loosen up (limbs) out of stiffness</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to heal, to cure</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deliver (someone), to set free, to release (+ <i>m</i> or <i>m-ꜥ</i>: from (something bad or evil))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deliver (a woman) in childbirth</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to finish up with work, to get off work</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) idle or at leisure, to relax</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to return home, especially after a day’s work (+ <i>m</i>: from; <i>r</i>: to (a place))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to establish, to build (a building) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisher or fowler (or both) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the king when making an offering of geese &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to suffer (something)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain</li></ol><p><i>wḫd</i> (“to be painful”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) an agent of decay and disease seen as originating in the fecal matter of the bowels, spreading to other body parts through the blood vessels, and forming abscesses there and manifesting as pus in the living, while manifesting as decomposition in the dead &lsqb;Medical Papyri and 18th Dynasty Magical Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>wḫd</i> (“to endure, to suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fool</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seek, to look for</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift up, to carry, to wear</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to speak out</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to brood over</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root (of a plant)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) the place where a body part is attached, such as the root of a tooth or the socket of an eye</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;hapax legomenon)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to shout for joy, to celebrate</li></ol></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Wag-festival, a festival honoring the dead and Osiris, celebrated on the 17th, 18th, or 19th day of the month of Thoth &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to 26th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) distant or far away</li><li>to be(come) long ago</li><li>to fall into, to start [<i>with</i> <b>r</b> <small>‘a condition or state’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Troy; Ilium</li></ol><p>Of Anatolian origin. Compare Hittite <i>𒃾𒇻𒊭</i> (Wi-lu-ša, “Wilusa”), Ancient Greek <i>&#42;Ϝίλιον</i> (&#42;Wílion).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie up, to tie together (a net) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dance (+ <i>n</i>: before (a god)) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a kind of lute or reed pipe</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, rope, line</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>tow rope for a clap net or for the solar barque &lsqb;since the Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>restraining rope used in hunting hippopotamoi &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>measuring rope &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>rope stretched in foundation ceremonies &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></ol><p><i>wꜣr</i> (“to tie up”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dominion, rule</li><li>was-sceptre</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) ruined, to fall to ruin</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thebes (an ancient Egyptian city; modern Luxor)</li><li>the nome with its capital at Thebes</li></ol><p>From <i>wꜣs</i> (“sceptre, rule”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Osorkon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road, path</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(water) wave</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lower Nubia</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, of historical usage, notably borne by the Achaemenid satrap Hystaspes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎻𐏁𐎫𐎠𐎿𐎱</i> (vi-š-t-a-s-p /⁠Vištāspaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papyrus stem (with an umbel)</li><li>symbol of Lower Egypt</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) a papyriform column or pillar</li><li>a papyrus-stem-shaped amulet conveying freshness and vigour</li><li>malachite</li><li>the happy or fortunate</li><li>offspring, generally in reference to the king as son of a god or Horus as son of Osiris</li><li>green plants in general</li><li>grain</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) green</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) fresh</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to thrive, to flourish</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>wꜣḏ</i>: green, fresh, thriving, vigorous</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Wadjenes, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>wꜣḏ</i> (“fresh, vigorous”) +&lrm; <i>ns</i> (“tongue”), thus literally ‘vigorous of tongue’; however, this is likely to be a later misinterpretation of an earlier name such as <i>wng</i> or <i>wꜣḏ-sn</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sea, encompassing both the Red and Mediterranean Seas</li></ol><p><i>wꜣḏ</i> (“green”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great”), thus literally ‘The Great Green’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Wadjet</li></ol><p>Nominalized from <i>wꜣḏ</i> (“green”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending), thus literally ‘the green one’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) a green mineral pigment primarily used as eyepaint</li></ol><p><i>wꜣḏ</i> (“to be green”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay (down), to put, to set (down)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to apply (a remedy)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to endure</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;as a non-attributive relative/emphatic form) used in introductory formulae for oaths, usually with a king or god as subject&#58; As long as … endures, …</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to leave aside</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be forbearing or patient</li><li>(with <i>r</i>) to be friendly or kindly toward</li><li>(catenative,&#32;with <i>r</i> and following infinitive) to be inclined to do</li></ol><p><i>wꜣḥ</i> (“to be lasting, to endure”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”), thus literally ‘to be lasting of heart’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Apries</li></ol><p>From <i>wꜣḥ</i> (“to endure”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus meaning ‘The heart of Ra endures’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Two Ladies name&#x20;notably borne by Tuthmosis III, a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>wꜣḥ</i> (“to endure”) +&lrm; <i>nsyt</i> (“kingship”) +&lrm; <i>mj</i> (“like”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“the sun, Ra”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>pt</i> (“sky”), thus literally ‘Enduring of Kingship like the Sun (Ra) in the Sky’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yield of the grain harvest, abundance of grain, perhaps especially spelt &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>wꜣḥ</i> (“to lay or set down”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) inundated</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>columned hall (figuratively as flooded land, with lotiform and papyriform columns)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alone</li><li>unique</li><li>as one, all together</li></ol><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) the singular indefinite article; a, an</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one each, singly</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>wꜥ</i> (“one”). The written form consists of <i>wꜥ</i> (“one”) + <i>zpwj snwj</i> (“twice, repeated”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) pure or clean</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to purify or bathe (oneself)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>wꜥb</i>: pure, clean</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the lowest level of priest, literally a “cleaner”, responsible for cleaning and purifying the temple and preparing ritual offerings</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleansing</li></ol><p><i>wꜥb</i> (“to be(come) clean”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pure or sacred robe worn by gods and the dead</li></ol><p><i>wꜥb</i> (“to be(come) pure, clean”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deflect</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) alone, to be(come) the sole or only one</li></ol><p>Compare <i>wꜥ</i> (“one, alone”), either the source of the verb or a derivation of it.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flee</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fugitive</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leg</li><li>district</li><li>flight, escape</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;waˁVr-</i> (“leg”), according to Orel and Stolbova’s very tentative reconstruction. If so, perhaps cognate with Proto-West Chadic *<i>war-</i> (“leg”), whence Goemai <i>warr</i>, and Proto-Central Chadic <i>&#42;wVr</i> (“leg”), whence Musgu <i>werē</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>feminine form of <i>wꜥ</i> (“one”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sole, single, only</li></ol><p><i>wꜥ</i> (“one, alone”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chufa</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to speak evil, to curse or blaspheme (+ <i>ḥr</i>: about, against) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to revile (someone or something) &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solitude, privacy &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>wꜥj</i> (“to be alone”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an unidentified place, possibly located in the region of Edom, often reconstructed as <i>Yahu</i>.</li></ol><p>Debated. Some have hypothesized a derivation from the theonym Yahweh, that is, Biblical Hebrew <i>יַהְוֶה</i> (YHWH).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea, lake &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>יָם</i> (yām), Arabic يَمٌّ (yamm) and cognates listed at that entry.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jaffa</li></ol><p>Compare Akkadian <i>𒅀𒁍</i> (Yapu), Hebrew <i>יָפוֹ</i> (Yāfō).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jordan (a river in West Asia in the Middle East, that empties into the Dead Sea, flowing through Israel, the Golan Heights, the West Bank and Jordan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יַרְדֵּן</i> (yardēn, “Jordan”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a demonym&#59; probably the Israelites, Israel</li></ol><p>If the term means <i>Israel</i>, as is considered most likely, then borrowed from Hebrew <i>יִשְׂרָאֵל</i> (yiśrāʾēl). (However, alternative readings have sometimes been proposed, such as Jezreel.)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>The second element is a rendering of the Semitic theonym Baal, ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;baʕl-</i> (“lord”); see also Egyptian <i>bꜥr</i> (“Baal”) as a loanword. The first element may be a transcription of a word derived from a Semitic root for ‘to know’ also reflected in <i>יָדַע</i>. The meaning of the whole is then perhaps ‘Acquaintance of Baal’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place hypothesized to be near Megiddo</li></ol><p>A compound of <i>ywd</i> (“hand, monument”) +&lrm; <i>(h)mꜥrwk</i> (“king”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Monument of the King’. Both components are borrowings from Semitic, ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;yad-</i> and <i>&#42;malk-</i>; it may be the entire place name was directly borrowed from Semitic. The <i>h</i> in the Egyptian form reflects a Semitic definite article: compare Hebrew <i>הַ־</i>.</p><p>Earlier hypotheses interpreting the word as ‘king of Judah’ are now largely rejected.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) only used in <i>ywnj-ꜥꜣ</i>; Ionia?</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the same source as Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωνία</i> (Iōnía).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a region bordering the Aegean Sea, usually equated with Ionia but possibly a part of central Greece &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Most likely a compound <i>ywnj</i> (“Ionia”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great”). Possibly, however, the final element <i>ꜥꜣ</i> could instead represent a phonetic value.</p><p>Ultimately from the same source as Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωνία</i> (Iōnía).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yaham (an ancient Canaanite city near Megiddo)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>יַחַם</i> (yaḥam).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) affirmative particle; really, indeed, but &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly a New Kingdom descendant of earlier <i>ꜣ</i> (intensifying particle). However, unlike that particle, <i>yꜣ</i> is not enclitic. It may have instead come from a contracted <i>jw</i> (clause-initial particle) +&lrm; <i>ꜣ</i> (intensifying particle).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Yaqub-Har, a pharaoh of the Fourteenth or Fifteenth Dynasty</li><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Yaqub-Har, a pharaoh of the Fourteenth or Fifteenth Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door bolt of wood or copper &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>man (male person) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>someone, anyone</li><li>being, creature (used of a snake) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>A type of fish &lsqb;22nd dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Speculatively, Ehret hypothesizes an origin in Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ji</i> (one, someone, somebody); as with other attempts at reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic, academic consensus is lacking. Compare Hebrew <i>זֶה</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>said person; this man</li></ol><p>From <i>z</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>jm</i> (“therein”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Senusret (also anglicized as Sesostris and Senwosret)</li></ol><p>From <i>z</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>wsrt</i> (“Wosret”), thus ‘man of Wosret’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to send (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accompany (someone) (+ <i>r</i>: to (some place); + <i>n</i>: to (someone else))</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to glide away, to slide, to tumble, to diverge</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to laugh</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) gentle or mild</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) merciful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentleness, mildness, friendliness</li><li>mercy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) gentle or kindly</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) merciful</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) happy, to rejoice &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>zf</i> (“to be(come) mild, merciful”) +&lrm; <i>-n</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) (metal) sword &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>masculine</i>) knife &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) clemency, mercy, gentleness</li></ol><p>Related to <i>zf</i> (“to slaughter, cut up”), <i>zfṯ</i> (“to slaughter”). Also compare Arabic سيف (sayf), Amharic <i>ሰይፍ</i> (säyf), Ancient Greek <i>ξίφος</i> (xíphos).</p><p><i>zf</i> (“to be(come) mild, merciful”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to slaughter, to butcher (animals) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to kill (enemies) in battle &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher</li></ol><p>Related to <i>zf</i> (“to slaughter, cut up”), <i>zft</i> (“sword, knife”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) a group of threatening underworld gods, ‘the Butchers’</li></ol><p><i>zfṯ</i> (“to slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burnt offering</li></ol><p><i>zj</i> (“to go”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>sḏt</i> (“fire”), thus perhaps literally ‘go-away of fire’. Some authors identify the first element with <i>zbj</i> (“to go, to lead”) rather than with <i>zj</i> or simply conflate the two verbs entirely.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rub</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physician</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Sokar</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desert</li><li>necropolis, cemetery</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to join, to come together</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to join, to put together</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lung</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite the land</li><li>to land a boat</li><li>to be buried</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial, interment</li></ol><p><i>zmꜣ</i> (“to join”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“land, ground”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gang, band, troop</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>zmꜣ</i> (“to unite, to join”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break through (a boundary, such as a wall or a series of boundary markers, by throwing it down)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to destroy (evildoers) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battlement or parapet on top of a wall</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of jar</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bleed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ʒin-</i> with an uncertain suffix -<i>f</i>, according to Orel and Stolbova’s very tentative reconstruction. If so, perhaps cognate with West Chadic *<i>ʒin-</i> (“blood”), whence Hausa <i>jini</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to pass by, to go beyond</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to surpass, to exceed</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come to an end, to pass</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great distress, calamity &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>znj</i> (“to surpass”) +&lrm; <i>mnt</i> (“suffering”), with <i>znj</i> in the infinitive and the two words in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘surpassing of suffering’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bigeh, an island at the first cataract of the Nile where Osiris was said to be buried</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>senet, a board game</li></ol><p><i>znj</i> (“to pass by, surpass”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locust</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;sulx̣am</i>, cognate with Hebrew <i>סָלְעָם</i> (solʿām, “locust”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time (as in <i>the first time</i> or <i>many times</i>), occurrence, instance</li><li>occasion, chance</li><li>mishap, mischance, incident</li><li>matter, business, affair</li><li>state, condition (of a thing)</li><li>character, kind (of a person)</li><li>cause, venture</li><li>deed, act</li><li>fault, misdeed</li><li>article, item, point (in a text)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) times (in multiplication)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) medicinal concoction, medicine, remedy, dose</li><li>portion of food</li><li>rest, remainder, remnant</li><li>particularly, remaining food</li></ol><p>From <i>zpj</i> (“to remain”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the beginning of time, the primeval time, creation</li></ol><p><i>zp</i> (“time, occasion”) +&lrm; <i>tpj</i> (“first”), literally ‘first time’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alternative transliteration of <i>zpꜣ</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to remain, to be(come) left over, abandoned</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to remain, to survive, to be left</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) left out, excluded</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;negated as part of the constructions <i>nj zp</i> or <i>nn zp</i>) to happen</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshing floor &lsqb;Old to Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>heap of grain stacked on the threshing floor to be threshed &lsqb;Old to Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two times, twice</li></ol><p><i>zpwj</i>, dual of <i>zp</i> (“time, occasion”), + <i>snwj</i> (“two”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rest, remainder, remnant &lsqb;since the Middle kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>remainder in calculations</li><li>remaining food</li></ol><p><i>zpj</i> (“to remain”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centipede</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram (male sheep)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>a type of disease &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>zmyt</i></li><li>a type of goose or duck, as a living creature or as food for the dead and the gods &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>z</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>From earlier <i>zꜣt</i> (“type of fowl”), from <i>zꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li></ol><p><i>zt</i> (“woman”) +&lrm; <i>ḥmt</i> (“woman”). This compound arose because the feminine ending <i>-t</i> in <i>zt</i> eventually became silent, making <i>zt</i> homophonous with <i>z</i> (“man”), so that it became necessary to add <i>ḥmt</i> to clarify.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drink (something liquid: water, beer, wine, etc.)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drink in general (+ <i>m</i>: to drink of (a drink), to drink from (a container or hand); + <i>ḥr</i>: to drink of (a body of water))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drink alcoholic drinks</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to take liquid medicine</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drink (a container), to consume the liquid contained in</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of the dead) to have (thirst) instead of something to drink, to ‘drink’ (thirst)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of trees and land) to be watered</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking bowl &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>drink, beverage</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of emmer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>which?</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when?</li></ol><p><i>zy</i> (“which”) +&lrm; <i>nw</i> (“time”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nest &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nestling (small bird confined to the nest)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) child</li></ol><p>From <i>zš</i> (“nest”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lotus flower</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>zš</i> (“nest”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sistrum &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>zšš</i> (“to play the sistrum”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canopied structure made of reed matting or linen; tent, booth</li><li>official hall or chamber, e.g. of a council, administrative office, king, or god</li><li>booth or chamber used as an embalmer’s workshop</li><li>council of royal advisors or officials</li><li>counsel, advice</li><li>plan, design, counsel</li><li>plot, evil scheme</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mythological booth or hall in which Anubis cleaned and embalmed the corpse of Osiris &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small wooden chapel &lsqb;Old Kingdom and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>temple &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>chamber in a temple &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>zḥ</i> (“booth, hall, embalmer’s workshop”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘booth of the god’, ‘divine booth’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hit, to strike &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to happen by chance</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chance occurrence</li><li>An internal organ or tissue of an animal eaten as food.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>kidney fat</li></ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) float made of tied-together reeds &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tear out (an eye) or uproot (papyrus)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to run, to flee, to tear off</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to run through (a place)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) deaf</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to write</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to paint</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) writing, record</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribe</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian hieroglyphic writing</li></ol><p><i>zẖꜣw</i> (“writings”) +&lrm; <i>mdw-nṯr</i> (“sacred writings, hieroglyphic script”, literally “god’s word”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘writings of the god’s word’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official in charge of cadastral registers showing the ownership and yield of agricultural land</li></ol><p>From <i>zẖꜣw</i> (“scribe”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ṯmꜣ</i> (“cadaster”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son &lsqb;since the Pyramid texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) male offspring of an animal &lsqb;18th dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the succeeding decan after some decan</li><li>protection &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>a magic spell</li><li>an amulet</li><li>a measure of area equivalent to one eighth of a <i>sṯꜣt</i> (“arura”) (about 345 square metres).</li><li>(Late Period,&#32;in the direct genitive construction <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./18ce177e.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="G39" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">N21&#58;Z1</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f50bd458.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="H" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">N21&#58;Z1</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>sṯꜣt-ꜣḥt</i>)) abbreviation of <i>sṯꜣt</i> (“arura”)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a product of an inflammation</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a type of ulcer</li><li>the object depicted in the hieroglyph <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./636b7378.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="V17" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, a type of shepherd’s mat</li><li>guard</li><li>troop of soldiers</li><li>association of people in some particular occupation</li><li>phyle, group, division</li><li>Used in reference to cattle.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A herd</li><li>A barrier</li></ol><li>a title of some kind &lsqb;18th dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a type of field</li><li>The object depicted in the hieroglyph <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./2da635ce.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="V16" alt="glyph" height="47"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>.</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a god who personifies a certain body of water &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fend off &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>perfective of <i>zꜣj</i></li><li>perfective of <i>zꜣw</i> (“to greet, watch for”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siamun</li></ol><p>From <i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘son of Amun’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Sinuhe</li></ol><p><i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>nht</i> (“sycamore”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘son of sycamore’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prince; son of the king</li></ol><p>From <i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”) in a direct genitive construction. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Sanakht, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>zꜣ</i> (“protector”) +&lrm; <i>nḫt</i> (“strong, victorious”), thus ‘strong/victorious protector’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchman &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>zꜣ(w)</i> (“guardian”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘guardian of the house’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siptah</li></ol><p><i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”), literally ‘son of Ptah’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Conventionally written before the cartouche containing a king’s nomen.</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> zꜣ,&lrm; rꜥ.</li></ol><p>From <i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Son of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sisobek or Sasobek</li></ol><p><i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>sbk</i> (“Sobek”) in a direct genitive construction, literally ‘son of Sobek’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homage</li><li>snake</li></ol><p><i>zꜣ</i> (“protection”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“earth”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘protection of the earth’, referring to prostration on the ground.</p><p><i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“earth”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘son of the earth’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siese</li></ol><p><i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣst</i> (“Isis”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘son of Isis’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackal</li><li>an official title of uncertain rank</li></ol><p>Possibly from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ǯiʔib-</i> (“beast of prey”). Compare Hebrew <i>זְאֵב</i> (zǝʾēḇ) and Arabic ذِئْب (ḏiʔb) from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ḏiʔb-</i> (“wolf”), as well as Chadic <i>ǯib-</i> “civet”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter</li></ol><p>From <i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>princess; daughter of the king</li></ol><p>From <i>zꜣt</i> (“daughter”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”) in a direct genitive construction. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magician, wizard, sorcerer &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to guard, to protect</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to beware, to guard oneself against</li><li>(transitive,&#32;in a noun clause,&#32;with a subjunctive verb as object) lest</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saïs, a city in Lower Egypt</li></ol><p>Most likely derived from a form of the verb <i>zꜣw</i> (“to guard, to protect”), perhaps via an earlier form <i>&#42;zꜣjw</i>, for which see the reconstructed pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asyut, a city in Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a libation</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pour out, to libate (water)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libation of water</li><li>stone on which a libation is poured &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake</li><li>garden with a pool</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windpipe, oesophagus, gullet</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to knead, to mash, to polish</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mix together (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) mixed (+ <i>ḥr</i> or <i>m</i>: with)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, nourishment</li></ol><p><i>wšb</i> (“to feed on”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recite, to read (aloud)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dig out</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dredge (a canal)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut out, to carve out</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remove, take away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take along</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rescue</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to suckle</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to educate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather waterskin &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>animal skin as a mat</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bulbous, curling ornament or protuberance attached to the front of divine standards, particularly that of Wepwawet &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Tybi</li></ol><p><i>šf</i> +&lrm; <i>bdt</i> in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘Swelling of emmer’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grasp</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to touch</li></ol><p>Compare <i>ḫfꜥ</i> (“to grasp, seize”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll of papyrus, scroll</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>šfd</i> (“to grasp”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to respect</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Sheftu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of injured or diseased body parts or floodwaters) to swell up &lsqb;medical papyri, Book of the Dead, and since the Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>respect</li><li>majesty</li><li>truth &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>šfj</i> (“to respect”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go, to walk</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) hot</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) feverish</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with Arabic حَمِيم (ḥamīm, “hot”), Hebrew <i>חַם</i> (ḥam, “hot”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) heat, fever</li></ol><p><i>šmm</i> (“to be hot, to be feverish”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to follow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral procession</li></ol><p><i>šms</i> (“follow”) +&lrm; <i>wḏꜣ</i> (“procession”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower, retainer</li><li>following, retinue</li></ol><p>From <i>šms</i> (“to follow”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>šmsw</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gait</li><li>a walking, a going</li><li>task, errand, business</li><li>execution of a task</li></ol><p><i>šmj</i> (“to go, to walk”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvest</li><li>the harvest season; one of three Egyptian seasons, spanning from March to July.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Upper Egypt (an ancient kingdom and region of the Empire of Egypt)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley of some kind; see the etymology section above for discussion</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to chant, to sing</li></ol><p>Traditionally considered a shortening of <i>jt-šmꜥ</i> (“Upper Egyptian barley”). Allen instead suggests that the word is a nominalized <i>nisba</i> adjective formed from <i>šmꜥ</i> (“thin”) +&lrm; <i>-(j)</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending), referring to barley with fewer grains than <i>jt-mḥ</i> (“full barley”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singer, musician</li></ol><p><i>šmꜥ</i> (“to chant, to sing”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singer</li></ol><p><i>šmꜥ</i> (“to chant, to sing”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The front of the upper body; the chest or breast</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish scale &lsqb;Coffin Texts to Amarna Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to investigate, to inquire into</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to curse, to conjure</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exorcise (a disease)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) round</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to encircle, to surround</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of baked goods</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure?</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle</li><li>cartouche</li><li>net</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>court (retinue and surroundings of the sovereign)</li><li>granary</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shendyt, a kind of skirt worn by Ancient Egyptian men, usually made of linen &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts, except for the Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(literary,&#32;figuratively) protector &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to denounce, to revile</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive off, to repulse, to repel (people, animals, bad weather, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold (someone) off, to keep (someone) away (+ <i>ḥr</i>: from (a person); + <i>r</i>: from (a place))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to expel, to banish (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hamper (someone’s steps) &lsqb;Late Period, Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to block (a door, a path)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to police (a district)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of fish) to dart about</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a measure of weight equivalent to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>12</sub></small> <i>dbn</i>, or 7.58 grams &lsqb;New Kingdom, until the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forced labor camp or workshop, labor establishment, often for captives and slaves &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>storehouse, magazine, storage space, especially for food (+ genitive: belonging to (someone), for storing (something))</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;rare) barracks</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) tomb</li></ol><p><i>šnꜥ</i> (“to detain”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>špsj</i>: noble, august</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>špss</i> (“to be(come) noble or splendid”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be ennobled, to be(come) noble, esteemed, rich, or splendid</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ennoble, to make noble, esteemed, rich, or splendid</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Shepseskare, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>špss</i> (“to be noble, august”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force, sustenance”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus ‘the noble (one) of the life-force of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Shepseskaf, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>špss</i> (“to be noble, august”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force, sustenance”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus ‘his life-force is noble’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finery, luxuries, riches</li></ol><p><i>špss</i> +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noblewoman &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>šps</i> (“nobleman”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ẖpꜣ</i> (“navel”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy, child</li><li>lad, young man</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) son, especially a younger son</li></ol><p>From <i>šrr</i> (“to be(come) little”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) little</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nostril</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the dual</i>) nose</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bow of a ship</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) alabaster (calcite)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lash, blow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sšp</i> (“to be(come) bright, to illuminate”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sšp</i> (“illumination, light of dawn”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) aware (+ <i>m</i>: of)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) treatment, prescription</li></ol><p><i>šsꜣ</i> (“to be wise, to be skilled”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one hundred</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calculation and collection of tax</li><li>(<i>collective noun</i>) A group of taxpayers (disputed, see <i>Land-Tenure in the New Kingdom</i> p. 197 for summary)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two hundred</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>štw</i> (“turtle, tortoise”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turtle, tortoise &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evil being that serves as an enemy of Ra</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) hidden or secret</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>štꜣ</i>: hidden, secret</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secret</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>šwj</i>: empty, devoid, lacking</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Shu (literally “Emptiness”), personification of the space between the earth and sky &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blank (sheet or roll of) papyrus &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>destitute or needy person, one who has nothing</li><li>light (of the sun or moon) falling on things, light as opposed to shadow; sunlight or moonlight &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>sun</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) epithet for the king &lsqb;since the Amarna Period&rsqb;</li><li>parasol, sunshade &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>protection &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically empty (+ <i>m</i>: of; to not physically contain)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of seats, litters, etc.) to be(come) unoccupied</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>,&#32;usually&#32;of people) to be(come) deprived or dispossessed of (a thing or possession), whether legally or by robbery; to (come to) lack</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;occasionally&#32;transitive) to be(come) devoid of, free of (a quality, action, or abstraction, typically a negative one); also (<i>usually negated</i>) to stop doing (an action)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>,&#32;of people,&#32;always negated) to be rid of (a person) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>,&#32;of limbs,&#32;always negated) to lack (an associated god)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) absent, lacking, unavailable, missing (+ <i>m</i>: from)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) lacking in sufficient quantities, deficient, not enough</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i>) to remove or distance oneself from</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;in religious texts) to rise, to ascend (+ <i>r</i>: to (the sky)) &lsqb;chiefly Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;in religious texts) to raise (someone’s arm) (+ <i>r</i>: to (the sky)) &lsqb;chiefly Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) dry, to dry up &lsqb;since the Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>sšwj</i> (“to empty”) &lsqb;Late Egyptian and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow</li><li>feather</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of edible plant or herb, also used medicinally in rubbing down a sick ox.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hay, dry rushes</li></ol><li>dry land &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>šwj</i> (“to be(come) dry”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commoner, poor man</li></ol><p><i>šwꜣ</i> (“to be poor”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grasp, to physically take up without force (+ <i>n</i>: to, for (oneself); + <i>m</i>: with, into (one’s hand); + <i>m-ꜥ</i>: from (someone))</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to accept, to receive, to take into one’s possession without force (objects, payments, bribes, land, a crown, etc.) (+ <i>n</i>: to, for (oneself); + <i>m-ꜥ</i>: from (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to receive, to be given (something abstract: favor, life, truth, pleasure, time, etc.)</li><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>m</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive,&#32;chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian) to accept, to admit (a delivery) (+ <i>m</i>: from (a place); + <i>m-dj</i>: from (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accept, to receive (offerings)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to partake of (food or drink), to take to eat or drink</li><li>(transitive,&#32;medicine,&#32;of the vessels of the body) to absorb (medicine, disease, poison, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to catch (fish)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to assume, to take (a seat or place)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accept, to assume, to take on (an office or title)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of people, buildings, or arms) to welcome, to admit, to cordially receive (someone; also, the dead into the afterworld)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take in, to harbor (exiles) &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to listen to (words, speech, praises, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe, to take in (air, breath, etc.)</li><li>(ambitransitive,&#32;of women or female animals) to receive sexually, to have sex (with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to become characterized by (an emotion or abstract quality: happiness, old age, dignity, etc.)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to be pleased by, to find agreeable or dear</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take on (an opponent) in battle</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to move into (a building or temple chamber) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set out on, to commence (a fight, a path, a new year, etc.)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm, a measure of length equivalent to 4 <i>ḏbꜥw</i> (“digits”) or <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>7</sub></small> of a <i>mḥ</i> (“cubit”) (about 7.5 centimetres) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>palm of the hand &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>grip, grasp &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>statue as a religious image &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>sphinx &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>image, portrayal, representation (+ <i>n(j)</i>: of (a god)) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>relief, image in relief &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pavilion</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>šzp</i> (“statue, image, sphinx”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>zš</i> (“nest”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field</li><li>countryside</li><li>swamp where birds or fish can be caught</li><li>boar, wild pig</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put in order, to establish, to decree, to determine</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to decide</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) meals, food</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Shabaka, a pharaoh of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p>Of Kushite origin, possibly Meroitic.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Shebitku, a pharaoh of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dig out (a canal, a tomb, a well, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dredge (a blocked-up canal)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leather object, possibly a sack &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a container for arrows, perhaps a quiver</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) part of a column made out of gold wire with inlays of various stones</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhinoceros</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) rings or circles of metal used as jewelry; rings, earrings, bracelets &lsqb;possibly attested in the Middle Kingdom; otherwise Late Egyptian to 25th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) peace</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to sue for peace</li></ol><p>From a Northwest Semitic form reflected in Hebrew <i>שָׁלוֹם</i> (šālōm).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sharuhen (an ancient Hyksos settlement in the Negev)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>שָׁרוּחֶן</i> (šārûḥen).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to travel, to go</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Shasu, Semitic nomads originally from the area around Edom and Mount Seir, later also throughout southern Canaan &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the land of the Shasu, the area around Edom and Mount Seir &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>possibly</i>) warlike nomads in general</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>šꜣs</i> (“to travel, to wander”). A borrowing derived from Hebrew <i>שָׁסָה</i> (šāsā) or <i>שָׁסַס</i> (šāsas, “to plunder”) has also been suggested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shat</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ushabti &lsqb;since the Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>šwꜣb</i> (“persea (tree)”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> +&lrm; <i>-j</i>, with persea perhaps being the material they were originally made from. The Late Period form <i>wšbtj</i> is apparently reshaped by folk etymology from <i>wšb</i> (“to answer”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> +&lrm; <i>-j</i>, thus literally ‘answerer’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Shoshenq, of historical usage, notably borne by several pharaohs of the 22nd and 23rd Dynasties</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to start, to begin</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from … until … (of a time) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>up to, as far as (of a place) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>šꜣꜥ</i> +&lrm; <i>r</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut off</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut or cut up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut down (trees)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cut off, to sever</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughter</li><li>wounding</li><li>terror</li><li>ill, misfortune</li><li>knife</li><li>papyrus scroll or book</li></ol><p><i>šꜥ</i> (“to cut off”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bread</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brick, block, ingot</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take (someone else’s seat in a boat) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to replace (something) (+ <i>m</i>: with (something else))</li><li>(transitive,&#32;mathematics) to convert (a value) (+ <i>m</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stand in for, to substitute for (someone) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to buy (something) &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to repay, to reward, to requite (good or evil deeds) (+ <i>n</i>: of (someone); + <i>m</i>: with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to punish (enemies) (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ḥr</i>: for) &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to change into (clothes) (+ <i>n</i>: to change (someone) into (some clothes)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dress or adorn (someone) (+ <i>m</i>: with, in) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of a person) to be supplied, provided, or armed with &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to supply (a place or building) (+ <i>m</i>: with) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to block up (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) adorned (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarcophagus (in general) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>specifically, the stone outer sarcophagus (in contrast to the inner sarcophagus)</li><li>shrine of a god &lsqb;Late Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḏbꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>replacement</li><li>recompense</li></ol><p><i>ḏbꜣ</i> (“to replace, to recompense”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten thousand</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) finger (inclusive of the thumb) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;Late Egyptian) digit: a finger, thumb, or toe</li><li>(<i>units of measure</i>) digit: a measure of length equal to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>28</sub></small> cubit (about 1.88&nbsp;cm). &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>small quantity (of a substance such as fat or honey), the amount that can be scooped up by a fingertip &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to point one’s finger at, especially in reproach or objection &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with the heart as object) to rebuke (one’s heart) &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to adulate, to pay homage (+ <i>n</i>: to) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seal up (wine, grain, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seal, to place a seal upon (+ <i>m</i> or (<i>rarely</i>) + <i>ḥr</i>: to seal with)</li></ol><p>Compare with Arabic إصبع (ʔiṣbaʕ, “finger”) and Mina <i>dzəbuŋ</i> (“five”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach, rebuke</li></ol><p><i>ḏbꜥ</i> (“to point a finger at”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signet, signet ring, seal &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>seal impression, seal</li></ol><p><i>ḏbꜥ</i> (“finger”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to say, to speak (words, names, praises, reports, speech, etc., including direct quotes) (+ <i>n</i> or (<i>archaic</i>) <i>ḫft</i>: to; + <i>ḥr</i>: to (someone of high standing); + <i>r</i>: against, about, to (someone); + <i>n</i>: for the sake of (someone); + <i>m</i>: with, from (the mouth))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to talk, to speak (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to speak (good, evil, truth, falsehood, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to narrate, to tell, to report (events or experiences)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tell about (a person or thing) (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to say in writing</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recite (a spell or other text) (+ <i>ḥr</i>: over (an amulet, etc.))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to intone, to sing (a song) aloud</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give away, to tell (a secret)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;law,&#32;of witnesses and the accused) to testify</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give or report (someone’s) name (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone else))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to call (+ <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>: (someone)) by (a name), to refer to as</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sing the praises of (something, someone’s power, etc.), to mention with praise</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to predict, to prophesy</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of people or hearts/minds,&#32;with sentence as object) to think to, to intend to (do something)</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) alternative form of <i>r ḏd</i> (introduces a direct quotation or object clause)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infinitive of <i>ḏd</i>: speech, speaking</li><li>infinitive of <i>ḏd</i>: manner of speech, language</li><li>stability, lastingness</li><li>djed pillar</li><li>epithet of the gods Ptah (in earlier writings) and Osiris (later)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Djedkare Isesi, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḏd</i> (“enduring, lasting”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) in two direct genitive constructions, thus ‘the enduring (one) of the life-force of Ra’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Djedkare Isesi, a pharaoh of the Fifth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḏd</i> (“enduring, lasting”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“life-force”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) in two direct genitive constructions, thus ‘the enduring (one) of the life-force of Horus’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>singular only</i>) recitation (+ <i>jn</i> or (<i>since the 19th Dynasty</i>) <i>n</i>: by; + <i>ḥr</i>: about; + <i>ḫft</i>: when doing); used as a title to introduce various kinds of text to be recited &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>introduces the words spoken by a particular god in temple inscriptions, monuments, and old religious texts</li><li>introduces the main bulk of a religious spell or utterance, after any prologues</li><li>introduces a line of text in general in some collections of religious utterances</li></ol><li>introduces the name of a god, without any following text to be recited &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) written in place of omitted text&#59; ‘the usual (words to recite)’</li><li>(rare,&#32;only attested in a single manuscript) introduces explanatory glosses to a text &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ḏd,&lrm; mdw.</li></ol><p><i>ḏd</i> (“to say”) +&lrm; <i>mdw</i> (“speech, words”), with <i>ḏd</i> in the infinitive.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Djedhor</li></ol><p><i>ḏd</i> (“to say”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”), thus literally ‘Horus says’. This might be either a shortened form of the name <i>ḏd-ḥr-jw.f-ꜥnḫ</i> (literally “Horus says, ‘He will live’”) or an ancient misreading of a hieratic name <i>ns-ḥr</i> (literally “the one belonging to Horus”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sting, to incite</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any crawling legless animal; snake, serpent, worm</li></ol><p>From <i>ḏdf</i> (“to form goosebumps, to stand on end”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to endure, to last</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Djedi</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Old Kingdom necropolis complex situated near modern Cairo: Djedu (now Abusir)</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) the afterlife</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of meat or fat) to be(come) fatty &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of animals) to be(come) fat &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of cereals) to ripen, to be(come) ripe &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of fields) to be(come) ripe with grain &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of the Nile) to make ripe, to ripen (grain or fields) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat</li><li>part of a plant used medicinally.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>pith</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḏfḏ</i> (“pupil (of the eye)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupil (of the eye) &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) food, provision</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a state of having been ‘given life (forever)’ achieved by the pharaoh</li></ol><p>Perfective passive participle of <i>rḏj</i> (“to give”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“life”), thus ‘given life’. Unlike other such passive participial clauses, this one has been fossilized without any resumptive pronoun (a construction like *<i>ḏj n.f ꜥnḫ</i> would be expected).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheet of papyrus for writing</li><li>roll of papyrus</li><li>official document or book of papyrus</li><li>papyrus basket</li><li>papyrus sandals</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn off to the side</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ḳrb-</i> or <i>&#42;ḳlb-</i>; compare Arabic ق ل ب (q l b).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rage, to be angry</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to subdue</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rage, wrath</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dnjt</i> (“dam”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skull</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wing</li></ol><p>Likely related to Arabic جَنَاح (janāḥ), either through inheritance or through borrowing; see there for more.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limit, end</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>since, from the time of</li><li>(<i>often with a verb in the terminative as object</i>) since, because</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>done, over, finished</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Djer, a pharaoh of the First Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>previously, before</li></ol><p><i>ḏr</i> (“since”) +&lrm; <i>bꜣḥ</i> (“phallus”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>since</li></ol><p><i>ḏr</i> (“since”) +&lrm; <i>ntt</i> (“that”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand</li><li>(<i>of an elephant</i>) trunk</li><li>(female) calf</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a town in Egypt at the site of modern El-Tod</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) ancestors &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḏrtj</i> (“falcon (as a god)”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limit, end</li></ol><p><i>ḏr</i> (“limit, end”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kite (falconiform bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) strange, foreign</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>ḏrḏr</i>: strange, foreign</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self</li><li>(<i>with a suffix pronoun</i>) -self</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with a suffix pronoun</i>) -self</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>himself</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by himself</li></ol><p>From <i>ḏs</i> (“self”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myself</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by myself</li></ol><p>From <i>ḏs</i> (“self”) +&lrm; <i>.j</i> (“my”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yourself</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by yourself</li></ol><p>From <i>ḏs</i> (“self”) +&lrm; <i>.k</i> (“your”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herself</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by herself</li></ol><p>From <i>ḏs</i> (“self”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“her”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) sacred</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to clear or keep clear (a road, a path) (+ <i>n</i>: for (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sunder, to separate, to isolate or segregate away (+ <i>r</i>: from)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) separated from</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sanctify, to make sacred &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Djoser, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Amenhotep I, a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḏsr</i> (“sacred”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘Sacred (One) of the Ka of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a strong ale with an anise flavor which gains a milky appearance when enough water is added to it &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>ḏsr</i> (“sacred”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cobra</li><li>static, unchanging eternity</li><li>body, form</li><li>statue</li><li>self</li><li>estate</li><li>serf</li><li>papyrus stalk</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) olive tree</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) olive (fruit)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Djet, a pharaoh of the First Dynasty</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forever</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic; compare Ugaritic <i>𐎇𐎚</i> (zt /⁠zêtu⁠/), Phoenician <i>𐤆𐤉𐤕</i> (zyt), Galilean Aramaic <i>זֵיתָא</i> (zētā), Classical Syriac <i>ܙܝܬܐ</i> (zaytā), Amharic <i>ዘይት</i> (zäyt, “olive oil”), Arabic زَيْتُون (zaytūn, “olive”), زَيْت (zayt, “oil, olive oil”), Hebrew <i>זַיִת</i> (záyit).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>ḏwj</i>: evil, bad, wicked</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the embodiment of evil, Apep</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) evil, bad, wicked, harmful</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the secretions of corpses, filth, etc.) to be(come) unclean</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>badly, wickedly</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay jug or jar, used especially for holding beer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to falsely speak ill of someone (+ <i>n</i>: to (a person of authority)); to malign, to slander, to tell on &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Likely from earlier <i>sḏwj</i> (“to slander”) by metathesis.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) evil</li><li>alternative form of <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Dw&#58;Z1</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (ḏw, “mountain”)</li></ol><p>Nominalized from <i>ḏw</i> (“evil, bad, wicked”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p><p><i>ḏw</i> (“mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p><i>ḏḏt</i> (feminine imperfective relative form of <i>rḏj</i>) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amon”), thus literally ‘(she) whom Amon gives’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Dedumose</li></ol><p><i>ḏḏw</i> (“who is given”) +&lrm; <i>ms(w)</i> (“birth”), thus ‘(the one) who is given birth’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thoth (god)</li><li>the month of Thoth</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Tuthmosis III, a pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḏḥwtj</i> (“Thoth”) +&lrm; <i>ms</i> (“to give birth”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine third-person singular stative ending), thus ‘Thoth is born’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the festival of Thoth, on which offerings were presented to the dead</li></ol><p><i>ḏḥwtj</i> (“Thoth”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather lacing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire drill (tool used to start fires)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audience hall</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Djahy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cross (a body of water)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ferry (a person)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interfere (+ <i>r</i>: with)</li></ol><p><i>ḏꜣj</i> (“to cross”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“land”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argument</li><li>civil war</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥnꜥ</i>) argue</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) oppose</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) male children or youths, boys, young men</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) (male) youth of a particular place</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) men as opposed to women or children</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) troops, soldiers</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) coming generation, generation yet to be born</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) peers, cohort, generation, people of the same age</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) young cattle, larger calves &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) need</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferry</li></ol><p><i>ḏꜣj</i> (“to ferry”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrongdoing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opponent, opposition &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) highway robber, brigand</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li></ol><p>Related to Arabic جُمْجُمَة (jumjuma, “skull”) and Biblical Hebrew <i>גֻּלְגֹּלֶת</i> (gulgṓleṯ, “skull”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>council, assembly, especially of magistrates; tribunal</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot (used for mixing drugs, burning fumigants, storing honey, etc.)</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>ḏꜣḏꜣ</i> (“head”). Compare the development of German <i>Kopf</i>, French <i>tête</i>, English <i>noggin</i>, each meaning ‘head’ from earlier ‘mug’/‘pot’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm, stormwind &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) altercation; fierce exchange of words</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) flatulence</li><li>a type of bread</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;medicine,&#32;of a person) to fart</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the sky) to be(come) stormy &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spearfish, to catch by spearfishing &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>ḏꜥr</i> (“to seek”)</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lay waste, to desolate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be laid waste, to be(come) desolate</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrum</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tanis (a historical city in Egypt)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seek out</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fresh river water</li><li>epithet for the primordial waters &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>waters of the inundation &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) water in general</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bread</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to clothe (a person or body part) (+ <i>m</i>: in, with (a garment)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) clothed (+ <i>m</i>: in, with (a garment))</li><li>(transitive,&#32;uncommon) to don, to put on (a garment) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hide, to cover up (a body part)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cover up (an object) with cloth, to cover or wrap (+ <i>m</i>: in, with (a cloth)) &lsqb;since the Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cover over, to cover (a place) (+ <i>m</i>: in, with (a substance: sand, rocks, floodwater, etc.; also, a crowd of people))</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of the sky) to cover (the earth)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to expand (a construction such as a temple or city) with further surrounding constructions (+ <i>m</i>: with (an annex, a defensive wall, etc.))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to furnish (a house)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of the king) to shield, to protect (Egypt) &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to bathe (something) in (light) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep (names, deeds, documents, etc.) secret</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment, piece of clothing &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>of the dead</i>) mummy wrappings</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) horsecloth, caparison</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) covering for a chariot</li><li>straining cloth, cloth through which a substance is strained, pressed, or sieved &lsqb;since the Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>bandage, cloth used for bandaging &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>cloth as a material, as used for wicks, sails, tents, bandages, etc.</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic حَبَّسَ (ḥabbasa), Hebrew <i>חָבַשׁ</i> (ḥāḇáš).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥbs</i> (“to clothe, to cover, to protect”) +&lrm; <i>bhn</i> (an article of clothing).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear a fan</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan-bearer</li></ol><p><i>ḥbs</i> (“to clothe, to cover”) +&lrm; <i>bht</i> (“fan, flabellum”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not want to see (someone), to be inaccessible to (someone’s) pleas</li><li>to be miserly or ungenerous toward (those in need)</li></ol><p><i>ḥbs</i> (“to clothe, to cover, to hide”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“facing, against”), thus ‘to cover the face toward’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gate in the afterworld</li><li>the porter of this gate, a minor god</li><li>a cavern in the afterworld</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥbs</i> (“(one that) clothes, covers, hides”) +&lrm; <i>bꜣg</i> (“the weary”), thus ‘veiler of the weary’, with ‘the weary’ a euphemism for the dead.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of apron</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ḥbs,&lrm; nṯr.</li></ol><p><i>ḥbs</i> (“garment”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘garment of the god’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title of a Lycopolitan priest &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥbs</i> (“clothed, covered, hidden”) +&lrm; <i>ḫprw</i> (“form”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘(the one) veiled of form’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) clothing</li></ol><p><i>ḥbs</i> (“to clothe”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloth, piece of cloth &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>wrapping, covering</li><li>clothing</li></ol><p><i>ḥbs</i> (“to clothe, to cover”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archery target</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to waddle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overthrow, to cast down, to prostrate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundred thousand</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a very large amount</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) snake</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a settlement in Egypt at what is now El Mo'alla</li></ol><p><i>ḥfꜣw</i> (“snake”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine suffix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake, worm</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hasten</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>course</li></ol><p><i>ḥjp</i> (“to hasten”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dagger, short sword</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic, ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ḥarb-</i>; compare Hebrew <i>חֶרֶב</i> (ḥéreḇ), Akkadian <i>𒄑𒀳𒌆𒆥</i> (ḫarbum).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cheer</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to praise, to acclaim</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise</li><li>one of the seven sacred oils used in mortuary rituals, made of myrrh, styrax resin, and acacia seeds</li></ol><p><i>ḥkn</i> (“to praise, to acclaim”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) magic, magic spell or act, sorcery</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Heka, the personification of magic</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the king of Egypt as a particular individual who serves as an incarnation of kingship</li><li>an incarnation<sup>?</sup> of a god (e.g. as a statue)</li><li>servant, slave</li><li>coward</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>moreover, and, in addition</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funerary priest; priest who supervised the cult of the dead and, under contract, supplied offerings to the kas of the dead</li></ol><p><i>ḥm</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘servant of the ka’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an official title; literally ‘royal servant’</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥm</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”) in a direct genitive construction. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥm</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘servant of the god’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high priest; the head of the priestly hierarchy of a temple during the New Kingdom.</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥm-nṯr</i> (“servant of god, priest”) +&lrm; <i>tpj</i> (“first”), literally ‘first servant of the god’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to give way, to yield, to retreat</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;imperative) turn back!</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to repulse, to drive away (+ <i>r</i> or <i>ḥꜣ</i>: from (a place))</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sit down, to sit (+ <i>ḥr</i>: on, at; + <i>m</i>: in; + <i>r</i>: beside, at; + <i>r</i> and infinitive: in order to do (something)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dwell, to stay or live (+ <i>m</i>: in, at; + <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>ḥr</i>) to besiege (a place) &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to occupy (a seat, etc.) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seat (someone) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li><li>wife</li><li>copper</li><li>the (female) pharaoh of Egypt as a particular individual who serves as an incarnation of kingship</li><li>an incarnation<sup>?</sup> of a goddess (e.g. as a statue)</li><li>(female) servant, slave</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥm</i> (“incarnation”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p><p>From <i>ḥm</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queen; wife of the king</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥmt</i> +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> in a direct genitive construction. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Royal Wife</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥmt-nswt</i> (“queen”) +&lrm; <i>wrt</i> (“great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priestess</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥmt</i> (“servant”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘servant of the god’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rudder, steering-oar</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) helmsman</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) skilled</li></ol><p>A participial form of <i>ḥm</i> (“to steer”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craftsmanship, skill, artistry</li><li>craftsmen (collectively)</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥmw</i> (“to be skilled”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artisan</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥmw</i> (“to be skilled”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmsman</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring (a temple, the land, etc.) into order</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to equip, to supply (buildings or people) (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to protect, to guard (something holy: a temple, tomb, or god) (+ <i>r</i>: against)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to order, to command (something to be done)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to order, to command (+ <i>n</i>: someone; + <i>r</i> and infinitive: to do (something))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to say forcefully, to impress (+ <i>n</i>: to, upon (someone))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to engage (people) to do something, to set to some task</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to entrust or charge (+ <i>n</i>: someone) to do (something)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to commend, to entrust (someone) (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone else))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go with speed and purpose, to hurry, to advance speedily (+ <i>r</i> and infinitive or + following clause: to go do (something); + <i>r</i>: to (a place); + <i>n</i>: to (a person); + <i>m</i>: from (a place); + <i>ḥnꜥ</i> or <i>jrm</i>: with (someone))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to attack, to move against an enemy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulrush</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to offer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collective) offerings</li><li>alternative form of <i>ḥnzkt</i> (“braided lock of hair”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥnk</i> (“to offer”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) a group of people or celestial beings of unclear, possibly originally religious, significance&#59; conventionally rendered sun-folk.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the king’s inner circle of personal attendants</li><li>the priesthood of Heliopolis</li><li>a group of divine beings associated with the north polar and eastern regions of the sky</li></ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) the people of a given place, especially Egypt</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) humankind</li></ol><p>Possibly ultimately from <i>nmj</i> (“to traverse, to travel”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to creep, to move stealthily &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoe</li><li>penis</li><li>part of a date, with medicinal applications</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hack up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hoe</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) narrow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swampy lake or canal &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a bucket or basin to hold papyrus &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the branch of the Nile connecting the river to the Fayum Oasis</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ends, far reaches, span (of time)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>span, term, duration of time &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>long time, eternity (+ genitive: endlessly many of (some span of time), an eternity of)</li><li>future, time to come</li><li>(<i>negated</i>) ends, limits of time &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) lifetime, lifespan (of someone) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) ends, far reaches, span (of space)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>uttermost ends, furthest reaches (of the sky) &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>span, space (of a unit of length) &lsqb;18th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>two sides (of a river) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be greedy or covetous (+ <i>ḥr</i>: after, over) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of lizard, possibly a gecko &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot, bowl</li><li>any item of property</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) ribs</li><li>boat of the god Sokar</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistress, lady</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) horn</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Henuttawy</li></ol><p><i>ḥnwt</i> (“mistress”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣwj</i> (“Egypt”, literally “the Two Lands”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Mistress of Egypt’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bind, to tie up</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braided lock (of hair) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥnzk</i> (“to bind, to tie up”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>along with, with (comitative)</li><li>and, in addition to</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) and furthermore; introduces a new subject in a letter, or else the proper text of the letter after any introductory formulae</li></ol><p><i>ḥnꜥ</i> (“together with, and”) +&lrm; <i>ḏd</i> (“speaking”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accompanying</li></ol><p><i>ḥnꜥ</i> (“along with”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>along with them, along with it</li></ol><p><i>ḥnꜥ</i> (“along with”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (adverbializing suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Apis</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steering oar for a ship: the implement depicted in the hieroglyph <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a3f986ca.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Aa5" alt="glyph" height="80"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to embrace</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hapy, a mortuary god, protector of the lungs of the deceased</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) hungry</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger</li><li>hungry man</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heqet, a frog-goddess of childbirth and fertility</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>king</li><li>governor, administrator</li></ol></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rule, govern</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Horus &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>an epithet of the king &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥqꜣ</i> (“ruler”) +&lrm; <i>jmt-pr</i> (“estate, bequest, will”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘ruler of the bequest’, in reference to the kingship mythologically bequeathed from Geb to Osiris and thereby to Horus. The term <i>jmt-pr</i> appears in its typical Greco-Roman Period form <i>jmj-pr</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for the god Osiris</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥqꜣ</i> (“ruler”) +&lrm; <i>jmꜣḫ</i> (“dignity, honor, reverence”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “ruler of reverence”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>local ruler, mayor</li></ol><p><i>ḥqꜣ</i> (“ruler”) +&lrm; <i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘ruler of the enclosure’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a foreign ruler generally</li><li>one of the Hyksos</li></ol><p><i>ḥqꜣ</i> (“ruler”) +&lrm; <i>ḫꜣst</i> (“foreign land”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘ruler of a foreign land’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) ruler</li><li>sceptre</li><li>rulership</li><li>a dry measure of volume equivalent to 10 <i>hnw</i>, or 4.8 litres.</li></ol><p><i>ḥqꜣ</i> (“ruler; to rule”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face (of a person or animal)</li><li>facial expression</li><li>sight, scope of vision</li><li>front surface (of a building, door, etc.)</li><li>bow (of a ship)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>upon, on, on top of</li><li>onto, on (with motion)</li><li>in, on (a vehicle, boat, etc.)</li><li>at, in (a place)</li><li>to, upon (a side)</li><li>in (water)</li><li>in, for (some duration of time)</li><li>at (some time)</li><li>and, in addition to</li><li>per, for each</li><li>from, originating from (a place)</li><li>because of, for the sake of</li><li>by means of, using</li><li>marked with, under (someone’s name or seal)</li><li>(<i>with following infinitive</i>) forms the periphrastic imperfective of a verb</li><li>(contracted from <i>ḥr ḏd</i>) saying; introduces a direct quotation</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horus, the falcon-headed Egyptian god of kingship &lsqb;since the 1st Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the currently reigning (living) pharaoh</li><li>conventional term introducing the serekh name of most pharaohs</li><li>(<i>only in the plural</i>) one of a number of minor Horus-gods &lsqb;19th Dynasty and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʕalay-</i>; compare Arabic عَلَى (ʕalā, “on, over”), Hebrew <i>עַל</i> (ʿal, “on”), Aramaic <i>עַל</i> (ʿal, “on”). If so, perhaps from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;x̣al</i>. However, some authors consider such a correspondence irregular.</p><p>According to Loprieno, formed from a stem <i>ḥaru-</i> + the Proto-Afroasiatic nominative case marker <i>&#42;-u</i>; in Egyptian, Proto-Afroasiatic case markers were generally lost, but <i>&#42;-u</i> became a glide <i>-w</i> instead when the stem ended in a vowel. The stem is probably related either to the preposition <i>ḥr</i> (“above”), with the theonym thus meaning ‘the One Above’, or to the verb <i>ḥrj</i> (“to be distant”), with the theonym meaning ‘the Distant One’, or to both.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the midst of</li><li>in the opinion of</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ḥr jb</i> (“in the midst of; in the opinion of”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) because … not</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“upon, because”) +&lrm; <i>jwtt</i> (“that not”), apparently formed following the model of <i>ḥr ntt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why?</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon, because of”) +&lrm; <i>mj</i> (“what”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>devoted or loyal to (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>mr</i> (“canal”), literally “upon (someone)’s canal”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>devoted or loyal to (someone) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>mw</i> (“water”), literally “upon (someone)’s water”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>devoted or loyal to (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>mw</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”), literally “upon (someone)’s water”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because, for</li><li>used in letters to introduce a topic</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“upon, because”) +&lrm; <i>ntt</i> (“that”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(spatially) behind</li><li>(temporally) after, at the end of</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ḥr,&lrm; sꜣ.</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterward, later</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>sꜣ</i> (“back”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on top of</li><li>on behalf of</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>tp</i> (“head”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why?</li><li>how?</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon, because of”) +&lrm; <i>zj</i> (“which”) +&lrm; <i>jšst</i> (“what”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥwj</i> (dual of <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Horus-Behdeti</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>bḥdtj</i> (“(one) of Edfu; Behdeti”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the king &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>an epithet of the god Horus of Edfu &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house, palace”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus “Horus in his house”. Compare <i>ḥr-m-ꜥḥ.f</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“forepart, front”), thus ‘Horus is at the fore’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harmachis or Horemakhet, a hypostasis of Horus as the deified Great Sphinx of Giza &lsqb;New Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣḫt</i> (“Akhet, place of sunrise”), thus literally meaning ‘Horus in the Akhet’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) the epithet of the deified Great Sphinx of Giza given on the Dream Stele &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr-m-ꜣḫt</i> (“Harmachis”) +&lrm; <i>ḫprj</i> (“Khepri”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”) +&lrm; <i>tm</i> (“Atum”), thus literally meaning ‘Harmachis-Khepri-Ra-Atum’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) an epithet of the king &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥḥ</i> (“palace”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus “Horus in his palace”. Compare <i>ḥr-m-pr.f</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>everyone</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“face”) +&lrm; <i>nb</i> (“every”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the king &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>nb</i> (“lord”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥḥ</i> (“palace”), thus “Horus, lord of the palace”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Harendotes, a form of Horus &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>nḏ</i> (“protecting”) +&lrm; <i>jt</i> (“father”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus ‘Horus who protects his father’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Horus the Child or Harpocrates &lsqb;since the 21st Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>pꜣ</i> (“the”) +&lrm; <i>ẖrd</i> (“child”), thus ‘Horus the Child’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Horus the Elder &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“great, elder”), thus ‘the elder Horus’, ‘Horus the Elder’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Harsomtus, a form of Horus &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>zmꜣ</i> (“uniting”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣwj</i> (“the Two Lands, Egypt”), thus ‘Horus who unites the Two Lands (Egypt)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Harsiese, a form of Horus &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a male given name, Harsiese</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>zꜣ</i> (“son”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣst</i> (“Isis”) with the latter two elements in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Horus, son of Isis’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hordjedef or Djedefhor</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>ḏd</i> (“stability”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus ‘Horus is his stability’. Possibly, if the name is written with honorific transposition, it could also be <i>ḏd</i> (“endures”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”), thus ‘He endures, (namely) Horus’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various gods with a royal function</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>usually</i>) epithet for Horus in various forms, especially as Horus of Edfu &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for Ra in various forms, including Sobek-Ra and Ra of Edfu &lsqb;New Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for Amun or Amun-Ra &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(rare,&#32;attested only once) epithet for Osiris &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>ḥrw</i> (“Horuses, lesser Horus-gods”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Horus of the Horuses’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horakhty, an aspect of the god Horus as the sun at dawn</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣḫtj</i> (“of the Akhet”). Later, in the New Kingdom, <i>ꜣḫtj</i> was reinterpreted as the dual of <i>ꜣḫt</i> (“Akhet”) instead of a <i>nisba</i> adjective derived from it, rendering a new interpretation of <i>ḥr-ꜣḫtj</i> as a direct genitive construction meaning ‘Horus of the Two Akhets’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upper, being above, lying upon</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supervisor, boss &lsqb;since the middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) distant</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fly off (to the sky)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to inspire dread or terror; to be(come) awesome or terrible</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dread</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to anticipate</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being in the midst (of)</li><li>(of a deity,&#32;followed by the name of a temple) indicates that a deity is worshipped in a particular temple, but that their main temple or shrine is elsewhere</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midst, middle</li><li>inmost parts (of someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥrj</i> +&lrm; <i>jb</i>, literally ‘being upon the heart (of)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) majordomo, head domestic servant &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥrj</i> (“(one) being above”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house”), thus ‘one who is above the house’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a high military rank; overseer of the troop</li></ol><p><i>ḥrj</i> (“being above”) +&lrm; <i>pḏt</i> (“bow (weapon)”), thus ‘(the one) over the bow’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being atop</li><li>chief, main</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief, head, leader</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥrj</i> (“being upon”) +&lrm; <i>tp</i> (“head”). The noun is thus literally ‘(he who is) upon the head’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower, blossom &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hostile snake in the Duat (underworld) &lsqb;Theban royal funerary literature and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>an obscure goddess, Hereret &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sky &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>sky as the dwelling-place of gods and the dead king</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;with following genitive) temple, earthly dwelling-place (of a god) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>temple roof &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>upper side, upper surface &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(female) supervisor, boss (+ genitive: of (wet nurses, harem women, a household, etc.)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the queen &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>epithet for the goddess Isis &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>inundation of the Nile &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>water in general &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>rock-cut tomb &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>particularly, one of the rock-cut royal tombs of the New Kingdom, such as those at the Valley of the Kings &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>necropolis (of a particular city) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>afterworld, realm of the dead &lsqb;Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) road, path, specifically one by land as opposed to water &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>female Horus, Horus-goddess, Horet</li><li>epithet for Isis or Hathor</li><li>conventional term introducing the serekh name of female pharaohs</li></ol><p><i>ḥr</i> (“upon, atop”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine nisba ending), thus ‘(the one) being atop’. Some early writings of the sense ‘sky’ use determinatives that perhaps instead suggest a connection with the verb <i>ḥrj</i> (“to be distant”).</p><p><i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) majordomo, head domestic servant</li></ol><p><i>ḥrt</i> (“(one) being above”) +&lrm; <i>pr</i> (“house”), thus ‘one who is above the house’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to travel by land &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for various gods &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare <i>ḥrt</i> (“road, path”).</p><p><i>ḥrt</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> suffix), thus ‘the celestial (one)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as well as, apart from, in addition to</li></ol><p><i>ḥrw</i> (“apart”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“regarding”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) cold or freezing &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) feces, excrement (of humans or animals) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break apart &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;rarely&#32;intransitive</i>) to determine the number of (something); to count or calculate, to reckon</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enumerate, to count out in series</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reckon with or settle up with (an offender) by inflicting punishment; to get even with, to exact merited retribution on</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pay back or punish (an evil deed)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;usually negated) to value, to care something for, to esteem (something)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) bone fracture</li><li>a quarter, one fourth (in general)</li><li>one quarter of a <i>sṯꜣt</i> (“aroura”) (equivalent to about 0.07 hectares)</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic حَسَبَ (ḥasaba, “to calculate, to count”), Hebrew <i>חָשַׁב</i> (ḥāšaḇ, “to think”), and Classical Syriac <i>ܚܫܒ</i> (“to think, to count, to calculate”). Alternatively identical in origin to the above word (‘to break apart’, etc.) by metaphorical extension; see the usage note below.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) worms<sup>?</sup> in the body</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to sing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;of people or animals) to behead &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of body parts, especially the head) to sever, to cut off &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to provide or supply (a person, place, or body part) (+ <i>m</i>: with (something))</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;with second object following <i>m</i>) to take to oneself, to take up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to round out, to complete</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people, bodies, and names) to perish, to be destroyed</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of hearts) to lose all courage</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to annihilate, to destroy, to eradicate (people, uncleanness, names, lies, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to quench (thirst)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to silence (mouths)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) content or satisfied (+ <i>ḥr</i> or <i>m</i> or (rarely, Late Period) <i>r</i> or (rarely, Greco-Roman Period) <i>n</i>: with) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to be(come) content or satisfied with &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) propitious, gracious, favorable, not hostile (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) peaceful</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) peaceable, pacified, propitiated, not hostile (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to reconcile with each other, to come to terms, to make peace</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a besieged city) to surrender (+ <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of natural phenomena) to be(come) peaceful, calm, not stormy or raging</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of years) to be(come) peaceful, not characterized by war</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to (come to) rest, to settle, to (stop and) rest (+ <i>m</i>: in, at (a place); <i>ḥr</i>: upon) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to come to, to get to (a place) &lsqb;18th Dynasty and Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to assume, to take (a seat or place) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to settle at, in, or on; to rest at (a place) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of celestial bodies) to set (+ <i>m</i>: on (the horizon), in (the west), etc.)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to satisfy, to make content &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to reconcile (hostile parties) with each other &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace, lack of war or hostility</li><li>contentment, satisfaction</li><li>graciousness, favorableness, mercy &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li><li>offering table, altar &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>offering, sacrifice, especially of food &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hetep or Hotep</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a funerary gift authorized by the king, generally not given by the king personally but by a local funerary establishment under the patronage of a particular god; a royal offering</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥtp</i> (“offering”) +&lrm; <i>ḏj</i> (perfective relative form of <i>rḏj</i>) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”), thus ‘an offering that the king gives’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Hotepsekhemwy, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḥtp</i> (“to be(come) content or satisfied; to be(come) peaceful, to reconcile”) +&lrm; <i>sḫm</i> (“divine power”) +&lrm; <i>-wj</i> (dual ending), thus ‘the Two Powers are content/satisfied’, or perhaps rather ‘the Two Powers are reconciled/at peace’, with the Two Powers likely a reference to Horus and Set.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Hetepheres</li></ol><p><i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“her”), thus literally ‘her face is content’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) offering</li><li>contentment, peace</li></ol><p><i>ḥtp</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace, calm, submission</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥtp</i> (“to pacify”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) offerings</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥtp</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>team or yoke of animals, particularly oxen</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoke</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hetes-sceptre</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mine (excavation)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) speaking, singing</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) drinking</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utterance, annunciation</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Hu, personification of utterance</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hit, to strike, to beat</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tread (a road)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḥꜣ</i> (“if only, would that”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) young (literally, in body or in akh) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to (re)gain the strength or vigor of youth, to be rejuvenated</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make young, to rejuvenate &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childhood, youth &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>rejuvenation &lsqb;since the 21st Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>youthful vigor</li><li>boy, child</li><li>young man, youth, lad</li><li>son, child (of someone, typically a god) &lsqb;New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>ribroast &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hit, to strike (+ <i>ḥr</i>: upon (the back)) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flow, to flood</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Huni, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥwj</i> (“to hit, to strike”) +&lrm; <i>nj</i> (an unidentified element).</p><p>The proper noun comes from the participle of the verb, thus ‘the hitting one’, ‘the striking one’, a posthumous variant of the king’s attested lifetime throne name <i>nswt-ḥw</i> (literally “the striking king”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) marriageable young woman, maiden</li><li>pupil of the eye</li></ol><p><i>ḥwn</i> (“young”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor man, wretch</li><li>inferior (in skill or accomplishment)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hauron, an Egyptian god, originating in the Canaanite pantheon, whose center of worship was in Giza.</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Ugaritic <i>𐎈𐎗𐎐</i> (ḥrn /⁠Ḥôrānu⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large structure</li><li>mansion</li><li>palace</li><li>temple</li><li>estate</li><li>rain</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shrine of Thoth in Heliopolis</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>jbṯt</i> (“bird trap”) in a direct genitive construction, thus “enclosure of the bird trap”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The temple of the ka of Ptah in Memphis, Egypt</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) Memphis, Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”) +&lrm; <i>ptḥ</i> (“Ptah”) with direct genitive constructions, thus literally ‘house of the ka of Ptah’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘house of the god’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avaris, a city in Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>wꜥrt</i> (“leg, part, district”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Hathor (the goddess of joy, love, and motherhood; one of the "Eyes / Daughters of Ra", the consort of Ra/Horus; often depicted as having a cow's head)</li><li>the month of Hathyr</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ḥwt</i> (“mansion”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”), literally “Mansion of Horus”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the temple at Edfu &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>the town of Edfu itself &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr-ḥrw</i> (“Horus of the Horuses”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘the enclosure of Horus of the Horuses’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in titles</i>) a significant building, particularly a palace or temple</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a temple in Heliopolis</li></ol><p><i>ḥwt</i> (“enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣt</i> (“great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to proclaim, to utter (+ <i>n</i>: to) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hazor (an important ancient Canaanite city in the Upper Galilee)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>חָצוֹר</i> (ḥāṣôr).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rot, to be(come) putrid</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putrefaction</li><li>matter seen as putrefying, such as feces or fluid discharged by a corpse</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥwꜣ</i> (“to putrefy”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) short in length &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of food and drink) to be(come) scarce, limited &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf, short person</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>may it please you</li></ol><p><i>ḥzj</i> (“to bless”) +&lrm; <i>.tj</i> (second-person singular stative ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to praise, to bless, to honor (someone, someone’s speech, someone’s conduct, etc.) (+ <i>ḥr</i>: for)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;uncommon) to honor (someone) with something, to favor, to reward (+ <i>m</i>: with)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to menstruate</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menstruation</li><li>natron &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>bronze &lsqb;since Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>amethyst</li></ol><p>Compare Hebrew <i>חַשְׁמַל</i></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blessing, favour</li><li>tall ewer or jar for water with a recurved body, a narrow neck, and a wide mouth, typically made of metal; hes-vase</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥzj</i> (“to bless”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favored one, esteemed one (+ following direct genitive: one favored by …, one esteemed by …)</li><li>epithet for one of the dead who have been judged righteous</li></ol><p>perfective passive participle of <i>ḥzj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>said person; this man</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥzy</i> (“favored one”) +&lrm; <i>jm</i> (“therein”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) wild or fierce</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesat, a sacred cow-goddess</li></ol><p>Feminine noun formed from <i>ḥzꜣ</i> (“milk”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine suffix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) white</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to set off at dawn</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mace</li><li>silver</li><li>damage</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) cheerful</li></ol><p><i>ḥḏ</i> (“to be(come) white”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”), thus ‘to be(come) white of face’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smash</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippopotamus</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the White Crown, Hedjet</li></ol><p>Nominalized use of the feminine form of <i>ḥḏ</i> (“white”). Nisba suffix present, as shown by the Demotic form <i>ḥṱy.t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) any <i>Allium</i> species; garlic or onions (as a cultivated plant, as a side dish to bread and meat, as an apotropaic against snakes and evil spirits, or used medicinally)</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥḏ</i> (“to be white”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>million &lsqb;since the 1st Dynasty, chiefly Old Egyptian&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a very large amount &lsqb;chiefly Middle and Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the floodwaters of the Inundation &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seek</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Heh or Huh, personification of an aspect of the primeval waters, possibly their infinity &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>ḥḥ</i>, in either its meaning of endlessness or floodwater.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armpit</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>if only, would that; expresses a wish, with uncertainty about it coming true, or a polite request</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back of the head</li><li>outside</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>behind</li><li>around</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ha, god of the western desert</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ḥww</i> (“to proclaim, to utter”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣ</i> (intensifying particle).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḥꜣ</i> (“if only, would that”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festival</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sed festival, a jubilee festival first celebrated after a king had ruled for thirty years and thereafter every three or four years</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣb</i> (“festival”) +&lrm; <i>sd</i> (“tail”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘festival of the tail’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Great Festival, one of the festivals at which offerings were given to the dead</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ḥꜣb,&lrm; wr.</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣb</i> (“festival”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“big, great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Festival of Sokar, a festival consisting of the dragging of the Sokar-bark and a procession called <i>pẖr ḥꜣ jnbw</i> (“circling around the walls”), celebrated on the 25th (later 26th) of Choiak</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣb</i> (“festival”) +&lrm; <i>zkr</i> (“Sokar”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Festival of Sokar’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to celebrate a festival</li><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to mourn</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surrounding</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) naked</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣ</i> (“behind, around”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to catch (a fish), to fish up &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;by extension) to fish (anything) out of water &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;by extension) to catch other entities, such as fowl, snakes, or the dead, with a net &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fish with (a net) &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to catch red-handed, to catch (someone) doing something forbidden</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fish &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare with Hebrew <i>חרם</i> (ḥerem, “net”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conceal, to hide</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to plunder</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forepart, front</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;of animals</i>) front part of the body, forepart</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) forehead, brow</li><li>bow, prow of a ship</li><li>beginning, point of time when something starts</li><li>opening, incipit, beginning of a text</li><li>vanguard of an army or fleet</li><li>place where a locality begins; outer limits</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) first (among some group), chief, foremost, most distinguished one</li><li>best, choicest (of something)</li><li>tomb &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a small unit of time, smaller than a <i>ꜣt</i> but larger than a <i>ꜥnt</i>&#59; approximately a second &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>food &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name of historical usage, notably borne by Hatshepsut</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”) +&lrm; <i>špswt</i> (plural of <i>špst</i> (“noblewoman”)) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘foremost of noblewomen’. A longer form of <i>ḥꜣt-špswt</i> is found as <i>ẖnmt-jmn-ḥꜣt-špswt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front, beginning”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm, part”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frontal</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) first</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heart</li><li>mind, where thought and emotions are experienced</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a jewel</li></ol><p>From <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p><p>The noun is traditionally regarded as simply a nominalized use of this <i>nisba</i> adjective, thus literally ‘the frontal (organ)’. However, note the different developments in Coptic. Some have instead suggested an Indo-European source for the noun; compare Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱḗr</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high official</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣtj</i> (“frontal”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘frontal of arm’, i.e. ‘one whose arm is in front’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>best oil for anointing</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) prow rope</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the feminine of <i>ḥꜣtj</i> (“frontal, first, best”), thus a <i>nisba</i> formed from <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine <i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abundance, riches</li><li>surplus, extra, excess</li><li>exaggeration</li><li>naked man</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣj</i> (“to bare, to be(come) naked”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) islanders, particularly of the Aegean</li></ol><p><i>ḥꜣw</i> +&lrm; <i>nbwt</i> (“Aegean islands”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;with the floor as object) to stumble</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to play (a game) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;lʕb</i>. Compare Arabic لعب (lʕb).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) excited or active</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) happy; to rejoice</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Nile River</li><li>the god Hapi</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) inundation of the Nile</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wick</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh of a body, sometimes as opposed to bones or organs</li><li>body of a person or god</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) body of an animal</li><li>(<i>with attached suffix pronoun</i>) -self &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) body parts, limbs (collectively)</li><li>fleet of ships &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rob (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to plunder (something) away, to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excitement, rejoicing</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placenta</li><li>child</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>perfective of <i>ḫbj</i> (“to dance”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dance</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break up (the soil), especially by ploughing</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break apart, to destroy (enemies or places)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of hippopotamus hunters) to plough through (water) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) ploughland</li><li>violence &lsqb;Late Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tail</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beard</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hack up</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go downstream, to go northwards</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of water) to flow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>across from, opposite, facing, before (in front of)</li><li>(<i>with an inanimate object</i>) in accordance with, according to</li><li>(<i>with an infinitive as object</i>) when, while</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>accordingly</li></ol><p><i>ḫft</i> (“across from, in accordance with”) +&lrm; <i>jrj</i> (“regarding it”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>considering that, in view of the fact that</li></ol><p><i>ḫft</i> (“facing”) +&lrm; <i>ntt</i> (“that”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in front of</li></ol><p><i>ḫft</i> (“across from”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opposing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, adversary</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫft</i> (“facing, opposite”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”); the noun is simply a nominalized use of the nisba adjective.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) enemy</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫft</i> (“facing, opposite”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine nisba ending”); the noun is simply a nominalized use of the nisba adjective.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>accordingly</li></ol><p><i>ḫft</i> (“across from, in accordance with”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (“adverbializing suffix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to nourish oneself, to feed (+ <i>m</i>: on, from) &lsqb;Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to illuminate (something), to brighten, to fill with light</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, meal &lsqb;Pyramid Texts, Book of the Dead, and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḫfꜣ</i> (“to nourish oneself, to feed”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bank, waterside</li><li>field &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grasp, to seize</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remain ignorant of; to not know or learn</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Letopolis</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighth</li></ol><p><i>ḫmn-</i>, root of <i>ḫmnw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Hermopolis</li></ol><p>Compare with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ṯamāniy-</i>.</p><p>From the numeral eight (<i>ḫmnw</i>), as Hermopolis was the cult centre of the eight gods of the Ogdoad.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Ogdoad, the group of eight gods representing aspects of the primeval waters worshipped at Hermopolis</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫmnw</i> (“eight”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think, to consider</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stab &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>three fourths</li></ol><p><i>ḫmtw</i> (“three”) +&lrm; <i>rw</i> (plural of <i>r(ꜣ)</i> (“part, piece, <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>…</sub></small>”)).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>third</li></ol><p><i>ḫmt-</i>, root of <i>ḫmtw</i> +&lrm; <i>-nw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to Guanche <i>amiat</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butt, grip</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phrase, utterance</li><li>matter, affair</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tread &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tread, especially of foreign lands &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to walk, to go &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend (a staff) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to plait, to entwine (mats or wreaths)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lower leg or foot</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne or other seat of a king or a god, including stools, litters, and seats in a boat &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>stairway &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḫnd</i> (“to tread”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of small oval or circular roll of bread</li></ol><p><i>ḫnf</i> +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to alight, to land</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smell (a smell)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe in, to inhale (air, wind, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make (something) smell good</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to please (someone) with a gift (+ <i>m</i>: of)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be pleased</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of goddesses) to rear (a god in the form of a child)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be friendly with</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend</li><li>a kind of beer</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) (female) nurse or attendant of a child, especially of a god in the form of a child</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) prostitute</li></ol><p><i>ḫnm</i> (“to attend on or rear a child”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p><i>ḫnm</i> (“to make pleased”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smell, aroma, fragrance, especially a pleasant one</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) breathed air, breath &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫnm</i> (“to breathe, smell, make sweet-smelling”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to snatch, to steal</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to catch (a ball)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to present (an offering, especially of water)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to confine, to restrain (enemies, criminals, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to forbid (a name) to be spoken &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hinder, to close off the way of (the dead in the afterworld)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prisoner, restrained enemy or criminal</li><li>harem (as an institution, not a building), including its inmates</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison</li></ol><p><i>ḫnr</i> (“to confine”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminality &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫnrt</i> (“prison”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine <i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khonsu</li><li>the month of Pachon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in front of</li><li>as foremost of; at the head of</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jar stand</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khentakhtai-perti, a god</li><li>the month of Pauni</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foremost</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go forward, to advance, to be(come) foremost</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go upstream, to go southwards</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Set</li></ol><p><i>ḫnt</i> (“at the head of”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Foremost of the Westerners, an epithet used for various gods of the dead such as Osiris and Anubis</li><li>Khentiamentiu, a god of the dead and patron god of the necropolis at Abydos</li></ol><p><i>ḫntj</i> (“foremost”) +&lrm; <i>jmntjw</i> (“westerners, i.e. the dead”), plural of <i>jmntj</i> (“western(er)”).</p><p>The word is first attested in the tombs of First Dynasty kings Den (<i>dn</i>) and Qaa (<i>qꜣ-ꜥ</i>), where it is associated with the dead kings. However, there is no certain evidence that it referred to a separate deity (rather than being an epithet for other deities) until the second half of the Fifth Dynasty.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Amun</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫntj</i> (“foremost, being in front of”) +&lrm; <i>jpt</i> (“Luxor, inner sanctum”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus “(the one) presiding over his inner sanctum/Luxor”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Anubis</li><li>an epithet of the god Osiris &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>a priestly title &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḫntj</i> (“foremost, being in front of”) +&lrm; <i>zḥ-nṯr</i> (“the hall in which Anubis embalmed Osiris”, literally “divine booth”), thus ‘(the one) presiding over the divine booth’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title, conventionally rendered ‘land-tenant’ or ‘attendant’ &lsqb;Old Kingdom to 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>orchard, plantation</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for Lebanon</li></ol><p><i>ḫntj</i> (“foremost”) +&lrm; <i>š</i> (“district, garden”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘foremost of the district/garden’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>previously, before</li></ol><p><i>ḫnt</i> (“at the head of”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (“adverbializing suffix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upstream or southward travel</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫntj</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to walk about, to fare</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to have enjoyment, to be glad</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rejoice over, to be happy about</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gladden, to please &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy</li><li>alternative form of <i>ḫntj-š</i> (“orchard”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of stinging insect</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to traverse, to travel through (a region or place) by foot or by boat</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;uncommon) to travel (+ <i>r</i>: to)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;medicine,&#32;of disease) to spread</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the dual</i>) buttock(s), butt, rump</li></ol><p>Possibly from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;paḫVǯ-</i>. Compare also Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;paḫ(i)ḏ-</i>, Arabic فَخِذ (faḵiḏ, “thigh”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to travel, to walk</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;euphemistic) to die, to pass away</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strange, weird, unfamiliar &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come into being, to start to exist &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come to exist or take place (in the currently relevant context); to occur, to happen, to appear, to arise (+ <i>m-ꜥ</i>: to happen to) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to evolve, to develop, to transform (+ <i>m</i>: to turn into, to become) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of time) to come, to arrive</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Djedefre, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Senusret I, a pharaoh of the Twelfth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫpr</i> (“manifestation, form”) +&lrm; <i>kꜣ</i> (“ka”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“Ra”), thus literally ‘the Manifestation of the Ka of Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the god Atum</li></ol><p><i>ḫpr</i> (“(one who) came into being”) +&lrm; <i>ḏs</i> (“self”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“his”), thus literally ‘He Who Came into Being by Himself’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khepri, the god personifying the morning sun</li></ol><p>Nominalization of the imperfect active participle of <i>ḫpr</i> (“to come into being”), thus literally, “the one coming into being”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarab</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physical form, shape &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>form, mode of being</li><li>visible form, manifestation</li><li>cultic image of a god</li><li>thing that has come into being, creation, development, creature</li><li>transformation, metamorphosis (usually of the dead)</li><li>stage of life, age</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫpr</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Blue Crown of the Pharaoh, sometimes referred to as the “war crown”.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreleg of an animal</li><li>strong arm (of a human)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) strength</li><li>khopesh</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the Big Dipper (literally “the Foreleg”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the presence of</li><li>into the presence of, to</li><li>by, through the choice or presence of</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with following subjunctive or nonverbal predicate</i>) then, and then, so; introduces a subordinate clause describing the inevitable result of an action in a preceding clause</li><li>(<i>with following suffix pronoun/subject and imperfective</i>) must, has to; introduces a subordinate clause describing a necessity</li><li>(<i>introducing a prepositional phrase</i>) then; also functions as a converter to allow a prepositional phrase to begin a sentence</li><li>(<i>with following suffix pronoun, and optionally also noun, indicating the speaker</i>) marks a non-future direct quotation; says, said</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fell</li></ol><p>Possibly derived from <i>ḫrw</i> (“voice”).</p><p>Compare Arabic خَرَّ (ḵarra, “to fall”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present, in the presence of</li><li>adjacent, situated nearby</li></ol><p><i>ḫr</i> (“in the presence of”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to oversee, to govern, to direct</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden mallet for hammering in a mooring post &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature and Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḫsr</i> (“to dispel”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) matters, happenings, affairs</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) state, condition</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) possessions</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) requirements (of food)</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) products (of a place)</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the feminine form of <i>ḫrj</i> (“present, being in the presence of”), itself a <i>nisba</i> adjective from <i>ḫr</i> (“in the presence of”); thus literally ‘what is in the presence (of)’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish, desire</li></ol><p><i>ḫrt</i> (“matters, state, requirements”, feminine of <i>ḫrj</i>, <i>nisba</i> of <i>ḫr</i> (“in the presence of”)) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘(what is) in the presence of the heart’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li><li>sound</li><li>enemy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with following dependent pronoun, noun, or both, indicating the speaker</i>) marks a non-future direct quotation; says, said</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>ḫr(w)</i> (quotative particle) +&lrm; <i>.fj</i> (third-person singular suffix pronoun). The significance of the apparently superfluous suffix pronoun is not clear.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li></ol><p>Compare <i>ḫrw</i> (“enemy”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strife, war</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫrwy</i> (“enemy”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) slaughter, butchery &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) sacrificial animals &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;figuratively) misfortune, ruin &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ḫsbḏ</i> (“lapis lazuli”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapis lazuli</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bar, to thwart, to fend off (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to prevent from doing)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to punish (+ <i>n</i>: to punish (someone))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to oppose</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break someone’s opposition</li><li>to defend against someone’s power</li></ol><p><i>ḫsf</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜣt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sail upstream</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dispel, to drive away, to remove</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Arabic خَسَرَ (ḵasara, “to lose”) or Hebrew <i>חָסֵר</i> (khasér, “absent, missing”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) thing, goods, possession, property</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;masculine</i>) something, anything</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) food, meal</li><li>wood</li><li>tree</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) timbers</li><li>stick, staff, pole, rod</li><li>mast</li><li>a rod, a measure of length equivalent to 100 <i>mḥw</i>, or 52.5 metres. In full <i>ḫt n nwḥ</i>.</li><li>(<i>only in set phrases; see derived terms below</i>) rear; what is behind or after</li><li>fire</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>throughout</li></ol><p><div>From an earlier form <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">x&#58;t</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (jḫt).</div></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) an unknown plant, a tree or shrub that either is hairy or has hairy-looking inflorescences, fruits, or seeds &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḫt</i> (“tree”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>šnj</i> (“hair”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;a kenning) plant-based food, often specifically corn or grain as food for humans in opposition to herbs (<i>smw</i>) for cattle &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ḫt</i> +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜥnḫ</i>, literally “the wood of life”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bird</li></ol><p><i>ḫt</i> +&lrm; <i>ꜥꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to retreat</li><li>to carve, to engrave</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seal</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic خَتَمَ (ḵatama, “to seal”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to reverse, to undo or turn back</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hatti &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the inhabitants of Hatti &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Khu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Khufu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḫw</i> (“shall protect”, subjunctive mood of <i>ḫwj</i> (“to protect”)) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”) +&lrm; <i>wj</i> (“me”), thus literally ’He shall protect me’; the longer version of the name, <i>ẖnmw-ḫw.f-wj</i>, reveals that ‘he’ is the god Khnum.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>ḫw.n</i> (perfect relative form of <i>ḫwj</i>) +&lrm; <i>jnpw</i> (“Anubis”), thus literally ‘(he) whom Anubis has protected’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rich man</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to protect, to defend (a person, body, or place) (+ <i>m-ꜥ</i> or (<i>Late Period</i>) <i>r</i>: against, from; + <i>m</i>: by means of, with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rule, to serve as protector of (a place)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to guard (someone to be restrained), to keep watch over in order to restrain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exclude, to exempt, especially from tax dues</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set aside for some purpose, especially to set aside as holy or sacred</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to prevent, to preclude, to avoid</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to furnish, to equip (+ <i>m</i> or (<i>Greco-Roman Period</i>) <i>ẖr</i>: with) &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what is to be avoided or precluded</li><li>particularly, evil deeds, baseness, wrongdoing &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>ritual pollution &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫwj</i> (“to protect, to avoid, to prevent”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pound</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to build</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, of historical usage, notably borne by Xerxes I, a pharaoh of the 27th Dynasty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠</i> (Xšayār̥šā).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck of a human</li><li>throat</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one thousand</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) herd</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government office</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be young</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be small</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a decaroura, a measure of area equivalent to 10 <i>sṯꜣwt</i> (“arourae”) or 1000 <i>mḥw-tꜣ</i> (“cubits of land”) (about 2.76 hectares).</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫꜣ</i> (“a thousand (<i>mḥw-tꜣ</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>tꜣ</i> (“land”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippopotamus</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) bent, to bend</li></ol><p>According to one suggestion, from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ḫab-</i> (compare Akkadian <i>𒀖𒀠</i> (/⁠upātum, ubātum⁠/, “pregnant or fat cow”)). However, an internal origin has also been proposed, from <i>ḫꜣb</i> (“to be bent”) in reference to the animal’s bent tusks; compare the relation between <i>db</i> (“hippopotamus”) and <i>db</i> (“horn, tusk”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) lamp</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of the sun) to shine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Khabash</li></ol><p>Possibly of Libyan origin.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to measure</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to examine (a person or organ) medically</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by the Predynastic king Khayu</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend (one’s arms or back) as a sign of reverence &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bow down in reverence &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to hold on to, to keep in one’s possession</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to succeed in</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Hurrian</li><li>more generally, a Syrian</li></ol><p>From Hurrian <i>𒄯𒊒</i> (ḫur-ru /⁠ḫurw⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign land</li><li>desert</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) foreigners</li></ol><p><i>ḫꜣst</i> (“foreign land”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (plural ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disease, illness</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pain, woe</li><li>alternative form of <i>ḫꜣwt</i> (“altar”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;always negated) to have mercy, to take pity (+ <i>jm</i>: to have mercy on, to spare) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Arabic غُرُوب (ḡurūb, “sunset”) and Hebrew <i>עֶרֶב</i> (érev, “evening, night”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altar</li><li>altar chamber</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plumb line</li><li>guideline</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫꜣj</i> (“to measure”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disease, suffering &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>haste</li><li>great difficulty</li><li>scramble</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) fast</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hurry, to hasten</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to winnow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw down</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cast aside, to discard</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to kill</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to abandon (property)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to send (a message)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Khaba, a pharaoh of the Third Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḫꜥ</i> (“rising, shining forth, appearing in glory”) +&lrm; <i>bꜣ</i> (“active or efficacious power”) in a direct genitive construction, thus loosely ‘one whose active power appears in glory’ (literally ‘(one) appearing-in-glory of active power’).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Khaemwaset</li></ol><p><i>ḫꜥ</i> (“appearing”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>wꜣst</i> (“Thebes”), thus ‘(the one) appearing in Thebes’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Khasekhemwy, a pharaoh of the Second Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ḫꜥ</i> (“appear”) +&lrm; <i>sḫm</i> (“divine power”) +&lrm; <i>-wj</i> (dual ending), thus literally ‘the Two Powers appear’, in reference to Horus and Set.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Khafre or Khafra, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>ḫꜥ</i> (“to appear”) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”) +&lrm; <i>rꜥ</i> (“sun, Ra”), thus meaning ‘he appears as Ra’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of celestial bodies) to rise</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to appear (in glory)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shine forth</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) rising of a celestial body; sunrise, moonrise, starrise</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) appearance in glory</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>appearance of the image of a god during a festival or procession</li><li>accession of a king to the throne</li></ol><li>crown (in general, of any kind)</li><li>crocodile &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) equipment, tools, implements</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>farm implements</li><li>cutlery, serving utensils</li><li>tackle of a ship</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) tomb goods</li><li>musical instruments &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) weaponry</li></ol><p><i>ḫꜥj</i> (“to appear in glory”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p><p>From the plural of <i>ḫꜥ</i> (“a kind of hoe”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the stative) to be sandaled, to be shod (with sandals)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to provide with sandals</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crate or box for holding fowl &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sebennytos, a city in Egypt</li><li>the twelfth nome of Lower Egypt, of which Sebennytos was the capital</li></ol><p>From <i>ṯb</i> (“calf”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘calf of the god’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition. The Akkadian and Greek spellings may reflect a form <i>ṯb n nṯr</i>, with an analytic genitive.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandal &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>sole of the foot &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) you (see usage notes)</li></ol><p>Compare with Kabyle <i>kemm</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) red (of a particular shade, perhaps that of red ink for writing)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) redness (as a symptom of illness)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ṯm</i> (feminine second-person singular dependent pronoun) + an element <i>-t</i> that forms older independent pronouns from dependent pronouns.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libyan</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cadaster, land registry</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes)</li></ol><p>From earlier *kina</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you two (see usage notes)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thinis &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where?</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift up, to elevate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to distinguish (+ <i>r</i>: to distinguish (something) from (something else))</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) distinguished or elevated</li></ol><p>Blaz̆ek (2004) compares Proto-Berber <i>&#42;ăknəʔ</i> (“to arrange, do”) and Somali <i>keen</i> (“to bring”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of drink, in later times certainly beer &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>each one</li><li>number (quantity)</li><li>loftiness, eminence, distinction</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thinis or This, a city that served as the first capital of a unified Egypt and later capital of the nome of Abydos</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>census, count</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ṯnw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawk</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne dais of a king or god, especially the dais of Horus &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>sedan chair</li><li>a litter for carrying a sacred animal &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>throne hall, especially in the context of the Sed festival</li></ol><p>Probably derived from <i>ṯnj</i> (“to elevate, to be distinguished”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake burrow</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) cave of a lion</li><li>a hypothetical cavern that serves as the source of the Nile</li><li>tomb</li><li>space or cavern in the afterworld</li><li>used as part of the names of various other specific caverns</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) chapel in a temple</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) throat, esophagus</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wink, to blink &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greater white-fronted goose (<i>Anser albifrons</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;uncountable) papyrus plant(s)</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;uncountable) area where papyrus grows; papyrus marsh</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gore with the horns</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stick or thrust (something) (+ <i>m</i>: into)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribe</li></ol><p>From Canaanite <i>&#42;sōpir-</i>. Semitic translation of Egyptian <i>zẖꜣw</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>you (see usage notes) &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ṯw</i> (masculine second-person singular dependent pronoun) + an element <i>-t</i> that forms older independent pronouns from dependent pronouns.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vertebra</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) spine</li><li>neck</li><li>support</li><li>sand dune</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) drought</li><li>sentence</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie, to knot</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weave</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to join or put together</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to build</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to organise or marshal, especially of troops</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to levy (troops or laborers)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to clot, to coagulate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise, to lift up, to pick up</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hound (<i>variously described as “greyhound”, “basenji”, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic. Cognates include Kabyle <i>aqjun</i>, Blin <i>ግድጝ</i> (gédéň) and Birgit <i>kájàŋ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knot &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>detachment of troops</li><li>range of mountains or hills</li></ol><p><i>ṯz</i> (“to tie, to knot, to marshal”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine suffix”)</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the dead (literally ‘(inhabitant) of the (desert) hills’)</li></ol><p><i>ṯzt</i> (“range of mountains or hills”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (nisba ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by the Predynastic king Tjesh</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive&#32;(with <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>)) to encounter, to meet (someone) on the way</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to touch (something) in order to harm it, to disturb, to molest</li><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive&#32;(with <i>ḥnꜥ</i>)) to engage (an enemy) in battle</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to gleam, to shine out &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;chiefly&#32;of body parts) to be(come) cheerful or display cheer &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(blue/green) faience &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>colorful glass &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of precious stone from Nubia &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ṯḥn</i> (“to gleam”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libya &lsqb;since the 1st Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the Libyans</li></ol><p><i>ṯḥn</i> (“to gleam”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) joy &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>droplet, drop</li><li>pellet</li><li>small pearl</li><li>small glob of earth</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put on (clothes)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cloak or veil oneself</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of wounds) to close</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandage</li><li>swaddling clothes</li><li>cloak</li><li>wrappings for a cultic image</li><li>mummy wrappings</li><li>foreskin</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make fast, to fasten, to take possession of</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vizier; the head of Egypt’s national administration &lsqb;Since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the highest official in enumerations</li><li>(<i>later</i>) a sub-official of the vizier</li><li>Used of various gods&#58; Thoth, Amon, and Har-wer. &lsqb;Since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) Used of the king. &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for the queen and for Hathor&#59; literally, ‘female vizier’ &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ṯꜣtj</i> (“vizier”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wind</li><li>a breath (an exhalation of air)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to steal &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rob (a person) of &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burgle away (from a house) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male, male person; man or boy</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to kill (a person or animal) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to kill generally in battle (+ <i>m</i>: among (enemies)) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) adorned or decorated</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adornment, decoration</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panoply, insignia</li><li>ornaments</li></ol><p><i>ẖkr</i> (“to be(come) adorned or decorated”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to approach (+ <i>m</i> or <i>n</i>: to approach (someone or some place)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Upper Egypt between Aswan and Thebes</li></ol><p><i>ẖn</i> (“interior”) +&lrm; <i>nḫn</i> (“Hierakonpolis”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to row</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to join, to unite [<i>with</i> <b>m</b> <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Khenemetneferhedjet</li></ol><p><i>ẖnmt</i> (“united with”) +&lrm; <i>nfr-ḥḏt</i> (“White Crown”), thus literally ‘united with the White Crown’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Khnum</li></ol><p>Possibly from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;xanam</i>; compare Arabic غَنَم (ḡanam, “sheep and goats”). Also compare Tarifit <i>amyan</i> (“goat”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Khnumhotep</li></ol><p><i>ẖnmw</i> (“Khnum”) +&lrm; <i>ḥtp</i> (“to be content”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (masculine singular stative ending), thus ‘Khnum is content’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throne name&#x20;notably borne by Khufu, a pharaoh of the Fourth Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ẖnmw</i> (“Khnum”) +&lrm; <i>ḫw</i> (“shall protect”, subjunctive mood of <i>ḫwj</i> (“to protect”)) +&lrm; <i>.f</i> (“he”) +&lrm; <i>wj</i> (“me”), thus literally ’Khnum, he shall protect me’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trouble, to disturb</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disturbance, tumult, turmoil</li><li>rebellion, civil war</li><li>troublemaker</li></ol><p>From <i>ẖnn</i> (“to trouble, to disturb”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Hathyr</li></ol><p><i>ẖnt</i> +&lrm; <i>ḥwt-ḥr</i> in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘water-procession of Hathor’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statue</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interior, inside (of a place, building, body part, etc.)</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;as a genitive modifying another noun) inner, covering the interior &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>space between any number of points, enclosed space</li><li>home, residence, abode</li><li>homeland, home, the interior of Egypt<sup>?</sup></li><li>residence of the king, with its associated administrative complex; palace, capitol, capital city, ‘the Residence’</li><li>temple or shrine as the residence of a god &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>goods that a land or mountain contains or produces; products, contents, abundance</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rowing crew of a boat</li></ol><p><i>ẖnj</i> (“to row”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>umbilical cord</li><li>navel</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ẖpꜣ</i> (“navel”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>under, (spatially) beneath</li><li>under, subordinate to</li><li>(<i>of people or their hands</i>) carrying, holding</li><li>(<i>of containers and spaces</i>) containing, holding</li><li>having, in possession of, provided with</li><li>in (a state or condition)</li><li>in, at (the beginning or end)</li><li>from the cause of, through</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>under the care or supervision of</li></ol><p><i>ẖr</i> (“under”) +&lrm; <i>st</i> (“place”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>at the front (of); before, preceding</li><li>(<i>temporal</i>) before, previous (to)</li></ol><p><i>ẖr</i> (“under”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childhood</li></ol><p><i>ẖrd</i> (“child”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>under it, under them</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lower, lying under</li><li>carrying, bearing, having</li><li>being in (a state or condition)</li><li>being the place where (someone) is</li></ol><p><i>ẖr</i> (“under”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“adverbializing suffix”).</p><p><i>ẖr</i> (“under”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (“nisba ending”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victim of wrong</li><li>evildoer</li></ol><p><i>ẖrj</i> (“(one) lying under, carrying, being in”) +&lrm; <i>jw</i> (“wrongdoing, wrong, misfortune”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necropolis, cemetery</li><li>the afterworld, the Duat</li></ol><p><i>ẖrj</i> (“lying under, being the place of”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”), thus literally ‘that which is under the god’ or ’place where the god is’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porter</li></ol><p><i>ẖrj</i> (“being under, carrying”) +&lrm; <i>qnj</i> (“bundle”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Old Kingdom title, sometimes rendered royal chamberlain</li></ol><p><i>ẖrj</i> (“lying under”) +&lrm; <i>tp</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>nswt</i> (“king”), thus literally ‘(one) under the head of the king’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lector priest</li></ol><p><i>ẖrj</i> (“(one) bearing”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣb</i> (“festival scroll”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assistant (also used as a title)</li></ol><p><i>ẖrj</i> (“(one) bearing”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession, belonging</li></ol><p><i>ẖr</i> (“having”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (“feminine nisba ending”), probably to be interpreted as a nominalized ‘reverse <i>nisba</i>’, i.e. as ‘that which is had’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daily needs</li></ol><p><i>ẖrt</i> (“possession, belonging”) +&lrm; <i>hrw</i> (“day”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small wooden box used to hold scrolls and scribal implements</li></ol><p><i>ẖrt</i> (“(thing) being under”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belowness, base, bottom</li></ol><p>An abstract noun formed from <i>ẖr</i> (“under”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) testicles &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the dual of <i>ẖrj</i> (“being under”), thus literally ‘the two underneath’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abdomen, belly</li><li>stomach, belly as the place where eaten food goes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) desire for food, hunger, gluttony</li><li>trunk, torso</li><li>trunk of the body as the seat of breath, life-force (ka), thoughts, emotions, etc.</li><li>womb</li><li>body, corpse &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>belly or bowels of a ship</li><li>underside of a body part</li><li>section of a building &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>individual mass or batch of a substance &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>&lsqb;22nd Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>copy or transcript of a document</li><li>wording of a document</li></ol><li>group of people or (especially) gods, body, corporation</li><li>group more generally, e.g. of stars, jackals, etc.</li><li>generation of people</li><li>alternative form of <i>ḥrst</i> (“carnelian”) &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Has been compared with Proto-Central Chadic <i>&#42;ḫway-</i> (“stomach, belly, intestines”), which would imply a tentative Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ḫVwVy-</i> (“stomach”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feeble man, failing man, coward</li><li>pitiful and vile man, wretch</li></ol><p>From <i>ẖzj</i> (“to be(come) failing, wretched”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) feeble, failing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) incapable, feeble, inept, incompetent, weak &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people,&#32;with <i>r</i> and following infinitive) to have difficulty (doing something), to need great effort (to do) &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of strength or the heart/mind) to be(come) feeble, failing, exhausted, weak, to give out (+ <i>m</i>: in (doing something)) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of woodwork) to rot, spoil, give out &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) wretched, to be(come) pitiful and bad</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;often of enemies) to be(come) wretched, pitiful, especially contemptibly so; to be(come) weak and vile</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) useless or cowardly in battle &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of speech, words, etc.) to be(come) bad, wretched, vile</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of deeds and coduct) to be(come) bad, wrong, mean, vile, to be(come) misdeeds</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of times) to be(come) bad, unpleasant to experience</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) cunning, devious (+ <i>r</i>: to be hostile against)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) rebel, malcontent</li></ol><p><i>ẖꜣk</i> (“to be(come) cunning, devious”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), literally ‘devious of mind’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack of cloth or leather</li><li>a dry measure of volume equivalent to 10 <i>ḥqꜣwt</i> (about 48 litres) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;.</li><li>a dry measure of volume equivalent to 4 <i>jpwt</i> or 16 <i>ḥqꜣwt</i> (about 76.8 litres) &lsqb;from Second Intermediate period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widow</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse</li><li>body of marsh water or lagoon in the Nile delta</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap of corpses</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to engage (an enemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shave</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Egyptian vulture (<i>Neophron percnopterus</i>) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>a bird in general &lsqb;11th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>intensifying or emphasizing particle, indeed &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to New Kingdom and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in clauses with a verbal predicate in the perfect</i>) marks a statement as hypothetical or contrafactual</li><li>(<i>Neo-Middle Egyptian</i>) also, and &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i>) to enter or tread (a place)<sup>?</sup> &lsqb;from Papyrus Westcar&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of feet) to tread &lsqb;Greco-Roman period&rsqb;</li><li>only used in <i>jrj ꜣ r gs</i>; possibly a variant of the verb ‘to tread’ above. &lsqb;from Papyrus Westcar&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ʔay-</i> (“bird of prey”). Compare also Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔayy-</i> (“bird of prey”).</p><p>Possibly from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ʔa-</i> (“to walk, to go”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stop, to take a break &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cease or stop [<i>with</i> infinitive <i>or</i> (in the Greco-Roman Period) <b>r</b> <i>or</i> <b>m</b> <i>or</i> <b>n</b> (+ infinitive) <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to avoid</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tarry or stay for a while [<i>with</i> <b>ḥr</b> <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to part with, to separate from &lsqb;Late Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb; [<i>with</i> <b>r</b>]</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fingernail &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ꜣbb</i> (“to separate from”).</p><p>Likely related to <i>ꜣbw</i> (“elephant, ivory, tusk”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;intransitive,&#32;with <i>r</i>) to separate from, to move away</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ꜣb</i> (“to avoid, to cease, to separate from”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>month</li><li>festival of the second day of the lunar month</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to desire, to want, to long for (a thing, a person, or a place) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;rarely&#32;intransitive&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>,&#32;with following infinitive) to want to do &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>,&#32;Late Egyptian) to take pleasure at, to be happy about &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔabay-</i>. Compare Hebrew <i>אבה</i> (ʾāḇā, “to be willing”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a type of bird &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a type of sacred fish &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brand, branding iron &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to 12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>&lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>an Abydene reliquary for the head of Osiris: the object depicted by the hieroglyph <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./55c33bff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="R17" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>.</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣbwt</i> (“family”)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ꜣbw</i> (“to brand”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cessation (+ <i>m</i>: cessation of (something))</li><li>elephant &lsqb;Old Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>elephant tusk &lsqb;Since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>ivory &lsqb;Since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) brand, branding iron</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a substance used medicinally</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elephantine (modern Aswan)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to brand (cattle or slaves) (+ <i>m</i> or + <i>ḥr</i>: to brand with (a seal or name, etc.)) &lsqb;since New Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scorch (the skin)</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣb</i> (“to tarry, avoid, cease”).</p><p>From Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;leb-</i> (compare Tangale <i>labata</i>, Mukulu <i>ʾelbi</i>).</p><p>Likely related to <i>ꜣb</i> (“fingernail”) via the ‘ivory, tusk’ sense.</p><p>Related to <i>ꜣbt</i> (“brand”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of cloth that serves as a cultic object or relic, sometimes associated with Horus and seen as having protective and mortuary functions &lsqb;Late Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣbwt</i> (“appearance, form”) +&lrm; <i>nṯr</i> (“god”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘form of the god’. However, the full form <i>&#42;ꜣbwt-nṯr</i> is unattested, as the word-final <i>t</i> had fallen silent and was often left unwritten by the time of this term’s earliest attestation. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) extended family, relatives, people of a household &lsqb;Sixth Dynasty to Middle Kingdom and Book of the Dead; rare thereafter&rsqb;</li><li>appearance, outward form &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>often followed by ‘the enemy’ in a genitive construction</i>) the symbol or effigy of an enemy to be ritually destroyed, often in the form of a sacrifical animal &lsqb;Late Period to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Sometimes hypothesized to be from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ʔab-</i>, in which case it would perhaps be cognate with Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʔabw-</i> (“father”).</p><p>Uncertain. Perhaps related to <i>ꜣbt</i> (“brand, branding iron”), <i>ꜣbw</i> (“brand, branding iron”). Less likely, some authors have connected it with <i>ꜣbj</i> (“to desire”), thus ‘desirable image’, or to <i>jꜣbt</i> (“left side”) because enemies were associated with the left.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lithe spotted big cat, including both leopards and cheetahs; ‘panther’ &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheetah, <i>Acinonyx jubatus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣby</i> (“lithe spotted big cat”) +&lrm; <i>mḥw</i> (“Lower Egypt”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘panther of Lower Egypt’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard, <i>Panthera pardus</i>, usually as a simile for bloodlust or fury</li></ol><p><i>ꜣby</i> (“lithe spotted big cat”) +&lrm; <i>šmꜥ</i> (“Upper Egypt”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘panther of Upper Egypt’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (female) lithe spotted big cat, including both leopards and cheetahs; female ‘panther’</li></ol><p><i>ꜣby</i> (“leopard or cheetah, panther”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Abydos</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) the afterlife</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Abydenes, the people of Abydos collectively &lsqb;26th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of fish, often used medicinally, and mythologically said to pilot the solar barque or, in later times, to be a form of the sun god &lsqb;since the medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Uncertain. A development from <i>ꜣbw</i> (“elephant”) +&lrm; <i>ḏw</i> (“mountain”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘elephant of the mountain’ in reference to the local topography, has been suggested.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to combine, to unite, to put together (something) (+ <i>n</i>: with, to) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i> or <i>ḥr</i>,&#32;medicine,&#32;of drugs) to mix into or with</li><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive&#32;(with <i>m</i>)) to join (someone)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with <i>m</i>) to suffuse with (something abstract, e.g. happiness, fragrance, strength, radiance)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to link (arms) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to merge, to unite, to mingle (+ <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with; + <i>n</i>, + <i>m</i>, + <i>r</i>: with, into)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to amass, to flock together</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to engage (in battle)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A type of liquid used medicinally.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a mixture or solution &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></ol><p>Probably from <i>ꜣbḫ</i> (“to combine”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) delighted</li></ol></ol><p>Probably a reduplication of <i>ꜣbj</i> (“to desire, to want, to be happy”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;chiefly of crocodiles) to be(come) aggressive or savage &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) raging, angry (+ <i>r</i>: with, toward, + <i>ḥr</i>: about, over)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) aggressively eager or rapacious, to raven (+ <i>r</i>: to be rapacious for, to raven after) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of fire) to rage at, attack, or harm (someone) &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;rare,&#32;of body parts including the heart) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be(come) feeble, listless, or failing</li><li>to quiver or palpitate</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) to decay &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to smear (a pot) (+ <i>m</i>: with (clay))</li><li>to smooth (a pot, etc.) (+ <i>m</i>: with (clay))</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggression, rage, fury</li></ol><p>Faulkner suggests this word may be a corrupt writing of <i>ꜣhd</i> (“to be(come) weak; to quiver”); Erman and Grapow instead suggest a connection with <i>jꜣd</i> (“to suffer”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rage at, to be aggressive toward</li></ol><p><i>ꜣd</i> (“to be aggressive or angry”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“regarding, against”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to prepare (beds or skins) for sleeping (in some unclear way); to make the bed</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocodile &lsqb;since the Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>aggressive or raging person, aggressor</li></ol><p><i>ꜣd</i> (“to be aggressive or raging”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (agent nominalizer).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to gorge oneself, to be greedy &lsqb;Coffin Texts to Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) yield or catch from hunting fish and fowl &lsqb;Book of the Dead and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to boil, to seethe &lsqb;medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to boil &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with following <i>n</i>) to acclaim, to praise (someone) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gluttony, greed &lsqb;12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>glutton, greedy person &lsqb;12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a minor god, ‘the Glutton’, ‘the Devourer’ &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>ꜣfj</i> (“to gorge oneself, to be greedy”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood, inundation &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of body parts) to be(come) weak or powerless</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to quiver or palpitate</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow, suffering &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>jhm</i> (“to mourn, to suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>. The expected form <i>&#42;jhmt</i> is unattested; by the time of the earliest attestation of this word, the sounds represented by <i>ꜣ</i> and <i>j</i> had already merged.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) pain &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) sadness, sorrow, misery, trouble &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) injury, illness &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) damage to property &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>sufferer, aggrieved or afflicted person</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) viscera in general (both of humans and of animals), including the brain</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) scum, contemptible person; a term of abuse</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) brain</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ꜣjs</i> (“viscera”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ḏnnt</i> (“skull”), literally “viscera of the skull”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;dual only) (pair of) bandages to hold together a gaping wound</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;hapax legomenon,&#32;of the elbow) to be(come) bent, to bend</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aker, the Egyptian god of the earth and the horizon</li><li>the earth (in parallel to the atmosphere <i>šw</i>, as a location traversed by the dead king) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) one of many gods representing forces of the earth, often imagined as serpents</li></ol><p>Cognate with Proto-Berber <i>&#42;a-ʔḱal</i> (“earth, land”) (whence Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⴰⴽⴰⵍ</i> (akal), Tuareg <i>ăkall</i>, and Tarifit <i>cař</i>).</p><p>False cognate of Sumerian <i>𒀳</i> (engar, “farmer”), and therefore unrelated to any of its descendants such as Akkadian <i>𒅅𒅗𒀸</i> (ikkarum), Hebrew <i>אִיכָּר</i> (ʔikkā́r), Arabic أَكَّار (ʔakkār), etc.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of external symptom of disease &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of fire) to burn</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be consumed by fire, to burn (+ <i>m</i>: to be burned by (fire))</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to be consumed by fire, to burn (+ <i>m</i>: to burn with (fire))</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Seizer: epithet for various divine entities in lion-like forms &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>epithet of Horus in the form of a lion</li><li>epithet of Horus in the form of a hieracosphinx</li><li>epithet of the king, presumably as Horus</li><li>used in reference to a gargoyle or waterspout, presumably in the form of Horus as a lion</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ꜣmm</i> (“to grasp, to seize”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mix (+ <i>ḥr</i>: with (something); + <i>m</i>: into (a resulting mixture)) &lsqb;Coffin Texts and medical papyri; sporadically later&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trample out (grapes for wine)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grasp, to physically seize (a person or thing; + <i>m</i>: with (one’s hand)) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of animals) to seize as prey (+ <i>r</i>: to attack as prey)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fold or clench together (one’s hands) &lsqb;26th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take (+ <i>n</i>: to, for (oneself)) &lsqb;Offering Ritual and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grip, grasp, fist &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣmm</i> (“to grasp”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;hapax legomenon) gripings or spasms &lsqb;Late Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>ꜣmm</i> (“to grasp, to clench”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of club or mace, the ames-sceptre, used as a weapon by the king and the gods &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>an epithet of the god Osiris &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jwms</i> (“exaggeration, nonsense”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wield the ames-sceptre &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) only used in <i>ꜣms-jb</i> (“to be(come) happy, to be(come) cordial”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣm</i> (“is grasped”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“she, it”) or <i>ꜣm</i> (“is grasped”) +&lrm; <i>sj</i> (“she, it”), thus literally ‘it is grasped’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) happy &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(with following <i>n</i>) to be(come) friendly or cordial to</li></ol><p><i>ꜣms</i> (verb with no independent meaning) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) burning, flame &lsqb;since the Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>(medicine,&#32;uncountable) name of a disease</li></ol><p><i>ꜣm</i> (“to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) burning &lsqb;Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣm</i> (“to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of perching bird &lsqb;Middle Kingdom and Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condyle or fork of a bone &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>ramus of the mandible &lsqb;Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of mash &lsqb;20th Dynasty and Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣmꜥ</i> (“perching bird”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>, so named because its attachment to the temporal bone was reminiscent of the claw of the <i>ꜣmꜥ</i>-bird grabbing something, as described in the Edwin Smith Papyrus 8.14–8.15.</p><p>Possibly related to <i>ꜣmj</i> (“to mix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird in general</li><li>small waterfowl, perhaps particularly a duck</li><li>alternative form of <i>jpdw</i> (“furniture”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) a constellation, literally ‘the Bird’, corresponding to modern Triangulum and Perseus</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come hastening, to rush onward (+ <i>r</i>: to, towards) &lsqb;18th Dynasty; with preposition, Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart) to beat more quickly out of love &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>(transitive,&#32;hapax legomenon) &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to copulate with</li><li>to hasten to, to rush to</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;uncountable) birds, fowl &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣpd</i> (“bird”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) A kind of animal given as an offering. &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>alternative form of <i>jpḥ</i> (“piglet”)</li><li>misspelling of <i>ꜣpd</i> (“waterfowl”)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The month of Apellaios in the Ancient Macedonian calendar &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀπελλαῖος</i> (Apellaîos).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people, things, and lands) to perish, to die, to fall to ruin &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of strength, fear, things, names, etc.) to fade away, to wane, to dwindle</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of mass) to be lost in the process of cooking</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart/mind) to become forgetful</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the arm) to become lame</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruin, devastation, destruction</li><li>misfortune</li></ol><p><i>ꜣq</i> (“to perish, to fall to ruin, to wane”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣq</i> (“to fade, to perish”) +&lrm; <i>-yt</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jqḥw</i> (“battle-axe”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drive off, to repulse (+ <i>ḥr</i> or <i>m</i>: from) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to harry or oppress (the poor)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;hapax legomenon)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>frustration</li></ol></ol><p>Compare <i>ꜣr</i> (“to drive off, to harry”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Ancient Greek, of historical usage, equivalent to English Alexander, notably borne by Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀλέξανδρος</i> (Aléxandros).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuller’s earth or more generally fuller’s mixture used to bleach cloth, for example including natron, oil, myrrh, and wine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, of historical usage, notably borne by Artaxerxes I</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎫𐎧𐏁𐏂𐎠</i> (a-r-t-x-š-ç-a /⁠Artaxšaça⁠/).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive</i>) to rush, make haste, to hurry, (<i>of water</i>) to flow quickly &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overtake (someone) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hurry (someone) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣs</i> (“to be(come) bald”)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;often reduplicated) quickly, hurriedly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣjs</i> (“viscera”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be hasty or impatient</li></ol><p><i>ꜣs</i> (“to rush, make haste”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flames</li></ol><p><i>ꜣzb</i> (“to glow fiercely, to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isis, an important goddess associated with magic and motherhood</li><li>a female given name, Iset or Aset, equivalent to English Isis</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Isetemkheb</li></ol><p><i>ꜣst</i> (“Isis”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣḫ-bjt</i> (“Chemmis”), thus ‘Isis is in Chemmis’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Isetnofret</li></ol><p><i>ꜣst</i> (“Isis”) +&lrm; <i>nfrt</i> (“beautiful”), thus ‘Isis is beautiful’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ꜣzḫ</i> (“to reap, to harvest”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ꜣzḫ</i> (“sickle”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>particular point in time, moment &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>span of time in general, time, while &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a small unit of time, smaller than an hour (<i>wnwt</i>) but larger than a <i>ḥꜣt</i>&#59; approximately a minute &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>striking power, readiness or ferocity to strike &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣt</i> (“mound”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣdw</i> (“aggressor”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣd</i> (“to be aggressive or angry”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣyt</i> (“to blanch<sup>?</sup>”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Atef, a white feathered crown worn by gods, and particularly by Osiris &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be crowned with the Atef crown &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;transitive) to be provided or equipped (± <i>m</i>: with) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣṯt</i> (“bed, bier”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of chest or coffer</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣṯp</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cargo of a ship</li><li>load to be carried</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) something wearisome or oppressive, burden</li></ol><p><i>ꜣṯp</i> +&lrm; <i>-w</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>. A form with the original <i>-ṯ-</i> as the second radical is unattested for this word.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣṯyt</i> (“nanny, nurse”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extent, length, expanse (of space) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) length, duration (of time)</li><li>death &lsqb;Middle Kingdom, New Kingdom, and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jw</i> (“wrongdoing, evil”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>happy or well-disposed one</li><li>farsighted one</li></ol></ol><p><i>ꜣw</i> (“long, extended”) +&lrm; <i>ḥr</i> (“face”), thus literally ‘the (one who is) long of face’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) wide, to be(come) long</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) long (in time or duration)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) rich or abundant in something, particularly offerings &lsqb;Late Period, Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;shortened from <i>ꜣwj-jb</i>) to rejoice, be(come) glad &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to extend, to stretch out (a hand or arm)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give as an offering</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to present or deliver, particularly a document to an authority</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to announce or present (a person) (+ <i>n</i>: to someone)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to arouse<sup>?</sup> oneself (to fight)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be generous</li><li>Used in <i>ꜣwj ḏrt r</i>.</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ꜣwj,&lrm; ḏrt.</li></ol><p><i>ꜣwj</i> (“to be long, to extend, to give an offering”) +&lrm; <i>ḏrt</i> (“hand”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) to lay hands on (someone) hostilely, to do violence to</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ‘to extend one’s hand to’ — see ꜣwj, ḏrt, r.</li></ol><p><i>ꜣwj</i> (“to extend (a hand)”) +&lrm; <i>ḏrt</i> (“hand”) +&lrm; <i>r</i> (“toward”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) happy &lsqb;chiefly Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>to rejoice, make merry &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣwj</i> (“be long”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”), thus literally ‘be long of heart’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extend one’s arm, especially in order to take or give (+ <i>n</i>: to someone)</li><li>to give as an offering &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣwj</i> (“to extend, to offer”) +&lrm; <i>ꜥ</i> (“arm”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tremble &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to early Middle Kingdom and Ebers Papyrus&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jwsw</i> (“balance scales”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length, duration (of time) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>offering, gift, donation (particularly of bread) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>altar &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a hidden chamber in a temple containing cult images &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣwj</i> (“to be long, to extend (a hand), to give an offering”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness, joy</li><li>a pectoral amulet in the form of the hieroglyphs <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6fa7d088.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Aw" alt="glyph" height="68"/><br /><img src="./3f72c79e.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="ib" alt="glyph" height="37"/></td>
</tr></table>
</td></tr></table> ligatured, presumably intended to bring the wearer happiness &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>realgar (red arsenic sulfide) &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Most likely from the infinitive of <i>ꜣwj-jb</i> (“to be happy”); however, some instead suggest a derivation directly from <i>ꜣwt</i> (“length”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”) in a direct genitive construction. The former would imply the noun is masculine; the latter, feminine. It is attested a few times modified by (apparently masculine) <i>nb</i>, suggesting that the former is the case, but the omission of feminine adjective endings is also not unusual.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the goddess Nekhbet</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering(s), gift(s) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣwj-ꜥ</i> (“to extend one’s arm, to offer”), thus literally ‘stretching out the arm’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocrator (usually as a title of a Roman emperor) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>autocrator</i> or Ancient Greek <i>αὐτοκράτωρ</i> (autokrátōr).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plant-derived substance from Punt, possibly a gold-colored resin, used as incense &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jerusalem</li></ol><p>From a West Semitic compound the second part of which is <i>&#42;salim</i>, which meant either “peace” or “Shalim (<i>the Canaanite god of twilight</i>)”. For more information see ירושלים.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drive out (+ <i>r</i>: from)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to do violence (+ <i>r</i>: to)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to serve &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb; [<i>with</i> <b>n</b> <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the face) to blanch, to turn pale with fear<sup>?</sup> &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bald-headed vulture</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to glow fiercely, to burn</li></ol><p>Perhaps compare <i>nsbw</i> (“flame”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to harvest or reap grain with a sickle</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to harvest or reap with a sickle</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvest, yield, what has been reaped</li><li>sickle &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the god Ash</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the stative,&#32;of meat, figs, or bread) to be(come) roasted, to roast &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roast meat, including burnt offerings &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint of roast meat</li></ol><p><i>ꜣšr</i> (“to be roasted”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of mash or dough made from grains, probably unleavened</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field; cultivated plot of land</li><li>earth or soil from arable land (used in brickmaking)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>akh</li><li>ghost, spectre, spirit of the dead interfering with the living</li><li>Used as an epithet for certain lesser gods and demons &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>the state of being an akh or qualities pertaining to that state: spiritual power or dignity</li><li>mental faculty or skill</li><li>thicket of papyrus</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) effective, to be(come) useful</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) splendid, radiant, glorious</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) an akh</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) well-disposed or serviceably minded (+ <i>n</i>: toward) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣḫ</i> (“to be effective or useful”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart, mind”), thus literally ‘to be effective of heart’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(with favoring person attached to <i>jb</i> as possessor) to find favour with (someone), to be(come) thought of highly by (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣḫ</i> (“to be(come) effective, useful, splendid”) +&lrm; <i>m</i> (“in”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”), literally “to be effective in (someone’s) heart”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with following noun or suffix pronoun</i>) it’ll do (someone) good, it’ll be good/beneficial for (someone)</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ꜣḫ,&lrm; n.</li></ol><p><i>ꜣḫ</i> (“to be(come) effective, useful, splendid”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“for”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mythological island, Chemmis, where Horus was said to have been reared in secret, most often identified with a lake site near Buto</li></ol><p><i>ꜣḫ</i> (“papyrus thicket”) +&lrm; <i>bjt(j)</i> (“king of Lower Egypt”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘papyrus thicket of the king of Lower Egypt’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by the 14th century <small>BCE</small> pharaoh Akhenaten, possible father of Tutankhamon and founder of Atenism</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜣḫ</i> (“effective”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“for”) +&lrm; <i>jtn</i> (“sun-disc, Aten”), thus literally meaning ‘Effective for the Aten’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>burning appetite</li><li>condition of the body and mind after eating a great deal, surfeit</li></ol></ol><p>Gardiner hypothesizes a relation with <i>ꜣḫfḫf</i> (“to blaze (of the eyes)”), for which compare also <i>ḫfꜣj</i> (“to illuminate”). Federn, following a different interpretation of the word, contrarily proposes a connection with <i>ḫfꜣt</i> (“food”), <i>ḫfꜣ</i> (“to fill with, to be swelled with”), and <i>ḫfḫf</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the eyes) to be fiery, to blaze &lsqb;Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps a reduplication of <i>ꜣḫf</i> (“burning appetite<sup>?</sup>”). Compare also <i>ḫfꜣj</i> (“to illuminate”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akhet; the region in the sky in which the sun tarries just before it rises (later also the region in which it tarries just after it sets), giving rise to the twilight</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) royal tomb</li><li>inundation season; the first of the three Egyptian seasons, spanning from July to November.</li><li>something beneficial or useful</li><li>flame, fire &lsqb;Book of the Dead to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>sacred eye of a god</li><li>uraeus</li><li>arable land &lsqb;New Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ꜣḫ</i> (“akh”).</p><p>From <i>jꜣḫj</i> (“to be inundated”).</p><p>From <i>ꜣḫ</i> (“to be effective, to be useful”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>From <i>jꜣḫ</i> (“to shine”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Akhetaten, Amarna: the capital city newly established and built by the pharaoh Akhenaten</li></ol><p><i>ꜣḫt</i> (“Akhet”) +&lrm; <i>jtn</i> (“Aten”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Akhet of the Aten’, i.e. the place where the sun rises.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Great Pyramid of Giza, including the surrounding complex of mortuary temples and tombs</li></ol><p><i>ꜣḫt</i> (“Akhet”) +&lrm; <i>ḫw.f-wj</i> (“Khufu”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally “Akhet (place of sunrise) of Khufu”. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of gods</i>) of, relating to, or dwelling in the Akhet, the region where the sun rises or sets</li><li>of or relating to the season of inundation</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dweller of the Akhet, the region where the sun rises or sets; chiefly used as an epithet for gods</li></ol><p><i>ꜣḫt</i> (“Akhet”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p><p><i>ꜣḫt</i> (“inundation season”) +&lrm; <i>-j</i> (<i>nisba</i> ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effectiveness, usefulness, capability</li><li>skills, expertise</li><li>magical power or efficacy</li></ol><p>An abstract noun derived from <i>ꜣḫ</i> (“to be effective”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A room of some kind.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>stillroom</li><li>storeroom</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of plants or fields) to be(come) verdant, to grow green with vegetation</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of people or popularity etc.) to develop happily or successfully, to prosper</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make verdant &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a constellation or other group of stars</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) Part of a ship’s sailing equipment.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>spars</li></ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collective) bones &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to nurse or rear (a child)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take care of, to nourish</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put a cargo in or on (a vessel, animal, or person); to load (+ <i>ẖr</i>, + <i>m</i>: to load with) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to load (a cargo) (+ <i>r</i>: into (a ship)) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;often&#32;figurative) to be(come) laden</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) laden with trouble or hardship; to be(come) oppressed or miserable</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cargo of a ship</li><li>load to be carried</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) something wearisome or oppressive, burden</li></ol><p><i>ꜣṯp</i> (“to load”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li><li>bier of Osiris</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣṯt</i> (“bed, bier”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child caretaker, nanny, nurse &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜣṯj</i> (“to care for”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scratch with claws or nails</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carve (an inscription or picture)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;hapax legomenon) scratch, scratch wound</li></ol><p><i>ꜣẖꜥ</i> (“to scratch, to carve”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mound of ruins &lsqb;Middle to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜣꜣt</i> (“a type of club or scepter”) &lsqb;Greco-Roman period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>name or epithet of a Nubian rebel leader &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare the Meroitic name <i>𐦠𐦫𐦡𐦫𐦡𐦵𐦬𐦢</i> (arereteli).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpreter, dragoman</li><li>foreigner (especially from Nubia)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harm, oppression, especially of the poor and unfortunate &lsqb;18th Dynasty to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜣfj</i> (“to gorge oneself, be greedy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storage case for scribal materials, papyri, and other implements &lsqb;Old and Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>&lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>part of a temple</li><li>a construction tool or material</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) &lsqb;11th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>An ankh-shaped implement</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to coat, spread, or patch up with plaster; to plaster &lsqb;18th and 20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to accuse</li><li>to injure, to harm</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to speak a foreign language, to gibber</li><li>&lsqb;Theban royal funerary literature&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥꜥw</i> (“to sleep”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) speaker of a foreign language, generally a foreigner or interpreter</li><li>a type of tree &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a liquid used medicinally &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥ</i> (“tomb”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place name &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>According to the Rösslerian school of Egyptian comparison, from earlier <i>&#42;lꜥꜥ</i>, from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;laɣ- (&#63;)</i> (“to speak”); compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;lɣz</i>, Arabic لَغَزَ (laḡaza, “to speak enigmatically, to use puzzling or riddle-like language”), Hebrew <i>לעז</i> (“to speak a foreign language”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥb</i> (“bowl”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥn</i> (“baboon”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥn</i> (“epithet of Thoth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) vessel, container, jar</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>nb-ꜣꜥꜥw</i> &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥꜥw</i> (“to sleep”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm, hand &lsqb;since the First Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>‘hand’ of an animal: hoof of a cow, foot of a crocodile, etc. &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>part, portion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>specific measure of a substance such as food, liquid, or myrrh; portion</li><li>individual item of something countable, item, piece &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>pair of something generally found in pairs &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><li>region</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>area, region, portion (of a country, piece of land, or building) &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>region of the sky where something, particularly the sun, is located</li></ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) state, condition</li><li>(chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;with following infinitive as a genitive) manner (of doing something, such as moving or flying)</li><li>official document, register, record &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>certificate, deed, warrant &lsqb;since the Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>dyke &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Late Egyptian</i>) wooden shaft, particularly as a carriage pole or drawbar</li><li>house &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;yad-</i> (“hand”).</p><p><div>From earlier <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">a&#58;t&#42;pr</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>ꜥt</i>, “room”), or else a formation from the same root.</div></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audience chamber &lsqb;Middle Kingdom, rarely later&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>part of the apartments of the king</li><li>part of a temple &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be dirty or impure, to pollute</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horn</li><li>flank (of an army)</li><li>horned crocodile</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Arabic عَابَ (ʕāba, “to shame, to disgrace”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make an offering &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜥb</i> (“to unite, to present”) +&lrm; <i>ḫt</i> (“thing”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥb</i> (“to unite”) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥbḫn</i> (“frog”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abrasax &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Ancient Greek <i>ἀβρασάξ</i> (abrasáx).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleanness, purity</li><li>purification, cleaning</li><li>cleaning agent</li><li>something wicked or bad</li><li>dirt, filth, pollution</li><li>lettuce</li></ol><p><i>wꜥb</i> (“to purify, to be pure”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p><p><i>ꜥb</i> (“to pollute, to make impure”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frog</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering-stone</li><li>tombstone</li><li>altar</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to boast (+ <i>m</i>: to boast of)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Neo-Middle Egyptian</i>) there &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fly (insect)</li></ol><p>According to the Rösslerian school of Egyptian comparison, from Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ðuṗṗ</i>, and so cognate to Hebrew <i>זבוב</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) brewer &lsqb;Old Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box, chest</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encampment, camp</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) roasted grain</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥwg</i> (“to roast”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acre, a city in Israel</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic, possibly from Hebrew <i>עַכּוֹ</i> (ʿAkkō).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to swallow, devour</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to know</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be neglectful or careless</li></ol><p><i>ꜥm</i> (“to swallow”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ammit or Ammut, an Egyptian goddess who devours the hearts of the dead who are judged unrighteous.</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ꜥm</i> (“(one) devouring”) +&lrm; <i>mwt</i> (“dead”), literally “devourer of the dead”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥm-mwt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Am-heh, an Egyptian god of the afterworld.</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥm</i> (“eat”) +&lrm; <i>ḥḥ</i> (“million”), literally “devourer of millions”.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple bakery &lsqb;New Kingdom to Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥm-mwt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn away &lsqb;Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥm-mwt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to massage [<i>with</i> <b>n</b> <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bread container</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;with or without <i>r</i>) to throw a throwing stick at</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throwing stick</li></ol><p><i>ꜥmꜥꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) again, once more, anew</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) back, to a previous condition or place</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) furthermore, in addition</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;negated) nor yet, nor</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;negated) nevermore, never again</li><li>(<i>Neo-Middle Egyptian</i>) here &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing tablet made of wood or metal &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>kind, pleasant man</li></ol><p>From a form of <i>ꜥnn</i> (“to turn back”).</p><p>Nominalized from the participle of <i>ꜥnj</i> (“to be beautiful, pleasant, kind”).</p><p>Compare <i>ꜥf</i> (“there”) and <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“here, there”), apparently paralleling the several series of demonstrative determiners with endings <i>-n</i>, <i>-f</i>, <i>-ꜣ</i>, for instance <i>pn</i>, <i>pf</i>, and <i>pꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian halfa grass, salt reedgrass (<i>Desmostachya bipinnata</i>) &lsqb;New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps a noun of instrument from the verb <i>ꜥnb</i> (“to close, to twine around”), as proposed by Osing; however, the meaning of this verb is not entirely clear.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;chiefly of people) to be(come) beautiful (+ <i>m</i> or <i>ẖr</i>: in (ornaments); + <i>ḥr</i>: in (a place); + <i>m</i>: as (someone)) &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;chiefly of people or rays of the sun) to be(come) pleasing (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to act in a pleasing manner, to be(come) cheerful, agreeable, friendly (+ <i>n</i> or <i>ḫr</i>: to (someone))</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) kind, benevolent, good (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone))</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive</i>) to turn back (+ <i>ḥr</i>: from)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come back, to return</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn back, to repel</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to render (plans, arrangements, etc.) ineffective or inoperable</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie up, to entwine &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the inundation) to flow in, to flood &lsqb;since the 22nd Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring (the inundation) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Anuket, personification of the Nile river.</li></ol><p><i>ꜥnq</i> (“to flow in, to flood”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw</li><li>nail, fingernail, toenail</li><li>talon of a bird</li><li>thumb &lsqb;Medical papyri and Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>carpenter’s adze &lsqb;Middle and New Kingdoms&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>jewelry</i>) ring &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>eyelet on a piece of jewelry &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>the smallest unit of time, smaller than a <i>ḥꜣt</i>&#59; approximately a tierce &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>an epithet of Hathor &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥnn</i> (“to turn back”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>From <i>ꜥnj</i> (“to be beautiful, pleasant, kind”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>, thus ‘the beautiful/pleasant/kind one’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anti, a falcon god.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anat, an Egyptian goddess of war and fertility.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrrh</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) few, to be(come) small in number</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dawn</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to live, to be(come) alive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(+ <i>m</i>) to subsist or live on (something)</li><li>(+ <i>m</i>) to dwell in (some place)</li><li>(+ <i>ḥr</i>) to occupy (a throne) or dwell in (a foreign land)</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the dead) to come to life or live again after death in an afterlife</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to ‘come to life’ (wake up or regain liveliness) upon the rising of the sun or upon seeing the sun</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to ‘come to life’ whenever one’s name is pronounced</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of a name) to still be remembered</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;especially&#32;of body parts) to be vigorous or full of vitality</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;medicine,&#32;of food) to be fresh</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;as a non-attributive relative/emphatic form) used in introductory formulae for oaths, usually with a king or god as subject&#58; As long as … lives, … &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>used at the beginning of inscriptions before the names of kings and gods&#58; originally ‘May … live’, later meaningless &lsqb;meaningless since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to swear (an oath); (with <i>m</i>) to swear by (someone) &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life (the state of being alive)</li><li>someone who lives, a living person or dweller</li><li>It is a piece of equipment illustrated among coffin offerings, at the foot end of the mummy, and depicted in the hieroglyph used to write its name. Possibilities include&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A sandal strap</li><li>An elaborate amuletic bow or knot</li><li>A sort of tied belt</li></ol><li>ankh (symbol of life)</li><li>(usually with <i>pꜣ</i> in Late Egyptian) oath, vow &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>prisoner of war &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>bouquet or garland of flowers, typically as an offering &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of beetle &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>an epithet of the sun &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>an epithet of the inundation &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>&lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>alabaster</li><li>something made from alabaster</li></ol><li>mirror &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a vessel in the form of an ankh, from which the gods pour life</li><li>a door leaf &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of document &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>sustenance, livelihood &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>billy-goat<sup>?</sup> &lsqb;19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>an honorific phrase used after the names of kings, queens, princes, etc., after the words of a king, ruler, or prince, after references to the royal household, and, more rarely, after the names of ordinary people.</li></ol><p>Traditionally considered to be a series of nouns <i>ꜥnḫ</i> +&lrm; <i>wḏꜣ</i> +&lrm; <i>snb</i>, literally meaning ‘life, prosperity, health (to him)’. More recently the phrase has instead been analyzed as a series of verbs, each with the unwritten stative ending <i>.w</i>, thus literally meaning ‘(may he be) alive, sound, healthy’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>an honorific phrase used after the nomen or praenomen of a (male) pharaoh.</li></ol><p><i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“to live”) +&lrm; <i>(.w)</i> (third-person masculine singular stative ending) +&lrm; <i>ḏt</i> (“forever”), literally meaning ‘alive forever’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ankhesenamun</li></ol><p><i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“to live”) +&lrm; <i>.s</i> (“she”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“for”) +&lrm; <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”), thus literally ‘she lives for Amun’. The written form demonstrates honorific transposition.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>an honorific phrase used after the names of queens and princesses.</li></ol><p><i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“to live”) +&lrm; <i>.tj</i> (third-person feminine singular stative ending), literally meaning ‘alive’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>an honorific phrase used after the nomen or praenomen of a (female) pharaoh.</li></ol><p><i>ꜥnḫ</i> (“to live”) +&lrm; <i>.tj</i> (third-person feminine singular stative ending) +&lrm; <i>ḏt</i> (“forever”), literally meaning ‘alive forever’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) pair of ears</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chin &lsqb;Middle & Late Egyptian&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to complain (+ <i>ḥr</i>: about)</li></ol><p>From Old Egyptian <i>jnꜥ</i> (“chin”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pass through (+ <i>ḥr</i>: to pass by)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fly &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare Hebrew <i>עָף</i> (ʿāp̄, “to fly”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to equip</li><li>(transitive,&#32;in the stative) to master (a skill or craft)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) equipped (+ <i>m</i>: to be equipped with)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beetle</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to enter (+ <i>m</i>: into; + <i>r</i>: to (a person); + <i>ḥr</i>: before or through; + <i>n</i>: to)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the sun) to set</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) monthly provisions of food, given both as salary and as rations</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) close friends, confidants</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ꜥq</i> (“to enter”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;hapax legomenon) ceremonial entrance of the king</li></ol><p><i>ꜥq</i> (“to enter”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of female servant &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of Hathor &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥq</i> (“to enter”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i> +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) correct</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) straight</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush reed</li><li>(reed) pen</li><li>pebble, including as used as a game piece &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>pit, stone of a fruit &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>stairway &lsqb;Theban royal tombs and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) goat &lsqb;since the Medical Papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>jꜥr</i> (“to ascend”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pack up or wrap up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to enclose</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to collect (offices or favors)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag, sachet</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tie on</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to complete, to stop</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to understand</li></ol><p>Sometimes proposed to be from a form such as Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;ʔar-ḳ-</i> (“to see, to understand”). Compare Proto-Cushitic <i>&#42;ʔark’-</i> (“to see, to understand”).</p><p>From a form of the verb above.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>last day of the year (as a festival), New Year's Eve</li></ol><p><i>ꜥrqy</i> (“last day of the month”) +&lrm; <i>rnpt</i> (“year”) in a direct genitive construction.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>last day of the month &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥrq</i> (“to tie on, to complete, to stop”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gate</li><li>doorleaf</li><li>hall of administration or judgement</li></ol><p>Connected by Loprieno to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;dalt-</i> (“door”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lower jaw, jawbone</li><li>scroll of papyrus</li><li>roll of leather</li><li>butt, rear end of a human &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>hindquarters of an animal &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>stem or stalk of a lotus plant &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥr</i> (“reed”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nearby area; vicinity, proximity, neighborhood</li></ol><p>From <i>jꜥr</i> (“to ascend, to approach”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to carry out, to execute (tasks and commands)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to produce or procure (an object)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to hire or appoint (people) for some task</li><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive&#32;with <i>r</i> or <i>r sꜣ</i>,&#32;of the inundation) to rise up over, to flood (fields) &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a kind of receptacle, perhaps a kind of basket</li><li>chin &lsqb;Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li><li>shrewmouse &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) A term relating to the renovation of divine barques. &lsqb;Saite Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥsṯrt</i> (“Astarte”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥsṯrt</i> (“Astarte”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Astarte &lsqb;since the New Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Semitic; compare Akkadian <i>𒀭𒈹</i> (Ištar, “Ishtar”), Ugaritic <i>𐎓𐎘𐎚𐎗𐎚</i> (ʿaṯtart, “Astarte”), and Hebrew <i>עַשְׁתֹּרֶת</i> (‘aštōreṯ, “Ashtoreth”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥsṯrt</i> (“Astarte”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limb</li><li>body part</li><li>room, chamber</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orchard, arbor</li></ol><p><i>ꜥt</i> (room) +&lrm; <i>nt</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ḫt</i> (“tree”), thus literally ‘room of tree’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughterhouse, abattoir, meat larder</li></ol><p><i>ꜥt</i> (“room”) +&lrm; <i>jwf</i> (“meat”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘chamber of meat’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school</li></ol><p><div>From <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">a&#58;t&#42;pr</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>ꜥt</i>, “room”) +&lrm; <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./1830a88b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="N14" alt="glyph" height="55"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./dc43dd89.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A24" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>sbꜣw</i>, “teaching”) in a direct genitive construction, thus literally ‘room of teaching’.</div></p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to press (a dough or mash) through a sieve or cloth, to strain, to sieve, especially in brewing beer or preparing medicines &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to press (grapes) for making wine</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) greedy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greedy person</li></ol><p><i>ꜥwn</i> (“to rob”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) four-legged animals in general, quadrupeds</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) sheep or goats, small livestock</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) flocks, herds</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robbery</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥwꜣj</i> (“to rob”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rob</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robber</li><li>the hippopotamus and crocodile, seen as enemies of Horus &lsqb;Greco-Roman period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Robber, a demon</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥwꜣj</i> (“to rob”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>&#32;or&#32;transitive) to summon (someone), to call (someone) over</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>) to call out to (someone)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>r</i>) to call on (a god) for aid, to invoke</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to specify, to provide (a name), to name names</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to read (something) aloud &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call, summons</li><li>a kind of coniferous tree whose wood and resin were used medicinally, traditionally rendered as ‘cedar’, likely the Cilician fir (<i>Abies cilicica</i>) &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>the wood of this tree</li><li>the resin of the <i>ꜥš</i>-tree</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) beer jug</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act as pilot of a ship</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilot of a ship</li></ol><p><i>ꜥš</i> (“to call”) +&lrm; <i>ḥꜣt</i> (“front, bow”), thus literally ‘to call the bow’; the noun is derived with the participle of <i>ꜥš</i>, thus literally ‘(the one) calling the bow’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to moan, to groan in pain &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥẖm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) many or much, to be(come) numerous or abundant</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to have many or much, to be(come) rich or abounding (+ <i>m</i>: in/with)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) of many kinds, varied, multifarious</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) common, usual, ordinary</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to happen often &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian) to be(come) too much, to be(come) excessive</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>ꜥšꜣ</i>: many, numerous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multitude</li><li>quantity, number</li><li>great extent or degree</li><li>lizard</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>often, many times</li><li>very</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʕaśar-</i> (“ten”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be(come) talkative, to chatter</li></ol><p><i>ꜥšꜣ</i> (“to be(come) numerous”) +&lrm; <i>r(ꜣ)</i> (“mouth, utterance”), literally ‘to be(come) numerous of mouths/utterances’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) multitude, crowd, company &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) the masses, the people</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥšꜣ</i> (“to be numerous”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) intact or whole</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reel in</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to learn about</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Adjib, a pharaoh of the First Dynasty</li></ol><p><i>ꜥḏ</i> (“whole”) +&lrm; <i>jb</i> (“heart”), thus ‘strong of heart’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>mꜥnḏt</i> (“the day-barque of Ra”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palace; building housing the king or his administration</li><li>temple (as the palace of a god)</li><li>celestial dwelling of a god</li><li>rope, cord (of a net or ferry) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to snare; to capture (game) with a net or rope</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to capture or seize (enemies, body parts, etc.)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to enclose (someone with one’s arms, a settlement with a ditch, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wipe off, to wipe away (moisture, tears, dirt, etc.)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry off after a bath</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet for Osiris &lsqb;New Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜥḥ</i> (“wiped away”) +&lrm; <i>rmyt</i> (“tears”), thus ‘(he) whose tears are wiped away’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple</li></ol><p><i>ꜥḥ</i> (“palace, temple”) +&lrm; <i>wr</i> (“big, great”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fight, battle</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) arrow</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) weapon in general</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;transitive</i>) to fight or make war (+ <i>ḥr</i>: for (the sake of); + <i>ḥnꜥ</i>: with, against; + <i>m</i>: by means of, with; + <i>r</i>: against)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a serekh name&#x20;notably borne by Hor-Aha, a pharaoh of the First Dynasty</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weapon</li></ol><p><i>ꜥḥꜣ</i> (“to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighter, warrior, soldier &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;often attributive) male &lsqb;since the 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>man, male human</li><li>gander</li><li>male god</li></ol></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Late Egyptian,&#32;intransitive) to be(come) quarrelsome, to quarrel (+ <i>m-dj</i>: to quarrel with (someone))</li></ol><p><i>ꜥḥꜣ</i> (“to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-wt</i> +&lrm; <i>-j</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stand up (+ <i>r</i>: to rise up against)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) present, to be in attendance</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) steadfast</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>ḥr</i>,&#32;Late Egyptian) to wait for (someone or something)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>n</i> or <i>ḥr</i>,&#32;Late Egyptian) to lack, to be missing (something), to need</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stela</li><li>heap, especially of riches or grain</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) masted ship &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>perfect of <i>ꜥḥꜥ</i></li><li>denotes subsequent action in a narrative; then</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>station, position</li><li>stance</li><li>stoppage, standstill</li><li>span of time, duration (+ <i>r</i>: duration of …, span lasting …)</li><li>lifetime</li><li>flotilla</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ꜥḥꜥ</i> (“to stand up”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise (something, especially the sky) up &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hang (someone)</li><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to raise oneself, to rise up, to ascend</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;of fleeing enemies) to leave, to slip away<sup>?</sup> &lsqb;20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥẖj</i> (“to fly, to fly away”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to quench, to extinguish (fire, thirst, wind, rebellion, etc.)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Late Egyptian,&#32;of water) to dry up</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fly (+ <i>m</i>: from, out of (a place); + <i>r</i>: to (a place)) &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fly away, to fly off &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fly to &lsqb;Greco-Roman period&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fly through (the sky) &lsqb;Greco-Roman period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>originally</i>) cultic image of a perching falcon</li><li>cultic image of a god in general</li><li>body of a god (as opposed to the god’s ba)</li><li>falcon</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(plural only,&#32;hapax legomenon) Some sort of images found in a royal morning audience hall.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sacred images, cultic images</li><li>paintings of animals and plants reacting to the rising of the sun</li></ol></ol><p><i>ꜥẖmw</i> (“falcons, cultic images”) +&lrm; <i>nw</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>ꜣḫt</i> (“place of sunrise”).</p></html>:/ʕoʔ/
<h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here</li><li>(<i>less common</i>) there</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfective active participle of <i>ꜥꜣj</i>: big, great, important</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greatness; prestige and power or importance &lsqb;since Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>a great deed (done to someone)</li><li>distinguished or respected person or god, great one</li><li>older person, elder</li><li>(<i>in titles</i>) leader, head (of something)</li><li>ass, donkey</li><li>doorleaf, single door</li><li>(<i>in the dual</i>) double door</li><li>sarcophagus lid</li><li>column, pillar</li><li>part of a ship: mast<sup>?</sup></li></ol><p>Possibly onomatopoeic, from the animal’s cry (hee-haw).</p><p>Sometimes compared to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;dalt-</i> (“door”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) foreman in charge of fifty men; pentecontarch</li></ol><p><i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great one”) +&lrm; <i>dyw</i> (“fifty”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) foreman in charge of ten men; decadarch, decurion</li></ol><p><i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great one”) +&lrm; <i>n</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>mḏw</i> (“ten”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of things) to be(come) pleasing or desirable &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) pleasant, agreeable &lsqb;Pyramid Texts to 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) self-pleasing, selfish &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make an offering (+ <i>n</i>: to (someone)) &lsqb;Late Period and Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of tree &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offerings, especially a large set of offerings to the gods, hecatomb</li><li>food for the dead in the afterworld</li><li>food, provisions, etc. in general</li><li>a kind of jug used for washing &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) self-pleasingness, selfishness</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥꜣb</i> (“to make an offering”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p>From <i>ꜥꜣb</i> (“to be pleasing, to be selfish”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of bread &lsqb;Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to thrash, to pound</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) physically big, large</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be(come) much, numerous, many in number or great in quantity</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of abstract things) to be(come) great in degree or magnitude, strong, extreme</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;of age) to be(come) high, great</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of strength and power) to be(come) strong, great, mighty</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of magic, protection, etc.) to be(come) strong, effective, great</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of time) to be(come) long</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people, gods, etc.) to be(come) older, senior, the elder among several</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people, names, etc.) to be(come) great, important, distinguished, exalted</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to be(come) superior, in charge</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of crimes) to be(come) grave, severe</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of words and decrees) to be(come) heavy, weighty, important</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the Nile) to be(come) high, swollen with floodwater, to be in inundation</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of festivals and funerals) to be(come) great, magnificent</li></ol><p>Considering hypotheses connecting Egyptian ⟨ꜥ⟩ to /d/, compare Proto-Afroasiatic <i>&#42;dâr-</i> (“to increase”), Arabic دَرَّ (darra, “to be abundant, plentiful”), and Old South Arabian <i>𐩵𐩧𐩧</i> (drr, “abundance, harvest”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A member of one of the foreign Asian peoples living north and east of the Egyptian borders; Asian, Semite &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>Syrian slave</li><li>herdsman &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>throwing stick, boomerang &lsqb;18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Semitic language. Compare Hebrew <i>עַם</i> (ʕam, “people, nation”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥm-mwt</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asian woman, Semitic woman &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to 18th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(female) Syrian slave</li></ol><p>From <i>ꜥꜣm</i> (“Semite”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine ending).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) a kind of basket</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach &lsqb;Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apep/Apophis, the evil being who tries to devour the sun every night, often depicted in the form of a vast water snake &lsqb;since the Book of the Dead&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aruna (an ancient Canaanite city)</li></ol><p>From Northwest Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>עִירוֹן</i> (ʿîrôn).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥsṯrt</i> (“Astarte”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ꜥsṯrt</i> (“Astarte”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precious stone or precious metal in general</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>gallstone</li><li>bladder stone</li></ol><li>stone vessel</li><li>great or important thing &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in particular</i>) significant difficult or bad thing</li><li>distinguished goddess, great one (as a title for goddesses)</li><li>swelling, lump &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>rule, dominion &lsqb;late 25th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>(female) donkey, she-ass &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and New Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>linen, linen cloth &lsqb;since the Old Kingdom&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of beverage &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><i>ꜥꜣj</i> (“to be(come) big, great, important”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p><p><i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“donkey”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i> (feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hard stone including sandstone and limestone</li></ol><p><i>ꜥꜣt</i> (“precious stone”) +&lrm; <i>rwḏt</i> (“firm”), thus ‘firm precious stone’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greatly</li></ol><p><i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“big”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i> (“adverbializing suffix”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spew, to ejaculate, to squirt (saliva, semen, etc.) &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beget, to produce &lsqb;Theban royal tombs&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semen &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li><li>a kind of noxious substance that acts as a factor of illness in the belly, heart, or limbs, said to be brought about by the dead or by malevolent gods &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) A symptom of illness relating to hair loss. &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><li>waterhole &lsqb;Pyramid Texts and Coffin Texts&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) strength, valor, potency &lsqb;11th and 20th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of the sun god&#58; ‘begetter’</li></ol><p><i>ꜥꜣꜥ</i> (“to ejaculate, to beget”) +&lrm; <i>-y</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spit, spittle, saliva &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>sweat &lsqb;Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Compare <i>ꜥꜣꜥ</i> (“to spew, to ejaculate; semen, noxious substance”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to comb (the hair) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jꜥb</i> (“small bowl”)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a kind of illness</li><li>a kind of remedy</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rate of payment</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) &lsqb;Middle Kingdom literature&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to wail</li><li>to jabber</li><li>to be fearful</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child &lsqb;Late Period&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Something undesirable which is to be kept away. &lsqb;Pyramid Texts&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ꜥꜥj</i> (“to wail, jabber, be fearful<sup>?</sup>”) +&lrm; <i>-w</i>, if not to be identified with <i>ꜥꜥw</i> (“sleep”).</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) accusation</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ꜣꜥꜥ</i> (“to accuse<sup>?</sup>, to harm<sup>?</sup>”) +&lrm; <i>-t</i>.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;always negated) to sleep &lsqb;Middle Kingdom to Greco-Roman Period&rsqb;</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of the heart) to flutter &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave of sleep</li></ol><p><i>ꜥꜥw</i> (“sleep”) +&lrm; <i>n(j)</i> (“of”) +&lrm; <i>qdd</i> (“sleep”), thus literally ‘a sleep of sleep’.</p></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of plant administered internally for medicinal purposes &lsqb;Medical papyri&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>z</i> (“man”).</li><li>(<i>Old Egyptian</i>) Logogram for <i>rmṯ</i> (“human, person”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>.j</i> (first person suffix pronoun).</li><li>(<i>Late Egyptian</i>) Logogram for <i>wj</i> (first person dependent pronoun).</li><li>Determinative for the relations, occupations, and personal names of men.</li><li>Determinative for the first person, as in <i>jnk</i>, <i>.kw</i>, <i>wj</i>.</li><li>Part of the combination <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">A1&#58;Z2</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, a logogram for <i>rḥw</i> (“comrades”) in certain personal names.</li><li>Part of the combination <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">A1&#42;B1&#58;Z2</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, a determinative for groups of people, as in <i>rmṯw</i> (“people”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for eating and drinking, as in <i>wnm</i> (“to eat”), <i>zwr</i> (“to drink”), <i>ḥqr</i> (“to be hungry”).</li><li>Determinative for speech and exclamations, as in <i>j</i> (“O&#33;&#59; to say”), <i>sḏd</i> (“to recount”), <i>gr</i> (“to be silent”).</li><li>Determinative for thinking and feeling, as in <i>kꜣj</i> (“to plan”), <i>mrj</i> (“to love”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>ḥmsj</i> (“to sit”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative meaning "supplicate", such as in <i>dwꜣ</i> (“praise”).</li><li>Determinative meaning "hide", such as in <i>sdgꜣ</i> (“be hidden”), and <i>jmn</i> (“hide”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for <i>jmn</i> (“hide”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for <i>jmn</i> (“hide”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for <i>wꜥb</i> (“pure, clean”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for <i>sṯ</i> (“to libate”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for <i>wꜥb</i> (“pure, clean”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>ḫfty</i> (“enemy”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for death, as in <i>mwt</i> (“to die”).</li><li>Determinative for enemies, as in <i>ḫftj</i> (“enemy”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A determinative for words with the sense of 'old,' 'distinguished.'</li><li>Biliteral phonogram for jk in the word <i>jky</i> (“miner”).</li><li>Ideogram for the words <i>jꜣk</i> (“old”), <i>smsw</i> (“eldest”), and <i>wr</i> (“great, chief”).</li><li>(<i>in hieratic</i>) Variant of the sign 𓀝.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for or determinative in <i>qꜣj</i> (“to be(come) high”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>jꜣs</i> (“to be(come) bald”).</li><li>Determinative for words relating to joy or happiness.</li><li>Determinative for words relating to mourning.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for "mummy", such as in <i>wj</i> (“mummy”).</li><li>Determinative for "statue" or "likeness", such as in <i>twt</i> (“statue”).</li><li>Determinative for "form" or "shape", such as in <i>qj</i> (“form”) and <i>ḫprw</i> (“forms, stages of growth”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>.j</i> (first-person suffix pronoun) when the speaker is female. &lsqb;since the 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>Determinative for the relations, occupations, and names of women and words associated with the female sphere in general.</li><li>Part of the combination <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">A1&#42;B1&#58;Z2</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, a determinative for groups of people, as in <i>rmṯw</i> (“people”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for pregnancy, as in <i>jwr</i> (“to conceive”), <i>bkꜣ</i> (“to be(come) pregnant”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>msj</i> (“to give birth”).</li><li>Determinative for giving birth, as in <i>msj</i> (“to give birth”), <i>pꜥpꜥ</i> (“to give birth”), etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𓁒</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./5e4b7e7e.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="B3" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>)</li><li>(rare,&#32;in a single hieratic manuscript) alternative form of <i>𓀂</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./aec981cf.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A3" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for nursing or breastfeeding, as in <i>mnꜥ</i> (“to nurse, to suckle”), <i>mnꜥt</i> (“wet nurse”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𓁔</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./687b6f63.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="B5" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>rnn</i> (“to bring up, to nurse”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in names of queens.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram or determinative in <i>rꜥ</i> (“the sun-god”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram or determinative in <i>rꜥ</i> (“the sun-god”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram or determinative in <i>ḏḥwtj</i> (“Thoth”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram or determinative in <i>ẖnmw</i> (“Chnum”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram or determinative in <i>ẖnmw</i> (“Chnum”) (same as <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
