</td></tr></table>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>km</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>wḥm-ꜥnḫ</i> (“(cyclically) repeating life”). &lsqb;since the 18th or 19th Dynasty&rsqb;</li><li>Determinative in <i>ḥqt</i> (“Heqat”), and in words for frog&#58; <i>qrr</i> (“frog”), <i>ꜥbḫn</i> (“frog”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for or determinative in <i>ḥfn</i> (“hundred thousand”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>ḥfnr</i> (“tadpole”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Uniliteral phonogram for <i>f</i>, as in <i>.f</i> and <i>f</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>ft</i> (“horned viper”).</li><li>Used with unclear signification in the word <i>jt</i> (“father”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Uniliteral phonogram for <i>ḏ</i>&#59; since Middle Egyptian sometimes used for <i>d</i>, as the sounds had merged in some phonetic environments.</li><li>Logogram for <i>ḏt</i> (“cobra”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>ḏḏ</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>jꜥrt</i> (“uraeus”).</li><li>Determinative in the names of goddesses and in the plural of <i>nṯrt</i> (“goddess”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for goddesses.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for snakes and worms, as in <i>ḥfꜣw</i> (“snake”) or <i>ḏdft</i> (“snake, worm”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𓆙</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./7d646c5f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="I14" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>)</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral for <i>jn</i>.</li><li>Determinative used in the words for various fish.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative used in the word bwt (“abomination”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>ꜥḏ</i> (used in the word <i>ꜥḏmr</i> (“district administrator”)).</li><li>Determinative used in the word <i>ꜥḏw</i> (“mullet”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>ẖꜣ</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>ẖꜣt</i> (“oxyrhynchus”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative used in words for fish.</li><li>Determinative used in <i>bzj</i> (“to introduce”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram for <i>nšmt</i> (“fish scale”).</li><li>Determinative used in <i>nšmt</i> (“fish scale”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative used in <i>špt</i> (“angry”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Triliteral phonogram for <i>ḫpr</i>, as in <i>ḫpr</i> (“to come into being”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>ḫprr</i> (“scarab beetle”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>ḫprr</i> (“scarab beetle”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Triliteral phonogram for <i>bjt</i>, as in <i>bjtj</i> (“King of Lower Egypt”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>bjt</i> (“bee&#59; honey”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>nswt-bjtj</i> (“King of Upper and Lower Egypt”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>ꜥff</i> (“fly”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>znḥm</i> (“locust”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>zpꜣ</i> (“centipede”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>ḫꜣ</i>, restricted in Middle Egyptian to use in <i>ḫꜣwt</i> (“altar”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>srqt</i> (“the goddess Serqet”).</li><li>Logogram for the name of King Scorpion (reading uncertain).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Phonogram for jm(ꜣ).</li><li>Determinative for "tree".</li><li>Determinative in <i>mꜥr</i> (“fortunate”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Variant of <i>𓆭</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for ḥn.</li><li>Determinative for "plant".</li><li>Determinative in <i>jzj</i> (“light”), <i>js</i> (“old”), and <i>jz</i> (“tomb”).</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) Used in place of 𓀀 both as a determinative and in first-person pronouns such as <i>.j</i> (“I”), based on phonological similarity with the word <i>j</i> (“reed”).</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) Used as a variant of 𓌤.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>ḫt</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>ḫt</i> (“wood”).</li><li>Determinative in words for types of wood, things made of wood, or the woody parts of plants.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram for <i>rnpt</i> (“year”) and <i>rnpt-ḥsb</i> (“regnal year”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>rnpj</i> (“young”) and <i>tr</i> (“time, season”).</li><li>When duplicated (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./0fad6de6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="M4" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./0fad6de6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="M4" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>), serves as an ideogram for <i>snf</i> (“last year”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in and ideogram for <i>tr</i> (“time, season”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in and ideogram for <i>tr</i> (“time, season”).</li><li>Determinative for some roots ending with the consonant sequences <b>tr</b> and <b>rj</b>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in and ideogram for <i>rnpj</i> (“young”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>šꜣ</i>.</li><li>Group-writing phonogram for <i>š</i>&#59; compare uniliteral <i>𓈙</i> (Gardiner number&#58; N37).</li><li>Logogram for <i>šꜣ</i> (“field, countryside, swamp”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>ꜣḫt</i> (“Akhet, inundation season”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative and ideogram for <i>zšn</i> (“lotus”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in and ideogram for <i>nḥbt</i> (“lotus bud”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in and ideogram for <i>wdn</i> (“to dedicate, offer”).</li><li>Sometimes substituted for <i>𓄲</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
