</td></tr></table> (<i>𓊌</i>).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𓈙</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𓈙</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𓈙</i></li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>šm</i>, as in <i>šmj</i> (“to go, walk”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <b>ḥm</b>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>pr</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>pr</i> (“house”).</li><li>Determinative for buildings and, less commonly, seats and places.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Uniliteral phonogram for <i>h</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>h</i> (“courtyard<sup>&#63;</sup>”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <b>nm</b> and <b>mr</b>.</li><li>Determinative in the word <i>mrrt</i> (“street”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Triliteral phonogram for <i>ḥwt</i>, as in <i>ḥwt</i> (“verse”), <i>ḥwtt</i> (“quarry”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>ḥwt</i> (“large building”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Triliteral and logogram for <i>jwn</i> (“pillar”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for ꜥꜣ.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Biliteral phonogram for <i>ꜥꜣ</i>.</li><li>Logogram for or determinative in <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“doorleaf”).</li><li>Determinative in words relating to opening something, as in <i>wn</i> (“to open”), <i>zn</i> (“to open”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Used to mark the name of a king, initially by being written next to it&#59; since the middle Naqada III period, it was instead attached to a rectangular enclosure that contained the royal name, particularly the Horus name. &lsqb;Since c. 3400 BCE&rsqb;</li><li>Logogram for <i>srḫ</i> (“serekh”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>srḫ</i> (“serekh”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>𓊁</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./363f3849.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O33" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>) used when enclosing the serekh name of a king in text written in horizontal rows rather than columns</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Uniliteral phonogram for <i>z</i>&#59; after Old Egyptian, this phoneme merged with <i>s</i>, so this glyph became an alternative phonogram for <i>s</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>z</i> (“door bolt&#59; being&#59; type of fish”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>ḫm</i> (“Letopolis”), by confusion with <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./243d5c24.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="R22" alt="glyph" height="12"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table> (<i>𓋉</i>). &lsqb;since the Middle Kingdom&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for or determinative in <i>jnb</i> (“wall”).</li><li>Determinative for walls, as in <i>wmtt</i> (“fortification, bulwark”), <i>snb</i> (“to overleap (a wall)”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>jnr</i> (“stone”).</li><li>Determinative for stone, as in <i>jnr</i> (“stone”), <i>ꜥꜣt</i> (“precious stone”), <i>dbn</i> (“deben, a measure of weight”), <i>ꜥr</i> (“pebble”), <i>ḏbt</i> (“brick”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for or determinative in <i>rwd</i> (“stairway”).</li><li>Logogram for or determinative in <i>ḫtw</i> (“platform, terrace, terraced hillside”).</li><li>Determinative for stairways and stepped constructions.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for words relating to ascent, as in <i>jꜥr</i> (“to ascend”), <i>qꜣy</i> (“ascent, high place”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative often used to denote walls, boundaries, or enclosed spaces, such as <i>šzp</i> (“to receive; palm of hand; statue, image”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>nwt</i> (“town, city”).</li><li>Determinative in names of settlements and inhabited places, as in <i>kmt</i> (“Egypt”), <i>wꜣst</i> (“Thebes”), <i>spꜣt</i> (“estate, farm”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>zp</i>, as in <i>zp</i> (“time”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>zpt</i> (“threshing floor”).</li><li>Used in <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">zp&#58;Z1&#42;Z1</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, a logogram for <i>zpwj snwj</i> (“twice”) that also functions as an iteration mark.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>jmw</i> (“boat”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>dpt</i> (“ship”) in <i>dpt-nṯr</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>ꜥḥꜥ</i> (“boat”) in <i>jmj-r ꜥḥꜥw</i>.</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) Logogram for <i>qꜣqꜣw</i> (“kind of riverboat”).</li><li>Determinative for boats and ships, as in <i>dpt</i> (“ship”) or <i>ḥꜥw</i> (“fleet”).</li><li>Determinative for verbs involving navigation and travel by water, as in <i>nꜥj</i> (“to travel by boat”) or <i>ḫdj</i> (“to go downstream”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Ideogram for <i>jst</i> (“seat, place”).</li><li>Biliteral phonogram for st.</li><li>Biliteral phonogram for js in the theonyms jsjr and jst.</li><li>Triliteral phonogram for ḥtm.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Uniliteral phonogram for <i>p</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>p</i> (“stool, socle”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for kp.</li><li>Triliteral phonogram for kꜣp.</li><li>Ideonogram for and determinative the word kꜣp (“fumigate”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Triliteral phonogram for nṯr.</li><li>Ideonogram for nṯr (“god”).</li><li>Determinative for god.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for ḏd (“pillar”).</li><li>(<i>when doubled</i>) Triliteral phonogram for ḏdw.</li><li>Ideogram for ḏd.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <b>ḫm</b>.</li><li>Ideogram for <i>mnw</i> (“the god Min”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Older variant of <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./243d5c24.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="R22" alt="glyph" height="12"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, the emblem of the god <i>mnw</i> (Min).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for sṯ.</li><li>Biliteral phonogram for sꜣṯ.</li><li>Determinative in and ideogram for the word tꜣ-wr (“starboard”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Uniliteral phonogram for <i>s</i>&#59; after Old Egyptian, as <i>s</i> and <i>z</i> merged, it could also be a phonogram for old <i>z</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>s</i> (“vessel of gold”).</li><li>Abbreviation for <i>snb</i> (“health”) in the formula <i>ꜥnḫ wḏꜣ snb</i> (“life, prosperity, health”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>ḥḏ</i>.</li><li>Logogram for <i>ḥḏ</i> (“mace”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <b>ḥḏ</b>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <b>ḥḏ</b>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Triliteral phonogram for <b>ḥḏḏ</b>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative for <i>ꜣqḥw</i> (“axe”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>tp</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <i>pḏ</i> or (<i>later</i>) <i>pd</i>, as in <i>pḏ</i> (“stretch”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>pḏt</i> (“bow”).</li><li>Determinative for words relating to bows.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in <i>jwnt</i> (“bow”).</li><li>Logogram for <i>pḏt</i> (“bow”). &lsqb;since the 12th Dynasty&rsqb;</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <b>rs</b>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Logogram for <i>qmꜣ</i> (“to throw, to create”).</li><li>Phonetic determinative for <i>ṯn</i>, as in <i>ṯnj</i> (“where”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>ꜥmꜥꜣt</i> (“throwstick”).</li><li>Determinative in <i>qmꜣ</i> (“to throw, to create”).</li><li>Determinative for foreign countries and people, as in <i>ṯḥnw</i> (“Libya”), <i>ꜥꜣm</i> (“Asiatic”), <i>nḥsj</i> (“Nubian”).</li><li>alternative form of <i>𓆱</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./bd9ece2b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="M3" alt="glyph" height="22"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>) as a phonetic determinative for <i>ḏꜥ</i>, as in <i>ḏꜥr</i> (“to seek out”).</li><li>alternative form of <i>𓊧</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./e5a4a72d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="P11" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>) in <i>mjnj</i> (“to moor”).</li><li>alternative form of <i>𓌘</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./0b8bfc81.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="T13" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>)</li><li>alternative form of <i>𓐨</i> (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./faa36e59.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Aa26" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>) in <i>sbj</i> (“rebel”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Determinative in and ideogram for <i>wrrt</i> (“chariot”).</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Triliteral phonogram for <b>šms</b>.</li></ol></html><h3>Egyptian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Biliteral phonogram for <b>qs</b>.</li><li>Determinative for "bone" and "tube" and in the words <i>qrs</i> (“to bury”) and <i>twr</i> (“pure”).</li><li>Ideogram for <i>gnwt</i> (“annals”) and <i>gnwtj</i> (“sculptor”). In the latter word, it is often doubled (<table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
